1 NOTE  MISC:   Adalric (or Ethic) obtained the Duchy of Alsace 662, d. 20 Feb . 6
2 CONC 90, head of the Alsatain House of Ethiconides, Duke of Alsace 662-6 90; M
2 CONC ayor of the Palace; deposed CHILDERIC III (751), the last of th e Faine
2 CONC ant (Merovingian) kings and became himself the first king of th e Frank
2 CONC s of the second race, 751-758; m. Berswinde, aunt to Count Wari nus and h
2 CONC is brother, St. Leger; Major of the Palce and Bishop of Autu n 663-677. A
2 CONC s king of Germany, known as PEPIN III; aided Pope Stephe n Ii against t
2 CONC he Lombards (754-755); fought against the pagan Saxons a nd forced them t
2 CONC o allow Christian missionaries to convert the.  CHARLE MAGNE, his son c
2 CONC ompleted their "conversion" to Christianity.  (aka PEP IN le BREF)  Pep
2 CONC in is depicted as an extremely brave man, not afraid o f taking on wild a
2 CONC nimals in circus games.  Shortly before his death, P epin the Short had d
2 CONC ivided his kingdom between his two sons, CHARLEMAG NE and CAROLMAN.  CA
2 CONC ROLMAN got Septimania, Toulousain, eastern Aquitai ne, Burgandy, Proven
2 CONC ce, Paris and Soissons, Rheims, Metz, Trier and St rasbourg; a coherent w
2 CONC hole, and fairly safe from invasion.  CHARLE's k ingdom was in the shap
2 CONC e of a crescent around that of CAROLMAN: it star ted at the Pyrenees, r
2 CONC eached the Garonne at Agen, covered all of weste rn France, Belgium and F
2 CONC risia, then turned east to take in, on the rig ht bank of the Rhine, He
2 CONC sse, Bavarian Nordgau and Thuringia.  The tw o brothers were crowned on t
2 CONC he same day, 9 Oct 768, CHARLES at Noyon a nd CAROLMAN at Soissons.  Wh
2 CONC en CAROLMAN had died suddenly in 771, CHAR LES had himself recognized b
2 CONC y CAROLMAN'S subjects as the recognized he ir, even though CAROLMAN had l
2 CONC eft two small children.  By this debatab le action, CHARLES reestablish
2 CONC ed unity and became the sole ruler of th is vast territory for Forty-th
2 CONC ree years, and extended it through new c onquests and shifting its cent
2 CONC er from Paris to Aix-la Chapelle, wher e he established a new capital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pepin the Short deposed Childerc III, the last of the Merovingian king s
2 CONC , in 750 by declaring him feeble-minded and putting him in a monaster y
2 CONC .  Pepin founded the Carolingian dynasty.  He aided Pope Stephen II a g
2 CONC ainst the Lombard (754-5).  He died 24 Sept 768 of a fever.
