1 NOTE  He was born, I think, in England in 1757 and came to this country a s a B
2 CONC ritish soldier during the REV war. When he saw what the British w ere d
2 CONC oing in this country he joined the American army. William HEBB ap plied f
2 CONC or a pension Nov.02,1818 in Preston County Virginia. Certificat e issue
2 CONC d July 03,1820 #17851-538022. Three years in the Virginia Conti nental l
2 CONC ine.
2 CONT
2 CONT !SOURCE: Documents include records from family bible in possession o f g
2 CONC .g.g.g.grandson, D.W.Garner.
2 CONT
2 CONT !SOURCE:  DAR records, microfilm #695780; elinsted in Westmoreland Cou n
2 CONC ty, Virginia, Mar. 17, 1777; served in Battle of Georgetown and Brand y
2 CONC wine; pension #17851-538022.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hebb was born in England in 1757 and died near Rowlesburg Wes t V
2 CONC irginia. in 1831, sometime between 1782 and 1790 he married Jemim a Was
2 CONC hington who was born near Fredricksburg on July 28, 1767. She wa s firs
2 CONC t married to a man named Smith Jenkins.Jemima was the daughter o f Thom
2 CONC as Washington as she was a niece of George Washington (4 times r emoved
2 CONC ). William Hebb came to this land as a soldier of the British Ar my and d
2 CONC eserted to join the Continental Army. He enlisted for three ye ars begi
2 CONC nning March 17, 1777, his regiment was the 3rd Virginia Dragoo ns under C
2 CONC apt. John Thorn, Lt John Bayless and Col. William Washingto n of Westmo
2 CONC reland County, Virginia. This unit was the life guard of Ge n. George W
2 CONC ashington.On August 24, 1777 the regiment marched thru Phil adelphia on t
2 CONC he way to the Battle of Brandywine Creek, after that defe at they fough
2 CONC t at Germantown and then at Valley Forge. That autumn the y were ordere
2 CONC d to Tapan NY and were surprised by a night attack and mo st of them we
2 CONC re killed. The remainder was sent south to the Carolinas . William was h
2 CONC onorably discharged at Bacon Bridge SC. March 18, 178 0 by Col. Willaim W
2 CONC ashington. He returned to western Virginia and spen t some time recuper
2 CONC ating as he had been wounded. Later on he volunteer ed his service to t
2 CONC he army to fight the Battle of Yorktown. In 1790 h e and Jemima returne
2 CONC d to western Virginia and settled on the west ban k of the Cheat River a
2 CONC bout two miles above Wolf Creek, now Preston Cou nty West Virginia.Will
2 CONC iam had been wounded three times and applied fo r a pension in 1808 and i
2 CONC t was granted July 3, 1820 for eight dollar s a month, he was then 62 y
2 CONC ears old. His service number was 17851 an d 38022. Invalid file records N
2 CONC ational Archives Washington D.C. Willia m and his beloved are laid to r
2 CONC est on the original Hebb farm about fiv e miles down the river below th
2 CONC e steel bridge on Rt 50 a few miles bel ow Rowlesburg, West Virginia. *
2 CONC copied from Hebb Family History by To m YocumWilliam was sent to Americ
2 CONC a during the Revolutionary War, a s a British soldier. After he landed, h
2 CONC e deserted and joined the Ameri cans. He was discharged from service be
2 CONC fore the close of the war, bu t when Cornwallis raided into Virginia, h
2 CONC e again took the field in com mon with the Virginia troops and was pres
2 CONC ent at the Battle of Yorktown , which resulted in the defeat of the Bri
2 CONC tish.* ref-History of Tucke r County. Fansler pg.415Two union sympathiz
2 CONC ers, taken prisoner by McCh esney in June, were still in Garnett's army
2 CONC . they were Sansome E. Pars ons and William Hebb. Parsons escaped. * re
2 CONC f-History of Tucker County . Fansler pg. 168Service Record of William H
2 CONC ebbWilliam Hebb born in En gland 1757, died in Preston County WVHebb se
2 CONC rved in the Continental li ne of Virginia. in American Revolution as pv
2 CONC t.*From the records of Nat ional Archives Washington D.C.William Hebb R
2 CONC ev. War file # 38022Name o f wife JemimaHebb enlisted in Westmoreland C
2 CONC ounty Virginia. March 1777 . Served in Capt. Thomas 3rd Reg. Light Drag
2 CONC oons(the life guard of Gen eral George Washington) He was discharged Ma
2 CONC rch 19, 1780 near Baton Br idge about 20 miles from Charleston South Ca
2 CONC rolina. His discharge wa s signed by Col. William Washington.He was in b
2 CONC attles of Brandywine an d Germantown. he was allowed a pension on his a
2 CONC pplication of Nov. 2, 1 818 while living in Preston County Virginia. (W V
2 CONC irginia) age 61 years .In 1820 he referred to his wife as being 63 year
2 CONC s old, name not state d in application. He referred to his sons as Robe
2 CONC rt age 20, and Rube n age 18. In papers he presented to Preston County V
2 CONC irginia. open cour t Aug. 5, 1820 gives his age as 62. States he is a r
2 CONC esident of the cou nty and a citizen of the U.S. as of the 18th of Marc
2 CONC h 1818. Stated hi s original declaration was made 11-2-1818 cert # 1758
2 CONC 1.He gives the fo llowing schedule: 2 horses, 12 head of cattle, 3 shee
2 CONC p, 2 sows wit h 8 pigs, 2 goats, my household furniture and farming imp
2 CONC lements are w orth about $20.00. I am in debt $110.00. I am by occupati
2 CONC on a laborer . My age renders me incapable of hard work. I have in fami
2 CONC ly myself, m y wife age 63, son Robert age 20 and Ruben age 18 years.* c
2 CONC opied fro m the DAR records, Washington D.C.Listed as pvt Virginia. sol
2 CONC dier pens ioned*DARWe are sometimes surrounded by bits of history that e
2 CONC ven thos e concerned do not realize.A few weeks ago I was chatting with M
2 CONC rs. Fr ances Ferguson about some people in the county and where most of t
2 CONC hie r ancestors came from. Frances of course is an authority on this so
2 CONC r t of thing, having made genealogy her lifetime hobby. She really doe s a g
2 CONC ood job and can come up with something relating to about anyone i n the a
2 CONC rea. She was telling me about one name that she had come acros s in her s
2 CONC tudies and it led to an interesting story. One I think man y people in t
2 CONC he area would be interested to hear.Billy was born in Eng land in 1757. L
2 CONC ike many of his descendants he liked to see what was o n the other side o
2 CONC f the hill and at an early age enlisted in the Briti sh Army and soon f
2 CONC ound himself in the Colonies. We do not know whethe r he was discharged f
2 CONC rom the army here but we do know that he joined t he Continental Army u
2 CONC nder Gen. George Washington and rode in Gen. Wash ington's life guard. H
2 CONC e was an excellent horseman and was with the cav alry at the Battle of C
2 CONC owpens in South Carolina, Billy had been wounde d and Col. William Wash
2 CONC ington's horse had been shot. Col. Washington t ook Billy's horse and w
2 CONC as riding it when he cut the fingers off the Br itish General's hand.Bi
2 CONC lly was wounded three times during the war. H e was honorably discharge
2 CONC d in South Carolina by Col. Washington at th e end of the war. When Bil
2 CONC ly was 60 years old he applied for a pension .When asked if he could pr
2 CONC ove if he had been wounded in the war, Bill y pulled off his shirt and s
2 CONC howed them the three musket ball holes thr ough his body. He received a p
2 CONC ension of $8 a month!!!After the war Bil ly settled in Westmoreland Cou
2 CONC nty Virginia. There he met and fell in l ove with a young widow who was h
2 CONC is own age. The young widow returned B illy's love and so he and Jemima w
2 CONC ere married.Jemima was from one of t he old aristocratic families of Vi
2 CONC rginia but she was adamantly agains t slavery. She freed all the slaves i
2 CONC n her possession and she and Bill y decided to go to the new frontier b
2 CONC eyond the Blueridge Mountains. Th ey settled in the area known as Seven I
2 CONC slands on Cheat River. They the n settled down to a frontier life and s
2 CONC tarted rearing thier family. Fi ve children were born to them, four boy
2 CONC s and one girl. Thomas, Robert , John Reuben and Nancy. As the children g
2 CONC rew up they married and sett led in the area and nearby counties, becom
2 CONC ing farmers, woodsmen and on e school teacher.Billy and Jemima are buri
2 CONC ed at the mouth of Wolf Cree k where it empties into Cheat River near w
2 CONC here US Rt. 50 comes down ov er the mountain near the river.By the way t
2 CONC he name of the young soldie r was William Hebb and his wife was Jemima W
2 CONC ashington Jenkins. Befor e her first marriage her name was Jemima Washi
2 CONC ngton, daughter of Thoma s Washington and niece of Gen. George Washingt
2 CONC on. So if any of you " r idge runners" can trace your ancestry back to W
2 CONC illiam and Jemima you c an claim Gen. George Washington as your Great U
2 CONC ncle and his brother an d niece as your great- grandfather and grandmot
2 CONC her.*copied from The Ol d Ridge Runner(the copy does not tell what coun
2 CONC ty or town it is from a nd does not show a date)
