2 CONT William Hebb was born in England in 1757 and died near Rowlesburg Wes t V
2 CONC irginia. in 1831, sometime between 1782 and 1790 he married Jemim a Was
2 CONC hington who was born near Fredricksburg on July 28, 1767. She wa s firs
2 CONC t married to a man named Smith Jenkins.
2 CONT Jemima was the daughter of Thomas Washington as she was a niece of Geo r
2 CONC ge Washington (4 times removed). William Hebb came to this land a s a s
2 CONC oldier of the British Army and deserted to join the Continental A rmy. H
2 CONC e enlisted for three years beginning March 17, 1777, his regimen t was t
2 CONC he 3rd Virginia Dragoons under Capt. John Thorn, Lt John Bayles s and C
2 CONC ol. William Washington of Westmoreland County, Virginia. This u nit was t
2 CONC he life guard of Gen. George Washington.
2 CONT On August 24, 1777 the regiment marched thru Philadelphia on the way t o t
2 CONC he Battle of Brandywine Creek, after that defeat they fought at Ger man
2 CONC town and then at Valley Forge. That autumn they were ordered to Tap an N
2 CONC Y and were surprised by a night attack and most of them were kille d. T
2 CONC he remainder was sent south to the Carolinas. William was honorabl y di
2 CONC scharged at Bacon Bridge SC. March 18, 1780 by Col. Willaim Washin gton
2 CONC . He returned to western Virginia and spent some time recuperatin g as h
2 CONC e had been wounded. Later on he volunteered his service to the a rmy to f
2 CONC ight the Battle of Yorktown. In 1790 he and Jemima returned t o western V
2 CONC irginia and settled on the west bank of the Cheat River abo ut two mile
2 CONC s above Wolf Creek, now Preston County West Virginia.
2 CONT William had been wounded three times and applied for a pension in 180 8 a
2 CONC nd it was granted July 3, 1820 for eight dollars a month, he was th en 6
2 CONC 2 years old. His service number was 17851 and 38022. Invalid fil e reco
2 CONC rds National Archives Washington D.C. William and his beloved ar e laid t
2 CONC o rest on the original Hebb farm about five miles down the riv er below t
2 CONC he steel bridge on Rt 50 a few miles below Rowlesburg, Wes t Virginia. *
2 CONC copied from Hebb Family History by Tom Yocum
2 CONT
2 CONT William was sent to America during the Revolutionary War, as a Britis h s
2 CONC oldier. After he landed, he deserted and joined the Americans. He w as d
2 CONC ischarged from service before the close of the war, but when Cornw alli
2 CONC s raided into Virginia, he again took the field in common with th e Vir
2 CONC ginia troops and was present at the Battle of Yorktown, which res ulted i
2 CONC n the defeat of the British.* ref-History of Tucker County. Fan sler pg
2 CONC .415
2 CONT
2 CONT Two union sympathizers, taken prisoner by McChesney in June, were stil l i
2 CONC n Garnett's army. they were Sansome E. Parsons and William Hebb. Pa rso
2 CONC ns escaped. * ref-History of Tucker County. Fansler pg. 168
2 CONT
2 CONT Service Record of William Hebb
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hebb born in England 1757, died in Preston County West Virgini a
2 CONT Hebb served in the Continental line of Virginia. in American Revolutio n a
2 CONC s pvt.
2 CONT *From the records of National Archives Washington D.C.
2 CONT William Hebb Rev. War file # 38022
2 CONT Name of wife Jemima
2 CONT
2 CONT Hebb enlisted in Westmoreland County Virginia. March 1777. Served in C a
2 CONC pt. Thomas 3rd Reg. Light Dragoons(the life guard of General George W a
2 CONC shington) He was discharged March 19, 1780 near Baton Bridge about 2 0 m
2 CONC iles from Charleston South Carolina. His discharge was signed by Co l. W
2 CONC illiam Washington.
2 CONT He was in battles of Brandywine and Germantown. he was allowed a pensi o
2 CONC n on his application of Nov. 2, 1818 while living in Preston County V i
2 CONC rginia. (W Virginia) age 61 years.
2 CONT In 1820 he referred to his wife as being 63 years old, name not state d i
2 CONC n application. He referred to his sons as Robert age 20, and Rube n age 1
2 CONC 8. In papers he presented to Preston County Virginia. open cour t Aug. 5
2 CONC , 1820 gives his age as 62. States he is a resident of the cou nty and a c
2 CONC itizen of the U.S. as of the 18th of March 1818. Stated hi s original d
2 CONC eclaration was made 11-2-1818 cert # 17581.
2 CONT He gives the following schedule: 2 horses, 12 head of cattle, 3 sheep , 2 s
2 CONC ows with 8 pigs, 2 goats, my household furniture and farming impl ement
2 CONC s are worth about $20.00. I am in debt $110.00. I am by occupatio n a l
2 CONC aborer. My age renders me incapable of hard work. I have in famil y mys
2 CONC elf, my wife age 63, son Robert age 20 and Ruben age 18 years.* c opied f
2 CONC rom the DAR records, Washington D.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as pvt Virginia. soldier pensioned*DAR
2 CONT
2 CONT We are sometimes surrounded by bits of history that even those concern e
2 CONC d do not realize.
2 CONT A few weeks ago I was chatting with Mrs. Frances Ferguson about some p e
2 CONC ople in the county and where most of thier ancestors came from. Franc e
2 CONC s of course is an authority on this sort of thing, having made geneal o
2 CONC gy her lifetime hobby. She really does a good job and can come up wit h s
2 CONC omething relating to about anyone in the area. She was telling me a bou
2 CONC t one name that she had come across in her studies and it led to a n in
2 CONC teresting story. One I think many people in the area would be inte rest
2 CONC ed to hear.
2 CONT Billy was born in England in 1757. Like many of his descendants he lik e
2 CONC d to see what was on the other side of the hill and at an early age e n
2 CONC listed in the British Army and soon found himself in the Colonies. W e d
2 CONC o not know whether he was discharged from the army here but we do k now t
2 CONC hat he joined the Continental Army under Gen. George Washington a nd ro
2 CONC de in Gen. Washington's life guard. He was an excellent horsema n and w
2 CONC as with the cavalry at the Battle of Cowpens in South Carolina , Billy h
2 CONC ad been wounded and Col. William Washington's horse had bee n shot. Col
2 CONC . Washington took Billy's horse and was riding it when he c ut the fing
2 CONC ers off the British General's hand.
2 CONT Billy was wounded three times during the war. He was honorably dischar g
2 CONC ed in South Carolina by Col. Washington at the end of the war. When B i
2 CONC lly was 60 years old he applied for a pension.When asked if he coul d p
2 CONC rove if he had been wounded in the war, Billy pulled off his shir t and s
2 CONC howed them the three musket ball holes through his body. He rec eived a p
2 CONC ension of $8 a month!!!
2 CONT After the war Billy settled in Westmoreland County Virginia. There h e m
2 CONC et and fell in love with a young widow who was his own age. The you ng w
2 CONC idow returned Billy's love and so he and Jemima were married.
2 CONT Jemima was from one of the old aristocratic families of Virginia but s h
2 CONC e was adamantly against slavery. She freed all the slaves in her poss e
2 CONC ssion and she and Billy decided to go to the new frontier beyond th e B
2 CONC lueridge Mountains. They settled in the area known as Seven Island s on C
2 CONC heat River. They then settled down to a frontier life and starte d rear
2 CONC ing thier family. Five children were born to them, four boys an d one g
2 CONC irl. Thomas, Robert, John Reuben and Nancy. As the children gre w up th
2 CONC ey married and settled in the area and nearby counties, becomin g farme
2 CONC rs, woodsmen and one school teacher.
2 CONT Billy and Jemima are buried at the mouth of Wolf Creek where it emptie s i
2 CONC nto Cheat River near where US Rt. 50 comes down over the mountain n ear t
2 CONC he river.
2 CONT By the way the name of the young soldier was William Hebb and his wif e w
2 CONC as Jemima Washington Jenkins. Before her first marriage her name wa s J
2 CONC emima Washington, daughter of Thomas Washington and niece of Gen. G eor
2 CONC ge Washington. So if any of you " ridge runners" can trace your anc est
2 CONC ry back to William and Jemima you can claim Gen. George Washingto n as y
2 CONC our Great Uncle and his brother and niece as your great- grandfa ther a
2 CONC nd grandmother.*copied from The Old Ridge Runner(the copy does n ot tel
2 CONC l what county or town it is from and does not show a date)
