1 NAME Golm (Gorm) "the old" Dem /Gammel/
2 GIVN Golm (Gorm) "the old" Dem
2 SURN Gammel
1 SEX M
1 _UID BFDB869C441548249EA2CB1378B3C1A934D3
1 _AMTID 162143273945:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  CONFLICT:  Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists Who Came to New Englan d b
2 CONC etween 1623 and 1650, Frederick Lewis Weis, Genealogical Publishin g Co
2 CONC unty, Inc., 1992 (states GOLM)   Chapman Family History, Beaucham p Wil
2 CONC liam Chapman, private publishing County, 1987 (states GORM)
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  King of Denmark and East Anglia
2 CONT
2 CONT MISC:  Gorm, the Old, so called from the length of his reign. He marri e
2 CONC d the beautiful Thyra Dannebod (Ornament of Denmark), daughter of Har o
2 CONC ld Klak. They had twin sons, Knud and Harold, rivals in glory. Knud w a
2 CONC s the favorite of his father, and had been absent sometime, and the K i
2 CONC ng fearing his death had vowed to kill the one that brought the new s o
2 CONC f his death. Finally the notice of his death was given and the Quee n, n
2 CONC ot risking to tell it to the King, made all the courtiers observ e an u
2 CONC nusual silence at the table and had the apartment covered with b lack. G
2 CONC uessing the reason, Gorm cried out: "Surely Knud, my dear son , is dead a
2 CONC s all Denmark is mourning." "Thou sayest so, not I." answer ed the Quee
2 CONC n; upon which the King sickened with grief and died in a go od old age, i
2 CONC n 941.
2 CONT
2 CONT *******ALIAS: Gorm "The Old" Haraldsson, King of Denmark
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate name found in file: Gorm "The Old" den Gamle, King of Denmar k
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm the Old (Knut I) - unified Denmark; died about 940.  (Ancestral R o
2 CONC ots & Complete Peerage say 936).
2 CONT
2 CONT A man whose soul was ever hostile to religion.  Thus did the Danish Hi s
2 CONC torian Saxo sum up the life of Gorm the old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm the old or Gorm den Gamle, appeared on the scene in the late nint h c
2 CONC entury and began unifying the country of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm's parentage is somewhat obscure, although we know that his fathe r w
2 CONC as Hardknut.  As we have seen, Snorri tells us that this Hardeknu t was t
2 CONC he son of Sigurd Orm-i-Oga, who in turn was the youngest son o f the le
2 CONC gendary Ragnar Lodbrok.  [Note: I have Gorm as great-grandso n of Harde
2 CONC canute, as I believe there were many generations between Rag nar Lodbro
2 CONC ok and Gorm.  I believe that "British Kings & Queens", "Roya lty for Co
2 CONC mmoners" and several other books support my ancestry.  Compl ete Peerag
2 CONC e states that Gorm assumed the throne in 899, which implie s his father
2 CONC 's death date.]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to other sources, Gorm's father Hardeknut came from Norway . W
2 CONC hile many Norwegian Vikings were seeking their fortune in the west , Ha
2 CONC rdeknut (according to this version) turned east and conquered th e Dani
2 CONC sh island of Sjaelland.  With little difficulty, he ousted the r ightfu
2 CONC l heir to the throne, a young man named Siegric, and assumed th e rule o
2 CONC f the island.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Hardeknut died, his son Gorm was accepted as king by the local in h
2 CONC abitants.  Gorm is sometimes given the double name Hardknut Gorm, an d b
2 CONC ecause of the Knut in his name, he may be listed as Knut I in the l ist
2 CONC s of Danish kings.  The root orm seen in the name Gorm (and in ou r Eng
2 CONC lish word "worm") means serpent, and it is found in Orm-i-Oga ("S nake E
2 CONC yes") and other royal names of the day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm was not content to rule only one small island, but began to exten d h
2 CONC is kingdom by conquest and barter.  By the end of his reign he ha d uni
2 CONC fied Jutland, Schleswig, many islands, part of Holstein, and som e prov
2 CONC inces of Norway and Sweden under his rule.  Thus Denmark under G orm wa
2 CONC s larger than the Denmark we know today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm was every inch a Viking, and his subjects were given to the wors t k
2 CONC ing of piracy.  The Danes plundered in Smolensk and Kiev in Russia .  I
2 CONC n 882 they journeyed to Aix-la-Chapelle where Karl the Great (Char lema
2 CONC gne) was buried, and stole the gold and silver decorations from hi s to
2 CONC mb. Over and over again, they harried France, and each time the in effe
2 CONC ctual Carolingian king Charles the Fat was forced to pay them grea t qu
2 CONC antities of silver in return for their agreeing to leave. . . .
2 CONT
2 CONT After Gorm and Tyra died, their son Harald Bluetooth erected at Jellin g t
2 CONC he largest Viking monument known.  It includes two rune stones, tw o hu
2 CONC ge mounds, the royal grave, and a church.  The inscription on th e larg
2 CONC er rune stone was intended not only to honor Harald's parents, b ut als
2 CONC o to make sure his own fame endured.  It reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT "King Harald commanded this monument to be made in memory of Gorm, hi s f
2 CONC ather, and in memory of Thorvi (Thyra) his mother - that Harald wh o wo
2 CONC n the whole of Denmark for himself, and Norway, and made the Dane s Chr
2 CONC istian."
2 CONT
2 CONT [Royal Families of Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev]
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  Having quite a different ancestry for Gorm from the above, whic h t
2 CONC hey admit is obscure.  The only thing in common with the ancestry g ive
2 CONC n above is that Gorm came from the island of Sjaelland, which his f ath
2 CONC er was King of.    However parents listed were Harald "Parcus" Frod oss
2 CONC on and Elfgifu Princess Wessex.
2 CONT
2 CONT ******
