2 CONT MISC:  The history of Fosters goes back well over a thousand years.  T h
2 CONC e name originated in Flanders during the early eight hundreds.  Fland e
2 CONC rs today has lost its national identity and simply makes up two of th e p
2 CONC rovinces of Belgium. However, for centuries it was the forefront o f Eu
2 CONC ropean politics and commerce, far overshadowing England and rivali ng F
2 CONC rance and the Roman empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard FOrester (or Forster) was surnamed "the Frislander", proba b
2 CONC ly in reference to his place of origin in the Norman-French area of F r
2 CONC isia, or his holdings there.  His sister Maud married William to Conq u
2 CONC eror; their father is Count Baldwin V of Flanders; their mother is Ad e
2 CONC la, dau. of King Robert of France, who d. 20 Jul 1031.  "Something no t w
2 CONC ell known is that Richard had two children out of wedlock, Nicholas , d
2 CONC . 1092, abbot of Saint-Sudouin, and a daughter who married the VIsc oun
2 CONC t of Bayeux.  Richard was poisioned by the men of his brother, wh o did n
2 CONC ot get the name "The Devil" for nothing".  The men of Richard i n turn m
2 CONC urdered Robert while on a pilgrimage to St. James de Compostel la, in S
2 CONC pain, not going to the Holy Land.  Also...Robert and Henerret a [sic] o
2 CONC f Falaise were married by the old way not that of the curch a nd so was n
2 CONC ever seen by the church as legal".
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  Accompanied his father and William the Conqueror in the B a
2 CONC ttle of Hastings and was knighted after the battle, given large lan d h
2 CONC oldings and settled in Scotland.
