1 NOTE  For many years it was assumed that Gundred, who married William de War r
2 CONC ene, was a daughter of William I and Matilda (as indicated in The Pla n
2 CONC tagenet Ancestry).  However it is now known that Gundred was a daught e
2 CONC r of Gherbod the Fleming (as indicated in Ancestral Roots).  The foll o
2 CONC wing information strongly suggests that Gundred's mother was Matild a (
2 CONC thus the mistaken notion that she was daughter of William I).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------- -
2 CONC --------------------
2 CONT copied from Bill Crawford's ancestry: crawfolk data base on World Conn e
2 CONC ct Project, rootsweb.com
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------- -
2 CONC --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Had Matilda of Flanders as many husbands as Adelaide, Countess of Pont h
2 CONC ieu, and, like her, issue by each&#00063; What was the real cause o f t
2 CONC he inhibition of her marriage with William, Duke of Normandy, 6 it s de
2 CONC lay for six years&#00063; What truth is there in the story of he r unre
2 CONC turned affection for the Angio-Saxon Brihtric Meaw, and of her v indict
2 CONC ive conduct to him after she became Queen of England&#00063; I h ave he
2 CONC sitated to believe in the popular tradition that Duke William gr ossly a
2 CONC ssaulted the daughter of Baldwin in the street or in her own ch amber, n
2 CONC ot that I have any doubt about his being capable of such an ou trage, b
2 CONC ut because he was too politic to commit it, and she was not th e woman t
2 CONC o have forgiven it, assuming that the offence was the simpl e refusal o
2 CONC f his hand on the ground of his illegitimacy. It is obvious , however, t
2 CONC hat the early life of Matilda is involved in mystery, an d it is highly p
2 CONC robable that a clearer insight into it would enable u s to account for m
2 CONC uch which we now reject as legend, or fail to reconc ile with acknowled
2 CONC ged facts. If there be any foundation for the stor y of William's bruta
2 CONC lity, the outburst of ungovernable fury might hav e been due to a much g
2 CONC reater provocation than has been assigned for it . Brihtric, the son of A
2 CONC lgar or Alfar, sumamed Meaw (Snow), from the e xtreme fairness of his c
2 CONC omplexion, an Anglo-Saxon Thegn, possessor o f large domains in England
2 CONC , had been sent on an embassy from King Edwa rd the Confessor to the Co
2 CONC nnt of Flanders. Matilda, we are told, fel l desperately in love with h
2 CONC im, and offered herself to him in marriage ! Either disgusted by her fo
2 CONC rwardness, or preferring another, he decli ned the flattering proposal. "
2 CONC Hell hath no fury like a woman foiled, " and she kept her wrath warm ti
2 CONC ll she was in a position to ruin the m an she had so passionately loved
2 CONC . She had no sooner become the Queen o f England than she induced Willi
2 CONC am to confiscate, on some pretence, al l Brihtric's estates, and obtain
2 CONC ed the greater proportion for herself . The unfortunate Thegn was arres
2 CONC ted at his house at Hanley, in Worces tershire, on the very day Saint W
2 CONC ulfstan had consecrated a chapel of h is building, dragged to Wincheste
2 CONC r, and died in a dungeon! The truth o f this story is supported by the i
2 CONC mpartial evidence of Domesday, in wh ich Hanley and the principal manor
2 CONC s held by Brihtric in the time of Ki ng Edward are recorded as the poss
2 CONC essions of Queen Matilda, and the re mainder passed to Fitz Hamon.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her hand had been rejected by the noble Saxon, it is presumed sh e b
2 CONC ecame the wife of a Fleming, named Gherbod, who appears to have hel d t
2 CONC he hereditary office of Advocate of the Abbey of Saint Bertin, in S t. O
2 CONC mers, and by whom she had at least two children, viz., Gherbod, t o who
2 CONC m William gave the earldom of Chester, and Gundred, "the sister o f Ghe
2 CONC rbod," and wife of William de Warren. Was this a clandestine or a n inf
2 CONC ormal marriage, which, as it has never been acknowledged by any c hroni
2 CONC cler, contemporary or other, might have been unknown to the Duk e of No
2 CONC rmandy, when he proposed to one whom he believed to be the maid en daug
2 CONC hter of the Count of Flanders, and the corporal chastisement in flicted
2 CONC , however unworthy of a man, passed over, sub silentio, for pru dential r
2 CONC easons, by the parties wlio had been guilty of a disgracefu l suppressi
2 CONC on of facts&#00063; The subsequent marriage under such circ umstances w
2 CONC ill awaken no surprise in any one who has studied the chara cter of Wil
2 CONC liam. Utterly unscrupulous, destitute of every generous, no ble, or del
2 CONC icate feeling, every action of his life was dictated by POL ICY alone. A
2 CONC n alliance with the Count of Flanders might be considere d by the craft
2 CONC y schemer sufficiently advantageous to warrant his overl ooking any obj
2 CONC ectionable antecedents in the conduct of a granddaughte r of a king of F
2 CONC rance, his first discovery of which had provoked his s avage nature int
2 CONC o a momentary ebullition of fury. Her being the mothe r of two children w
2 CONC as a point in her favour with a man whose sole moti ve for marrying was t
2 CONC he perpetuation of a dynasty, and the fair prospe ct of legitimate issu
2 CONC e, in whose veins the blood of the Capets shoul d enrich that of the Fu
2 CONC rrier of Falaise, would overcome any hesitatio n at espousing the widow o
2 CONC f an Advocate of St. Bertin. On the other ha nd, Count Baldwin would be t
2 CONC oo happy to embrace the opportunity of rei nstating his daughter in a p
2 CONC osition befitting her birth, and, as wel l as the lady herself, gladly c
2 CONC ondone past insults for future advantag es and the hope of smothering, i
2 CONC n the splendour of a ducal wedding, th e awkward whispers of scandal.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have said thus much simply to show the view that may be taken of the s
2 CONC e mysterious circumstances, in opposition to the rose-coloured repres e
2 CONC ntations of some modern historians, who, upon no stronger evidence, e l
2 CONC evate the Conqueror into a model husband, and describe Matilda as th e p
2 CONC erfection of womankind.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------- -
2 CONC --------------------Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (121:23), (162:23) , (
2 CONC 169:23).
2 CONT An interesting story is told in Cokayne's "Complete Peerage"(Glouceste r
2 CONC ). According to the Tewkesbury Abbey Chronicle, Brictric, sonof Algar , o
2 CONC therwise known as Brictric Mawr, a great thegn of the time ofEdwar d th
2 CONC e Confessor, held, with Tewkesbury, various lordships inWorcester , Glo
2 CONC ucester, Somerset, and other counties. Maud [Matilda],afterward s wife o
2 CONC f WILLIAM THE CONQUERER, in her youth wished tomarry Brictric , who ref
2 CONC used her. When she became Queen, sheimprisoned him, and on hi s death s
2 CONC hortly afterwards his manors, the
2 CONT (later) honour of Gloucester, were given to her.
2 CONT For many years it was assumed that Gundred, who married William de War r
2 CONC ene, was a daughter of William I and Matilda (as indicated in The Pla n
2 CONC tagenet Ancestry).  However it is now known that Gundred was a daught e
2 CONC r of Gherbod the Fleming (as indicated in Ancestral Roots).  The foll o
2 CONC wing information strongly suggests that Gundred's mother was Matild a (
2 CONC thus the mistaken notion that she was daughter of William I).
