2 CONT MISC:  Robert de Montgomery, eldest son, sometimes called "Robert of B e
2 CONC lesme,"the town in Perche in Normandy where he had great possessions . H
2 CONC e had the great inheritance of his father and mother in Normandy, w as C
2 CONC ount of Belesme and Alencon and in England was Earl of Arundel an d Shr
2 CONC ewsbury and other manors of his father. It is impossible to do fu ll ju
2 CONC stice to the character and life of Robert in these notes. He wa s knigh
2 CONC ted by King William at the seige of Fresney in 1073 in recognit ion of h
2 CONC is valorous and chivalrous conduct during the seige. He was be tween 45 a
2 CONC nd 50 in 1098, when at his brother's death he succeeded to t he inherit
2 CONC ance. He was the oldest, but for some reason his brother Hug h succeede
2 CONC d. He took the part of Robert of Normandy, eldest son of Wil liam I, ag
2 CONC ainst King HenryI, and because of his rebellion against Henr y he was f
2 CONC orced to forfeit his possessions in England when retiring in to Normand
2 CONC y, and continuing his turbulent practices there he was seize d, impriso
2 CONC ned, and the next year brought over to England, where he wa s confined a
2 CONC t the Castle of Warham in the County of Dorset and starve d himself to d
2 CONC eath. He was accounted the richest and most wicked man o f the age he l
2 CONC ived in. If wanting in the wisdom and prudence of his fa ther, he proba
2 CONC bly surpassed him in boldness and valour. A true soldier , he was ever r
2 CONC eady to face danger and adventure and apparently neve r happy unless in t
2 CONC he turmoil and horrors of war. This ended the Hous e of Montgomery in E
2 CONC ngland as a powerit had been earlier.ALIAS: Rober t II de Belleme
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Belleme and Alecon.
2 CONT Exiled 1102 from England by Henry I and lost English Lands.Alternate n a
2 CONC me found in file: Robert II De Belleme
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Belleme and Alecon.
2 CONT Exiled 1102 from England by Henry I and lost English Lands.
2 CONT Shrewsbury, Earldom of: Under the system then prevailing the Earldom p a
2 CONC ssed to an elder brother, Robert de Belleme, who constructed Bridgnor t
2 CONC h Castle and continued the family policy of harrying the Welsh.  He r e
2 CONC belled against Henry I and in 1102 was deprived of the Earldom of Shr e
2 CONC wsbury/Shropshire, together with his English and Welsh estates. [Burk e
2 CONC 's Peerage, p. 2604]
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT According to Winston Churchill in "A History of  the English Speakin g P
2 CONC eople", the Montgomeries (a very great house of Norman England) sid ed w
2 CONC ith Robert, Duke of Normandy, against his brother Henry I, in th e war o
2 CONC f succession after William Rufus, William The Conqueror's desig nated h
2 CONC eir for England was killed in a hunting accident [in which Henr y I was i
2 CONC nvolved--some think more than an "accident"].  Henry I destro yed the p
2 CONC ower of the Montgomeries starting in September, 1100.  He cap tured Rob
2 CONC ert in Normandy in the battle at Tinchebrai and combined Engl and and N
2 CONC ormandy again.
2 CONT --------------
