1 NOTE  OCCUPATION:  Vicomte of the Hiesmois, Seigneur of Montgommeri in Norma n
2 CONC dyMISC: Roger de Montgomerie, Count of Montgomerie and Viscount d'Exm e
2 CONC s in Normandy, and subsequently Earl of Shrewsbury, Arundel and Chich e
2 CONC ster in England and Montgomery in Wales, was one of the most powerfu l a
2 CONC nd influential nobles at the court of William the Conqueror. This R oge
2 CONC r accompanied William to England and led the main forces in the att ack a
2 CONC t Hastings. He is said to have been the man who conceived the ide a, an
2 CONC d who mainly helped to carry it out, of the Conquest of England . He he
2 CONC aded his own vassals and provided a vast contingent of ships, n o less t
2 CONC han sixty, to carry out the expedition. His was the master min d of Nor
2 CONC mandy, and after William I was firmly established Roger Montgo mery was m
2 CONC ade Viceroy to govern Normandy in connection with Queen Mati lda, but l
2 CONC ater when news of the conspiracies reached them he took Roge r back to E
2 CONC ngland with him where he was given nearly the whole of Coun ty Salop, w
2 CONC ith the Earldom of Shrewsbury, he had been made Earl of Aru ndel also, a
2 CONC nd had 28 manors in Dorset and also in Somerset, 77 in Sus sex, besides t
2 CONC he City of Chichester and the Castle of Arundel and man y others in dif
2 CONC ferent counties. Bry, a great Antiquarian, summarizes t he collective e
2 CONC vidence of historical manuscripts into the fact that Ro ger Montgomery, t
2 CONC he first Earl of Arundel and Count de Exmes or Hieme s in Normandy, was a d
2 CONC escendant of the ancient House of Hiemes, who ha d held that county at l
2 CONC east 300 years prior to the reign of William th e Conqueror. He asserts p
2 CONC ositively that he has seen the manuscripts th at prove this long line i
2 CONC n France. Roger married 1st Mabel de Belesm e or Belleme, daughter of W
2 CONC illiam II, Count of Belesme and Alencon o f France, about 1044. She die
2 CONC d 1086 and he married 2nd Adeliza, daught er of Everard de Pusay, stand
2 CONC ard bearer of Robert,Duke of Normandy, th e Crusader. Roger's first wif
2 CONC e was the daughter of William of Belesme , surnamed "de Talvas," a name d
2 CONC erived from a species of buckler he wo re, or as some say a nickname de
2 CONC noting his great cruelty. He married H ildeburga, a daughter of Arnulph
2 CONC , a chevalier, a very nobleman. Willia m was a man of savage and violen
2 CONC t temper. On his wife's protesting aga inst his enormities and condemni
2 CONC ng them openly, he caused her to be st rangled. Roger greatly increased h
2 CONC is wealth and his influenceby his ma rriage with Mabel, whose grandfath
2 CONC er, Ivers de Criel, had Alencon an d Belesme in France from Richard II, D
2 CONC uke of Normandy. We have told ab ove how Roger's brother Gilbert was ki
2 CONC lled by Mabel in the attempt t o kill Arnold or Ernauld, like a second L
2 CONC ucrezia Borgia. Gilbert was t hen in the flower of his youth, but she e
2 CONC vidently had no remorse, an d not satisfied with the contratempts, then p
2 CONC oisoned three noblemen, i ncluding Arnold, to make things sure. The oth
2 CONC er two recovered but Arno ld, having no one to nurse him, died. In reve
2 CONC nge for this Hugh Bonel , a son of Arnold's uncle, forced entry by nigh
2 CONC t into the chamber of t he Countess and cut off her head as she lay in b
2 CONC ed. He and his accompl ices escaped by destroying the bridges behind th
2 CONC em. Mabel had possesse d Eschafour and Montreuil for 26 years, keeping h
2 CONC im out of his inherit ance. (Ordericus, the historian, who lived at thi
2 CONC s time, tells these g rewsome tales, but he was a monk in the Abbey of S
2 CONC t. Everoult, which w as foundedby the family of Arnulph Giroie, and was p
2 CONC rejudiced agains t the Montgomeries and Belesmes and perhaps they were n
2 CONC ot as bad as h e painted them.) Mabel seems to be a wicked and cruel wo
2 CONC man, haughty , worldly minded, crafty and a babler, but Roger's 2nd wif
2 CONC e was the op posite, being of the highest French nobility, remarkable f
2 CONC or her goo d sense and piety. He had five sons and four daughters by Ma
2 CONC bel: Hugh , Robert, Arnold, Rogerand Philip, Emma, Maud, Mabel and Sybi
2 CONC l. (Sybi l married William Fitz-Hamon and their daughter Mabel married R
2 CONC obert , called the Consul, a famous hero, and son of Henry I, King of E
2 CONC nglan d, by Elizabeth de Bellomont, from whom you also deseend through t
2 CONC he d 'Audleys and Touchets.)in a monastery
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Montgomery, Vicomte d'Hiemes, Seigneur de Alenon in Norman d
2 CONC y jure uxoris, Regent of Normandy and England, Earl of Arundel (Susse x
2 CONC ) (1067), 1st Earl of Shrewsbury and Shropshire (1070), Earl of Chich e
2 CONC ster and MontgomeryAccording to Winston Churchill, the Montgomeries ( a v
2 CONC ery great house of Norman England) sided with Robert, Duke of Norma ndy
2 CONC , against his brother Henry I, in the war of succession after Willi am R
2 CONC ufus, William The Conqueror's designated heir for England was kill ed i
2 CONC n a hunting accident.  Henry I destroyed the power of the Montgome ries s
2 CONC tarting in September, 1100.  He captured Robert in Normandy in t he bat
2 CONC tle at Tinchebrai and combined England and Normandy again.Shrews bury, E
2 CONC arldom of: In early December 1074 Roger de Montgomery was creat ed Earl o
2 CONC f Shropshire or Shrewsbury.  As with other medieval earldoms , little d
2 CONC istinction was then made between the county town and count y proper whe
2 CONC n designating a specific name for a title, chiefly becaus e an earl, wh
2 CONC o was then more or less and official, albeit often heredi tary, was inc
2 CONC onceivable except as earl of a county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger was son of another Roger de Montgomery, seigneur of the Norman p l
2 CONC aces (St Germain-de-Montgomery and Ste-Foy-de-Montgomery) of that nam e i
2 CONC n the Calvados region.  He was a prominent member of the nobles gro upe
2 CONC d around William (later William I of England, The Conqueror) of Nor man
2 CONC dy well before the 1066 invasion of England but stayed behind in No rma
2 CONC ndy during the actual enterprise.  The year after Hastings he wen t to E
2 CONC ngland and received land grants in Sussex.  He is thought to hav e cons
2 CONC tructed the Castle of Montgomery (now in Powys, but formerly nam ed Mon
2 CONC gomeryshire after his family), doing so shortly before the Domes day Su
2 CONC rvey.  [Burke's Peerage, p. 2604]
2 CONT
2 CONT *********
