1 NOTE  MISC:  Arnulph was the fifth son of Roger de Montgomerie, Earl of Shre w
2 CONC sbury and Arundel. He was probably named after his uncle Arnulph de B e
2 CONC lêsme. After his father's death, and the assumption of the crown on t h
2 CONC e death of William Rufus by Henry I., he joined his brothers in resis t
2 CONC ance to the usurpation, and, as is more fully stated in the account g i
2 CONC ven of Robert de Belêsme, his brother, shared in his ill success, an d w
2 CONC as banished the kingdom. Arnulph crossed into Ireland in the year 1 100 t
2 CONC o obtain succors for Duke Robert's cause, and was successful in h is mi
2 CONC ssion. He subsequently "sent Gerald, his steward, to Murckhart, o r Mur
2 CONC tagh O'Brien,King of Munster, desiring his daughter Lafracoth i n marri
2 CONC age, which was easily granted." In right of his wife, however , he subs
2 CONC equently aspired to get possession of her father's kingdom. D uring the i
2 CONC nvasion of Ireland by Magnus, King of Norway, the Irish, st ruck with t
2 CONC error, called on the Normans, and Arnulph, who was at Pembr oke, collec
2 CONC ted his auxiliaries and hastened to them. Magnus was repuls ed; but the I
2 CONC rish, tasting blood, became more savage and made a sudde n attempt to m
2 CONC urder the Normans. Their king also carried off his daugh ter, Arnulph's w
2 CONC ife, and resolved to kill Arnulph himself as the rewar d of his allianc
2 CONC e; but he discovered the fraud and made his escape. hi s life he subseq
2 CONC uently spent in Normandy, we have but slight account . Ordericus Vitali
2 CONC s states that Arnulph and his brothers Roger, Phili p and Everard, "had n
2 CONC o share in their father's inheritance, their tw o elder brothers, Rober
2 CONC t and Hugh, having divided between themselves t he whole on both sides o
2 CONC f the sea." Arnulph had therefore but little i f any territory of his o
2 CONC wn to defend from Henry's anger against the fa mily; and his exertions w
2 CONC ere all given on his brother's behalf. But "a fter many thankless enter
2 CONC prises for his brother Robert, he became indi gnant, and went over to t
2 CONC he duke, to whom he ceded the castle of Alme n Sches which he had taken b
2 CONC y surprise." And during Robert's oppressio ns in Sées, many of the prov
2 CONC incials left him to join Arnulph. This wa s within two or three years f
2 CONC rom the time of their leaving England. Th e next we find of him is in t
2 CONC he year 1118, when the Alençais, desirin g to free themselves from the o
2 CONC ppressions of Stephen, Count of Mortain , their new master, they, in co
2 CONC ncert with Arnulph, secretly called t o their succor the Count of Anjou
2 CONC , who finally conquered the city an d castle. The count held them until t
2 CONC he year following, when by treat y remitting them to Henry I for that p
2 CONC urpose, the latter reinstated Ar nulph's nephew William in their posses
2 CONC sion. About a year later than th is, we find he had returned to Ireland
2 CONC , was reconciled to his father-i n-law to outward appearances at least, a
2 CONC nd was reunited to his wife; b ut on the morrow of his new nuptials fel
2 CONC l asleep after abanquet, fro m which he never awoke. The castle of Pemb
2 CONC roke had been built by him , probably before his father's death; and in t
2 CONC he year 1095 it was stro ng enough to resist all the attempts of the We
2 CONC lsh. The first one buil t at Pembroke is described as "a poor, slight c
2 CONC astle of stakes and tur f, which afterwards, on his return to England, h
2 CONC e left to the care o f a discreet man, Giraldus de Windesor, his consta
2 CONC ble and chief office r, with a small garrison, who were presently besie
2 CONC ged by the whole for ce of South Wales. But Giraldus made such a vigoro
2 CONC us defence, rather b y courage than strength, that they were obliged to r
2 CONC etire without succ ess."  "That this powerful and proud Norman found a f
2 CONC ortress there o f the character thus ascribed to it is probable, but it i
2 CONC s not possibl e that he should have long suffered the capital of his ne
2 CONC wly-acquire d possessions to continue so slenderly protected. . . . . T
2 CONC he superio r grandeur that characterizes the ruins of the castle and to
2 CONC wn of Pemb roke, with its various magnificent appendages, enable us to f
2 CONC orm an es timate of the importance Arnulph de Montgomerie attached to h
2 CONC is new co nquest."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:   "Genealogical History and Pedigree of the family of Montgome r
2 CONC y"                  Thomas Harrison Montgomery  Call Number: R929.2 M 7
2 CONC 88
