1 NOTE  ALIAS: Hamo Dentatus de Crevequer, Sire de Cruelly
2 CONT
2 CONT From: "Sinclair" <labehotiere@wanadoo.fr>
2 CONT Date: Mon, 10 Jun 2002 13:04:42 +0200
2 CONT
2 CONT E.A. Freeman was definitively William's principal panegyrist. He make s n
2 CONC o memtion of any St Clair rebels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamon was killed Val-es-dunes he was Lord of Creully, Torigny, Evrec y a
2 CONC nd Thaon. He was not the Comte (Earl) of Corbeil or Mortain.  The f irs
2 CONC t three Counts were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 996 to 1032 Mauger de Normandie, comte de Mortain et de Corbeil
2 CONT 1032 to 1048 Guillaume 1er de Normandie, surnomm Werleng ou Guerleng , c
2 CONC omte de Mortain et de Corbeil
2 CONT 1049 to 1104 Robert 1er de Conteville, comte de Mortain et de Cornouai l
2 CONC les
2 CONT
2 CONT (The name of Thaon appears very early in archives of Normandy. Hamon o f t
2 CONC he Teeth, Lord of Creully, Torigny, Evrecy and Thaon was one of th e fe
2 CONC udal rebels at the time of the uprising that was defeated by Willi am t
2 CONC he Bastard at Val - es-dunes (1047). Lchaud of Anisy in his note s on t
2 CONC he barony and the church of Thaon it is mentioned by Wace Roma n de Rou
2 CONC .) His father was Malger his Mother: Coeur-en-Auge
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1035, Robert, Duke of Normandy died. Although William was illegitim a
2 CONC te, he was Robert's only living son, and so inherited the father's ti t
2 CONC le. Gilbert, Count of Brionne, became William's guardian. A number o f N
2 CONC orman barons would not accept an illegitimate son as their leader a nd i
2 CONC n 1040 an attempt was made to kill William. The plot failed by the y di
2 CONC d manage to kill Gilbert.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was a Norman born and bred. William's cousin Guy of Burgundy , h
2 CONC is rival was in every sense a Frenchman. His connexion with the duc al h
2 CONC ouse was on distaff side, but uncontested legitimacy. This gave hi m an e
2 CONC xcuse for claiming the duchy in opposition to the bastard.. Will iam af
2 CONC ter the death of Gilbert, gave the island fortress of Brionne i n the R
2 CONC isle to Guy. The partition of the duchy was Guy's aim. Willia m was to b
2 CONC e dispossessed; Guy was to be duke in the lands east of Dive ; the grea
2 CONC t lords of Western Normandy were to be left independent. S t Clair fief
2 CONC s lay to the North and East of a line from Rouen to Caen . The lords of t
2 CONC he Bessin and the Cotentin revolted, their leader bein g Neal, Viscount o
2 CONC f Saint-Sauveur in the Cotentin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The victory at Val-es-dunes was decisive, and the French King, whose h e
2 CONC lp had done so much to win it, left William to follow it up. He met w i
2 CONC th but little resistance except at the stronghold of Brionne. Guy him s
2 CONC elf vanishes from Norman history. William had now conquered his own d u
2 CONC chy, and conquered it by French help. For once King Henry had kept hi s w
2 CONC ord.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Conqueror's battle at Val-es-dunes was a tourney of horsemen on a n o
2 CONC pen table-land just within the land of the rebels between Caen an d Mez
2 CONC idon. William with the aid of Henry, King of France, William gain ed a g
2 CONC reat victory at Val--Dunes, which led, to the capture of the tw o stron
2 CONC g castles of Alenon and Domfront.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sinclair
2 CONT
2 CONT ********
