1 NOTE  MISC:  Also spelled GHENT and GAND
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  Lord of Folkingham and Hunmandby, County Lincoln; Probabl y a
2 CONC rrived in England in 1066; was a commander in York, 1068, and was t ake
2 CONC n prisoner there by the Danes in 1069.  He w as a tenant-in-chief a nd o
2 CONC ne fo the largest landholders in County Lincoln in 1086, Folkinha m bei
2 CONC ng the head of his baronyMISC:  Also spelled  de Ghent
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert de Gant, son of Baldwin, Earl of Flanders, by Maud, sister o f W
2 CONC illiam the Conqueror, accompanied his uncle into England and, parti cip
2 CONC ating in the triumph of Hastings, obtained a grant of the lands o f a D
2 CONC anish proprietor named Tour, with numerous other lordships. Thi s Gilbe
2 CONC rt happened to be at York, anno 1069, and had a narrow escape w hen the D
2 CONC anes in great force, on behalf of Edgar Etheling, entered th e mouth of t
2 CONC he Humber and, marching upon that city, committed lamentab le destructi
2 CONC on by fire and sword, there being more than 3,000 Norman s slain. Like m
2 CONC ost of the great lords of his time, Gilbert de Gant dis gorged to the c
2 CONC hurch a part of the spoil which he had seized, and amon gst other acts o
2 CONC f piety restored Bardney Abbey, County Lincoln, whic h had been utterly d
2 CONC estroyed many years before by the Pagan Danes, Inq uar and Hubba. He m, A
2 CONC lice, dau. of Hugh de Montfort, and had issue, H ugh, who assumed the n
2 CONC ame Montfort; Walter, his successor; Robert, Lor d Chancellor of Englan
2 CONC d, anno 1153; and Emma, m. to Alan, Lord Percy . This great feudal chie
2 CONC f d. in the reign of William Rufus. [Sir Berna rd Burke, Dormant, Abeya
2 CONC nt, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's P eerage, Ltd., London, 18
2 CONC 83, p. 227, Gant, Earls of Lincoln]
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the two Norman commander who assisted William the Conqueror red u
2 CONC cing Yorkshire, where he held the garrison at York.  He received gran t
2 CONC s of 172 manors and lordships and became Baron of Folkingham.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Both Burke and Brian Tompsett agree that Baldwin, [6th] Count o f F
2 CONC landers, was Gilbert's father. However, I cannot find any referenc e to W
2 CONC illiam the Conqueror having a sister named Maud - the only docum ented s
2 CONC ister I find is Adelaide. Brian Tompsett shows Baldwin VI "th e Peaceab
2 CONC le," Count of Flanders and Artois, and Richilda, Countess o f Hainault a
2 CONC nd Namur, as the parents of Gilbert, and that's what I'm s ticking with
2 CONC .ALIAS: Gilbert de Ghent
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert de Gant, son of Baldwin, Earl of Flanders, by Maud, sister o f W
2 CONC illiam the Conqueror, accompanied his uncle into England and, parti cip
2 CONC ating in the triumph of Hastings, obtained a grant of the lands o f a D
2 CONC anish proprietor named Tour, with numerous other lordships. Thi s Gilbe
2 CONC rt happened to be at York, anno 1069, and had a narrow escape w hen the D
2 CONC anes in great force, on behalf of Edgar Etheling, entered th e mouth of t
2 CONC he Humber and, marching upon that city, committed lamentab le destructi
2 CONC on by fire and sword, there being more than 3,000 Norman s slain. Like m
2 CONC ost of the great lords of his time, Gilbert de Gant dis gorged to the c
2 CONC hurch a part of the spoil which he had seized, and amon gst other acts o
2 CONC f piety restored Bardney Abbey, County Lincoln, whic h had been utterly d
2 CONC estroyed many years before by the Pagan Danes, Inq uar and Hubba. He m, A
2 CONC lice, dau. of Hugh de Montfort, and had issue, H ugh, who assumed the n
2 CONC ame Montfort; Walter, his successor; Robert, Lor d Chancellor of Englan
2 CONC d, anno 1153; and Emma, m. to Alan, Lord Percy . This great feudal chie
2 CONC f d. in the reign of William Rufus. [Sir Berna rd Burke, Dormant, Abeya
