1 NOTE  "...Grandfather Arthur Avington Mann was a Virginian, born in Louisa C o
2 CONC unty in 1811.  His father, Arthur Mann, was born in the same state, a n
2 CONC d died at the age of sixty-six.  The Mann family is of English origin , t
2 CONC he progenitor of the family in the United States having crossed th e At
2 CONC lantic and made settlement in Virginia about 1630; the family wer e rep
2 CONC resented in Jamestown when it was burned by the Indians in 1632.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Arthur A. Mann removed to Clark County, IL, where he later married Wi n
2 CONC nifred Shields, who was born in Putnam County, Ind., the daughter o f W
2 CONC illiam Shields, born in Tennessee, of Scotch-Irish descent, and a f arm
2 CONC er in Clark County, and also in Buchanan County, Mo., whither he mo ved i
2 CONC n 1846.  After spending five years in Missouri, he migrated to Li nn Co
2 CONC unty, Ore., where he continued to farm until his death, in 1896 , when i
2 CONC n his ninety-seventh year.  Arthur A. Mann was employed at far ming in I
2 CONC llinois and Missouri.  He made his first trip to California i n 1849, c
2 CONC rossing the plains with an emigrant train, to search for gold .  He min
2 CONC ed in Eldorado County for about two years, then returned Eas t via Pana
2 CONC ma, to join him family, and a little later moved to Pottawat tamie Coun
2 CONC ty, Iowa, where he farmed until May 10, 1860, when he agai n hearkened t
2 CONC o the call of California, and with his wife and six child ren crossed t
2 CONC he plains with a train of emigrants, he being elected cap tain of the t
2 CONC rain to pilot it safely to its destination.  Once more i n California, h
2 CONC e settled on mile west of Dayton, where he died in 1862 , when but fift
2 CONC y-one years of age.  Mrs. Mann lived until August 29, 1 914, when her d
2 CONC eath occurred at the age of ninety years.  There were s ix children in t
2 CONC heir family:  William, of Chico Vecino; James, who die d in January, 19
2 CONC 17; John, who died October 22, 1913; Joseph A., of The rmalito; Mary E.
2 CONC , Mrs. Darge, who died October 22, 1878; and Cynthia A nn, Mrs. Garriso
2 CONC n of Oakland."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Her [Mary Elizabeth Mann's] father Arthur Arron, sometimes Aaron, Avi n
2 CONC gton or Abington, Mann was the son of the Arthur Abington Mann of Vir g
2 CONC inia.  Some say that the family came originally from the Isle of Mann , o
2 CONC ff the coast of Ireland, and went to England.  Arthur and Winifre d (Sh
2 CONC ields) Mann had six children - William, James, John, Joseph, Cynt hia a
2 CONC nd Marty Elizabeth - born in Iowa.  See Sources - HISTORY OF BUTT E COU
2 CONC NTY 1918 by Mansfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Arthur A. Mann first came to California around the Horn (1849) to loc a
2 CONC te a future home, then went to Iowa and brought his family overland ( 1
2 CONC 860).  Enroute, one of the oxen died.  He was compelled in the emerge n
2 CONC cy to substitute his wife's own "milk cow" at the yoke.  This, accord i
2 CONC ng to his wife, Winnefred (Shields), almost broke her heart.  In Chic o
2 CONC , they were first at the John Crouch place.  In the short time that A r
2 CONC thur Mann lived in the Chico area before his early death, he became a c
2 CONC quainted with many pioneers including John Bidwell.  He died about on e m
2 CONC onth after the George Darge family arrived.  Soon the Manns and Dar ges i
2 CONC ntermarried. -------A rumor exists to the effect that Winnefred S hield
2 CONC s may have been part Indian."  Source:  1980 Supplement to Hear t of th
2 CONC e Family Tree by Lynn Balmer, p. 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  Birth date above for Arthur Avington Mann does not match birt h d
2 CONC ate at cemetery---1811 vs. 1818
2 CONT
2 CONT The following written by Linda M. Mann:  "MANN FAMILY.  Arthur A. Man n w
2 CONC as the son of Arthur Mann and Nancy Banks Thompson.  He was born o n 22 O
2 CONC ctober 1811 or 1818 in Louisa County, Virginia.  On 17 July 184 2 he ma
2 CONC rried Winnefred Shields in Clark County, Illinois.  She was bor n 16 Se
2 CONC ptember 1825/26 in Putnam County, Indiana and was the daughte r of Will
2 CONC iam Shields and Winnefred/Martha Curl Shields.---Arthur and W innefred M
2 CONC ann had six children:  William, James, John, Joseph Aaron, M ary Elizab
2 CONC eth and Cynthia Ann.  All their children were born in Iowa e xcept John w
2 CONC ho was born in Missouri.---In 1849 Arthur A. Mann left hi s family in M
2 CONC issouri and traveled overland to California to work in th e El Dorado C
2 CONC ounty gold mines for a year or more.  He returned via Ist hmus of Panam
2 CONC a, moved his family to Pottawattamie County, Iowa, and fa rmed until 18
2 CONC 60.  Then he took his family by wagon train to California , he was chos
2 CONC en captain of the train.  On the plains, when one of th e oxen died, he w
2 CONC as compelled to substitute a milk cow belonging to hi s wife, Winnie.  T
2 CONC his, according to her almost broke her heart.---Th e arrived in Chico, C
2 CONC alifornia, on 15 September 1860.  They lived fo r a time at the site of t
2 CONC he John Crouch place, then settled a mile wes t of Durham.  In the shor
2 CONC t time before his early death on 22 October 1 862, he became acquainted w
2 CONC ith many pioneers including John Bidwell, f ounder of Chico.  Arthur wa
2 CONC s the eleventh burial in the Chico Cemetery .---The George Gotlieb Darg
2 CONC e family arrived in the fall of 1862.  Thre e of the Darge children mar
2 CONC ried three of the Mann children.  Their chi ldren were double cousins t
2 CONC o each other."
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Eva Alice Mann Tull, Arthur Mann died of a cancer on th e l
2 CONC ip.  This information was taken from a compilation presented at th e Ma
2 CONC nn family reunion in June 1976 by Fern Repetti.
