1 NOTE  "... James Mann, was born in Clark County, IL, and came with the Man n f
2 CONC amily to California in 1860, crossing the plains with teams.  The y arr
2 CONC ived at the present site of the Crouch place on September 15, o f that y
2 CONC ear, and located a mile west of Dayton.  James Mann farmed an d raised s
2 CONC tock until 1896, at which time he engaged in saw milling a t Cohasset f
2 CONC or a few years.  His next venture was as a horticulturist , which work h
2 CONC e successfully followed until his retirement.  He died i n January 1917
2 CONC .  He married Augusta Darge, who was born in Wisconsin a nd crossed the p
2 CONC lains to California with her parents in 1862.  She ha d nine children, f
2 CONC ive of whom are living, Mrs. Griffith being the olde st.  The mother di
2 CONC ed in Cohasset."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1880 census College City, Colusa, CA:  William Mann, aged 36, student , l
2 CONC isted as a brother-in-law of head of household, Albert G. Garrison , ag
2 CONC ed 25, school teacher, bp IA, father US, mother IN; his wife Cynth ia [
2 CONC Mann], aged 23, bp IA, father Virginia, mother IN.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following written by Linda M. Mann:  "James Mann was the second ch i
2 CONC ld of Arthur A. Mann and Winnefred Shields Mann.  He was born on 2 Au g 1
2 CONC 845 in Clark County, Illinois.  He emigrated to California with hi s pa
2 CONC rents when he was 15 years old.---He married Augusta Darge on 22 A pr 1
2 CONC 872 in Butte City, Colusa County.  She was born on 1 Dec 1853 in K ekos
2 CONC kee, Wisconsin, to George Gotlieb Darge and Marie Wilhelmine Carol ine (
2 CONC Nurmberg) Darge.  She emigrated to California with her parents wh en sh
2 CONC e was seven years old.---James and Augusta had nine children: Mar y Lav
2 CONC inia, Grace Pearl, William Arthur, Edward Aaron, Oliver Hartley , Minta B
2 CONC ell, Eva Alice, Everett Jay, and Oro Edith.---James was a ran cher and a s
2 CONC awmill owner.  He built a sawmill in Cohasset which was la ter purchase
2 CONC d by Eugene Griswold who operated it until 1925.  James ow ned acreage i
2 CONC n Butte City, Cohasset and Rock Creek areas.  He homestea ded 160 acres o
2 CONC n Rock Creek in 1889.---In 1881-2 he bought the Eads an d Thompson plac
2 CONC es at Rock Creek.  He planted black walnut trees and fi gs from slips o
2 CONC f the old Darge tree in Chico.  While they were livin g on the Eads ran
2 CONC ch, two of their young daughters, Grace who was 13 an d Minta who was 5
2 CONC , died within 3 days of each other of diphtheria (Nov ember 1887).  Edw
2 CONC ard had died in infancy in 1878 and William, a fine y oung man, died of t
2 CONC yphoid in 1899 when he was 23.---All of these, exce pt Edward, were bur
2 CONC ied in the fami8ly cemetery on the Eads ranch.  Jam es and Augusta were b
2 CONC uried there too, even though they had difference s and divorced in 1892
2 CONC .  This small graveyard is called the Rock Cree k Cemetery.  Augusta di
2 CONC ed on 14 Aug 1901 in Cohasset and James died o n 12 Jan 1917 in the hos
2 CONC pital in Oroville, California.---Augusta appli ed for a homestead befor
2 CONC e she died and left 173.46 acres to her childr en in her will.  It was l
2 CONC ocated 1/2 mile SE of the intersection of Mu d Creek Road and Musty Buc
2 CONC k Road on the W side of Big Chico Creek."
2 CONT
2 CONT Butte County Great Register, CA:  James Mann, aged 21, bp IL, farmer , r
2 CONC esidence Little Butte, registered July 28, 1867; James Mann, aged 3 8, b
2 CONC p IL, farmer, residence Rock Creek, registered August 1, 1884.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is an account by Eva Alice Mann Tull, compiled by Fern R e
2 CONC petti and presented at the June 1976 Mann family reunion:  "Dad bough t t
2 CONC he Eads ranch in 1881/2 and planted the walnuts from seed and the f igs f
2 CONC rom slips.  The lowland was virgin soil.  He cleared the land.  H ad an o
2 CONC ld man by the name of Johnston to help him and one Chinaman.  H e moved t
2 CONC he family to Rock Creek just a short time before Hartley wa s born in 1
2 CONC 879.  Henry Childers, Jane Wilson and Jenny Lowell or Heat h were the f
2 CONC irst teachers.  I went to school right after Minta Bell di ed.  Was onl
2 CONC y four and a half years old and knew all my letters in eve ry form.  I c
2 CONC ould tell time and could add all simple single numbers .  I was born on t
2 CONC he Thompson place and lived there until I was almos t six.  Moved to Co
2 CONC hasset, back to the Eads place on Rock Creek.  Fath er and mother owned a
2 CONC ll three places.  There are two barns that were b uilt by my father, ac
2 CONC tually three, for the first one was set a fire i n 1887 by my father's o
2 CONC ldest sister's boy, who was ten years old at th e time.  The barn was r
2 CONC ebuilt before we moved to Cohasset.  This bar n is still standing on th
2 CONC e Eads place and has been kept up in A-1 shap e.  The one at Cohasset h
2 CONC as withstood the years without anything don e to it what-so-ever.  It i
2 CONC s made of young sapling trees that were gro wing on the place.  He used a b
2 CONC oard axe and a square hammer and saw .  About all the board or sawmill l
2 CONC umber that was used was the board w alls and manger.  The flooring for h
2 CONC ay was peeled cedar post backing a nd the tie stay on top of the manger w
2 CONC as also a fir sapling.  All th e lumber used came from the Keefer sawmi
2 CONC ll, which was located in the l ower settlement.  The square nails came f
2 CONC rom Chico and right at that t ime, a few wire or round finishing nails w
2 CONC ere bought to try out and co nsidered to be too easily bent, but a very s
2 CONC hort time later, the wir e nail was put into use for every purpose -- t
2 CONC hat was in 1889.-------T he fence around the Thompson place on Rock Cre
2 CONC ek was finished in the y ear 1887.  We had diphtheria that year, and my f
2 CONC ather fenced in the fa mily cemetery -- that was after Minta and Grace d
2 CONC ied of the disease .  There are five members of the family buried there
2 CONC .  Two acres wer e fenced off and most of that fence is still standing.  T
2 CONC o keep the ca ttle out, I put a cyclone fence around the five graves (D
2 CONC ad, Mother, M inta, Grace and Will).  There are other graves there, but h
2 CONC ave never b een kept up, so therefore unmarked and misplaced.  Mr. Rose n
2 CONC ow owns t he Eads place.-----The house in Cohasset was torn down in the m
2 CONC id 1950 s.  Uncle Joe Mann helped build it.  Uncle Joe was also a very g
2 CONC ood ca rpenter and his penmanship was beautiful."
2 CONT
