1 NOTE  "George Lacy Griffith.   A native son of the Golden West, George Lac y G
2 CONC riffith first saw the light of day on July 7, 1868, on a ranch fou r mi
2 CONC les north of Chico.  He is a son of John L. Griffith, whose sketc h is p
2 CONC resented in that of J. W. Griffith on another page of this histo ry.  G
2 CONC eorge L. Griffith lived at home, assisting his father on the hom e ranc
2 CONC h and going to the district school.  At the age of twenty-thre e he beg
2 CONC an ranching for himself, taking over the management of his fat her's ra
2 CONC nch and operating it for two years.  During this period he mar ried, on A
2 CONC pril 2, 1893, in Chico, Miss Vina Mann, who was also a nativ e of Calif
2 CONC ornia, born near Butte City, Glenn County. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1894, Mr. and Mrs. Griffith began raising stock and doing genera l f
2 CONC arming, meeting with such success that they felt justified in buyin g t
2 CONC heir present ranch of one hundred forty-five acres, on Rock Creek , nin
2 CONC e miles north of Chico, on the Cohasset road.  This is known as t he Go
2 CONC re place, and is one of the historical places of Butte County, i t bein
2 CONC g the scene of the Indian fight and massacre; the Hickok childre n were m
2 CONC urdered on a spot near here.  The old Keefer grist mill, wher e the fir
2 CONC st pioneers had their flour made, was located on this ranch ; and there w
2 CONC as once an Indian rancheria here.  Fifty-five acres of th e ranch is fi
2 CONC ne bottom land.  The property is watered by the Keefer di tch, which ru
2 CONC ns through the ranch; alfalfa, beans, and garden truck ar e raised, and t
2 CONC here is a fine peach orchard.  All these products, adde d to the revenu
2 CONC e from the sheep and hogs, make a very satisfactory ret urn on the orig
2 CONC inal investment.  For about twenty years before the Gri ffiths purchase
2 CONC d the place, Chinese gardeners had operated the ranch s uccessfully.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mr. and Mrs. Griffith are parents of four children.  Russell graduate d f
2 CONC rom the Chico High School and the Chico Normal.  He taught school s ucc
2 CONC essfully for eighteen months, then enlisted in the United State Arm y, a
2 CONC nd is now in the officers' training school at Fortress Monroe.  Ar dye i
2 CONC s also a graduate from both the above-named schools, and is teach ing i
2 CONC n Shasta County.  Ellis and Homer are students in the Chico Hig h Schoo
2 CONC l.  Mr. Griffith is a Democrat, and Mrs. Griffith is a Republic an.  In 1
2 CONC 911, Mr. and Mrs. Griffith purchased a residence on Laburnu m Street, C
2 CONC hico Vecino, where they lived for five years while their ch ildren were a
2 CONC ttending the Normal School; but in 1916 they returned t o the home in t
2 CONC he country, that they might keep it in better conditio n by personal su
2 CONC pervision."
2 CONT
2 CONT Note below that George Griffith lists his place of birth as Paradise , C
2 CONC A:
2 CONT
2 CONT Butte County, CA, Delayed Birth Records:  Ardye Griffith, DOB Septembe r 2
