1 NOTE  "Eva and Ed Tull had eleven children....Over the years, Eva and Ed liv e
2 CONC d in a number of places---in Siskiyou, Shasta, and Butte Counties.  I n 1
2 CONC 914 they moved to Anderson, Shasta County; then to Dorris & Ukiah , Sis
2 CONC ki8you County, then back to Butte County in 1940.  In 1918 whil e they w
2 CONC ere living in Dorris, Siskiyou County near a lumber mill, thei r third s
2 CONC on, Jesse, became ill.  The doctor prescribed a certain medic ine.  A t
2 CONC ouching incident happened when it was necessary to refill th e prescrip
2 CONC tion.  Older brother James was sent to town for the refill .  He took a b
2 CONC uckboard, a light wagon, and drove quite a distance to t he drugstore, b
2 CONC ut on the way home the medicine somehow slid off the wa gon.  He was ma
2 CONC de to go back to look for it - in the dark --!  Althoug h he found the m
2 CONC edicine, his feelings of chagrin and guilt remained wi th him for a lon
2 CONC g time in his life when the blame was really not his .  This incident w
2 CONC as told by his daughter, Nona Ruel, of Redding, Cali fornia.  Eva and E
2 CONC d Tull were in Ukiah for four years in the 1930's .  When they moved to C
2 CONC hico Ed was a logging contractor.  As I remembe r Eva, she was a pleasa
2 CONC nt, cheerful person.  She spent her last month s at the Riverside Conva
2 CONC lescent Home in Chico." pp. 14-15, and p. 1 o f the 1980 Supplement - s
2 CONC ee sources.
