1 NOTE  Robert II 131690, king of Scotland (137190), nephew and successor o f D
2 CONC avid II </encyclopedia/D/David2.html>. He was the first sovereign o f t
2 CONC he house of Stuart, or Stewart (see Stuart </encyclopedia/S/Stuart. htm
2 CONC l>, family), which eventually succeeded to the English as well as t he S
2 CONC cottish throne. The son of Walter the Steward and Marjory, daughte r of R
2 CONC obert I, he was regent three times (133335, 133841, and 13465 8) for D
2 CONC avid II during the latter's exile and captivity. He thus led t he resis
2 CONC tance to Edward de Baliol </encyclopedia/B/BaliolE.html> and E dward II
2 CONC I of England. Robert rebelled against his uncle in 1363 when D avid rec
2 CONC ognized Edward III as his successor. On David's death (1371) , however, h
2 CONC e succeeded peacefully to the throne, in accordance with t he successio
2 CONC n law adopted in 1318. Robert's first marriage took plac e after the bi
2 CONC rth of several of his sons, but their succession to th e throne was leg
2 CONC itimized by an act of Parliament in 1373. Through mos t of his reign th
2 CONC e government was dominated by two of these sons : Joh n, earl of Carric
2 CONC k (later Robert III </encyclopedia/R/Rbrt3-Sc.html> ) and Robert Stuart <
2 CONC /encyclopedia/S/StuartRAlb.html>, later 1st duk e of Albany. The Scots i
2 CONC n alliance with France fought off several Engl ish invasions; they inva
2 CONC ded England without assistance in 1388 and wo n a great victory at Otte
2 CONC rburn. Robert was succeeded by Robert III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Founder of the Stuart dynasty in Scotland and England. In 1318 the Sco t
2 CONC tish parliament passed a decree providing that if Robert Bruce died w i
2 CONC thout sons, his grandson should be his successor; a son, later King s D
2 CONC avid II, was born to Bruce in 1324. In 1326 Robert succeeded his fa the
2 CONC r as steward of Scotland and in 1331 David was crowned king. Davi d was e
2 CONC xiled to France in 1333 and Robert served as his regent until h is retu
2 CONC rn in 1341. During Robert II's reign, Scotland was twice invade d by th
2 CONC e English, in 1384 and in 1385. Because of old age infirmity, R obert d
2 CONC id not aid the Scottish barons in resisting these invasions, no r did h
2 CONC e join in their retaliatory expedition against the English in 1 388.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Scots, first of the Stuart line, was only son of Walter Stuar t a
2 CONC nd Marjory, daughter of Robert Bruce, and was born in 1316. He too k pa
2 CONC rt in the battle of Halidon Hill, and acted a prominent part in th e af
2 CONC fairs of government during the minority of David II. He held the o ffic
2 CONC e of regent of Scotland during the captivity in England of his unc le D
2 CONC avid, 1346-1357, and succeeded to the throne on his death, in 1371 . He w
2 CONC as engaged for several years in war with England, in which he wa s aide
2 CONC d by the French; lived chiefly in retirement during his latter y ears, t
2 CONC he Earl of Fife being named regent in 1389; and died at Dundona ld Cast
2 CONC le, in 1390.
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 MAR 1316
1 DEAT
2 CAUS Dundonald Castle
2 DATE 13 MAY 1390
