1 NOTE  Old Families of Salisbury and Amesbury Massachusetts vol 1 Page 327
2 CONT 107 THOMAS STEVENS, Dea., of Amesbury, "sawyer" and "husbandman;" m.Ap r
2 CONC il 15, 1670[Nb], (19) MARTHA3 BARTLETT, who d. Sep. 8, 1718[A]. He wa s
2 CONC granted a "common right" in A., March, 1668-9;(*) oath al. 1677;"trai n
2 CONC ing band" 1680; selectman 1686; free. 1690; schoolmaster 1693; d.Apri l 1
2 CONC 4, 1729[A]; will Nov. 29, 1723;(+) April 28, 1729.
2 CONT (*) (66) JOHN1 STEVENS, Sen., deeded land on the west side of the Powo w
2 CONC to THOMAS STEVENS, both of S., Jan., 1667-8; but no relationship wasm e
2 CONC nt. Thomas was of S., Sep., 1668, but of A., Oct., 1669, when he boug h
2 CONC thouse and land of Ezekiel Wathen, formerly Edward Goe's.
2 CONT (+) In his will he ment. his sons Roger and Thomas, the daus. of his d a
2 CONC u.Martha decd. and Robert Hoyt, and spoke of his chil. in general; bu t d
2 CONC idnot give names of others. His sons Roger Stevens and Abel Merril l we
2 CONC renamed as exec. The other chil. are named in deeds, especially on e da
2 CONC ted1729.
2 CONT
2 CONT This information was found among my grandmothers(Maybelle Stevens) pap e
2 CONC rsthat were given to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1693    Thomas Stevens chosen schoolmaster,
2 CONT 1695    Thomas Stevens and others to take care of the common land with t
2 CONC he privilege of planting part of it to set "appletrees" for the towns u
2 CONC se and were also allowed the fallen wood for their "payns".
2 CONT 1667      deeded land on the west side of the Powow River to his sonTh o
2 CONC mas, January.  [This represents at least part of the settlement ofhal f o
2 CONC f his estate upon his oldest son at this time according to amodifie d f
2 CONC orm of primogeniture and is the reason he is not mentioned inthe wi ll o
2 CONC f 1686.]
2 CONT 1669   Thomas Stevens was chosen to hire gramer school teacher.
2 CONT 1669   Thomas moved to Amesbury where he bought a house and land ofEze k
2 CONC iel Wathen (formerly Edward Goe's) in October.
2 CONT 1670   Was 34 when he married
2 CONT 1680   Thomas a member of the Amesbury Training Band, a militia unit o f
2 CONC 56 men formed for protection from Indians.
2 CONT 1728    March 7, Thomas Stevens chosen deacon of the Second Church.
2 CONT 1729    April 14, Deacon Thomas Stevens died.  He was one of the leadi n
2 CONC gmen during a large part of his life all of the important offices a t s
2 CONC omeperiod.  He was selectman for seventeen years and was twice elec ted
2 CONC representative.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon Thomas Stevens aged 34, married Martha Bartlett, aged 17, April 1
2 CONC 5, 1670, daughter of Christopher Bartlett.
2 CONT
2 CONT Compiled Tuesday, Aug. 6,1991
2 CONT by Richard W. Stevens
2 CONT
2 CONT THE LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT OF THOMAS STEVENS OF
2 CONT AMESBURY, MASS.
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, the twenty-ninth day of November and in they e
2 CONC ar of our Lord one thousand seven hundred twenty and three, I ThomasS t
2 CONC evens of Amesbury in the county of Essex within his Majesty's provinc e
2 CONC of Massachusetts Bay in New England, yeoman, being weak in body but o f
2 CONC perfect
2 CONT mind and memory, thanks be given unto God:  Therefore calling to min d t
2 CONC hemortality of my body and knowing that it is appointed for men onc e t
2 CONC o diedo make and ordain this my last will and testament--that is t o sa
2 CONC yprincipally and first of all I give and recommend my soul into th e ha
2 CONC ndof God that gave
2 CONT it--hoping through the merits, death, and suffering of my Savior, Jesu s
2 CONC Christ, to have full and free pardon and forgiveness of all my sins a n
2 CONC dto inherit everlasting life:  and my body I commit to the earth to b e
2 CONC decently buried at the discretion of my Executors hereafter named,Not h
2 CONC ing doubting but at the general resurrection I shall receive the same a
2 CONC gain by the mighty power of God:  and as touching such worldly estate w
2 CONC herewith it hath pleased God to bless me in this life, I give, demise ,
2 CONC and dispose of the same in the following manner and form--that is t o s
2 CONC ay
2 CONT
2 CONT Firstly, I will that all those debts and duties as I do owe in right a n
2 CONC dconscience to any manner of person or persons whatsoever shall be we l
2 CONC land truly contented and paid or ordained to be paid in convenient ti m
2 CONC eafter my decease by my Executors hereafter named.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath to my well-beloved son Roger Stevens thenor t
2 CONC herly end of my homestead living bounded as followeth:  northerly ont h
2 CONC e Country Road so running southerly down toward Merrimack River to aw h
2 CONC ite ash at the southeasterly corner and at the southwesterly cornerwi t
2 CONC hin three feet to the south of a walnut tree so to range to [the]whit e a
2 CONC sh aforementioned; and also all my meadow in thehigglety-piggletie s so c
2 CONC alled and also that lot of meadow at the beachformerly purchase d of Ja
2 CONC mes Sanders of Haverhill; and also that piece ofmeadow whic h I have at a p
2 CONC lace called Fox Island; and also a piece ofmeadow whic h I have at a pl
2 CONC ace called the Towne Creek, all these meadowslying an d being in the to
2 CONC wnship of Salisbury in Essex aforesaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath to my well-beloved son Thomas Stevens theso u
2 CONC therly part of my said living bounded southerly on [the] MerrimackRiv e
2 CONC r and northerly on the before bequeathed land bounded with a whiteas h a
2 CONC t the northeasterly corner; and so westerly within three feet to th eso
2 CONC uth of a walnut tree at the northwesterly corner--with all housings ,or
2 CONC chards, cellars and all other [of] the premises to the samebelongin g--
2 CONC and also a piece of meadow at the beach lying in Salisbury asafores aid w
2 CONC hich I purchased of Daniel Moody and the fine intent andmeaning i s tha
2 CONC t my said son Thomas Stevens to enter in full possession ofthe ab ove b
2 CONC equeathed premises here bequeathed to him at my decease andnot be fore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I do reserve a privilege of a draft-way for my two sons beforem e
2 CONC ntioned for their benefits as followeth from the north division to th e
2 CONC River Merrimack; and from the south division to the Country Road wher e
2 CONC they shall deem most convenient.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath all my land which lyeth southerly from my s o
2 CONC nThomas Stevens's land being part of two lots and which I bought of T h
2 CONC omasBarnard and the other of Thomas Weed lying and being in Amesbur y i
2 CONC n Essexaforesaid; to and among my children and grandchildren Hanna h Ho
2 CONC yt andAbigail Hoyt to have an equal part betwixt them with my sai d chi
2 CONC ldren itbeing in satisfaction of my daughter Martha's portion, no w dec
2 CONC eased, thesaid lands to be in equal proportion to and among my ch ildre
2 CONC n andgrandchildren in manner as aforesaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT And it is to be understood and my intent and meaning is that as to an y o
2 CONC fthe
2 CONT particulars before-mentioned and bequeathed be by law and justice take n
2 CONC away
2 CONT from any one of these to bequeathed that the rest shall bear therin eq u
2 CONC al
2 CONT proportion herein contained accordingly.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I likewise constitute, make and ordain my sons Roger Stevens and A b
2 CONC el
2 CONT Merrill my Executors of this my last will and testament and I do hereb y
2 CONT revoke and disannul all and every other former testament, wills, named ,
2 CONT willed, and bequeathed ratifying and confirming this and no other to b e m
2 CONC y
2 CONT last will and testament.  In witness whereof I have hereunto set my ha n
2 CONC dand
2 CONT seal the day and year before mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, sealed, published,
2 CONT pronounced, and declared
2 CONT by the aforesaid Thomas Stevens
2 CONT as his last will and test-
2 CONT ament in the presence of
2 CONT us the subscribers, in view of
2 CONT
2 CONT Orlando Bagley, Ju.
2 CONT William Currier                             THOMAS STEVENS
2 CONT John Nichols
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief Jurrorists--April 28, 1729 Before the Honorable John Appleton Ri g
2 CONC htJudge of the Probate of Wills decreed in said County of Essex--Orla n
2 CONC doBagley, Right and Jonathan Nichols personally appeared and made oat h t
2 CONC hatthey were present and saw Thomas Stevens, late of Amesbury decea sed
2 CONC , sign,
2 CONT seal and heard him publish and declare in front of witnesses writtenin s
2 CONC trument to be his last will and testament and when he so did he was o f
2 CONC good understanding and of a discovering, and they together with Willi a
2 CONC mCurrier set to their hands at and in view of same taken in his prese n
2 CONC ceas witnesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT I now attest Honorable John Appleton, R.J.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon which this will is proved, approved, and allowed being presente d b
2 CONC y
2 CONT Roger Stevens, one of the children therein named who having given mout h t
2 CONC o
2 CONT provide witness to accept or refuse Executorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last will and testament
2 CONT of Thos. Stevens late of
2 CONT Amesbury, Declared, Proved,
