1 NOTE  This information was found among my grandmother's (Maybelle StevensSar g
2 CONC eant) papers.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Stevens, Sergeant; of Salisbury, "husbandman," probably born abou t
2 CONC 1611.  Married Katherine, who died July 31, 1682.  He was believed t o b
2 CONC eone of the first settlers from England because he received land i n th
2 CONC efirst division from the King of England, 1640-1645; he was a comm oner a
2 CONC ndwas taxed in 1650, 1652, and in 1654; he singed petitions of 1 658-16
2 CONC 80,ect.  Sergeant John Stevens Sr. was a member of the Salisbur y Churc
2 CONC h in1687.  He died February 1688-1689.  His will April 12, 1686 , Novem
2 CONC ber 26,1689; mentioned "Bro. Severans".
2 CONT Thought to be the parents of Deacon Thomas Stevens.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Merrill's History of Amesbury and Merrimac
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639    The name of John Stevens is among "those that have lots andpro p
2 CONC ortions granted "in the town of Colchester in the first divsion.
2 CONT 1644    John Stevens and others chosen surveyors of roads and all to w o
2 CONC rkon lawful notice under 5 fine.
2 CONT 1645    January 6th "Ordered 40's to John Stevens and others for their s
2 CONC ervices".
2 CONT 1645    Prudential Man John Stevens and others were empowered to rat e a
2 CONC lltown business except granting land.
2 CONT 1648    John Stevens and others fined 12 d for disorderly talking inme e
2 CONC ting.
2 CONT 1650    John Stevens named surveyor of highway.
2 CONT 1654    One of 41 men who signed the Articles of Agreement Between the i
2 CONC nhabitants of Old Town and     Those of the New Town, which later bec a
2 CONC meAmesbury (among the 19 who signed with their marks).
2 CONT 1658    Signed petition about church matters.
2 CONT 1658    A Sergeant in the "foot company" of Salisbury.
2 CONT 1665    Purchased approx. 2 3/4 acres of a farm of 100 acres which Sam u
2 CONC elHall originally bought of        Samuel Dudley and which he sold t o t
2 CONC hetown of Salisbury 1657.
2 CONT 1686    Made last will and testament; signed with his mark, April 12.
2 CONT 1687    Member of Salisbury Church known as "Sgt. Stevens".
2 CONT 1689    Property appraised, February 8.
2 CONT 1689    Property inventory filed in court, November 26.
2 CONT
2 CONT Compiled Tuesday, Aug. 6, 1991
2 CONT by Richard W. Stevens
2 CONT Jack Stevens, Library Volunteer, of The Phillips Library of EssexInsti t
2 CONC ute, 132 Essex Street, Salem, MA 01970 (508) 744-3390, provided mewit h t
2 CONC he following information on the early wills of Essex County fromTh e Es
2 CONC sex County, Mass., Probate Index, 1638-1840 which lists docketnumb ers f
2 CONC or:
2 CONT John Stevens d. Jan 13 1689 - 16353
2 CONT Thomas Stevens d. April 14, 1729 - 26439
2 CONT
2 CONT I was later able to obtain photocopies of these wills only withthe con s
2 CONC iderable help of Mr. Jack Stevens, who personally went to theEssex Co u
2 CONC nty Probate Court, 36 Federal Street, Salem, MA 01970, on hislunch ho u
2 CONC r after a monetary donation was made to the Essex Institute tocover c o
2 CONC sts, postage, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT OF JOHN STEVENS OF SALISURY,MASS.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, I John Stevens, Sr. of the town ofSalisbury , M
2 CONC assachusetts Colony in New England, Husbandman, being agedand wea k in b
2 CONC ody but of sound and perfect memory (praise to be given togod fo r the s
2 CONC ame) and knowing the uncertainty of this life on earth andbein g desiro
2 CONC us to settle things in order; do make this my last will &testa ment in m
2 CONC anner and form following:  That is to say first &principally , I commen
2 CONC d my soul to Almighty God, my creator, assuredlybelieving th at I shall r
2 CONC eceive full pardon and free remission of all mysins and b e saved by th
2 CONC e piteous death and merits of my blessed Saviorand Redeem er, Christ Je
2 CONC sus: And my body to the earth from whence it wastaken t o be buried in d
2 CONC ecent and Christian manner as to my Executorshereafte r named shall be t
2 CONC hought meet and convenient:  And as touchingsuch wor ldly effects as th
2 CONC e lord in mercy hath lent me, my will andmeaning i s the same shall be e
2 CONC mployed and bestowed as hereafter by mywill is ex pressed:  And first I d
2 CONC o reveal, announce, prostrate, and makevoid al l wills by me formerly m
2 CONC ade and declare I appoint this my lastwill an d testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give & bequeath unto my son John Stevens all land where he no w
2 CONC dwells lying between ye lands of Mr. Thos. Bradbury in that place, as a
2 CONC lso the remainder of Sunner's neck, and that plain at the end of Brow n
2 CONC 'slot north of the Merrimack River, as also my coverlet which I bough t o
2 CONC fMr. Gines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give & bequeath to my Son Benjamin Stevens all my living in t h
2 CONC etown where he now lives, with my six-acre planting lot and my pastur e
2 CONC ,which is over against Thos. Toland-son's late deceased, as also al l o
2 CONC f myland lying next unto the land where Nathaniel Brown now dwell s fro
2 CONC m yehighway through the swamp to Mr. Bradburies land as also my g reat b
2 CONC rasskettle, my long table, the little table which I bought of m y broth
2 CONC erSeverans, and my cabirons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath to my son Nathaniel Stevens all my Sweepage l
2 CONC ot of salt marsh lying at a place commonly called the beach.  Also, m y
2 CONC will is that my Executors shall pay unto ye children of my said sonNa t
2 CONC haniel five pounds in good current pay evenly to be divided amongstth e
2 CONC m.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath unto my daughter Mary Osgood five shilling s t
2 CONC obe paid by my executors:  including her portion of my pewter, exce pti
2 CONC ngtwo platters: otherwise dispatched as in my will doth appear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath unto Benony Tucker, my grandson, two platte r
2 CONC s,a flock bed and bolster and a feather pillow included, the coverle t w
2 CONC hichwas of goodman Bowen's weaving and my musket and sword with al l th
2 CONC e stockof cattle which he hath already, including sheep and mare :  and a
2 CONC ll myupland lying in Hall's farm so called and two acres of th at cow c
2 CONC ommonmeadow formerly belonging unto the wife of John Evans so n and whi
2 CONC ch wasbought of Sgt. Evans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I give and bequeath unto my two sons John Stevens, & BenjaminSt e
2 CONC vens all the rest of my upland, including meadow which I have inSalis b
2 CONC ury with all my common rights with all the privileges andappurtenance s t
2 CONC hereunto belonging in any way appertaining as to beequally divide d bet
2 CONC ween them they paying unto my daughter Mary Osgood'schildren te n pound
2 CONC s in good pay to be equally divided amongst them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:  I do bequeath my son John Stevens and my son Benjamin Stevens m y
2 CONC Executors unto this my last will and testament:  And in confirmatio n o
2 CONC fall the above in this my will contained; I do here set my hand an d se
2 CONC althis twelfth day of April:  in the year of our Lord one thousan d six
2 CONC hundred eighty-six.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed Sealed & Delivered                              The   -|-     m a
2 CONC rk
2 CONT before us (my farming ourterlimbs)            of John   -|-    Stevens
2 CONT William Bulwoll -|-      Sr.
2 CONT Wimond: Bradbury
2 CONT
2 CONT Wm. Bulwoll and Wimond Bradbury give oath in court atSalem 26th March , 1
2 CONC 689 That they were present and saw John Stevens, Sr.of Salisbury si gn, s
2 CONC eal and heard him declare this above written to behis last wil l & test
2 CONC ament, and that he was then to their bestunderstanding of a di sposing m
2 CONC ind.
2 CONT Attest Benjamin Gemish Chase
2 CONT
