2 CONT Notes for John Chynn and Alice *Unknown (Smoot)
2 CONT 1. Marriage source: Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol.
2 CONT 27, 1919, page 297-8
2 CONT 2. Marriage source: Edwards Family History, DAR Records, Film #872806,
2 CONT Husband - John Chynn
2 CONT 3. Individual source: Genealogies of Virginia Families, Vol. II, CO-HA ,
2 CONT William & Mary Quarterly, page 630
2 CONT 4. Individual source: Branch, Harris, Jarvis and Chinn Book, pg. 258,
2 CONT SLFHC
2 CONT 5. Individual source: IGI Virginia, 1984
2 CONT 6. Died source: Genealogies of Kentucky Families, Vol. A-M, Arizona
2 CONT Capital Library
2 CONT 7. Died source: Ansearchin News, Vol. 3-6, January 1956 - October 1959 ,
2 CONT page 42.
2 CONT 8. Will source: Lancaster County, Virginia Wills, Deeds & Inventories , f
2 CONC ilm
2 CONT #32135-MFHC
2 CONT 9. Died source: Lancaster County, Virginia Wills, Deeds & Inventories , f
2 CONC ilm
2 CONT #32135-MFHC
2 CONT 10. Edwards Family History DAR Records Film #872806
2 CONT 11. Will source: Lancaster County Wills, Vol. 2, 1654-1702
2 CONT Came to Virginia on the "James" in about 1657. Settled in Lancaster Co u
2 CONC nty,
2 CONT Virginia, and was a planter. His plantation was called Oakley.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Book: Crossroads in Kansas- A Stearns-Ross Genealogy, by Phyllis Ros s K
2 CONC ostner, CW 1982 by Mennonite Press, Inc., N. Newton, Kansas 67117 , Cla
2 CONC n Ross pages 195-290)
2 CONT "The Chinn Family: The Chinns are an ancient family in England and Sco t
2 CONC land, where we find the name under the seal CHEYNE, bearing one of th e o
2 CONC ldest and most illustrious Coats of Arms in Great Britain. The orth ogr
2 CONC aphy of the name has been changed many time from the Norman-Frenc h tit
2 CONC le, De Cheyne. We find it variously written in Nisbett's Heraldr y and o
2 CONC ther works on the subject as: Le Chein, Cheyne, Chynn, Chinn, t he last b
2 CONC eing adopted as the spelling of the name in America. In Londo n there w
2 CONC as an avenue called for the family of ancient time, "Cheyne-R ow". (P) T
2 CONC he Chinns are supposed to have originated from Humphrey, Duk e of Glouc
2 CONC ester, fourth son of Henry IV of England. The emblem is a du cal corone
2 CONC t with a maltese cross surmounted with three purple feather s and a gre
2 CONC yhound, also bluebells. The motto "Dieu Pour Nos" or "God B less Us". T
2 CONC here have been some statements linking John Chynn to the de scendancy o
2 CONC f Sir Walter Raleigh, however there is no proof of such a r elationship
2 CONC . (P) The Chinns in America are decendants of John Chynn Wh o arrived i
2 CONC n the 1600's aboard "The James" and settled at a place call ed Morattic
2 CONC o, in Virginia. It has been said that he was one of three b rothers who i
2 CONC migrated to this country. Little is known of his early li fe and all ef
2 CONC forts to trace him in England have so far been unsuccessf ul."
2 CONT (Chinn)
