1 NAME Verna Mae /Hebb/
2 GIVN Verna Mae
2 SURN Hebb
1 SEX F
1 _UID 7DDBD33A49A941B4B3DA087B3064469ED4E9
1 _AMTID 162143279793:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  Copied from The Parsons Advocate(February 26,1975)
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War veteran's daughter is 90
2 CONT
2 CONT A ten room house in the Pulp Mill Bottom Parsons is still home to Vern a W
2 CONC illiams even though she has lived with her daughter, Nellie, in Rid gel
2 CONC ey more than 20 years. Travel is not easy when you are 90 years old , b
2 CONC ut she insists on coming back home to see the doctor and visit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Verna Mae Hebb was one of the twelve children of Tucker County's las t s
2 CONC urviving veteran of the Civil War, John C. Hebb. Memories of her fa the
2 CONC r's engagement in the battles are hazy, now,  but she says a "canyo n" m
2 CONC ade him lose his hearing and a battle near Rowlesburg left him cri pple
2 CONC d. " Daddy always took us to school," she said smiling as she tol d of t
2 CONC he iced-jammed Cheat River being a common walkway. One mornin g a large c
2 CONC hunk of ice broke loose carrying her father downstream. He r brothers r
2 CONC an down the embankment, rescuing him from the water. She i nsisted they w
2 CONC eren't frightened, they had always lived nearby and didn 't consider th
2 CONC e river dangerous.
2 CONT
2 CONT Verna was four years old but says she can remember standing on the hil l
2 CONC side near Thier home during
2 CONT the summer of 1888 watching houses and animals being swept downstrea m i
2 CONC n one of the most destructive floods ever to occur in Tucker County .
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Marcella Lipscomb Hebb resided on a farm high enough to mis s t
2 CONC he damaging water. Verna Mae was born May 3, 1884, near Macedonia . She a
2 CONC ttended two schools in the area, but was unable to attend regul arly be
2 CONC cause she was needed on the farm. John Hebb lived to be 93 year s old a
2 CONC nd one of Vernie's brothers, Solomon Hebb, who lives in Parsons , is no
2 CONC w 95 years old. Only one other of the twelve children, Mrs. Mea rl Goss
2 CONC , also of Parsons is living.
2 CONT
2 CONT Those deceased include Albert Thornton, Charles William, Clayton, John n
2 CONC y E., Mrs. Pearl Corrick, Mrs. Augusta Lee Phillips, Mrs. Addie Mille r
2 CONC , Mrs. Mandy Miller, and Jenny,  who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vernie recalls driving the horses while Johnny guided the plow. When a s
2 CONC ked if the family had a large garden she replied, " Ohio, we just plo w
2 CONC ed anywhere we wanted to plant sumpin."
2 CONT
2 CONT She stayed at home until one of her brothers got married "... and fetc h
2 CONC ed his wife home," then she went to Bull Run to live with her sister , M
2 CONC andy. Mandy's children Misses Grace and Gladys Miller and thier bro the
2 CONC r, Charlie, all live in Parsons now.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vernie was about 20 years old when she came to Parsons to live in wit h f
2 CONC amilies and help care for the elderly. Here she met Samuel Roby Wil lia
2 CONC ms. " His sister had married my brother and one day he made up a pr opo
2 CONC sal for a marriage with me. He just kept on and kept on and kept o n ji
2 CONC bbin' at me. He drank. Not much, ya understand, just enough to mak e hi
2 CONC m silly. And I just up and tol him, ' I'll not marry any man tha t drin
2 CONC ks." He promised he'd stop and I just tol him, 'If ya don't I'l l walk o
2 CONC ff, I got two legs!' "
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Williams were married May 5, 1905 in the Evangelical Unit e
2 CONC d Brethren Church in Parsons and celebrated 58 wedding anniversaries . S
2 CONC he says he kept his promise and was a good and loving husband. Mr W ill
2 CONC iams died Oct. 31, 1963.
2 CONT
2 CONT The EUB Church where they were married is now St. Paul's United Method i
2 CONC st Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their first child died at birth, another Elsie died at three or four , d
2 CONC uring an influenza epidemic.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was visiting the home of her eldest son and his wife, Cecil and Da i
2 CONC sy Williams, at Fork Mountain, during her interview. Three of the chi l
2 CONC dren, Ray, Mrs. Ruby Hapney and Mrs. Nellie Williams, all live in Rid g
2 CONC eley where Mr. Williams went to work after the Pulp Mill closed in Pa r
2 CONC sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another daughter, Mrs. Maxine Parsons, lives in Ft. Ashby; Roy William s l
2 CONC ives in Clarksburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roy was the father of three children and has two grandchildren. Ray ha s f
2 CONC ive children and four grandchildren; Maxine has nine children and 1 1 g
2 CONC randchildren; and Ruby has four children and five grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Williams has 21 grandchildren and 22 great-grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT One granddaughter, Nancy Hapney Parsons of Bretz, near Parsons was rai s
2 CONC ed in her grandmother's home. " She came to stay with me when her mot h
2 CONC er went to the hospital to have another baby and she just never wen t h
2 CONC ome," Mrs. Williams explained. All her children were grown when Nan cy c
2 CONC ame into her home and she says, "Nancy was a good girl. She neve r gave m
2 CONC e a lick of trouble." Nancy and Jared Parsons are the parent s of two c
2 CONC hildren, Michael, five and Lesley, three.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is doubtful that any of Mrs. Williams' grandchildren will ever kno w t
2 CONC he terror of typhoid fever, but it was not uncommon when her childr en w
2 CONC ere small and both Mrs. Williams and her son, Cecil, were bedfas t for a l
2 CONC ong time. her most vivid memory is regaining consciousness an d not rec
2 CONC ognizing her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Williams first went to housekeeping on a farm near Parson s
2 CONC , then rented a few homes in Parsons before buying the ten room hous e w
2 CONC hich she still owns. When her parents were no longer able to care f or t
2 CONC heir farm they, too, bought a home in the Pulp Mill Bottom, wher e thei
2 CONC r daughter Mrs. Goss still lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pointing to a faint scar on her forehead, Mrs. Williams told how she a n
2 CONC d two of her grandchildren, Sonny and Janet May Parsons, were involve d i
2 CONC n a train wreck. She had been in Ridgeley "...because My Man wa s a wor
2 CONC kin there," and was bringing the children home for a weekend vi sit. Tw
2 CONC o trains collided and she was taken by handcar to a doctor wher e she w
2 CONC as treated and brought back to the depot. Neither of the childr en were i
2 CONC njured.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same injury was reopened when " Nellie and me and Grandpap" were g o
2 CONC ing to Kingwood to the Buckwheat Festival and were involved in a ca r w
2 CONC reck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Williams' hearing is quite poor, but she still is able to hook ru g
2 CONC s and does some quilting. Her daughter takes care of the house work a n
2 CONC d cooking and helps her care for her tiny chihuahua, Mitzi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many Senior Citizens are apprehensive about being interviewed, but Ver n
2 CONC ie Williams was very much an exception. Her daughter-in-law had expla i
2 CONC ned who was coming and she was quite anxious to talk and apologized f r
2 CONC equently that she could not remember enough to tell a good story, eac h t
2 CONC ime beginning another tale. She enjoyed posing for the picture, ins ist
2 CONC ing on wearing her apron and explained that she always wore one.
