2 CONT Copied from the Parsons Advocate(dates unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Thomas F. Hebb returned on Saturday last from Rowlesburg, where h e h
2 CONC ad been attending the reunion of his company of the Civil War. He w as v
2 CONC ery much pleased with his trip and says that the town of Rowlesbur g is a h
2 CONC ummer, and says that the people down there say that the railro ad is co
2 CONC ming up river to Parsons. We have been looking for it for thes e many y
2 CONC ears and will receive it with open arms when it does come. Th e Colonel m
2 CONC et with many of his old comrades while at the reunion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crossed the River(Parsons Advocate)
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Thomas F. Hebb, the subject of this sketch, was born in 1845, i n P
2 CONC reston county, and died on the 12 day of Nov. 1916, at The Soldier s Ho
2 CONC me at Old Point Comfort, Virginia. he married Lavina S. Bowman, th e da
2 CONC ughter of the late Samuel W. Bowman. He resided nearly all of hi s life i
2 CONC n Tucker county, except a short period when he was a railroade r and li
2 CONC ved at Rowlesburg. During the late Civil War , he was a soldie r, and s
2 CONC erved during the entire war. After the war he located on a far m below S
2 CONC t. George, where he was proprietor of the Black Water Hotel , until the b
2 CONC uilding was destroyed by fire, after which he moved to Pa rsons and con
2 CONC ducted for a few years the Cheat River Hotel and reside d ever afterwar
2 CONC ds in the city of Parsons. He was well known not only t hroughout Tucke
2 CONC r county, but also thru out the adjoining counties. H e was a man who w
2 CONC as kind and generous and one to whom it gave great pl easure in accommo
2 CONC dating his neighbors and friends. He was good and kin d to his family a
2 CONC nd always remembered a friend. His funeral services w ere conducted at h
2 CONC is home in the city of Parsons on Thursday the 16, b y Revs. J.W. Bedfo
2 CONC rd, A.M. Hammond, after which he was buried beside h is wife in the Par
2 CONC sons Cemetery, there to rest until the judgement day . He leaves the fo
2 CONC llowing relatives-children- Mrs. Myrtle Randolph, Mr s. Catherine Hall, M
2 CONC rs. Rose Davis, Misses Nellie, Elizabeth and Claud ie and Harry Hebb; a
2 CONC lso two brothers John H. Hebb of Buffalo, and Jose ph Hebb of Parsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sympathy of the community is with the relatives and friends of Col . H
2 CONC ebb in their bereavement. Thus one by one the old residents of Tuck er c
2 CONC ounty are called to cross that mystic river from which no travele r eve
2 CONC r returns.
