1 NOTE  copied from The Parsons Advocate( December 22, 1976)by Marilyn McClai n S
2 CONC mith
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaker sets Revive Old Memories
2 CONT
2 CONT "There's a lot of memories in these," Mrs. Merl Goss said as she expla i
2 CONC ned, one- by- one where many of the more than 300 pairs of salt and p e
2 CONC pper shakers that line her kitchen walls had come from. Almost all o f h
2 CONC er 80 years have been spent in Tucker County and her friends and lo ved o
2 CONC nes have contributed these and many more sets that she has no roo m to d
2 CONC isplay. Then hobby began when Mrs. Dency Marteney, a native of P arsons w
2 CONC ho now lives in Arizona, gave Mrs. Goss a chef and a cook, com plete wi
2 CONC th all white clothes and shiny black faces. She took several s ets down f
2 CONC rom the shelves reminiscing about when she had received the m and who h
2 CONC ad given them to her, hardly hesitating as she went from on e pair to t
2 CONC he next.
2 CONT
2 CONT Delphia Merl was the youngest of 12 children of John Carrico Hebb an d M
2 CONC arcella Margaret Lipscomb Hebb and is the third of the twelve to ha ve b
2 CONC een interviewed as a Parsons Advocate Senior Citizen. Solomon Ebsw orth H
2 CONC ebb, now 97 years old, was featured Dec 3,1970, and the late Ver na Mae H
2 CONC ebb Williams' story was told on Feb. 26, 1976. There were als o twins, J
2 CONC ohnny E. and Jenny who both died young, she in infancy and h e at 21 of t
2 CONC yphoid fever. Others were Albert Thornton, Charles William , Ira Clayto
2 CONC n, Mrs. Pearl Corrick, Mrs. Augusta Lee Phillips, Mrs. Add ie Miller an
2 CONC d Mrs. Mandy Miller.
2 CONT
2 CONT Merl attended Green Valley School at Buffalo Creek and number Six Scho o
2 CONC l and says when the river froze they could walk across to the school , o
2 CONC therwise they rode in a boat, and she finished eighth grade. Her me mor
2 CONC ies of school are quite vivid and she says some of her teachers wer e L
2 CONC eslie Repair, Ethel Shaffer, Floyd Ball and Clarence Moore. Both Mr . B
2 CONC all and Mr. Moore have also been interviewed as Advocate Senior Cit ize
2 CONC ns and both men wrote their own stories. The Rev. Mr. Moore, now 8 7 ye
2 CONC ars old, lives in Clarinca, Iowa. " His brother Truman Moore an d I wer
2 CONC e playing one day and I fell and broke my collar bone. Mr. Bal l was ou
2 CONC r teacher then and he didn't really believe I'd hurt myself, " she said
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT She doesn't remember any discussions about Santa Claus when she wa s a c
2 CONC hild, but the children always hung their stockings up on Christma s Eve a
2 CONC nd found them filled with candy and cookies the next morning .  "I was p
2 CONC retty big when I got my first doll. In fact I think I stil l have her u
2 CONC pstairs somewhere. She had a china head, she was a prett y thing," she r
2 CONC ecalled. The family made the trimmings for the tree eac h year with pap
2 CONC er chains and popcorn strings.
2 CONT
2 CONT She shook her head and said she didn't really know how her mother mana g
2 CONC ed, but somehow she found the time to raise sheep, shear them, card a n
2 CONC d spin wool and color it, then spent hours knitting so that each of h e
2 CONC r 12 children could have two pairs of socks and a pair of mittens eve r
2 CONC y winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was about 1912 when she met Francis Freal Goss, whom everyone kne w a
2 CONC s Dan. she was a cook in a logging camp and he was a lumberman. the y w
2 CONC ere married in April 1913 in a church at Etam. Mr Goss was a nativ e of O
2 CONC akland.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Those were the happiest years of my life," Mrs, Goss said as she tol d o
2 CONC f their life in the lumbering camps. She described double houses, e ach w
2 CONC ith three rooms, that the companies built for their employees. Wh en on
2 CONC e section of the woods was cleared, the buildings were moved ont o flat
2 CONC cars and the whole community moved to a new location. "You coul d hear t
2 CONC he hoot owls hoot, the wild cats squalling, the screech owl s a screech
2 CONC ing, go fishing along the streams. It was real interestin ' living," sh
2 CONC e declared. The family moved to Spring Creek, near Lewisb urg, when the
2 CONC ir oldest child, Gertrude, was old enough to go to school .
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten years after they were married, Mr. and Mrs. Goss moved into Parson s i
2 CONC n the home where she now lives, with her parents in order to help c are f
2 CONC or them. Their youngest child Lucille, was born there and she sai d " I w
2 CONC ouldn't go any other place. It's really too big for me now, bu t I like t
2 CONC o have lots of room when the family comes to see me."
2 CONT
2 CONT Six years later when their six children ranged in age from eight month s t
2 CONC o ten years, Mr. Goss died as the result of an automobile accident . Mr
2 CONC s. Goss raised a big garden, worked as a domestic help for other f amil
2 CONC ies, and for 25 years was a burler at Dorman Mills in Parsons. Ref lect
2 CONC ively she said, " I raised a pretty good bunch of kids. They've be en r
2 CONC eal good to me."
2 CONT
2 CONT The oldest, Mrs. David(Gertrude) Beck, died shortly after the birth o f h
2 CONC er fifth child. two of their children died in infancy. Mrs. Mary Be ck B
2 CONC urdette of Beverly has five children, John, Debbie, Steve, Jeani e and R
2 CONC obbie. Melvern Beck makes his home with his grandmother. Mrs. W endel ( M
2 CONC argaret"Skeeter"Beck) Slater of Riverside, calif., has two so ns, Micha
2 CONC el and Scott and a daughter Marilyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The late Johnnie Goss and his wife, The former Christeen Repair, hav e t
2 CONC wo sons and two daughters. Mrs. Goss lives in Parsons near her moth er-
2 CONC in-law. Mrs. Carol Jean Goss Flynn has three daughters, Debbie, Chr ist
2 CONC i and Kellie Jean; Johnnie Goss Jr. is the father of Michael and Fr ed; D
2 CONC onald T. and Saundra Syrian Goss are the parents of Donald Jr.,Mi chael a
2 CONC nd Shawn; and Mrs. Linda Knotts is the mother of Rebecca, Pamel a, Paul a
2 CONC nd Audrey Pennington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo Goss and his wife were the parents of Sandra Goss; he and his pres e
2 CONC nt wife, the former Irene Carr. are the parents of Leo Jr. and Margar e
2 CONC t. They also are residents of Parsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Mr. and Mrs. Bill (Margaret Goss) Harlow are deceased. they had n o c
2 CONC hildren, but raised Margaret Slater from the time of her birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald Carl Goss and the former Hilda Gauer, also of Parsons, are th e p
2 CONC arents of two daughters and a son. Mr. and Mrs. Ernest (Donna Goss ) Ca
2 CONC rr have three daughters, Jeana, Julie and Holly; Mr. and Mrs. Euge ne(J
2 CONC eanie Goss) Bonner have two, Michael and Amy; Danny and Belva Dill y Go
2 CONC ss have a daughter, Beth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Robert(Lucille Goss stone) Beal of Cleveland has six children. Mr . a
2 CONC nd Mrs, Fred "Buddy" Stone are the parents of Steve, David and Done lle
2 CONC ; Mrs. Judy Rhoades is the mother of Carl, Dawn Lynn and Dusty; bot h L
2 CONC arry and Frankie Stone each have a daughter; the youngest son, Rich , i
2 CONC s not married; the youngest daughter is Mrs. Linda Mistur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Goss Also raised her granddaughter, Mary, and Melvern has lived w i
2 CONC th her for more than 20 years. " Henry Thompson was just like one o f u
2 CONC s too," she said. The late Mr. Thompson was in the armed forces whe n h
2 CONC is grandmother's home burned and she died and after that, Mrs. Gos s sa
2 CONC ys, he was in her home frequently and many of the sets of salt an d pep
2 CONC per shakers were gifts from him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter-in-law Christeen, and granddaughter, Linda, held a surpri s
2 CONC e birthday party for Mrs. Goss when she celebrated her eightieth birt h
2 CONC day last March 16.  Most of the family was present and she says "I to l
2 CONC d Rev. Gutshall, 'Just look, one old woman responsible for so many! ' "
2 CONT
2 CONT She laughingly calls her cat, Fluffy, "the boss" of their home, and hi s p
2 CONC icture is included among the many, many photos she proudly display s of h
2 CONC er children, grandchildren and great-grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Goss had a heart attack three or four years ago and has moved he r b
2 CONC ed downstairs but is still able
2 CONT to do a lot of her own housework. She occasionally goes to town althou g
2 CONC h her eyesight is getting quite bad. She has had cataracts removed fr o
2 CONC m one eye and is slated to have them removed from the second, but wan t
2 CONC s to wait until after Christmas because she doesn't want to be away f r
2 CONC om home during the holidays.
