2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  copied from the Parson Advocate December 3, 1970
2 CONT
2 CONT SAUL HEBB SAYS PEOPLE OF STATE SHOULD STAND FIRM AGAINST PROPOSED DAM
2 CONT
2 CONT The gentleman stands about 6 feet tall of slim build, and at the tim e o
2 CONC f his interview wore a red and black checkered flannel shirt, a pai r o
2 CONC f green trousers held up by suspenders, and a grey-colored hat.
2 CONT
2 CONT A gold watch and chain was carried in his right-hand pocket, and he wo r
2 CONC e gold rimmed glasses. He smoked a pipe during most of the conversati o
2 CONC n. His name is Solomon Elsworth Hebb.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 90 he seems to manage quite well. He lives in a five or six roo m h
2 CONC ouse with his son Allen S. Hebb.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Mill Run one mile above St. George on March 15, 1880. H i
2 CONC s parents were John C and Marcella Margaret (Lipscomb) Hebb.
2 CONT
2 CONT He has 11 sisters and brothers, four of whom are still living. Mrs. Al b
2 CONC erta Mae Williams, Mrs. Pearl Corrick, and Mrs. Merle Goss, also of P a
2 CONC rsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Those deceased are: Mrs. Mandy Miller, Mrs. Augusta Lee Phillips, Mrs . A
2 CONC ddie Miller, Jenny Hebb who died when she was only two years old, C har
2 CONC les William Hebb, Albert Thornton Hebb and Johnny E. Hebb.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended school at Macedonia and quit at the age of 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saul, as he is so nicknamed, said he has never been in the service, bu t t
2 CONC hat he would have liked to have fought in the Spanish-American Wa r in 1
2 CONC 898 if he had known how to go about joining.
2 CONT
2 CONT He called himself a "greenhorn" for not knowing anything about joinin g t
2 CONC he service.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saul Hebb began working as a cook's helper at a logging firm located o n t
2 CONC he Cheat River. He remembered that a man named John Repair happene d to b
2 CONC e the foreman at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He soon began "rafting" logs down the river to a saw mill called Pt. M a
2 CONC rion Mill that later was called Dewing Lumber County
2 CONT
2 CONT Hebb explained that there were as many as a 100 logs per raft. Two o r t
2 CONC hree men would get on the raft with their oars guiding it down th e riv
2 CONC er. He said his father was a steersman who guided the rafts throu gh th
2 CONC e channels.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a raft of logs was delivered to the mill, Saul said they collect e
2 CONC d their pay and walked some 16 miles back home. He said the hike onl y t
2 CONC ook them about 3 hours to make.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hebb was a young boy in 1888, but he said that he could remember tha t i
2 CONC t was the flood that washed all the crops out. He said " The Chea t Riv
2 CONC er was so full of logs you could walk across it." He also remembe red w
2 CONC hat he described the men calling an "ark"- actually a shanty buil t on a b
2 CONC oat- that was brought in with oxen to haul the logs that had b een wash
2 CONC ed away during the flood.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I went to mining about 1900 in Tunnelton for about 20 years." he said .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1902 Saul worked at the Pulp Mill in Parsons and then was employe d b
2 CONC y the Parsons Tannery. He said that he could remember that Wakelan d Cr
2 CONC oss was the foreman.
2 CONT
2 CONT He worked for the lumber company called Riley & Riley in 1927 and cont i
2 CONC nued at the job until the timber gave out, most of the timber being t a
2 CONC ken from Green Mountain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, not long after working in the mines, he became a "hostler" on t h
2 CONC e B&O Railroad. . In plainer terms, he fired up the engines with coal . H
2 CONC e kept this job for two years, after which he returned to the coa l min
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was at this time when he was struck by slabs of loose slate causin g a h
2 CONC ip injury. He was admitted to the Fairmont Hospital and so missin g an e
2 CONC xplosion that later occurred in the mines.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later worked during World War II at Hill's Pool Romm for five years .
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 14, 1902, Saul Hebb married Alice Maude Thomas who died in 1 9
2 CONC 57 of a cerebral hemorrhage.
2 CONT She was living in Maryland at the time he was introduced to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of his courting was done on foot or sometimes by horse and buggy . D
2 CONC uring the winter a sleigh was used as his means for transportation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bean stringings, log rollings, apple cuttings, and silent movies, whic h h
2 CONC e termed as "walk-it-talkies" that were shown in the old Victoria t hea
2 CONC tre were several entertaining pastimes during his courtship.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife had 10 children: Mrs. Mabel Tekit, Morgantown; Mrs. Et h
2 CONC el Thawley, Baltimore: Mrs. Blanche Mae Manly, Baltimore: Mrs. Hele n M
2 CONC iller, Parsons: Russell Hebb, Smithville, O; Allen Stanley Hebb, a t ho
2 CONC me; Charles William Hebb, Parsons; John Wendall Hebb, Nutter Fort ; Har
2 CONC ry Hebb, Clarksburg and Guy Hebb who died at the age of two month s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He joined the Methodist Church when he was about 16 years old, "bu t I h
2 CONC aven't been a very active member I'm afraid," he said.
2 CONT
2 CONT He has also been  a member of the United Mine Workers of America.
2 CONT
2 CONT He has lived in a number of different places in Tucker County, some o f w
2 CONC hich being Haddix, Holly Meadows, Pulp Mill Bottom and several othe r p
2 CONC laces in Parsons. During the flood of 1955, he said he lived on Qua lit
2 CONC y Hill. After the floods, he said he helped clean up the streets.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his relatives whom he called Uncle Tom Hebb once owned and ra n a h
2 CONC otel located near to where the present hardware store now stands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saul Hebb said that he spends his time making gun stalks, hammer and h a
2 CONC tchet handles, sleeping and watching T.V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hebb was very quick to say that he agreed with whatever undertaking th e g
2 CONC overnment might take as to the goings on in the Vietnam war, but wh en i
2 CONC t came to the questions as to whether he agreed or disagreed wit h the c
2 CONC oming of the Rowlesburg Dam, he firmly stated, "The people of T ucker C
2 CONC ounty and West Virginia have not got the backbone of a jelly fi sh if t
2 CONC hey let them put it in!"
