1 _UID C12EE650BFA346DBA819C665E65BA935B386
1 _AMTID 162143280569:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  He is a farmer and lives five miles below St. George-ref History of Tu c
2 CONC ker County Fansler pg.415-416
2 CONT Dateline 1938 Newspaper Clipping
2 CONT (Don't know paper)
2 CONT Parsons' Oldest Resident to Mark Ninety-Third Birthday
2 CONT By Blair B. Poling
2 CONT Parsons, May 25. ___The oldest man in Parsons will celebrate his
2 CONT ninety-third birthday anniversary Friday.  He is John C. Hebb, better
2 CONT known as "Uncle Jack".
2 CONT He fought in the Union army during the Civil war, enlisting in
2 CONT the Sixth West Virginia regiment, Company F, at Rowlesburg, and was
2 CONT stationed on a hill above Rowlesburg for eighteen months with the
2 CONT artillery.  This has resulted in his being hard of hearing as he said
2 CONT the cannons were deafening while he was stationed there.
2 CONT After the war was over, he was a woodsman in Tucker county and
2 CONT served as a steerman on the Cheat river for a long time while rafting
2 CONT timber into Rowlesburg.
2 CONT His wife, Marcella Lipscomb, whom he married in 1871, died
2 CONT eighteen years ago.
2 CONT This aged pioneer was born near Rowlesburg, Mar 27,1845, a son of
2 CONT Thornton and Catherine Sanders Hebb.  He has spent most of his life in
2 CONT Tucker county and makes daily trips to the business section of
2 CONT Parsons.
2 CONT He is the last of his family, all brothers and sisters being
2 CONT dead.
2 CONT Members of the family and nearby relatives will be at his home to
2 CONT honor him Friday.
2 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 11, Ed. 1, Tree #1647, Date of Import: Nov 9, 199 8
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dateline 1938 Newspaper Clipping
2 CONT (Don't know paper)
2 CONT Parsons' Oldest Resident to Mark Ninety-Third Birthday
2 CONT By Blair B. Poling
2 CONT Parsons, May 25. ___The oldest man in Parsons will celebrate his
2 CONT ninety-third birthday anniversary Friday.  He is John C. Hebb, better
2 CONT known as "Uncle Jack".
2 CONT He fought in the Union army during the Civil war, enlisting in
2 CONT the Sixth West Virginia regiment, Company F, at Rowlesburg, and was
2 CONT stationed on a hill above Rowlesburg for eighteen months with the
2 CONT artillery.  This has resulted in his being hard of hearing as he said
2 CONT the cannons were deafening while he was stationed there.
2 CONT After the war was over, he was a woodsman in Tucker county and
2 CONT served as a steerman on the Cheat river for a long time while rafting
2 CONT timber into Rowlesburg.
2 CONT His wife, Marcella Lipscomb, whom he married in 1871, died
2 CONT eighteen years ago.
2 CONT This aged pioneer was born near Rowlesburg, Mar 27,1845, a son of
2 CONT Thornton and Catherine Sanders Hebb.  He has spent most of his life in
2 CONT Tucker county and makes daily trips to the business section of
2 CONT Parsons.
2 CONT He is the last of his family, all brothers and sisters being
2 CONT dead.
2 CONT Members of the family and nearby relatives will be at his home to
2 CONT honor him Friday.
2 CONT
2 CONT STORY ON CIVIL WAR VETERAN REPRINTED FROM 1933 NEWSPAPER
2 CONT
2 CONT The following article which appeared in a newspaper in June 1938 was b r
2 CONC ought into The Advocate office recently by Clayton Hebb of Parsons , a s
2 CONC on of the late John C. Hebb of Parsons, the Civil War veteran abo ut wh
2 CONC om the story is written. Mr. Hebb died January 18, 1941, at the a ge of 9
2 CONC 5. The story was written by the late Don Garber of Parsons.
2 CONT PARSONS, June 17.-- One of the few remaining boys who wore Blue in th e d
2 CONC ark days of the Civil War, John C. Hebb, of Parsons, relates some v ery i
2 CONC nteresting tales and strongly denies that the first Officer to b e kill
2 CONC ed in the Civil War was at Parsons, although he says the first o fficer w
2 CONC as killed in Tucker County, near Hannahsville. In regard to th is fact, w
2 CONC hich he says history never recorded, Mr. Hebb states that h e knows of L
2 CONC ieut. MacHesney being killed near a voting place close t o Hannahsville b
2 CONC y Capt. Miller and MacHensey's horse was shot too. H e had come through f
2 CONC rom Virginia and suddenly encountered Capt. Miller 's troops in Tucker C
2 CONC ounty.
2 CONT "The dead lieutenant was buried there that night," Hebb said, " but th e r
2 CONC ebels came back later, dug up his body and took it to Virginia. I t was t
2 CONC here that the first officer in the war was killed and not at Co rricks F
2 CONC ord in Parsons, where Gen. Garnett was said to have been the f irst off
2 CONC icer killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born In Preston
2 CONT This aged resident was born in Preston County, near Rowlesburg, May 27 , 1
2 CONC 845, a son of Thornton and Catherine(Sanders) Hebb.
2 CONT "I was living there when the war began but was too young to enlist. Fi n
2 CONC ally I threatened to run away from home and my father had me join th e a
2 CONC rmy," Mr. Hebb said " I enlisted in the Sixth West Virginia regimen t, C
2 CONC ompany F, on June 1863, in Rowlesburg. The regiment was under th e comm
2 CONC and of Capt. Hall. Our regiment was started for Grafton and the n to th
2 CONC e front lines but about that time General Lee made his raid thr ough Pe
2 CONC nnsylvania and we were scattered along the railroad in variou s places. P
2 CONC art of the company ended up at Parsons where the Methodis t Protestant C
2 CONC hurch now stands and another group settled on what is ca lled the Donah
2 CONC ue lot in East Parsons."
2 CONT
2 CONT Father was Officer
2 CONT Hebb's desire to join the army came from the fact that his father wa s a l
2 CONC ieutenant in Company F. Before he became a lieutenant he had acte d as a s
2 CONC cout for General McClellan and was often gone for weeks at a t ime. He w
2 CONC as warned that if he was caught as a spy he would be shot s o was persu
2 CONC aded to enlist.
2 CONT Hebb resumed by saying that " from Grafton we went to Terra Alta, an d o
2 CONC thers were sent to Grafton, Wheeling, and Rowlesburg. There were hi gh t
2 CONC restles to cross over and the officers were afraid the enemy migh t get t
2 CONC hrough and blow up the bridges. A number of bridges were bein g guarded a
2 CONC t Rowlesburg and I was sent on a high hill with 17 men wit h artillery. O
2 CONC n this hill, overlooking theRowlesburg section, was wher e I was statio
2 CONC ned for eighteen long months. One night a couple of us t ook some horse
2 CONC s out of town to keep the rebels from getting them. We h ad gone but a s
2 CONC hort distance when we heard a horse coming at double qu ick time. The r
2 CONC ider warned us to be careful that The enemy was close."
2 CONT
2 CONT Hear the Enemy Near
2 CONT " We continued on down the road," the veteran said " and came to a bar n
2 CONC . we went up in the loft and hid among the hay as the night was ver y c
2 CONC old. Several times we thought we heard the enemy and finally we wer e s
2 CONC ure of it. Peering out through the boards, we saw a group of the en emy o
2 CONC utside on horseback. we were somewhat scared but in a few minute s they s
2 CONC hurred thier horses and cut across the field to where there wa s a hous
2 CONC e with a light burning."
2 CONT Telling of one unusual experience, he told of how his father took on e c
2 CONC ompany around a hill and encountered some of the enemy. A stiff fig ht t
2 CONC ook place and one of the enemy was shot through the chest. " Docto r He
2 CONC nry was called to give him aid," Hebb said " and after examining t he w
2 CONC ound, pulled a silk handkerchief clear through the man's body fro m the c
2 CONC hest to his back, and he finally recovered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Artillery Deafens
2 CONT Hebb is hard of hearing and stated this was due to his 18 months wit h t
2 CONC he artillery on the hill near Rowlesburg. " Our drill master told u s t
2 CONC o stand close to the cannon when it was fired and to stop running b ack f
2 CONC rom it. The noise was terrible! We would often be signaled from R owles
2 CONC burg by a red flag being raised to fire the cannon as a salute wh en so
2 CONC me general was passing through." Mr. Hebb said he never lost a da y's g
2 CONC uard duty except a few while sick.
2 CONT This aged veteran said all the boys in the army were given a vote whet h
2 CONC er they were 21 years of age or not. " My first vote was cast there, " h
2 CONC e said " and i voted for Abe Lincoln. At the time Lincoln was shot , I w
2 CONC as at Cheat Bridge and the news reached us through a dispatch lat er th
2 CONC at Lincoln had died."
2 CONT
2 CONT Was a Lumberman
2 CONT After the war ended, Hebb returned to his home in the Rowlesburg commu n
2 CONC ity. In a short time he went to timbering on Limestone mountain, nea r P
2 CONC arsons, when the section was mostly a wilderness with only a few ru gge
2 CONC d mountain roads. His partner was Davy Lipscomb. Hebb was a steersm an o
2 CONC n the Cheat River for some time while rafting timber to Rowlesburg . Du
2 CONC ring the flood of 1888 which swept through Tucker County, Hebb liv ed o
2 CONC n the Repair farm, a few miles below this city.
2 CONT He said the water practically ruined his garden and wheat fields tha t y
2 CONC ear. In 1871, he was married at Licking Falls, Tucker County, to Ma rci
2 CONC lla Lipscomb, who dies in Parsons 11 years ago. To them was born 1 2 ch
2 CONC ildren.
2 CONT Out of the twelve children, the following are living: Charles, Mearl , S
2 CONC aul and Mrs. Roby Williams, of Parsons; Albert of Morgantown; Clayt on o
2 CONC f Etam; Mrs. Ada Miller of Elkins, and Mrs. Pearl Rosier of Arden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Family
2 CONT This venerable Parsons resident is the last of his family, having no b r
2 CONC others or sisters. He is a member of the Methodist Episcopal Church o f L
2 CONC imestone Mountain and has led a Christain life. While going down th e s
2 CONC unset trail, he enjoys telling  of his early days and how he firs t sta
2 CONC rted to smoking.
2 CONT In telling of his starting to smoke, he laughed heartily as his memor y w
2 CONC ent back to one night in his grandmother's home when she called ou t an
2 CONC d asked him to stay home in case some of the neighbors would com e in. G
2 CONC lancing at her corncob pipe, he filled it with tobacco and star ted puf
2 CONC fing. The smoke curled around the room and pretty soon his hea d began t
2 CONC o swim. " it seemed as if the floor would not stand still" h e said. He g
2 CONC ot very ill and swore off pipes until he joined the army . Then he boug
2 CONC ht a pipe but very soon threw it away and smoked no  mor e until the en
2 CONC d of the war, but has smoked steadily ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT Traces Ancestors
2 CONT While tracing his ancestry, he said the farthest back he could trace i t w
2 CONC as that his father's uncle, William Hebb, had come to this countr y wit
2 CONC h the British army in the Revolutionary War, but deserted and joi ned t
2 CONC he American forces. He later married Mima Washington, sister of G eorge W
2 CONC ashington.
