1 NOTE  He was murdered by the order of his wife and her lover, Roger Mortimer b
2 CONC ecause Edward was a practicing homosexual.  The traditional story i s s
2 CONC oto avoid making marks on the outside of hes body, his murderers bu rne
2 CONC dout his bowels with a red hot poker through a hollow horn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Murdered
2 CONT Name Prefix:<NPFX> King
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8WKN-JD
2 CONT [janet skelton.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Murdered
2 CONT
2 CONT [janet skelton.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT He lacked the royal dignity of his father and failed miserably as King . H
2 CONC e inherited his father's war with Scotland and displayed his inepti tud
2 CONC e as a soldier. Disgruntled Barons, already wary of Edward as Princ e o
2 CONC f Wales, sought to check his power from the beginning of his reign . He r
2 CONC aised the ire of the nobility by lavishing money and other rewar ds upo
2 CONC n his male favorites. Such extreme unpopularity would eventuall y cost E
2 CONC dward his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I's dream of a unified British nation quickly disintegrated und e
2 CONC r his weak son. Baronial rebellion opened the way for Robert The Bruc e t
2 CONC o reconquer much of Scotland. In 1314, Bruce defeated English force s a
2 CONC t the battle of Bannockburn and ensured Scottish independence unti l th
2 CONC e union of England and Scotland in 1707. Bruce also incited rebell ion i
2 CONC n Ireland and reduced English influence to the confines of the Pa le.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward's preference for surrounding himself with outsiders harkened ba c
2 CONC k to the troubled reign of Henry III. The most notable was Piers Gave s
2 CONC ton, a young Gascon exiled by King Edward I for his undue influence o n t
2 CONC he Prince of Wales and, most likely, the king's homosexual lover. T he a
2 CONC rrogant and licentious Gaveston wielded considerable power after b eing r
2 CONC ecalled by Edward. The magnates, alienated by the relationship , rallie
2 CONC d in opposition behind the King's cousin, Thomas, Earl of Lanc aster; t
2 CONC he Parliaments of 1310 and 1311 imposed restrictions on Edward 's power a
2 CONC nd exiled Gaveston. The barons revolted in 1312 and Gavesto n was murde
2 CONC red - full rebellion was avoided only by Edward's acceptanc e of furthe
2 CONC r restrictions. Although Lancaster shared the responsibilit ies of gove
2 CONC rning with Edward, the king came under the influence of ye t another de
2 CONC spicable favorite, Hugh Dispenser. In 1322, Edward showe d a rare displ
2 CONC ay of resolve and gathered an army to meet Lancaster a t the Battle of B
2 CONC oroughbridge in Yorkshire. Edward prevailed and execu ted Lancaster. He a
2 CONC nd Dispenser ruled the government but again acquire d many enemies - 28 k
2 CONC nights and barons were executed for rebelling an d many exiled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward sent his queen, Isabella, to negotiate with her brother, Frenc h K
2 CONC ing Charles IV, regarding affairs in Gascony. She fell into an ope n ro
2 CONC mance with Roger Mortimer, one of Edward's disaffected Barons, an d per
2 CONC suaded Edward to send their young son to France. The rebellious c ouple i
2 CONC nvaded England in 1326 and imprisoned Edward. The King was depo sed in 1
2 CONC 327, replaced by his son, Edward III, and murdered in Septembe r at Ber
2 CONC keley Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Baker, in reference to Edward I in A Chronicle of the King s o
2 CONC f England, makes a strong indictment against Edward II: "His grea t unf
2 CONC ortunateness was in his greatest blessing; for of four sons whic h he h
2 CONC ad by his Queen Eleanor, three of them died in his own lifetime , who w
2 CONC ere worthy to have outlived him; and the fourth outlived him, w ho was w
2 CONC orthy never to have been born."
