2 CONT
2 CONT Taillefer, Isabelle of Angouleme, Queen of England -
2 CONT
2 CONT She was betrothed to Hugh before she married John. After John's deat h s
2 CONC he retired to her native city and eventually married Hugh after abo ut 3
2 CONC years. Countess of Angouleme 1202.[Talcott.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Taillefer, Isabelle of Angouleme, Queen of England -
2 CONT
2 CONT BARONIAL ORDER OF RUNNEMEDE Historians, basing their knowledge or dedu c
2 CONC tions on the statements of the few prejudiced chronicles of the earl y p
2 CONC ortion of t he Plantagenet period, and earlier, differ in details o f t
2 CONC he certainty of gre at and minor events leading up to and causing t he f
2 CONC inal insurrection of the barons of England; but in the main they a gree t
2 CONC hat there were efforts to give and establish in the Anglo-Norman , or E
2 CONC nglish nation, a certain definite se t of laws which we would ca ll a C
2 CONC onstitution. Therefore, the uprising of the barons, in 1212-1215 , may b
2 CONC e termed the culmination of desire for a tangible form of gover nment. T
2 CONC he spirit of enlightment was upon them, and their eyes w ere w ide open t
2 CONC o the abuse of the kingly power, and the outcome was the Mag na Charta, t
2 CONC he first great public act of the English nation after it r ealized i ts o
2 CONC wn identity. The ascent of John, Count of Montaigne, (yo unger brother o
2 CONC f t he late king, Richard I), to the throne on Ascensio n Day in 1199, h
2 CONC e being crowned at Westminister, instead of Arthur, so n and heir of hi
2 CONC s elder brother , who would have succeeded had he bee n alive, was appa
2 CONC rently satisfactory to the majority of the barons an d the people gener
2 CONC ally, as his succession sprang out of a national cho ice as well as an a
2 CONC lleged regal inheritance, though it w as not so i n his duchy of Norman
2 CONC dy, nor with the French king, Philip II, and hi s nobles, who with his m
2 CONC other championed the right of Arthur's success ion . However, next year K
2 CONC ing John made his peace with KingPhilip, an d visited h im in Paris, an
2 CONC d might have enjoyed his regal rights peace fully had it not be en that h
2 CONC e was hampered and handicapped from the s tart by a few discontented ec
2 CONC clesiastics, who were sufficiently influe ntial to make his reign an ex
2 CONC ceedi ngly troublesome one to the end. I n his first coronation oath Jo
2 CONC hn swore that, bad laws being destroyed , he would substitute good ones
2 CONC , and exercise true j ustice in his rea lm. Therefore, early in his rei
2 CONC gn the propriety of fixed law s was the subject of the greatest interes
2 CONC t, and it was championed at the fir s t by the northern barons, Robert F
2 CONC itzwalter, Eustace de Vesci and SA IER de Q UINCEY being the leaders in t
2 CONC he matter. These barons, on th e part of the whole baronage, kept at th
2 CONC e king, persistently requestin g him to confirm a code of definite stat
2 CONC utes. To conciliate them, an d to gain, if possible, a cessatio n, or a
2 CONC t least a respite from thei r importunities, John, as had his predecess o
2 CONC rs,promised the restorati on of those liberties the barons, and very pr
2 CONC obably "the people", thou gh they had no voice in the matter, so earnes
2 CONC tly desired, a confirmati on of the charter of rights and liberties gra
2 CONC nted by his grand father , HENRY I, and the revival or adoption of the o
2 CONC ld Anglo-Saxon code. Jo hn's first seven regal years had been historica
2 CONC lly uneventful, when th e Unive rsal Church for some purpose again arou
2 CONC sed ill-will between hi m and Philip, a nd rebellion in John's duchy, w
2 CONC hich had come to look u pon him only as an alie n. To secure Normandy, J
2 CONC ohn tried to raise a s ufficient force in England, but found his subjec
2 CONC ts generally indiffere nt to his aim, since they had lost int erest in t
2 CONC he land of their fore fathers, and it was, any way, only a personal mat
2 CONC ter of the King.
2 CONT Queen of England
