2 CONT
2 CONT John was the leader of the English forces which lost to William Wallac e
2 CONC in the battle of Stirling Bridge in 1297 as depicted in the film"Brav e
2 CONC heart".
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT EARLDOM OF SURREY (VII) 1240
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN (DE WARENNE), EARL OF SURREY, only son and heir, by 2nd wife, bor n
2 CONC in or after August 1231. His mother released to him his father's land s i
2 CONC nNorfolk on 1 April 1248, and the King allowed him to have seisin , tho
2 CONC ughhe was not of age. In December 1252 he was forbidden to take p art i
2 CONC n thedispute between the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop- elect o
2 CONC fWinchester. In 1254 he joined the King at Bordeaux; and on 1 4 Septemb
2 CONC eroffered himself as hostage for the King's debt there. In 12 55 he joi
2 CONC nedthe other nobles in their resistance to the influx of fore igners in
2 CONC toEngland; in September he was instructed to escort the Kin g of Scotla
2 CONC nd tothe King. About the same time he was granted to himsel f and heirs w
2 CONC eeklymarkets at several of his manors. In 1257 he went wi th Richard, E
2 CONC arl ofCornwall, King elect of the Romans, to Almain. By w rit dated 14 M
2 CONC arch1257/8 he was summoned to Chester to perform his ser vice against t
2 CONC heWelsh, and frequently thereafter until 1294. In June 1 258 he was pre
2 CONC sentat a Parliament at Oxford, when the nobles demande d the observance o
2 CONC fMagna Carta. In July he was one of the Earls assign ed as escort to th
2 CONC eKing's brothers to Dover and Witsand; in October h e was sick at Hamps
2 CONC teadMarshall. In March 1260 he was summoned to Lond on with horses and a
2 CONC rms,and was directed to live at Clerkenwell or th e New Temple. In Apri
2 CONC l 1260the King returned from France, bringing wit h him many foreigners a
2 CONC ndexasperating the nobles, including the Earl . By 28 December the Earl h
2 CONC adgone overseas in the service of Prince Ed ward. On 20 March 1262/3 he
2 CONC landed at Dover, and in May he was sum. t o Worcester; in August he was
2 CONC appointed to treat of peace with Llewelin , son of Griffith. On 18Septe
2 CONC mber he was granted all the lands of th e Normans which he had, orwhich f
2 CONC ell within his fee. Between October a nd December he with many ofthe ma
2 CONC gnates joined Simon de Montfort and P rince Edward. On 16 Decemberhe as
2 CONC sented to the King's agreement to sub mit the question of theProvisions o
2 CONC f Oxford to arbitration by the Kin g of France. On 24 Decemberhe was ap
2 CONC pointed Keeper of the counties o f Surrey and Sussex. In April1264 he g
2 CONC arrisoned the Castle of Rocheste r, where he was besieged forabout a we
2 CONC ek by Simon de Montfort, until h e was relieved by the King; andhe was i
2 CONC n the Prince's army at the batt le of Lewes on 14 May, whence heand the K
2 CONC ing's brothers fled to Pevens ey, subsequently crossing toFrance. On 10 M
2 CONC ay 1265 he landed at Pembro ke with William de Valence anda body of arm
2 CONC ed men, and the King issue d warnings to the sheriffs andothers not to e
2 CONC ncourage them. On 24 Ma y he sued for the return of hislands. On 8 June P
2 CONC rince Edward joined t hem at Ludlow and on 28 June theywere holding man
2 CONC y towns. On 1 Augus t they reached Kenilworth. On 7 May1266 it was orde
2 CONC red that archers sh ould be sent to London to him; and on27 May, with W
2 CONC illiam de Valence , he went to Bury St. Edmunds to fine theAbbey and th
2 CONC e burgesses. Towa rds the end of May 1267 his negotiations toinduce the E
2 CONC arl of Gloucest er to submit were successfully concluded; inthe Octave o
2 CONC f Michaelmas h e received a pardon for excesses committed inthe recent t
2 CONC ime of distur bance; and on 10 March 1267/8 was pardoned fornon-observa
2 CONC nce of the Pr ovisions of Oxford. On the Octave of St. John in1270 he q
2 CONC uarrelled i n Westminster Hall with Alan la Zouche, and attackedhim so v
2 CONC iolently t hat he died on 10 August, his son escaping withdifficulty. T
2 CONC he Earl fl ed to his castle at Reigate, pursued by PrinceEdward, and be
2 CONC gged for m ercy; and on 4 August 1270 he was pardoned on hisundertaking t
2 CONC o pa y a substantial sum to the King. On 20 November 1272, 4days after t
2 CONC h e King's death, he swore allegiance to Prince Edward, thenon his way h o
2 CONC me from a Crusade; the Earl was one of the guardians of therealm unti l h
2 CONC is return. In March 1277/8 he was assigned with others toescort th e Ki
2 CONC ng of Scotland to London; and at Michaelmas 1278 was presentin Par liam
2 CONC ent when the King did his hornage. In 1278, he was interrogatedund er t
2 CONC he Statute of Quo Warranto as to the title by which he held hislan ds.[
2 CONC m] In September 1285 he was going with the King to Scotland. On 14 Febr
2 CONC uary 1289/90 he was going as the King's envoy to Scotland, and o n 20Ju
2 CONC ne 1290 was appointed with others to treat with the Scots. On 2 8 Augus
2 CONC tPrince Edward appointed him his procurator in the negotiation s for hi
2 CONC smarriage to Margaret Queen of Scotland, and on 1 September h e wasaccr
2 CONC edited to meet the envoys of the King of Norway in the Isle o fOrkney. O
2 CONC n 16 April 1291 he was summoned to be at Norham with horse s andarms in c
2 CONC onnection with the claims to the throne of Scotland; an d in thesame ye
2 CONC ar was appointed Keeper of Scotland. He was appointed t o thecustody of t
2 CONC he sea-coast on 16 September 1295, of the counties be yondTrent on 5 Oc
2 CONC tober, and of the castle of Bamburgh on 6 October. O n 18October he was a
2 CONC bout to go to Scotland on the King's service. On 2 7 April1296 he was a
2 CONC ttacked by the Scots near Dunbar, but repulsed the m withoutloss, and t
2 CONC he castle of Dunbar was captured. On 3 September h e wasappointed Keepe
2 CONC r of the realm of Scotland. In May 1297 he set ou t forScotland, but re
2 CONC mained in the north of England for safety, unti l he wasordered by the K
2 CONC ing, then in Flanders, to take action agains t the Scots.In August the S
2 CONC cots attacked his advance guard, under Henr y de Percy, butwere repelle
2 CONC d, and many submitted; but on 10 Septembe r the Earl wasdefeated with g
2 CONC reat slaughter at Stirling, and fled to B erwick, which heabandoned and l
2 CONC ost. On 10 December 1297 he was appoint ed Captain of thearmy to oppose t
2 CONC he invading Scots; and in January an d February 1297/8marched into Scot
2 CONC land. In 1299 and 1300 he was in Sco tland, but he was atthe Archbishop
2 CONC 's banquet in September 1299 after t he King's marriage. InJuly 1300 he w
2 CONC as at Carlisle with the King, an d he commanded the 2nddivision at the s
2 CONC iege of Carlaverock. On 1 Marc h 1300/1 he was appointedwith others to t
2 CONC reat with the envoys of Phili p, King of France, on lossesinflicted by t
2 CONC he Scots. His seal was appen ded to the Barons' Letter tothe Pope, whic
2 CONC h bears date 12 February 130 1/2.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in August 1247, Alice, uterine sister of the King, beingdau g
2 CONC hter of Hugh (LE BRUN), COUNT OF LA MARCHEand LORD OF LUSIGNAN ANDVAL E
2 CONC NCE, by Isabel, widow of JOHN, KING OF ENGLAND, daughter of Ademar,CO U
2 CONC NT OF ANGOULÊME. She died 9 February 1255/6. The Earl died aboutMicha e
2 CONC lmas 1304 at Kennington, near London, and was buried before thehigh a l
2 CONC tar at Lewes Priory. [Complete Peerage XII/1:503-7, (transcribedby Da v
2 CONC e Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT [m] He is alleged to have produced in open court an ancient rusty swor d
2 CONC saying "Here, my Lord, is my warrant. My ancestors coming in with Wil l
2 CONC iamthe Bastard won their lands with the sword, and with the sword I w i
2 CONC llhold them against all comers." He won the sympathy of his brother n o
2 CONC bles,and the King gave in.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------- -
2 CONC ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Warren (Plantagenet), Earl of Warren and Surrey, was but fivey e
2 CONC ars of age at the time of his father's decease, and was placed in war d
2 CONC with Peter de Savoy, the Queen's brother. When he attained majority , h
2 CONC eattached himself zealously to Henry III in his conflicts with th e bar
2 CONC onsand maintained the cause of the king with his sword at the bat tle o
2 CONC fLewes. His lordship was a person of violent and imperious tempe r and w
2 CONC asoften betrayed into acts of great intemperance, as in the ins tance o
2 CONC fassaulting Sir Alan Zouch and his son, Roger, in Westminster H all, wh
2 CONC enhe almost killed the one and wounded the other. And again, wh en Edwa
2 CONC rd Iissued the first writs of Quo Warranto, his lordship bein g questio
2 CONC ned asto the title of his possessions, exhibited to the justi ces an ol
2 CONC d swordand unsheathing it said, "Behold my lords, here is m y warranty; m
2 CONC yancestors coming into this land with William the Bastard , did obtaint
2 CONC heir lands by the sword, and I am resolved with the swor d to defend th
2 CONC emagainst whomsoever shall endeavour to dispossess me, b ut our progeni
2 CONC torswere sharers and assistants therein." The earl was c onstituted, by K
2 CONC ingEdward, general of all his forces on the north of T rent for the bet
2 CONC terrestraining the insolences of the Scots; whereupo n he marched intoS
2 CONC cotland and so terrified the inhabitants that they i mmediately sued fo
2 CONC rpeace and gave hostages for their future good condu ct. But the war so
2 CONC onafter breaking out afresh, his lordship sustaine d a signal defeat at
2 CONC Strivelin where his troops fled first to Berwick , and thence intoEngla
2 CONC nd. The earl m. 1st, 1247, Alice, dau. of Hugh l e Brun, Count de laMar
2 CONC ch, and half sister by the mother of King Henr y III, and 2ndly, Joan,d
2 CONC au. of William, Lord Mowbray, and by the forme r only had issue, Willia
2 CONC m,Alianore, and Isabel. His lordship d. in 130 4, and was s. by hisgran
2 CONC dson, John de Warren (Plantagenet). [Sir Berna rd Burke, Dormant,Abeyan
2 CONC t, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Pe erage, Ltd., London,1883
2 CONC , p. 569, Warren, Earls of Surrey]
