1 NOTE  SIR JOHN LUMLEY, brother and heir, born 2 February 1382/3, was alread y a k
2 CONC night at the time of his brother's death. On 20 March 1404/5, alt hough h
2 CONC e had not then proved his age, he had a patent for livery of al l the p
2 CONC ossessions of which his father and brother were seised on the d ay on w
2 CONC hich they made forfeiture, the Earl of Somerset having surrende red his p
2 CONC atent thereof, and John having done homage and fealty. Upon h is petiti
2 CONC on in Parliament, November 1411, he obtained a declaration un der the G
2 CONC reat Seal, with the assent of the Lords Spiritual and Tempora l, that h
2 CONC e was restored to the name and capacity to hold the possessio ns of his l
2 CONC ate father and brother, notwithstanding the corruption of b lood betwee
2 CONC n
2 CONT himself and them. He took part in the French campaigns under Henry V , a
2 CONC nd was slain at the battle of Baugé in 1421.
2 CONT
2 CONT He m, Felicia, widow of John WODECOK, citizen and mercer of London, an d d
2 CONC aughter of Sir Matthew REDMAN, of Newcastle. He died as aforesaid , 22 M
2 CONC arch 1421.  His will dated 24 Apr 1418, with a codicil in Englis h adde
2 CONC d at Melun, 19 Aug 1420 (during its siege by the English), was p roved a
2 CONC t Lumley Castle, 2 Sep 1421.  [Complete Peerage VIII:270-1, (tr anscrib
2 CONC ed by Dave Utzinger)]
