1 NOTE  Sir Ralph de Lumley, 1st and last Lord (Baron) Lumley, so created by w r
2 CONC it of summons 28 Sep 1384, JP (North Ride Yorks 1394); born c1360; ca p
2 CONC tured by Scots at Battle of Otterburn but released by 1389, Captain o f B
2 CONC erwick-upon-Tweed 1391; took part 1399 in attempt by the Earl of Hu nti
2 CONC ngdon to restore Richard II (his half-brother) and was beheaded b y the c
2 CONC itizens of Cirencester Jan 1399/1400, being posthumously attain ted and h
2 CONC is peerage forfeited March 1400/1; married Elizabeth, daughte r of 3rd L
2 CONC ord (Baron) Neville (of Raby), and had [Sir John] with 11 ot her childr
2 CONC en (including an eldest son Thomas died 31 May 1400, and a 4 th son Mar
2 CONC maduke, Master of Trin Hall Cambridge, Chancellor of Cambrid ge Univers
2 CONC ity, Bishop of Carlisle 1430 and Lincoln 1451 and Treasure r of England 1
2 CONC 446).  [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BARONY OF LUMLEY (I)
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH DE LUMLEY, brother and heir, was aged 13 or 14 at his brother' s d
2 CONC eath, John de Neville [Lord Neville] of Raby being his guardian. H e ha
2 CONC d order for livery of his lands 20 August 1383. He was summoned t o Par
2 CONC liament from 15 September 1384 to 30 September 1399, by writs dir ected R
2 CONC adulpho de Lomley, whereby he is held to have become LORD LUMLE Y. Cert
2 CONC ain French prisoners, taken by Ralph Lumley, then chivaler, i n Septemb
2 CONC er 1384 had leave to return home to arrange their ransom. I n January 1
2 CONC 385 he was in the retinue of Henry de Percy, Earl of Northu mberland, i
2 CONC n Scotland, and in 1387 and 1388 associated with him in th e defence of B
2 CONC erwick-on-Tweed. In 1388 he was also a commissioner of a rray in Cheste
2 CONC r Ward, County Durham. At the battle of Otterburn (" Che vy Chase," 19 A
2 CONC ugust 1388) he was taken prisoner by the Scots, but wa s at liberty by O
2 CONC ctober 1389, when he was under orders for Berwick, th en in the keeping o
2 CONC f the Earl of Nottingham, a service on which he app ears not to have pr
2 CONC oceeded. In the autumn of 1391 he was appointed cap tain of Berwick und
2 CONC er the Earl of Northumberland. In October 1392 he o btained from the Ki
2 CONC ng permission to build and crenellate a castle at ( Little) Lumley. He w
2 CONC as on the commission of the peace in the North 'Ri ding of Yorkshire, 1
2 CONC 394 and 1397. He was in the Parliament at Westmins ter, September 1397, w
2 CONC hen all the prelates, lords, and commons were sw orn on the shrine of E
2 CONC dward the Confessor to maintain their proceeding s in support of King R
2 CONC ichard's coup d'état, and also sat in the firs t Parliament of Henry IV (
2 CONC 1399), and with all the other prelates and p eers assented to the impri
2 CONC sonment of the late King; but at Christmas 1 399 he joined the unsucces
2 CONC sful conspiracy of Richard's half-brother, t he Earl of Huntingdon, to m
2 CONC urder Henry IV and restore Richard. He wa s taken, with others of the c
2 CONC onspirators, by the townsfolk of Cirences ter, and beheaded in January 1
2 CONC 399/1400. He was attainted of treason i n Parliament in March 1400/1 wh
2 CONC ereby his peerage was forfeited, his po ssessions having been granted a
2 CONC lready on 22 January 1399/1400 to John , Earl of Somerset, brother of H
2 CONC enry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eleanor, daughter of the abovesaid John (NEVILLE), LORD NEV I
2 CONC LLE of Raby (by his ist wife, Maud PERCY), and sister of Ralph (NEVIL L
2 CONC E), 1st EARL OF WESTMORLAND, and of Thomas (NEVILLE), LORD FURNIVALLE . S
2 CONC he was still living in 1441.  [Complete Peerage VIII:269-70, (trans cri
2 CONC bed by Dave Utzinger)]
