1 NOTE  Sir Thomas de Mowbray, KG, b. 22 Mar 1365/6, d. Venice, 22 Sep 1399, L o
2 CONC rd Mowbray, Segrave, and Stourton, Earl of Nottingham 1383, Earl Mars h
2 CONC al of England 1384, Duke of Norfolk 1397; m. (1) Elizabeth, Barones s S
2 CONC trange of Blackmere, dsp 1383; m. (2) July 1384, Elizabeth Fitz Ala n.  [
2 CONC Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas de Mowbray [succeeded elder brother John, died unmarried just p r
2 CONC ior to 12 Feb 1382/3], 6th Lord (Baron) Mowbray and 7th Lord (Baron ) S
2 CONC egrave, also 1st Duke of Norfolk, so created 29 Sep 1397, as also e arl
2 CONC ier 12 Feb 1382/3 Earl of Nottingham and 12 Jan 1385/6 Earl Marshal ; i
2 CONC n addition 3rd Earl of Norfolk (as which succeeded his grandmothe r 24 M
2 CONC arch 1398/9), KG (c1383); born 22 March 1365/6; Marshal of Engla nd 138
2 CONC 5; served against the Scots 1385 and a Franco-Hispanic-Flemish f leet o
2 CONC ff Margate March 1386/7, a Lord Appellant 1387/8; Keeper of Berw ick an
2 CONC d Roxburgh and Warden of the East March 1389; Captain in Calai s Feb 13
2 CONC 90/1-95/6; King's Lt in Artois, Calais, Flanders and Picardy 1 392; Jt A
2 CONC mbassador to France Feb 1396/7 and Rhine Palatinate June 1397 ; quarrel
2 CONC ed with the Duke of Hereford (later Henry IV), each accusin g the other o
2 CONC f treason; both banished 1398; after his old enemy had us urped the thr
2 CONC one as Henry IV the conferring of the Dukedom of Norfol k was annulled b
2 CONC y Parliament 6 Oct 1399; married 1st 15 March 1382/3 E lizabeth (dsp 23 A
2 CONC ug 1383), daughter and heiress of 1st Lord (Baron) S trange of the 1360 c
2 CONC reation; married 2nd July 1384 Elizabeth (marrie d 3rd by 19 Aug 1401 S
2 CONC ir Robert Goushill (by whom she was mother of El izabeth, who married S
2 CONC ir Robert Wingfield, of Letheringham) and 4th b y 3 July 1414 Sir Gerar
2 CONC d Usflete and died 8 July 1425, leaving furthe r issue), widow of Sir W
2 CONC illiam de Montagu (dsp), eldest son of the Ear l of Salisbury, and daug
2 CONC hter of 11th/4th Earl of Arundel, and died o f plague in Venice 22 Sep 1
2 CONC 399.  [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas de Mowbray, 6th baron, then seventeen years of age, who was cre a
2 CONC ted Earl of Nottingham, as his brother had been, by charter, dated 1 2 F
2 CONC ebruary, 1383, and three years afterwards was constituted Earl Mars hal b
2 CONC y reason of his descent from Thomas, of Brotherton, his lordshi p being t
2 CONC he first who had the title of earl attached to the office. I n the 10th R
2 CONC ichard II [1387], his lordship participated in the naval v ictory achie
2 CONC ved by Richard, Earl of Arundel, over the French and Spani ards, and th
2 CONC e subsequent conquest of the battle of Brest. In the 16t h of the same r
2 CONC eign, he was made governor of Calais, and in four year s afterwards obt
2 CONC ained the king's charter of confirmation of the offic e of earl marshal o
2 CONC f England to the heirs male of his body, and that t hey, by reason of t
2 CONC he said office, should bear a golden truncheon, ena meled with black at e
2 CONC ach end, having at the upper end the kings arms , and at the lower, the
2 CONC ir own arms engraven thereon. Moreover, he stoo d in such favour that t
2 CONC he king, acknowledging his just and hereditar y title to bear for his c
2 CONC rest a golden leopard with a white label, whi ch of right belonged to t
2 CONC he king's eldest son, did, by letters patent , grant to him and his hei
2 CONC rs authority to bear the golden leopard fo r his crest, with a coronet o
2 CONC f silver about his neck instead of the la bel; and the same year appoin
2 CONC ted him justice of Chester and Flintshir e for life. In the 18th Richar
2 CONC d, he attended the king into Ireland, bu t, afterwards siding with the p
2 CONC arasites who controlled that weak and u nfortunate prince, he not only a
2 CONC ided in the destruction of his father- in-law, Richard, Earl of Arundel -
2 CONC - being one of the chief persons tha t guarded the unhappy nobleman to t
2 CONC he place of execution -- but he i s also accused of being an accomplice i
2 CONC n the murder of Thomas, of Wood stock, Duke of Gloucester, the king's u
2 CONC ncle. Certain it is that he wa s at this period in high estimation with t
2 CONC he prevailing party and obta ined a grant of all the lands of the unfor
2 CONC tunate Lord Arundel, with th ose of Thomas Beauchamp, Earl of Warwick, w
2 CONC hich had also vested in th e crown be forfeiture. These grants bore dat
2 CONC e 28 September, 1396, an d the next day he was created Duke of Norfolk (
2 CONC his grandmother, Margar et, Duchess of Norfolk, being still alive). Pro
2 CONC sperous, however, as th is nobleman's career had hitherto been, it was d
2 CONC oomed eventually t o a disgraceful termination. Henry, Duke of Hereford (
2 CONC afterwards Henr y IV), having accused his Grace of Norfolk, of speaking d
2 CONC isrespectfull y of the king, a challenge ensued, and a day was named fo
2 CONC r the combat , when the lists were accordingly set up at Gosford Green, C
2 CONC oventry, a nd the king and court were present, but just as the combatan
2 CONC ts were ab out to engage and the charge had been sounded, Richard inter
2 CONC fered, an d by the advice of his council, prohibited the conflict, bani
2 CONC shing th e Duke of Hereford for ten years,m and the Duke of Norfolk for l
2 CONC ife - - who, thereupon going abroad, d. at Venice, of the pestilence, b
2 CONC ut, a ccording to Sandford, of grief, in 1400. The duke, who along with h
2 CONC i s other great honours, was a knight of the Garter, m. 1st, Elizabeth , d
2 CONC au. of John, Lord Strange, of Blackmere, but had no issue; he m. 2n dly
2 CONC , Lady Elizabeth FitzAlan, dau. of Richard, Earl of Arundel, siste r an
2 CONC d co-heir of Thomas, Earl of Arundel, and widow of William de Mont acut
2 CONC e, by whom he had issue, Thomas, John, Isabel, Margaret, and Eliza beth
2 CONC . [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Pee rage
2 CONC s, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 387, Mowbray, Earls o f Nott
2 CONC ingham, Dukes of Norfolk, Earls-Marshal, Earls of Warren and Sur rey]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas, 6th Lord Mowbray, created after the decease of his brother, Ea r
2 CONC l of Nottingham and Duke of Norfolk, Earl Marshal, and K.G. His grace , f
2 CONC or his military services, was authorised to bear for his crest a go lde
2 CONC n leopard with a coronet of silver. In 1398 he was accused by Henr y Bo
2 CONC lingbroke, Earl of Hereford, of having spoken slanderously of th e king
2 CONC , when he charged with a design of destroying the principal memb ers of t
2 CONC he nobility. Norfolk denied the accusations and desired Herefo rd to pr
2 CONC ove its truth in single combat. The lists were set at Coventr y in the p
2 CONC resence of the king and the peers of the disputants, but o n the eve of t
2 CONC he contest the king interposed and forbade the combat. B oth Norfolk an
2 CONC d Hereford were banished, the former for life and the ot her for ten ye
2 CONC ars. Norfolk d. of the plague at Venice in 1399. By hi s first wife, El
2 CONC izabeth, dau. and heir of John, Lord Strange, of Black mere, he had no i
2 CONC ssue. By his second wife, Elizabeth, dau. and co-hei r of Richard FitzA
2 CONC lan, Earl of Arundel and Surrey, he left two sons an d two daus., viz., T
2 CONC homas, John, Margaret, and Isabel. [Sir Bernard Bu rke, Dormant, Abeyan
2 CONC t, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerag e, Ltd., London, 188
2 CONC 3, p. 485, Segrave, Barons Segrave of Barton Segra ve]
