1 NOTE  He died at the house of Sir John Gates in Bethnal Green, just north of L
2 CONC ondon. He was buried at Letheringham,, Suffolk, England; Burial place h
2 CONC as also been listed as Stepney, East of the Tower, London, Middlesex, E
2 CONC ngland. Buried in great state with a grand porcession and a huge feas t
2 CONC 'for all comers.'
2 CONT
2 CONT He Comptroller of King's Household, Knighted for bravery at battle ofS p
2 CONC urs, Knight of the Garter.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1513. He served with distinction in the campaign in France. After Henr y
2 CONC VIII routed the French at Guinegate at the Battle of the Spurs andthe r
2 CONC eby captured Tourraine (Therouane) and Tournai. He was knighted inTou r
2 CONC raine for his bravery in that campaign.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1516. He accompanied Charles Somerset abroad. (1520-1522). He alsoacco m
2 CONC panied Henry VIII to the Field of the Cloth of Gold in France (withhi s w
2 CONC ife and five other Wingfields) and to subsequent meetings withEmper or C
2 CONC harles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1523. He served in France (where his uncle, Sir Richard Wingfield ofKi m
2 CONC bolton Castle, Knight of the Garter, the famous diplomat, grandfather o
2 CONC f Captain Edward-Marie Wingfield, founding father of Jamestown, Virgi n
2 CONC ia, wasa general).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1536. Again serving in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1539. He became Vice-Chamberlain to Henry VIII, and Privy Councellor . H
2 CONC ewas appointed Captain of the Yeomen of the Guard on March 9th.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1540. One of the King's Justices at the arraingnment of Sir EdwardKnev e
2 CONC tt for ' striking in the Court and so continued Captain of theKing' s B
2 CONC odyguard until 1551-2 (Edward VI succeeding in 1547) whenadvanced t o C
2 CONC omptroller.'
2 CONT
2 CONT 1540. 'As Captain of the King's Guard he was at the entrance of Anne o f
2 CONC Cleves', Henry's 4th Queen (into the country at Deal, Kent and journe y
2 CONC ingto court via Blackheath). Thomas Wingfield , eldest son of Sir Hen r
2 CONC yWingfield, Governor of Orford Castle, was then the 1st Governor of D e
2 CONC alCastle.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1540. Arrested Thomas Cromwell at the Council Board.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1541. On April 23 at 2nd Vespers at the Palace of Westminister he wa s ,
2 CONC with the Duke of Norfolk and others. honored with the Badge of theG art
2 CONC er, and installed as a Knight of the Garter at Windsor Castle, with the E
2 CONC arl of Surrey on May 22nd .
2 CONT
2 CONT 1546. Arrested the Earl of Surrey and conducted him to the Tower onDec e
2 CONC mber 12th.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1547. He was Captain of the Guard at Henry VIII's funeral at St. Georg e
2 CONC 'sChapel. Windsor Castle. He was also an executor of the King's will . K
2 CONC ingHenry left him L200.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1549. He arrested the Protector Somerset, the Regent for Edward VI, at W
2 CONC indsor Castle on October 11th and conveyed him with a huge escort bac k
2 CONC to London, then through the streets to the Tower on October 14th.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1552. He died August 21st at young Sir John Gates' place in BethnalGre e
2 CONC n, just north of London. He was buried in great state with a grandpro c
2 CONC ession and a huge feast 'for all comers' in Stepney, just east of the T
2 CONC ower. His great house (one of many) was near St. Anne's, LimehouseChu r
2 CONC ch and was still known as 'Wingfield's Rents' in 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Harvey, Reyce's Beviary of Suffolk, 1618, ed. 1902; Beltz &He n
2 CONC nell, History of the King's Bodyguard of the Yeomen of the Guard,1904 ; J
2 CONC oycelyn Wingfield's VTF.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: David Faris, "Plantagent Ancestry of Seventeenth-CenturyColon i
2 CONC sts" (Baltimore:
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Pub. County, 1996), p. 126.
