1 NAME Gilbert 'The Red Earl' /Clare/
2 GIVN Gilbert 'The Red Earl'
2 SURN Clare
1 SEX M
1 _UID 748EFC6B4A8144F092013E1F2F9567D75AAA
1 _AMTID 162143282575:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  Gilbert, 10th Earl de Clare was only 23 years old when he was killed a t
2 CONC Bannockburn in 1314. Gilbert did not have any children and so his dea t
2 CONC hbrought an end to the male line of the Clare family. The estates sho u
2 CONC ldhave been immediately divided between Gilbert's three sisters, Elea n
2 CONC or,Margaret and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first, Edward II refused permission for the sisters to inherit this l
2 CONC and. The king knew that these estates would give great power to thewo m
2 CONC en's husbands. It was only when Edward was convinced that these menwo u
2 CONC ld support him in his struggle with his barons, that he gavepermissio n f
2 CONC or the women to inherit the estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 14th century it was common for the king to be involved inselect i
2 CONC ng husbands for the daughters of his most powerful barons.Eleanor, th e e
2 CONC ldest daughter, was only 14 years old when she was forcedto marry t he k
2 CONC ing's main adviser, Hugh Despenser, in 1306. As Eleanor wasmarrie d to o
2 CONC ne of his most loyal subjects, Edward was willing to allowher t o inher
2 CONC it her share of the Clare estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret was more of a problem as she was a widow when Gilbert was kil l
2 CONC edat Bannockburn. In 1307 Edward II had arranged for 14 year old Marg a
2 CONC retto marry his favourite knight, Piers Gaveston. Five years later Ga v
2 CONC estonwas murdered by the king's opponents. Before Margaret was allowe d t
2 CONC oinherit her share of the Clare estates in 1317 she had to agree t o ma
2 CONC rryHugh de Audley, one of the king's loyal knights.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, the youngest daughter, was also a widow in 1314. Elizabethm a
2 CONC rried John de Burgh, son of the Earl of Ulster, when she was 14 but h e
2 CONC died five years later. While Elizabeth was waiting for her inheritanc e
2 CONC ,the marcher lord, Theobald Verdun kidnapped her and took her to hisc a
2 CONC stle at Alton where he married her against her will. However, Theobal d
2 CONC Verdun died six months after the wedding.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward II decided to keep Elizabeth in custody at Bristol Castle. Thef o
2 CONC llowing year, she was granted her share of the Clare inheritance when s
2 CONC he agreed to marry Roger Damory, another one of the king's supporters .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1322 Roger Damory changed sides and fought for the Earl of Lancaste r
2 CONC at Boroughbridge. Damory was captured during the battle and was later e
2 CONC xecuted for treason. Elizabeth had remained loyal to Edward and she w a
2 CONC sallowed to keep her estates. The king now decided it would be bette r i
2 CONC fElizabeth remained a widow.
