1 NOTE  *  Titles Lord Despenser; 2nd Baron Despenser
2 CONT *  Cause of Death Hanged, drawn, and quartered for his support of Edwa r
2 CONC dII
2 CONT
2 CONT DE SPENSER, HUGH (c. 1287 - 1326), courtier. Known as 'the younger' t o d
2 CONC istinguish him from his father, like whom he was a leading figure a t E
2 CONC dward II's court, he was clearly the King's favourite by 1321. Hi s acq
2 CONC uisitiveness, especially in the Welsh Marches, led to the formati on of a
2 CONC n anti-Despenser coalition; after this was defeated at Boroughb ridge(Y
2 CONC orks) in 1322, his greed and arrogance reached new heights. H e alienat
2 CONC ed public opinion to such an extent that, when Roger Mortime r and Isab
2 CONC ella of France invaded in 1326, no one raised a finger to he lp him, an
2 CONC d he and his father were executed by the new regime.
2 CONT This Hugh is a mess on Rootsweb, I studied it for days till I decide d t
2 CONC he odds were He did not have Himself as most dictate. This is the w ay I h
2 CONC ave it as its easier to understand, "GOD" ONLY knows the truth h ere I g
2 CONC uess.
2 CONT
2 CONT BARON LE DESPENSER
2 CONT Barton, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT Buried In Tewkesbury Abbey.
2 CONT Said Fortune Increased Enormoously By Violence In 1321-6.
2 CONT Earl Gloucester.  Lord Glamorgan On Eleanor's Brother's Death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 84ZQ-94
2 CONT
2 CONT Winchester, Hampshire, Leicestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> [EARL OF WINCHES
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 84ZQ-94
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh le Despenser ("The Elder Despenser"), 1st Lord (Baron) le Despens e
2 CONC r of the 1295 Creation and 1st and last Earl of Winchester, so create d 1
2 CONC 0 May 1322; called to Parliament by writ 24 June 1295, thus being d eem
2 CONC ed to have been created a baron; sole person of rank to take Edwar d II
2 CONC 's part in the quarrel with his nobles over the notorious royal fa vour
2 CONC ite Piers Gaveston, whom Edward was eventually induced to banish ; late
2 CONC r represented Edward  in negotiating a treaty with his nobles a t the t
2 CONC ime of Gaveston's murder by them in 1312; at Battle of Bannockb urn 131
2 CONC 4; banished from court by the machinations of his enemies Feb 1 314/15; d
2 CONC isinherited and exiled in perpetuity Aug 1321, through malig n influenc
2 CONC e over Edward; this judgement reversed Jan 1321/2 and May 13 22; and af
2 CONC ter Edward II had fled to Wales was convicted as a traitor a nd hanged 2
2 CONC 7 Oct 1326, when all his honours were forfeited.  [Burke' s Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and his son were favorites of King Edward II (a weak king) and he l
2 CONC ped him throw off the mastery of Thomas, Earl of Lancaster.  Edward' s r
2 CONC eliance on the Despencer's drew the ire of his wife Isabel.  She ha d b
2 CONC ecome the mistress of Roger de Mortimer while on a diplomatic missi on t
2 CONC o France.  In September 1326 the couple invaded England, execute d the D
2 CONC espencers, and deposed Edward II in favor of his son, Edward II I.  See E
2 CONC ncyclopedia Britannica, Edward II.
