1 NOTE  [janet skelton.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice married in August 1338 (when aged about 8), Elizabeth, daughte r
2 CONC of Hugh Despenser, the younger by Eleanor, daughter and coheir of Gil b
2 CONC ertde Clare, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, ''but though thus marri e
2 CONC d at8, yet he had no issue by her till about 12 or 13 years after.' ' H
2 CONC e diedat Berkeley Castle, ''never thoroughly cured of the wounds h e re
2 CONC ceived atPoitiers,'' 8 June 1368, aged 37 and upwards, and was bur ied w
2 CONC ith hismother at St. Augustine's, Bristol. Inquisition post morte m 12 J
2 CONC uly(1368) 42 Edward III. His widow died 13 July 1389, and was bu ried a
2 CONC t StBotolph's, London. Source: CPII: 130
2 CONT Elizabeth Despenser, Lady Berkeley, was probably the youngest of HughD e
2 CONC spenser the younger and Eleanor de Clare's children. After her father '
2 CONC sdownfall and execution, she escaped the fate of 3 of her elder siste r
2 CONC s -being sent to a nunnery for life - due to her extreme youth. In Au g
2 CONC ust1338, young Elizabeth Despenser was delivered by the prioress of W i
2 CONC x tothe care of her aunt Elizabeth de Burgh, Lady of Clare. The youn g g
2 CONC irlwas part of her aunt's household for about a year and a half, wh en s
2 CONC hewas sent to Tewkesbury to live with the Berkeleys. Elizabeth Des pens
2 CONC ermaintained a close relationship with her aunt up until the latte r's d
2 CONC eathin 1360. This information is from Frances Underhill's book "F or He
2 CONC r GoodEstate: The Life of Elizabeth de Burgh."
2 CONT Elizabeth Despenser and Maurice, Lord Berkeley, had seven children tha t
2 CONC survived infancy. But apparently only two (sons) had issue themselves .
