1 SEX M
1 _UID F8896415C2E74F758E8D8CC80EA6A9386B77
1 _AMTID 162143282687:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  Also Duke of Normandy, Count of Anjou. Crowned, 27 May 1199.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are quite a number of ironies connected with the reign of John , f
2 CONC orduring his reign all the vast Plantagenet possessions in France e xce
2 CONC ptGascony were lost. From now on, the House of Anjou was separate d fro
2 CONC m itslinks with its homeland, and the Crown of England eventuall y coul
2 CONC dconcern itself solely with running its own affairs, free from C ontine
2 CONC ntalintrigue. But that was later. In the meantime, John's mishan dling o
2 CONC f hisresponsibilities at home led to increased baronial resista nce and t
2 CONC o thegreat concessions of the Magna Carta, hailed as one of t he greate
2 CONC stdevelopments in human rights in history and the precursor o f the Uni
2 CONC tedStates Bill of Rights. It was also in John's reign that th e first i
2 CONC ncometax was levied in England. In an attempt to recover hi s lost land
2 CONC s inFrance, John introduced his tax of one thirteenth on in come from r
2 CONC entsand moveable property, to be collected by the sheriffs.
2 CONT
2 CONT To be fair to the unfortunate John, his English kingdom had been drain e
2 CONC dof its wealth for Richard's wars in France and the Crusade as well a s t
2 CONC heexorbitant ransom. His own resources were insufficient to overcom e t
2 CONC heproblems he thus inherited. He also lacked the military abilitie s of h
2 CONC isbrother. It has been said that John could win a battle in a su ddendi
2 CONC splay of energy, but then fritter away any advantage gained i n a spell
2 CONC of indolence. More than one historian wrote of John as havin g the ment
2 CONC alabilities of a great king, but the inclinations of a pett y tyrant.
2 CONT
2 CONT John alienated his vassals in Aquitaine by divorcing his first wife,Is a
2 CONC bella of Gloucester (who had failed to give him a son and heir), andt a
2 CONC king as his second wife the teenage daughter of the Count of Angoulem e
2 CONC ,a political move that brought him no gain. The young woman was alrea d
2 CONC ybetrothed to Hugh de Lusignan of Poitou, and John was summoned to ap p
2 CONC earbefore Philip ll his nominal overlord in France. After all his lan d
2 CONC s inFrance were forfeited for his refusal to appear, John seized thei n
2 CONC itiative, marched to Poitier, seized young Arthur, and released Elean o
2 CONC rof Aquitaine, who was held captive. He then threw everything away by r
2 CONC eleasing the most dangerous of his prisoners, who continued the revol t
2 CONC against him and worse, he had Arthur of Brittany killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Arthur was murdered, it was the end for John's hopes in France. T h
2 CONC eact alienated just about everybody, and Philip now pressed home hisa d
2 CONC vantage. The King of England's ineptitude and lack of support, despit e
2 CONC winning some victories in some provinces, eventually caused him to fl e
2 CONC eacross the Channel, never to return. It was the greatest reverse suf f
2 CONC eredby the English Crown since the Battle of Hastings in 1066. When J o
2 CONC hnreached England, the only French lands left to him, apart from Gasc o
2 CONC ny,was the Channel Islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip had not been the only one to be upset by John's repudiation ofI s
2 CONC abella. The English barons were also indignant. They had begun to los e
2 CONC confidence in their feudal lord. After Richard's death, they had litt l
2 CONC efaith in a victory over the King of France and became weary of fight i
2 CONC ngJohn's wars, deserting him in droves. When John began to direct his a
2 CONC ttention to matters in England, he was unable to gain their confidenc e
2 CONC .William the Lion of Scotland seized the opportunity to reassert hisc o
2 CONC untry's claim to Northumberland and Cumberland, though his age and la c
2 CONC kof allies prevented him from achieving his aims. John's greatestprob l
2 CONC ems, apart from the mistrust of his barons, lay not with Scotland,bu t w
2 CONC ith the Church of Rome, now under a strong and determined Pope,Inno cen
2 CONC t lll.
2 CONT
2 CONT Innocent, Pope from 1198 to 1216 was the first to style himself "Vica r o
2 CONC fChrist." He proved to be a formidable adversary to the English Kin g.T
2 CONC heir major dispute came over the appointment of the new Archbisho p ofC
2 CONC anterbury at the death of Hubert Walter in 1205. John refused t o accep
2 CONC tStephen Langton, an Englishman active in the papal court at Ro me. He w
2 CONC aspunished by the Interdict of 1208, and for the next five yea rs, Engl
2 CONC ishpriests were forbidden from administering the sacraments, e ven from
2 CONC burying the dead. Most of the bishops left the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT York had been without an archbishop since 1207 when John's half brothe r
2 CONC Geoffrey had fled to the continent after a quarrel over church taxes . I
2 CONC n1209, Innocent excommunicated John, who was eventually forced to s ubm
2 CONC itby accepting Langton as his primary Church leader. Not only that , bu
2 CONC t hehad to place England under the direct overlordship of the papa cy, a
2 CONC nd itwas this humiliation that completely destroyed his politica l cred
2 CONC ibility.In the meantime, however, John had successfully dealt wi th the p
2 CONC roblem ofIreland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King had already been in Ireland, sent by his father to try tocomp l
2 CONC ete Henry's plans to bring the feuding Irish chiefs and independentNo r
2 CONC man lords to order. He had failed miserably, and the behavior of hisu n
2 CONC disciplined troops quickly led to his ignominious withdrawal from tha t
2 CONC troubled land. The campaign of 1210 was more successful. ManyAnglo-No r
2 CONC man lords had consolidated major landholdings and were indefiance o f r
2 CONC oyal authority. John's efforts to bring them to heel provedto be on e o
2 CONC f the few successes of his seventeen-year reign. He alliedhimself w ith t
2 CONC he Irish chiefs, and with their help was able to dispossessthe po werfu
2 CONC l Walter and Hugh de Lacy. He placed the royal Justiciar incharg e of I
2 CONC reland and had castles built at Carrickfergus and Dublin tostren gthen E
2 CONC nglish control over the country.
2 CONT It was time for the king of England to turn back to France. In 1212,Jo h
2 CONC n's plans to re-conquer his former French possessions led to therevol t o
2 CONC f his barons. His request for money and arms was the flash point.Wh en t
2 CONC he northern barons refused to help, John took an army to punish th ereb
2 CONC els. Only Langton's intervention effected a reconciliation. Theexp edit
2 CONC ion to Poitou then proceeded, but ended in total failure with thed efea
2 CONC t by Philip at Bouvines. His continued disregard of feudal law and cust
2 CONC oms, allied to the disgrace of the defeat in France and loss oflan ds, w
2 CONC ere now seized on by the majority of English barons who presented their g
2 CONC rievances at Runnymede, on June 15, 1215.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Magna Carta, the "Great Charter" was something of a compromise, at r
2 CONC eaty of peace between John and his rebellious barons, whose chiefgrie v
2 CONC ance was that of punishment without trial. Archbishop Langton drewu p t
2 CONC he grievances into a form of statements that constitute a complexdo cum
2 CONC ent of 63 clauses. Though John's signature meant that baronialgriev anc
2 CONC es were to be remedied, in later years, the charter became almost a man
2 CONC ifesto of royal powers. In fact, for the next 450 years, even tho ughJo
2 CONC hn reluctantly signed the charter, all subsequent rulers of Engla ndfun
2 CONC damentally disagreed with its principles. They preferred to seeth emsel
2 CONC ves as the source of all laws and thus above the law.
2 CONT For posterity, however, the two most important clauses were 39, whichs t
2 CONC ates that no one should be imprisoned without trial and 40, whichstat e
2 CONC s that no one could buy or deny justice. Also of particular interesti s t
2 CONC he provision that taxes henceforth could not be levied except witht he a
2 CONC greement of leading churchmen and barons at a meeting to which 40d ays n
2 CONC otice was to be given. In addition, restrictions were placed on t hepow
2 CONC ers of the king's local officials to prevent them from abusing th eirfi
2 CONC nancial, administrative and judicial powers. Weights and measure s were
2 CONC regulated, the safety of merchants ensured and the privileges o f theci
2 CONC tizens of London were confirmed. The most lasting effect of the somewha
2 CONC t vague conditions of the Magna Carta was the upholding ofindiv idual r
2 CONC ights against arbitrary government.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baronial rebellion in England was not crushed by the provisions signe d a
2 CONC tRunnymede. John spent the rest of his reign marching back and fort htr
2 CONC ying to stamp out opposition that was led by Prince Louis of France ,so
2 CONC n of Philip ll, but achieving little. One persistent legend is tha t he
2 CONC lost all his baggage train, including the Crown jewels in the mars hy a
2 CONC reaknown as the Wash in the county of Norfolk. The angry and frust rate
2 CONC d kingdied in October 1216. His burial at Worcester, however, show ed t
2 CONC hat thecenter of Plantagenet rule was now firmly established in En glan
2 CONC d, and notFrance (both Henry ll and Richard l had been buried in A njou
2 CONC ).
