1 NOTE  Henry III, who succeeded [his father, King John] when he was only nine y
2 CONC ears old,...played a large part in an important event connected wit h t
2 CONC hehistory of Lewes which had a far-reaching effect on the developme nt o
2 CONC fEngland.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry appears to have been a man of artistic tastes as well aspos s
2 CONC essing a fondness for magnificence. For years during his reign therew a
2 CONC s misgovernment, for Henry had no sense of political justice. Hisextr a
2 CONC vagance, coupled with several bad harvests, brought grave discontenta n
2 CONC d hastened matters to a climax, and to a conflict between King andbar o
2 CONC ns. Simon de Montfort, Duke of Leicester, a nephew of the King,becam e t
2 CONC heir leader, and in the spring of 1264 gather together a force of15 ,00
2 CONC 0 men and began to march to the relief of the Cinque Ports on theco ast o
2 CONC f Sussex, which the King was threatening.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King and his army arrived in the neighbourhood of Lewes in May. De M
2 CONC ontfort's men had also entered the county and were encamped at thevil l
2 CONC age of Fletching in the Weald, eight miles away. The King was staying w
2 CONC ith his brother,... known as the King of the Romans, at the Priory of S
2 CONC t. Pancras, at Southover. His son, Prince Edward, was the guest of Jo h
2 CONC nde Warenne, Earl of Surrey, at Lewes Castle....
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Montfort seems to have made a last-minute attempt to come tot e
2 CONC rms with the King...but Henry refused to listen [to the bishops thatw e
2 CONC re sent to him]. [On the break of day, Wednesday, 13 May 1264, deMont f
2 CONC ort's men set out for field of battle, on a long march. They hadappar e
2 CONC ntly spent the night before in prayer. But the King and his companyha d s
2 CONC pent the night in feasting at the Priory and were] in such a lively and r
2 CONC iotous state that the Prior and his monks were shocked. [The King was s
2 CONC urprised that the other force was so near, and hastily collecte d hisfo
2 CONC rces to meet it.] The King himself led the centre of his army w ith his
2 CONC banner bearing a white dragon fluttering in the breeze, givin g as hisb
2 CONC attlecry, 'Symon, je vous defie'.
2 CONT
2 CONT The clash between the two armies took place on Mount Harry, which is n o
2 CONC tfar from the present racecourse. The name of that point of the downs , i
2 CONC s,however, believed to have no connexion with the king. [At first t heb
2 CONC attle went in the King's favour, and Prince Edward, commanding th e arm
2 CONC yon the left, drove some of de Montfort's men down the hillside t o the
2 CONC Weald by Offham, probably close to where there is now a disused c halkp
2 CONC it. So sure was he that the day was theirs, that he followed ther etrea
2 CONC ting men for miles, eventually returning to the top of the down s tofin
2 CONC d that the King's army had been beaten and his soldiers were fl eeingin a
2 CONC ll directions.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Montfort strangely enough brought along with his army threeun f
2 CONC ortunate aldermen of London who had recenty shut the drawbridge ofLon d
2 CONC on Bridge in his face. [He imprisoned them in a kind of coachprotecte d w
2 CONC ith metal plates. The Kings' men had not been able todistinguish d e Mo
2 CONC ntfort himself during the battle; but thought the coachprobably co ntai
2 CONC ned him. They thrust spears through the iron grating atthe side an d th
2 CONC e aldermen were killed. The story used to be told thatKing Henry s urre
2 CONC ndered to Simon de Montfort on the field of battle, butthe King ac tual
2 CONC ly fell back to the Priory and fortified himself and someof his me n in
2 CONC side. His brother, King of the Romans, also fled and oldballads sa y he t
2 CONC ook refuge in a windmill, where he was afterwardsdiscovered an d taken p
2 CONC risoner. But all mills in Henry's day werewatermills. The mil l in ques
2 CONC tion may have been a very small watermillknown to have stoo d on the ba
2 CONC nks of the Winterbourne in Southover.]
2 CONT
2 CONT [The beaten army was pursued through Lewes and there was fighting in t h
2 CONC enarrow streets, continuing down the slopes through the East Gate an d o
2 CONC nto the narrow bridge over the river, where there was a frightful m ele
2 CONC e,and on toward the marshes beyond where many men were lost. 5,00 0 men w
2 CONC eresaid to have perished in the fighting. Earlier a band of d e Montfor
2 CONC t'smen were captured and imprisoned in Lewes Castle. Simon at tacked th
2 CONC ecastle to release them, and the castle defenders apparentl y used some
2 CONC kind of fire missile in return. Many of these fell on the t own and set
2 CONC fire to thatched roofs on the houses beneath the castle. D e Montfort's
2 CONC men then turned their attention to the Prior where the Kin g was. They s
2 CONC etthe building alight with their fire missiles, to the in dignation of t
2 CONC hemonks; but with the help of soldiers the flames were m astered and th
2 CONC echurch was saved. The King was forced to surrender, an d a treaty, the
2 CONC Mise of Lewes, was drawn up, helping to further the rig hts of the peop
2 CONC leof England and the parliament.]
