1 NOTE  Henry II ruled over an empire which stretched from the Scottish borde r t
2 CONC othe Pyrenees. One of the strongest, most energetic and imaginative rul
2 CONC ers, Henry was the inheritor of three dynasties who had acquiredAqu ita
2 CONC ine by marriage; his charters listed them: 'King of the English,Duk e o
2 CONC f the Normans and Aquitanians and Count of the Angevins'. The Kings pen
2 CONC t only 13 years of his reign in England; the other 21 years werespe nt o
2 CONC n the continent in his territories in what is now France. Henry'sr apid m
2 CONC ovements in carrying out his dynastic responsibilities astonishe dthe F
2 CONC rench king, who noted 'now in England, now in Normandy, he mus t flyrat
2 CONC her than travel by horse or ship'. By 1158, Henry had restore d to theC
2 CONC rown some of the lands and royal power lost by Stephen; Malco m IV ofSc
2 CONC otland was compelled to return the northern counties. Locall y chosensh
2 CONC eriffs were changed into royally appointed agents charged wi thenforcin
2 CONC g the law and collecting taxes in the counties. Personallyin terested i
2 CONC n government and law, Henry made use of juries andre-introdu ced the se
2 CONC nding of justices (judges) on regular tours of thecountry t o try cases f
2 CONC or the Crown. His legal reforms have led him to beseen a s the founder o
2 CONC f English Common Law.
