1 NOTE  Duchess of Acquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was a beautiful and strong woman of great ability, a patro n o f
2 CONC the arts and a master politician. She had sophisticated tastes,su rroun
2 CONC ded herself with troubadours, and founded a literary court. Herid eals h
2 CONC elped establish the idea ofromantic "courtly love."
2 CONT
2 CONT She instigated revolt among her sons against their father Henry II, wh o
2 CONC had Eleanor imprisoned for 16 years. After her imprisonment, Henry op e
2 CONC nly lived in adultery with his mistress "The Fair Rosamond," whom man y l
2 CONC ater chroniclers claimed was mysteriously murdered by a jealous Ele ano
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Henry II's death, Eleanor ruled as Regent, while Richard I was o n c
2 CONC rusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor has been immortalized by Katherine Hepburn's portrayal of he r i
2 CONC n the film, "The Lion in Winter."
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor also appears as a character in Shakespeare's play KING JOHN
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Poitou, Queen consort first of King Louis VII of France an d t
2 CONC hen of King Henry II of England. Daughter and heiress of GuillaumeX , D
2 CONC uke of Aquitaine, she married Louis in 1137 shortly before his acce ssi
2 CONC on to the throne. She accompanied him on the Second Crusade from114 7 t
2 CONC o 1149. Eleanor bore Louis two daughters, but in 1152 theirmarriag e wa
2 CONC s annulled. Soon afterward Eleanor married Henry, Duke ofNormand y and C
2 CONC ount of Anjou, uniting her vast possessions with those ofher hu sband. L
2 CONC ouis VII feared this powerful combination, and when Henry asce nded the E
2 CONC nglish throne in 1154, the stage was set for a long struggl e between t
2 CONC he English and French kings. Eleanor bore Henry three daugh ters and fi
2 CONC ve sons, and two of the latter, Richard I and John,became k ings of Eng
2 CONC land. Because of Henry's infidelities, especially his relat ionship wit
2 CONC h Rosamond, Eleanor's relations with her husband grew strai ned, and in 1
2 CONC 170 she established a court of her own at Poitou. She sup ported her so
2 CONC ns in their unsuccessful revolt against Henry in 1173 and was held in c
2 CONC onfinement by Henry until 1185. Her efforts helped Richar dsecure the t
2 CONC hrone in 1189. While Richard was on the Third Crusade and later held ca
2 CONC ptive in Europe from 1190 to 1194, Eleanor was active i n forestalling t
2 CONC he plots against him by his brother John and in collec ting the ransom f
2 CONC or his release. She brought about a reconciliation be tween the two bro
2 CONC thers, and on Richard's death in 1199 she supported J ohn's claims to t
2 CONC he throne over those of Arthur I of Bretagne. Eleanor 's court at Poito
2 CONC u was the scene of much artistic activity and was not ed for its cultiv
2 CONC ation of courtly manners and the concept of courtly l ove. She wasthe p
2 CONC atroness of such literary figures as Wace, Benoît d e Sainte-More,and C
2 CONC hrestien de Troyes. In literature Eleanor has appea red as the jealous m
2 CONC urderess of the fair Rosamond, but she was apparen tly innocent of this c
2 CONC rime. She was an able and strong-minded woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Renowned in her time for being the most beautiful woman in Europe, th e w
2 CONC ife of two Kings and mother of three, Eleanor of Aquitaine was on e oft
2 CONC he great heroines of the Middle Ages. At a time when women were r egard
2 CONC ed as little more than chattel, Eleanor managed to defy conventio n as s
2 CONC he exercised power in the political sphere and crucial influenc e over h
2 CONC er husbands and sons.  Eleanor of Aquitaine was born into a Eu rope dom
2 CONC inated by feudalism. In the twelfth century there was no conce pt of na
2 CONC tionhood or patriotism, and subjects owed loyalty to their rul er, rath
2 CONC er than thestate. Europe was split into principalities calle d feudator
2 CONC ies, each under the rule of a king, duke, or count, and pers onal alleg
2 CONC iance, orfealty, was what counted. This was expressed in th e ceremony o
2 CONC f homage, in which a kneeling vassal would place his hand s between tho
2 CONC se of his overlord and swear to render him service and ob edience.  In t
2 CONC his martial world dominated by men, women had little pla ce. The Church
2 CONC 's teachings might underpin feudal morality, yet when i t came to the p
2 CONC racticalities of life, a ruthless pragmatism often cam e into play. Kin
2 CONC gs and noblemen married for political advantage, and w omen rarely had a
2 CONC ny say in how they or their wealth were to be dispose d in marriage. Up
2 CONC on marriage, a girl's property and rights became inve sted in her husba
2 CONC nd, to whom she owed absolute obedience.  It is fai r to say, however, t
2 CONC hat there were women who transcended the mores o f society and got away w
2 CONC ith it: the evidence suggests that Eleanor o f Aquitaine was one such. T
2 CONC here were then, as now, women of strong cha racter who ruled feudal sta
2 CONC tes and kingdoms, as Eleanor did; who mad e decisions, ran farms and bu
2 CONC sinesses, fought lawsuits, and even, by s heer force of personality, do
2 CONC minated theirhusbands.  Poitou was the mo st northerly of Eleanor's feu
2 CONC datories: its northern border marched wit h those of Brittany, Anjou, a
2 CONC nd Touraine, andits chief city was Poitie rs. Perched on a cliff, with i
2 CONC mpressive ramparts, this was the favouri te seat of its suzerains. To t
2 CONC he east wasthe county of Berry, and to t he south the wide sweep of the d
2 CONC uchy ofAquitaine, named "land of water s" after the great rivers that d
2 CONC issectedit: the Garonne, the Charente , the Creuse, the Vienne, the Dor
2 CONC dogne, and the Vézère. The duchy als o incorporated the counties of Sai
2 CONC ntonge,Angoulême, Périgord, the Limo usin, La Marche, and the remote re
2 CONC gion ofthe Auvergne. In the south, s tretching to the Pyrenees, was the w
2 CONC ine-producing duchy of Gascony, o r Guienne, with its bustling port ofB
2 CONC ordeaux, and the Agenais. All the se lands comprised Eleanor's inherita
2 CONC nce.  The Aquitanian lordships an d their castles were controlledby oft
2 CONC en hostile and frequently feudin g vassals, who paid mere lipservice to t
2 CONC heir ducal overlords and wer e notorious for their propensity to rebel a
2 CONC nd create disorder. These t urbulent nobles enjoyed a luxuriousstandard o
2 CONC f living compared with th eir unwashed counterparts in northern France, a
2 CONC nd each competed with h is neighbour to establish in his castle asmall b
2 CONC ut magnificent court . Renowned for their elegance, their shavenfaces a
2 CONC nd long hair, the Aq uitanian aristocracy were regarded bynortherners a
2 CONC s soft and idle, whe reas in fact they could be fierce andviolent when p
2 CONC rovoked. Self-inter est was the dominant theme in theirrelations with t
2 CONC heir liege lords: s uccessive dukes had consistently failed to subdue t
2 CONC hese turbulent lord s or establish cohesion within their own domains.
2 CONT The authority of the dukes of Aquitaine held good, therefore, only i n t
2 CONC heimmediate vicinities of Poitiers, their capital, and Bordeaux. Al -th
2 CONC oughthey claimed descent from Charlemagne and retained his effigy o n t
2 CONC he coinage of Poitou, they did not have the wealth or resources t o ext
2 CONC end their power into the feudal wilderness beyond this region, an d sin
2 CONC ce their military strength depended upon knight service from thei r unr
2 CONC ulyvassals, they could not rely upon this. Consequently, Aquitain e lag
2 CONC gedbehind northern France in making political and economic progre ss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, the duchy was wealthy, thanks to its lucrative export tr a
2 CONC dein wine and salt, and it was a land in which the religious life flo u
2 CONC rished. Its rulers erected and endowed numerous fine churches and abb e
2 CONC ys, notably the famous abbey at Cluny-"a pleasaunce of the angels"-an d t
2 CONC he Aquitanian Romanesque cathedrals in Poitiers and Angoulême,buil t in a s
2 CONC tyle typified by elegant archways with radiating decoration an d lively b
2 CONC ut grotesque sculptures of monsters and mythical creatures.
