2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey IV, also called GEOFFREY PLANTAGENET, by name GEOFFREY THE FA I
2 CONC R, French GEOFFROI PLANTAGENET, or GEOFFROI le BEL (b. Aug. 24, 1113- -
2 CONC d.Sept. 7, 1151, Le Mans, Maine [France]), count of Anjou (1131-51) , M
2 CONC aine,and Touraine and ancestor of the Plantagenet kings of Englan d thr
2 CONC ough his marriage, in June 1128, to Matilda (q.v.), daughter of H enry I o
2 CONC f England. On Henry's death (1135), Geoffrey claimed the duch y of Norm
2 CONC andy; he finally conquered it in 1144 and ruled there as duk e until he g
2 CONC ave it to his son Henry (later King Henry II of England) i n 1150.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey was popular with the Normans, but he had to suppress a rebell i
2 CONC on of malcontent Angevin nobles. After a short war with Louis VII o f F
2 CONC rance, Geoffrey signed a treaty (August 1151) by which he surrender ed t
2 CONC he whole of Norman Vexin (the border area between Normandy and Île -de-
2 CONC France) to Louis. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97] -------------- ----
2 CONC --------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Plantagenet family name was originally just a nickname for Geoffre y
2 CONC . He many times wore a sprig with yellow flowers in his hat.  The flo w
2 CONC er was named "genet" or "genistae"in the French of the times--thus hi s n
2 CONC ickname was "Plant-a-Genet".  Genet was supposedly a traditional fl owe
2 CONC r of the Anjou family dating back to the time of Fulk, The Great, C oun
2 CONC t of Anjou 898-941 who was scourged (in order to atone for past sin s) w
2 CONC ith broom twigs of the Genet while on pilgrimage in Jerusalem.   M ost p
2 CONC eople of the times had personal nicknames such as "Beauclerc",  " Curtm
2 CONC antel","Longshanks", and "Lackland", but Geoffrey's stuck and eve ntual
2 CONC ly (many generations later) became the family name.  Geoffrey's i mmedi
2 CONC ate descendants were probably not known as the Plantagenet famil y at t
2 CONC he time they lived, it was only later that the Plantagenet famil y name w
2 CONC as applied to all descendants of Geoffrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey was also known as " The Plantagenet" , and more commonly as G e
2 CONC offrey V, The Fair, Count of Anjou and Maine. Hewas Duke of Normand y 1
2 CONC 144-1150 abdicating abt 1151. Start of the Angevin dynasty of the E ngl
2 CONC ish royal house. Reigning ffrom 1154 to 1485, the Plantagenet king s in t
2 CONC he main line of descent were: Henry II, Richard I, John, Henry I II, Ed
2 CONC ward I, Edward II, Edward III, and Richard II. Through the Hous e of La
2 CONC ncaster, Henry IV, Henry V, and Henry VI. Through the House o f York, E
2 CONC dward IV, Edward V, and Richard III
