1 _UID 821393F9EE194E889C5D95E708AD3FBF26CB
1 _AMTID 162143283388:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  CONFLICT:  Ancestral file 4.15 states him as William FLEMING, born ab t 1
2 CONC 173
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH:  Adult 1184
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  Justiciar of England 1198-1213, Earl of Essex (right of f i
2 CONC rst wife) was created in 1199ALIAS: Geoffrey de Mandeville
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the decease of William de Mandeville, 3rd Earl of Essex, much dis p
2 CONC ute arose regarding the inheritance: Beatrix, his aunt and heir, in t h
2 CONC e first place, preferring her claim, sent Geoffrey de Say, her younge r s
2 CONC on, to transact the business for the livery thereof, but Geoffrey F itz
2 CONC Piers insisted upon the right of Beatrix, his wife. Nevertheless, G eof
2 CONC frey de Say, in consideration of 7,000 marks promised to be paid o n a c
2 CONC ertain day, obtained an instrument in right of his mother, unde r the k
2 CONC ing's seal, for the whole of the barony, but the said Geoffre y de Say, m
2 CONC aking default of payment, this Geoffrey FitzPiers, bein g a man of grea
2 CONC t wealth and reputation, made representation that the b arony was the r
2 CONC ight of his wife and, promising to pay the money, obtai ned livery ther
2 CONC eof and procured the king's confirmation of his title . One of the earl
2 CONC iest acts of this feudal lord was to dispossess the m onks of Walden of c
2 CONC ertain lands which they had derived from his predec essors, a proceedin
2 CONC g followed by a long controversy, which, after bein g referred to the P
2 CONC ope and the King, was finally compromised. Upon th e removal of Hubert, A
2 CONC rchbishop of Canterbury, from the office of Just ice of England by Rich
2 CONC ard I, this Geoffrey was appointed to succeed hi m, and at the coronati
2 CONC on of King John, 26 June, 1199, he was girt wit h the sword as Earl of E
2 CONC ssex, and then served at the king's table. Bei ng nominated patron of t
2 CONC he monastery at Walden, he appears soon afte r to have been received wi
2 CONC th great ceremony by the monks and perfectl y reconciled to those holy f
2 CONC athers. In the 7th King John, he had a gra nt of the castle and honour o
2 CONC f Berkhamstead, with the knights' fees th ereunto belonging to hold to h
2 CONC im and the heirs of his body, by Aveline , his 2nd wife. His lordship m
2 CONC . 1st, Beatrix de Say, by whom he had is sue, Geoffrey, William, Henry, a
2 CONC ll of whom assumed the name of Mandevi lle, and Maud, m. to Robert de B
2 CONC ohun. He m. 2ndly, Aveline ---, and ha d an only son, John FitzPiers, L
2 CONC ord of Berkhamstead. His lordship, who m Matthew Paris characterizes as "
2 CONC ruling the reins of government so th at after his death the realm was l
2 CONC ike a ship in a tempest without a pi lot," d. 2 October, 1213, and was s
2 CONC . by his eldest son, Geoffrey de Ma ndeville. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dorma
2 CONC nt, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinc t Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., Lo
2 CONC ndon, England, 1883, p. 353, Mand eville, Earls of Essex]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT GEOFFREY FITZ PETER (d. 1213), earl of Essex and chief justiciar of En g
2 CONC land, was a sheriff, a justice itinerant and a justice of the fores t u
2 CONC nder Henry II. During Richard's absence on crusade he was one of th e f
2 CONC ive justices of the king's court who stood next in authority to th e re
2 CONC gent, Longchamp. In 1190 Fitz Peter succeeded to the earldom of Es sex, i
2 CONC n the right of his wife, who was descended from the famous Geoff rey de M
2 CONC andeville. In attempting to asset his hereditary rights over W alden pr
2 CONC iory Fitz Peter came into conflict with Longchamp, and revenge d himsel
2 CONC f by joining in the baronial agitation through which the regen t was ex
2 CONC pelled from his office. Though refusing to give him formal inv estiture o
2 CONC f the Essex earldom, Richard appointed him justiciar in succ ession to H
2 CONC ubert Walter (1198). Fitz Peter continued Walter's policy o f encouragi
2 CONC ng foreign trade and the development of the towns; many o f the latter r
2 CONC eceived, during his administration, charters of self-gov ernment. He wa
2 CONC s continued in his office by John, who found him an abl e instrument of e
2 CONC xtortion. He profited to no small extent by the spoli ation of church l
2 CONC ands in the period of the interdict. But he was not a ltogether trusted b
2 CONC y the king. The contemporary "Historie des ducs" de scribed Fitz Peter a
2 CONC s living in constant dread of disgrace and confisc ation. In the last y
2 CONC ears of his life he endeavoured to act as a mediat or between the king a
2 CONC nd the opposition. It was by his mouth that the k ing promised to the n
2 CONC ation the laws of Henry I. (at the council of St . Albans, Aug. 4, 1213
2 CONC ). But Fitz Peter died a few weeks later (Oct. 2 ). Fitz Peter was neit
2 CONC her a far-sighted nor a disinterested statesman ; but he was the ablest p
2 CONC upil of Hubert Walter, and maintained the tra ditions of the great bure
2 CONC aucracy which the first and second Henries ha d founded.
