1 NOTE  William de Mandeville, Earl of Essex, like his deceased kinsman, espou s
2 CONC ed the cause of the barons and stoutly maintained it, even after th e d
2 CONC ecease of King John, being one of those who then assisted Lewis o f Fra
2 CONC nce in the siege of Berkhamstead Castle, occupied by the king's f orced
2 CONC . A sally having been made, however, from the garrison, much of t he ba
2 CONC ggage of the besiegers was captured and, amongst other things, th e ban
2 CONC ner of the Earl William. His lordship seems to have made his peac e soo
2 CONC n after, for we find him engaged in the Welsh wars. He d. in th e flowe
2 CONC r of his age, 25 December, 1227, and, as he left no issue, th e Earldom o
2 CONC f Essex devolved upon his sister, Mary, Countess of Herefor d, while th
2 CONC e lands which he inherited passed to his half-brother, Joh n FitzPiers. [
2 CONC Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extin ct Peerages, B
2 CONC urke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 353, Man deville, Earls o
2 CONC f Essex][TIMS FILE.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Visitation of Essex 1558 - Harleian MS  In the Barrington Family Histo r
2 CONC y is:  "Humphrey [Barrington] is said to have married Amicia, daughte r o
2 CONC f Sir William de Mandeville, third son of Geoffrey de Mandeveill, f irs
2 CONC t Earl of Essex.  This Sir William de Mandeville, on the death of h is b
2 CONC rother Geoffrey without issue, became himself third Earl, and in a ll a
2 CONC ccounts of the family is stated to have died without issue, and in deed t
2 CONC his must have been the case, as all his estates descended to hi s fathe
2 CONC r's sister's grandchild, Beatrix de Say, whose husband, Geoffre y Fitzp
2 CONC iers, was in consequence created Earl of Essex.  This Amicia, t he wife o
2 CONC f Humphrey de Barenton, if the daughter of William de Mandevi lle, must h
2 CONC ave been a natural child.  She seems to have inherited cons iderable pr
2 CONC operty, as in the Close Rolls in the reign of Henry the Thi rd, is an o
2 CONC rder to the Sheriff of Essex to give to Humphrey de Barento n and Amici
2 CONC a his wife peaceable possession of their lands in Shrevingt on, Waleden a
2 CONC nd Teyden on their doing the service for them they had be en accustomed t
2 CONC o perform.  If however Robert Fitzwalter of any other p erson should re
2 CONC quire any service from the said lands, the Sheriff wa s to appoint a da
2 CONC y for his appearance in Court before the King, when f ull justice shoul
2 CONC d be done to him.  The chattells which had been sieze d, and of which R
2 CONC obert kept possession, were to be restored without an y delay.  This Ro
2 CONC bert Fitzwalter was father of William de Mandeville' s second wife Chri
2 CONC stiana.  There is a very curious agreement (which un fortunately is so s
2 CONC tained as to be illegible in some places [if it cou ld be found now, 20
2 CONC 00AD, might ultra-violet light help&#00063;] betwee n Humphrey de Baren
2 CONC ton and Alan de Sepero, by which Humphrey became se curity with Alan to B
2 CONC enjamin and his sister Cissorie, Jews at Cambridg e, for the payment of t
2 CONC wo pounds per annum for eight years.  The firs t payment to be at Micha
2 CONC elmas next after the marriage of Earl Willia m to the daughter of the E
2 CONC arl of Albermarle.  Alan de Sepere giving t o Humphrey, as a security, a m
2 CONC ortgage on all the fee Humphrey held und er him ; so that should he, Hu
2 CONC mphrey, be called upon to pay any part o f the debt, he should have ful
2 CONC l possession of the fee, without any res triction, until he was repaid a
2 CONC ll the money he had advanced. The Ear l William named in this agreement
2 CONC , was the William de Mandeville, whos e daughter Amicia Humphrey marrie
2 CONC d, and the agreement refers to his, W illiam's, marriage to his first w
2 CONC ife, Haurse, or Aricia, daughter of W illiam le Gros, Earl of Albermarl
2 CONC e."
