1 NOTE  Geoffrey de Mandeville (which surname he assumed), who, in the 15th Ki n
2 CONC g John (1314-15), had livery of the lands of his inheritance and th e s
2 CONC ame year, bearing the title of Earl of Essex, the king gave him t o wif
2 CONC e, Isabel, Countess of Gloucester, 3rd dau. and co-heir of Willia m, Ea
2 CONC rl of Gloucester, and was, in her right, Earl of Gloucester, an d was s
2 CONC o styled in the convention with King John, 1215 (which Isabel h ad firs
2 CONC t been m. to King John himself, but repudiated on account of co nsangui
2 CONC nity). This nobleman afterwards distinguished himself amongst t he baro
2 CONC ns who rebelled again the tyrannical power of John, and was on e of the t
2 CONC wenty-five lords chosen to enforce the observance of Magna C arta, abou
2 CONC t which period, attending a tournament at London, he receive d a wound f
2 CONC rom a lance, which proved mortal. His lordship d. in 1216 , and leaving n
2 CONC o children, was s. by his brother, William. [Sir Bernar d Burke, Dorman
2 CONC t, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Pee rage, Ltd., Lon
2 CONC don, England, 1883, p. 353, Mandeville, Earls of Essex]
