1 _AMTID 162143283738:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  OCCUPATION:  Matilda of Scotland; b. 1079; d. 1 may 118; m. 11 nov 110 0
2 CONC , HENRY I, Beauclerc, King of England, 1100-1135; b. 1070; d. 1 Dec . 1
2 CONC 135 (He m. (2) 29 Jan. 1121, ADELIZA of Louvain, b. ca. 1103; d. 2 3 Ap
2 CONC r. 1151 (s.p. by this marriage); she m. (2) 1138, William d'Aubign y, E
2 CONC arl of Arundel, d. 12 Oct. 1176).  King of England 1100.   HENRY I , HA
2 CONC D ISSUE BY A NUMBER OF MISTRESSES. He was crowned king 5 Aug 110 0 at a
2 CONC ge 32.  The Charter of Henry I was the foreerunner of the Magn a Charta
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH:  On 1 Dec 1135,King Henry I of England died at Rouen, in the pl a
2 CONC ce known as Lyons-la-Foret.  The Normans kept his intestines, and th e r
2 CONC est of his body the English carried away to a tomb (in the abbey a t Re
2 CONC ading)
2 CONT
2 CONT MISC:  Henry I was born in the year 1068---a factor he himself regarde d a
2 CONC s highly significant, for he was the only son of the Conqueror bor n af
2 CONC ter the conquest of England, and to Henry this meant he was heir t o th
2 CONC e throne. He was not an attractive proposition: he was dissolute t o a d
2 CONC egree, producing at least a score of bastards; but far worse he w as pr
2 CONC one to sadistic cruelty---on one occasion, for example, personall y pun
2 CONC ishing a rebellious burgher by throwing him from the walls of hi s town
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death of William the Conqueror, Henry was left no lands, merel y 5
2 CONC ,000 pounds of silver. With these he bought lands from his elder br oth
2 CONC er Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy, only to see them taken back a gai
2 CONC n a few years later by Robert, in unholy alliance with his brothe r Wil
2 CONC liam Rufus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry could do little to avenge such treatment, but in England he foun d n
2 CONC umerous barons who were tired of the exactions and ambitions of the ir k
2 CONC ing. He formed alliances with some of these, notably with the impo rtan
2 CONC t de Clare family. He and some of the de Clares were with Willia m Rufu
2 CONC s on his last hunting expedition, and it is thought that the kin g's de
2 CONC ath was the result of Henry's plotting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Certainly he moved fast to take advantage of it; leaving Rufus's bod y u
2 CONC nattended in the woods, he swooped down on Winchester to take contr ol o
2 CONC f the treasury. Two days later he was in Westminster, being crowne d by t
2 CONC he Bishop of London. His speed is understandable when one realis es tha
2 CONC t his elder brother, Robert [Curthose], was returning from the c rusade
2 CONC , and claimed, with good reason, to be the true heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry showed great good sense in his first actions as King. He arreste d R
2 CONC anulph Flambard, William's tax-gatherer, and recalled Anselm, the e xil
2 CONC ed Archbishop. Furthermore, he issued a Charter of Liberties whic h pro
2 CONC mised speedy redress of grievances, and a return to the good gove rnmen
2 CONC t of the Conqueror. Putting aside for the moment his many mistres ses, h
2 CONC e married the sister of the King of Scots, who was descended fro m the r
2 CONC oyal line of Wessex; and lest the Norman barons should think hi m too p
2 CONC ro-English in this action, he canged her name from Edith to Mat ilda. N
2 CONC o one could claim that he did not aim to please.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1101 Robert Curthose invaded, but Henry met him at Alton, and persu a
2 CONC ded him to go away again by promising him an annuity of D2,000. He ha d n
2 CONC o intention of keeping up the payments, but the problem was tempora ril
2 CONC y solved.
2 CONT
2 CONT He now felt strong enough to move against dissident barons who might g i
2 CONC ve trouble in the future. Chief amongst these was the vicious Rober t o
2 CONC f Bellme, Earl of Shrewsbury, whom Henry had known for many years a s a d
2 CONC angerous troublemaker. He set up a number of charges against hi m in th
2 CONC e king's court, making it plain that if he appeared for trial h e would b
2 CONC e convicted and imprisoned. Thus Robert and his colleagues we re forced i
2 CONC nto rebellion at a time not of their own choosing, were eas ily defeate
2 CONC d and sent scuttling back to Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Normandy Robert Curthose began to wreak his wrath on all connecte d w
2 CONC ith his brother, thus giving Henry an excellent chance to retaliat e wi
2 CONC th charges of misgovernment and invade. He made two expeditions i n 110
2 CONC 4-5, before the great expedition of 1106 on which Robert was defe ated a
2 CONC t the hour-long battle of Tinchebrai, on the anniversary of Hast ings. N
2 CONC o one had expected such an easy victory, but Henry took advanta ge of t
2 CONC he state of shock resulting from the battle to annex Normandy . Robert w
2 CONC as imprisoned (in some comfort, it be said); he lived on fo r 28 more y
2 CONC ears, ending up in Cardiff castle whiling away the long hou rs learning W
2 CONC elsh. His son William Clito remained a free agent, to pla gue Henry for m
2 CONC ost of the rest of his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT In England the struggle with Anselm over the homage of bishops ran it s c
2 CONC ourse until the settlement of 1107. In matters of secular governmen t l
2 CONC ife was more simple: Henry had found a brilliant administrator, Rog er o
2 CONC f Salisbury, to act as Justiciar for him. Roger had an inventive m ind, a k
2 CONC een grasp of affairs, and the ability to single out young me n of promi
2 CONC se. He quickly built up a highly efficient team of administr ators, and e
2 CONC stablished new routines and forms of organisation within w hich they co
2 CONC uld work. To him we owe the Exchequer and its recording sy stem of the P
2 CONC ipe Rolls, the circuits of royal justiciars spreading th e king's peace
2 CONC , and the attempts at codification of law. Henry's goo d relationships w
2 CONC ith his barons, and with the burgeoning new towns owe d much to skilful a
2 CONC dministration. Certainly he was able to gain a larg er and more reliabl
2 CONC e revenue this way than by the crude extortion hi s brother had used.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1120 came the tragedy of the White Ship. The court was returning t o E
2 CONC ngland, and the finest ship in the land was filled with its young m en, i
2 CONC ncluding Henry's son and heir William. Riotously drunk, they trie d to g
2 CONC o faster and faster, when suddenly the ship foundered. All hand s excep
2 CONC t a butcher of Rouen were lost, and England was without an heir .
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's only legitimate child was Matilda, but she was married to th e E
2 CONC mperor Henry V of Germany, and so could not succeed. But in 1125 he r h
2 CONC usband died, and Henry brought her home and forced the barons to sw ear f
2 CONC ealty to her---though they did not like the prospect of a woman r uler. H
2 CONC enry then married her to Geoffrey of Anjou, the Normans' tradit ional e
2 CONC nemy, and the barons were less happy---especially when the newl y-weds h
2 CONC ad a terrible row, and Geoffrey ordered her out of his lands . In 1131 H
2 CONC enry, absolutely determined, forced the barons to swear fea lty once mo
2 CONC re, and the fact that they did so is testimoney of his cont rolling pow
2 CONC er. Matilda and Geoffrey were reunited, and in 1133 she pro duced a son w
2 CONC hom she named for his grandfather. If only Henry could li ve on until h
2 CONC is grandson was old enough to rule, all would be well.
2 CONT
2 CONT But in 1135, against doctor's orders, he ate a hearty meal of lampreys , g
2 CONC ot acute indigestion, which turned into fever, and died. He was bur ied a
2 CONC t his abbey in Reading---some said in a silver coffin, for whic h there w
2 CONC as an unsuccessful search at the Dissolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source: Who's Who in the Middle Ages, John Fines, Barnes & Noble Book s
2 CONC , New York, 1995]After discussing with Betty Knoche, the various opin i
2 CONC ons from different sources about the mistresses and illegitimate chil d
2 CONC ren of Henry I (who probably had more than any other English monarch) , I d
2 CONC iscovered an Appendix D in Volume XI of The Complete Peerage whic h lis
2 CONC ts all of the "known" children and connects them, where possible , with t
2 CONC he known mistresses.  I have scanned the text and included i t in the n
2 CONC otes below.  Please excuse any errors in scanning/translatin g the text
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY I'S ILLEGITIMATE CHILDREN (According to the Complete Peerage)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I and Charles II were the only Kings of England to beget a larg e b
2 CONC rood of bastards. Charles IIÆs children have already been catalogue d i
2 CONC n vol. VI, Appendix F; from which it appears that Charles, who ha d no l
2 CONC egitimate issue, is credited with 8 sons and 6 daughters, but th at the p
2 CONC aternity of one of those sons is not certain. This record is e asily be
2 CONC aten  by Henry I. Besides his 2, or possibly 3, legitimate chi ldren, i
2 CONC t seems fairly certain that he was the father of at least 9 so ns and 1
2 CONC 1 daughters.  Moreover it is possible that one of the 9 sons r eally re
2 CONC presents brothers of the same name; and finally it remains dou btful wh
2 CONC ether a certain unnamed daughter of the King was identical wit h one of t
2 CONC he 11 or was yet another addition to his extensive family.
2 CONT
2 CONT No complete catalogue of HenryÆs bastards is given by any contemporar y w
2 CONC riter. However, Robert de Torigny, in his additions to the Gesta No rma
2 CONC nnoram Ducum of William de Jumieges, enumerates 6 sons, mentionin g the m
2 CONC arriage of the first, gives the marriages of 6 daughters, of wh om he n
2 CONC ames only three, and refers to another daughter, also unnamed , who was s
2 CONC till unmarried when he wrote.  Particulars of HenryÆs othe r children h
2 CONC ave to be collected from different sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is impossible to set out the names of the children in the order i n w
2 CONC hich they were born, as the date of birth is not known for any of t hem
2 CONC ; nor is it possible to arrange them according to their maternal pa ren
2 CONC tage, for in most instances this is not recorded.  Indeed, Robert d e T
2 CONC origny does not name the mother of any of the 13 children whom he e num
2 CONC erates except the unmarried daughter, although his omissions may oc cas
2 CONC ionally be made good from other sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons were as follows, nos. 1 to 6 being those named by Robert de T o
2 CONC rigny.  He states that no. 1 was the eldest son, and it is certain fr o
2 CONC m other evidence that no. 2 was the 2nd son ; but it is doubtful ho w f
2 CONC ar the others follow any chronological or other order. It seems lik ely t
2 CONC hat no. 6 was 3rd of the six, because when Robert wrote, probabl y not e
2 CONC arlier than 1142, nos. 3, and 5 were still young and unmarrie d (or so h
2 CONC e says); but no. 6 had died soon after his father, being the n a marrie
2 CONC d man, leaving issue.  Where the 3 remaining sons should b e inserted i
2 CONC s again uncertain; but no. 8 should probably follow no. 2 , and no. 9 c
2 CONC ome after no. 3, each pair being apparently children of t he same mothe
2 CONC r. On the other hand, it is possible that Henry had two o r more mistre
2 CONC sses
2 CONT simultaneously.
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) ROBERT the King's Son, styled also De Caen (e), the name and ident i
2 CONC ty of whose mother are uncertain (f); b. probably circa 1090; cr. i n 1
2 CONC 122 (June-Sep.) EARL OF GLOUCESTER; m. Maud (named also Mabel and S iby
2 CONC l) (g), da. and h. of Robert FitzHamon, LORD of GLAMORGAN, by Sibyl , d
2 CONC a. of Roger (de Montgomery), 1st EARL of SHREWSBURY; d. 31 Oct. 114 7 a
2 CONC t Bristol; bur. at the Priory of St. James, Bristol.  For fuller pa rti
2 CONC culars of Robert and for his issue, see ante, vol. v, pp. 683-86, s ub G
2 CONC loucester.  [Note: According to "Corrections and Additions to CP" , ano
2 CONC ther source indicates his mother is Nest verch Rhys, which is als o dis
2 CONC counted.  Volume V of CP, published in 1926 indicates the mothe r was "
2 CONC Sibyl, daughter of Robert Corbet a burgess in Caen", but Volum e XI, pu
2 CONC blished in 1949, discounts that in note "f" below.  The mothe r is offi
2 CONC cially unknown.  Many say that the mother of Robert was a Fre nch woman a
2 CONC nd that he was born in Caen.]
2 CONT
2 CONT (e) He attested charters regularly as Robert "filius, Regis"; but he i s t
2 CONC wice styled Robert de Caen (de Cadomo) by Orderic (ed. Le Prevost) , vo
2 CONC l. v, pp. 121, 122.  The statement in the Dict. Nat. Biog. that Ro bert w
2 CONC as born at Caen, citing Orderic, seems to be a deduction from th ese pa
2 CONC ssages. That he was "known ... as Robert 'de Caen' from his birt hplace
2 CONC " is also amassed by Round, Family Origins, p. 214.
2 CONT
2 CONT (f) The statement by Pezet, cited ante, vol. v, p. 683, that she was S i
2 CONC byl, dau.. of Robert Corbet, a burgess of Caen, seems to arise from c o
2 CONC nfusion with Henry IÆs mistress Sibyl, dau. of Robert Corbet of Alces t
2 CONC er (see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT (g) Her name seems to have been Maud (Round, Cal. Docs., no. 799; Orde r
2 CONC ic, vol. iii, p. 318); but she is called Mabel by William of Malmesbu r
2 CONC y, Hist. Nevella (Rolls Ser.), pp. 529, 587, and by Robert of Glouces t
2 CONC er (Rolls Ser.), II 8876, 8883, and Sibyl by Robert de Torigny, in hi s a
2 CONC dditions to Will. de Jumieges, p. 306.  As Earl Robert claimed to b e a b
2 CONC anner-bearer (signifer) of the see of Bayeux by hereditary righ t (Rec. d
2 CONC es Hist. de France, vol. xxiii, p. 700), that office was prob ably here
2 CONC ditary in his wifeÆs family, and may have been appurtenant t o the land
2 CONC s which she inherited.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) RICHARD, whose mother was Ansfride, a lady of unknown parentage, w i
2 CONC dow of Anskiill, a knight who was a tenant of Abingdon Abbey. He wa s b
2 CONC . ante 1101 ; served against the French in 1119, and was captured a t L
2 CONC os Andelys, but was set free with his comrades by King Louis, becau se t
2 CONC hey had taken sanctuary in the church of N.D. du Grand Andely; wa s wit
2 CONC h his father at the siege of Evreux and the battle of Bremule, 2 0 Aug. 1
2 CONC 119; and in Sep. was sent to raise the siege of Breteuil. He w as betro
2 CONC thed to Amice, da. of Ralph de Gael, LORD of  MONTFORT in Brit tany and B
2 CONC RETEUIL in Normandy, with whom he was to receive all her fat herÆs Norm
2 CONC an lands; but he d. s.p. immediately afterwards, being drown ed in the w
2 CONC reck of the White Ship, 25 Nov. 1120 (h), and Amice m. Robe rt, 2nd EAR
2 CONC L of LEICESTER. (see ante vol. vii, pp. 529-30, sub Leicest er).
2 CONT
2 CONT (h) His body was washed up many days later, far from the scene of th e s
2 CONC hipwreck.
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) RAINALD of DUNSTANVILLE, whose mother was Sibyl, named also Adel a a
2 CONC nd Lucy, da. and in her issue coh. of Robert Corbet, of Alcester, C oun
2 CONC ty Warwick, and Longden, Salop; which Sibyl, after her liaison wit h He
2 CONC nry I, m. Herbert FitzHerbert.  Rainald held land in Wiltshire i n 1130
2 CONC .  He was cr., circa Apr. 1141, EARL OF CORNWALL, by his half-si ster t
2 CONC he Empress Maud.  He m. Beatrice, da. and h. of William FitzRich ard. H
2 CONC e d. spms. leg. 1 July 1175 at Chertsey, Surrey, when his Earldo m reve
2 CONC rted to the Crown, and was bur, at Reading Abbey. For fuller par ticula
2 CONC rs see ante, vol. iii, p. 429, sub Cornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) ROBERT the KingÆs son, whose mother Ede or Edith was apparently da . o
2 CONC f Forn, probably identical with Forn Sigulfson, lord of Greystoke ( Cum
2 CONC berland) and a tenant-in-chief in County York; which Edith, after h er l
2 CONC iaison with Henry I, m. Robert de Oilli, a royal Constable and con stab
2 CONC le of Oxford Castle. Robert held land in Devonshire in 1130. He su ppor
2 CONC ted his half-sister, the EMPRESS Maud, in the Civil War. He wa s a grea
2 CONC t tenant-in-chief, his servitium debitum being 100 knights. H e m. Maud
2 CONC , dame du Sap in Normandy, widow of William de Courcy, and da . and h. o
2 CONC f Robert de Avranches, by whom he had an only daughter.  H e d. 31 May 1
2 CONC 172.
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) GILBERT, still young and unmarried in (&#00063;) 1142. Nothing mor e i
2 CONC s known of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) WILLIAM de Tracy or Tracey, whose mother is unknown, and who d. so o
2 CONC n after his father, leaving (by an unknown wife) a daughter and hei r (
2 CONC l).
2 CONT
2 CONT (l) Grace, who m. John de Sudeley, of Sudeley Castle and Toddington, C o
2 CONC unty Gloucester, 3rd s. of Harold de Ewias, lord of Ewias (County Her e
2 CONC ford) and Sudeley, s. and h. of Ralph, Earl of Hereford, s. of Dreu , C
2 CONC ount of the French Vexin, by Godgifu, sister of Edward the Confesso r.  T
2 CONC he 1st son, Ralph de Sudeley, suc. his father at Sudeley; the 2n d son, W
2 CONC illiam of Toddington, took his mother's name of Tracy or Trace y; hence R
2 CONC alph de Sudeley confirmed a gift of his brother William de T racy to Gl
2 CONC oucester Abbey.  The direct line of Tracy of Toddington beca me extinct o
2 CONC n the death of Henry (Tracy), 8th Viscount Tracy, in 1797 ; but cadets o
2 CONC f this very ancient house may still exist.  [Note: Accor ding to Ancest
2 CONC ral Roots (line 222-27), Grace was not a daughter of Wil liam, but of u
2 CONC nknown parents; and Grace's son John was b. bef 1114 ("o f age by 1135"
2 CONC , admittedly from a "bef 1130" marriage, but there is n o way Grace fit
2 CONC s as daughter of William "b. c 1190" with a son born th at early.  Ther
2 CONC efore I have Grace's father as an unknown Henry de Trac y.]
2 CONT
2 CONT (7) Henry the King's son, whose mother was Nest, da. of Rhys ap TEWDWR , P
2 CONC rince of South Wales where Henry was born, and wife of Gerald de Wi nds
2 CONC or. He was slain during Henry IIÆs invasion of Anglesey in 1157, le avi
2 CONC ng (by an unknown wife) 2 sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT (8) FULK the King's son, and Richard the tutor, witnessed a gift to Ab i
2 CONC ngdon Abbey by William, s. of Anskill and Ansfride, the mother of Hen r
2 CONC y IÆs s. Richard, all abovenamed; the gift being made in consideratio n o
2 CONC f his mother having been bur, in the abbey. The obvious inference i s t
2 CONC hat Folk was a yr. s. of Henry and Ansfride, and was being brough t up a
2 CONC t the abbey in charge of his tutor.  In any event he must have b een a s
2 CONC on of Henry I. Fulk probably became a monk at Abingdon or d. yo ung.
2 CONT
2 CONT (9) WILLIAM, brother of the Queen [Sibyl of Scotland], who was one o f H
2 CONC enry IÆs illegitimate daughters (see below), was presumably a son o f S
2 CONC ibyl Corbet, and may be supposed to have accompanied his sister t o Sco
2 CONC tland. As ôWillelmensus frater reginaeô, his name occurs among th ose o
2 CONC f the witnesses to the foundation-charter (of doubtful authentici ty) o
2 CONC f Scone Priory, issued by Alexander I and Queen Sibyl, circa 1120 ; and a
2 CONC gain to a charter of Alexander for Scone in 1124.  Sibyl had d . s.p. i
2 CONC n 1122 and Alexander d. s.p. in 1124, and as there is no mor e trace of W
2 CONC illiam in Scotland, it is likely that he returned to Engla nd.  Probabl
2 CONC y he is William the KingÆs son who attested a charter of R obert de Ton
2 CONC i, 1129-33. In 1166 William frater comitis Reginaldi was h olding half a k
2 CONC nightÆs fee in Devonshire under Robert the KingÆs son , and 4 fees in C
2 CONC ornwall, as William frater Comitis, under Earl Rainal d of Cornwall. Ea
2 CONC rl RainaldÆs brother attested 2 charters of the ear l as ôWillelmo frat
2 CONC re meo." and issued a charter as "Willelmus de Mari sco frater Reginald
2 CONC i comitis Cornubie," in which he mentions his wif e Alice.  He was livi
2 CONC ng in 1187.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughters were as follows, the first 7 being in the same order a s i
2 CONC n the list of Robert de Torigny; who gives the marriages of nos . 1 to 6
2 CONC , but omits the Christian names of 4, 5, 6 and 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) MAUD, whose mother was Edith, of whom nothing is known (b).  She m . i
2 CONC n 1103, Rotrou, COUNT of Perche, styled the Great, s. and h. of Geo ffr
2 CONC ey, Count of Perche, by Beatrice, da. of Hilduin, Count of Montdidi er a
2 CONC nd  (jure uxoris) Count of Roucy.  Rotrou had gone on the 1st Crus ade i
2 CONC n 1096.  In 1105 and 1114 he went to Spain, to help his cousin Al fonso I
2 CONC , King of Navarre and Aragon, against the Moors.  In 1114 he as sisted H
2 CONC enry I at the siege of Belleme, which he had long before claim ed as hi
2 CONC s hereditary right. The King granted him the Belleme fiefs.  H e was pr
2 CONC esent at the death of his royal father-in-law in 1135. In 113 7 Stephen g
2 CONC ave him Moulins; but in 1141 he made terms with Geoffrey Pl antagenet.  M
2 CONC aud was drowned in the wreck of the White Ship, 25 Nov. 1 120, leaving 2 d
2 CONC aughters. Rotrou m., 2ndly, before 1127, Hawise, da. o f Walter de Sali
2 CONC sbury, and sister of Patrick, 1st EARL OF Salisbury. H e d. in 1144 at t
2 CONC he siege of the Tower of Rouen (20 Jan. to 23 Apr.) b y Geoffrey Planta
2 CONC genet, and his widow m., as his 2nd wife, Robert, 1s t Count of Dreux, 3
2 CONC rd s. of Louis VI (Le Gros), King of France; whic h Robert styled himse
2 CONC lf Count of Perche and lord of Belleme during th e minority of his step
2 CONC son.
2 CONT
2 CONT (b) As her daughter married in 1103, she cannot be the daughter of For n
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) MAUD, who m. Conan III, Duke of Brittany, s. of Alan Fergant, Duk e o
2 CONC f Brittany, by his 2nd wife, Ermengard, da. of Fulk IV, Count of An jou
2 CONC .  Maud had 1 son and 2 daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) JULIANE, who m. in 1103, Eustace de Pacy, styled also de Breteuil . L
2 CONC ord of Breteuil and Pacy, illegitimate son of William on Breteuil , 1st s
2 CONC . of William (FitzOsbern), 1st EARL OF HEREFORD (ante, vol. vi , p. 449
2 CONC , note "c", sub Hereford).  In 1119 Eustace took part in the r ebellion a
2 CONC gainst Henry I, who besieged Juliane in Breteuil. She fled t o Pacy, an
2 CONC d in the autumn of 1119 she and her husband were pardoned b y the King. A f
2 CONC ew years later she became a nun at Fontevrault. Eustac e d. at the begi
2 CONC nning of Lent, 1136. They had issue 2 sons and 2 daugh ters.
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) &#00063;Eustacie&#00063; (k) who m. William Gouet III, LORD or MON T
2 CONC MIRAIL and other fiefs in that part of Perche which, at a much late r d
2 CONC ate, became known as Perche-Gouet; who was 2nd but 1st surv. s. an d h. o
2 CONC f William Gouet II, LORD of Montmirail and Chateau-du-Loir, an d (jure m
2 CONC atris) of Alluye and Brou, by his wife Eustache, and was b. a nte 1080.  H
2 CONC is elder br. Hugh having d. v.p. he became the heir, and j oined with h
2 CONC is father and mother Eustache, and his brothers Robert an d Matthew, in t
2 CONC he foundation of the Priory of St. Gilles des Chateigni ers as a cell o
2 CONC f Tiron.  In 1114, as William Gouet junior (juvenem), h e was one of th
2 CONC e nobles (optimates) of Theobald, Count of Chartres, wh om the Count ca
2 CONC lled in to advise him. In 1116, with his father and mot her, he gave ju
2 CONC dgement in a dispute between the abbey of Marmoutier an d Gaston de Bro
2 CONC u. He suc. his father, probably about 1117. He has bee n confused with h
2 CONC is father, and with his s. and h., William Gouet IV , with whom the lin
2 CONC e ended.  [Note: "Correction and Additions to CP" i ndicates that her n
2 CONC ame is Mabel.]
2 CONT
2 CONT (k) R. de Torigny does not name her, and Marx does not try to ascertai n h
2 CONC er name; nor has it been found in charters.  She is called Eustaci e by R
2 CONC amsay, presumably through confusion with her mother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) CONSTANCE, named also MAUD, who m. Roscelin de Beaumont, hereditar y v
2 CONC icomte of Maine, styled Vicomte de Beaumont, Lord of Beaumont-le-Vi com
2 CONC te (alias Beaumont-sur-Sarthe), Fresnay and Ste.-Suzanne, s. of Ral ph d
2 CONC e Beaumont, by sister of Guy de Laval.  Henry I gave South Tawto n (Dev
2 CONC on), to Roscelin de Beaumont in marriage with his da. Constance . They h
2 CONC ad 2 sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) ALICE, named also ALINE, who m. Matthew de Montmorenci, 1st s. an d h
2 CONC . of Bouchard de Montmorenci, by his 1st wife, Agnes, da. of Yves I I, C
2 CONC ount of Beaumont-sur-Oise.  She d. after having sons by Matthew, w ho m
2 CONC . 2ndly, Adelaide, widow of Louis VI (Le Gros), King of France, da . of H
2 CONC umbert II, Count of Savoy, by Gisele, da. of William, Count of B urgund
2 CONC y; by whom he had no issue. Matthew was Constable of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT (7) ISABEL, whose mother was Isabel (or Elizabeth), da. of Robert (d e B
2 CONC eauchamp), Count or Meulan and 1st Earl of Leicester, by Isabel (o r El
2 CONC izabeth), da. of Hugh the Great, Count of Vermandois; which last-n amed I
2 CONC sabel m., 2ndly, William (de Warenne), 2nd Earl of Surrey (see a nte, v
2 CONC ol. vii, p. 526, sub Leicester). The youngest of the Isabels wa s still u
2 CONC nmarried when Robert de Torigny wrote, and so far as is know n she neve
2 CONC r married. Her mother m. Gilbert (FitzGilbert, styled also d e Clare), 1
2 CONC st Earl of Pembroke, and she seems to have lived with her m other durin
2 CONC g the life and after the death of her stepfather (see ante , vol. x, Ap
2 CONC pendix H, p. 102).
2 CONT
2 CONT (8) SIBYL, whose mother was probably Sibyl Corbet. She m. Alexander I , K
2 CONC ing of Scotland, with whom she is said to have been joint founder o f S
2 CONC cone Priory.  She gave ôBeeth," a valuable property in Fifeshire, t o t
2 CONC he abbey of Dunfermline. She d. s.p., suddenly, 12 or 13 July 1122 , on t
2 CONC he island of Loch Tay. Alexander d. s.p. 23 Apr. 1124 and was bu r. at D
2 CONC unfermline Abbey, being suc, by his br. David.
2 CONT
2 CONT (9) MAUD, abbess of Montivilliers, is called a sister of the Empress M o
2 CONC od by the Valasse Chronicle. Traditionally she was identified with He n
2 CONC ry lÆs daughter by Isabel de Beaumont, doubtless because IsabelÆs dau g
2 CONC hter was the only one in Robert de TorignyÆs list not recorded to b e m
2 CONC arried to another person. The compilers of Gallia Christiana seem s ome
2 CONC what sceptical of MaudÆs royal parentage; but this appears to be un rea
2 CONC sonable, as the writer of the Valasse Chronicle was a contemporary.
2 CONT
2 CONT (10) GUNDRED, The Pipe Roll of 130 mentions Gundred, sister of Rainal d d
2 CONC e Dunstanville. Nothing more is known of her.  [Note: "Correction s and A
2 CONC dditions to CP" indicates that the Rainald referred to here i s not the i
2 CONC llegitimate son of Henry I, but another Rainald de Dunstanv ille, and t
2 CONC herefore Gundred is not an illegitimate daughter of Henr y I either.]
2 CONT
2 CONT (11) ROHESE, who m., not later than 1146, Henry de la Pomerai, a grea t D
2 CONC evonshire baron, s. and h. of Joscelin de la Pomerai. He fought fo r He
2 CONC nry I in the rebellion of 1123, and in the KingÆs later years wa s a de
2 CONC puty or assistant Constable in his Household. In 1136 he was on e of St
2 CONC ephenÆs commanders in Normandy.  He prospered under Henry II. H e was d
2 CONC ead in 1167. His wife was probably living in 1175 or 1176. The y left s
2 CONC ons, Henry and Joscelin.  [Note: Ancestral Roots argues that R ohese wa
2 CONC s daughter of Sybil Corbet, but by her husband Herbert FitzHer bert, po
2 CONC inting out that her daughter married William de Tracy, who wou ld have b
2 CONC een the daughter's 1st cousin, if she were also descended fro m Henry I
2 CONC .]
2 CONT
2 CONT (12) Finally there is the question of the identity of the unnamed daug h
2 CONC ter whom Henry I had agreed to give to William de Warenne. The King a s
2 CONC ked Anselm what he ought to do, seeing that the parties were relate d i
2 CONC n the 4th generation on one side and in the sixth on the other. The re i
2 CONC s no evidence as to whether the girl was one of the 11 daughters a lrea
2 CONC dy enumerated or another. The archbishop protested against the mar riag
2 CONC e and it never took place. William de Warenne was probably the 2n d Ear
2 CONC l of Surrey, the only man of that name known to be living at th e time, w
2 CONC ho was 4th in descent from the common ancestors: the parent s of Gunnor
2 CONC , Duchess of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was evidently devoid of racial prejudices in the choice of his m i
2 CONC stresses. the six whose names are known, the 2 Ediths must have bee n E
2 CONC nglish; Ansfride and Sibyl Corbet were presumably Norman.  Nest wa s We
2 CONC lsh; Isabel de Beaumont was Norman on one side, French on the othe r.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I has been credited with 2 more daughters, for whom he was not r e
2 CONC sponsible:
2 CONT
2 CONT (i) In the Index to Le Prevost's edition of OrdericÆs Historia Ecclesi a
2 CONC stica, under "Helie de Saint-Saens," there are the entries: "Epouse I a f
2 CONC ille naturelle de Robert Courte-Heuse Ensuite Ia fille naturelle d e He
2 CONC nri Ire. IV, 232"; and under ôHenri Ire": ôUne de ses filles natur elle
2 CONC s epouse Helie de SaintùSums. IV, 232."  However, the compiler ha s mis
2 CONC understood the passage cited, which refers to Helie's marriage wi th a d
2 CONC aughter of Duke Robert. The alleged 2nd marriage and the KingÆ s allege
2 CONC d daughter are alike fictitious.
2 CONT
2 CONT (ii) Orderic, in his -account of the war between Henry I of England an d L
2 CONC ouis VI of France, speaks of William de Chaumont as the KingÆs son- in-
2 CONC law.  This has been misunderstood as referring to the King of Engla nd, a
2 CONC nd WilliamÆs wife is included among Henry lÆs daughters by Ramsay ; but c
2 CONC harter evidence proves that she was the daughter of the King o f France
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT HenryÆs benefactions to the Church caused the monkish historians to pa l
2 CONC liate his sins and to find excuses for his lust; but they could not a v
2 CONC ert the fatal consequence.  When the White Ship was wrecked on the de a
2 CONC dly rock, a boat was launched and the KingÆs only legitimate son an d h
2 CONC eir was being rowed to safety. It was the cries of his illegitimat e ha
2 CONC lf-sister, the Countess of Perche, which induced him to return t o the w
2 CONC reck, where they sank together.  [THE COMPLETE PEERAGE, Volum e XI, App
2 CONC endix D, pp. 105-121]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I was born in the year 1068---a factor he himself regarded as hi g
2 CONC hly significant, for he was the only son of the Conqueror born afte r t
2 CONC he conquest of England, and to Henry this meant he was heir to th e thr
2 CONC one. He was not an attractive proposition: he was dissolute t o a degre
2 CONC e, producing at least a score of bastards; but far worse he w as prone t
2 CONC o sadistic cruelty---on one occasion, for example, personall y punishin
2 CONC g a rebellious burgher by throwing him from the walls of hi s town.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death of William the Conqueror, Henry was left no lands, merel y 5
2 CONC ,000 pounds of silver. With these he bought lands from his elder br oth
2 CONC er Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy, only to see them taken back a gai
2 CONC n a few years later by Robert, in unholy alliance with his brothe r Wil
2 CONC liam Rufus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry could do little to avenge such treatment, but in England he foun d n
2 CONC umerous barons who were tired of the exactions and ambitions of the ir k
2 CONC ing. He formed alliances with some of these, notably with the impo rtan
2 CONC t de Clare family. He and some of the de Clares were with Willia m Rufu
2 CONC s on his last hunting expedition, and it is thought that the kin g's de
2 CONC ath was the result of Henry's plotting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Certainly he moved fast to take advantage of it; leaving Rufus's bod y u
2 CONC nattended in the woods, he swooped down on Winchester to take contr ol o
2 CONC f the treasury. Two days later he was in Westminster, being crowne d by t
2 CONC he Bishop of London. His speed is understandable when one realis es tha
2 CONC t his elder brother, Robert [Curthose], was returning from the c rusade
2 CONC , and claimed, with good reason, to be the true heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry showed great good sense in his first actions as King. He arreste d R
2 CONC anulph Flambard, William's tax-gatherer, and recalled Anselm, the e xil
2 CONC ed Archbishop. Furthermore, he issued a Charter of Liberties whic h pro
2 CONC mised speedy redress of grievances, and a return to the good gove rnmen
2 CONC t of the Conqueror. Putting aside for the moment his many mistres ses, h
2 CONC e married the sister of the King of Scots, who was descended fro m the r
2 CONC oyal line of Wessex; and lest the Norman barons should think hi m too p
2 CONC ro-English in this action, he changed her name from Edith to Ma tilda. N
2 CONC o one could claim that he did not aim to please.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1101 Robert Curthose invaded, but Henry met him at Alton, and persu a
2 CONC ded him to go away again by promising him an annuity of D2,000. He ha d n
2 CONC o intention of keeping up the payments, but the problem was tempora ril
2 CONC y solved.
2 CONT
2 CONT He now felt strong enough to move against dissident barons who might g i
2 CONC ve trouble in the future. Chief amongst these was the vicious Rober t o
2 CONC f Bellme, Earl of Shrewsbury, whom Henry had known for many years a s a d
2 CONC angerous troublemaker. He set up a number of charges against hi m in th
2 CONC e king's court, making it plain that if he appeared for trial h e would b
2 CONC e convicted and imprisoned. Thus Robert and his colleagues we re forced i
2 CONC nto rebellion at a time not of their own choosing, were eas ily defeate
2 CONC d and sent scuttling back to Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Normandy Robert Curthose began to wreak his wrath on all connecte d w
2 CONC ith his brother,
