2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION: 7th Duke of Normandy; King of England 1066-1087.  WILLIA M I o
2 CONC f Normandy and I of England; succeeded his father at the age of s even.  N
2 CONC ature and his fearful upbringing had made WILLIAM a stern prac itcal ma
2 CONC n, who ruled by force and not by dreams.  But he was also prov ided wit
2 CONC h imagination --- imagination needed by a great constructive r uler.  C
2 CONC rowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066, died 9 Sept. 108 7.  He h
2 CONC ad fallen from a horse while he was burning the town of Mante s in Fran
2 CONC ce, he was taken to Suburb of Rouen were he laid dying.  He l ived six w
2 CONC eeks after the fall.  Said to be buried in an unseenly fashi on.  By co
2 CONC nquest 1st King of House of Normandy line of kings.William I , byname W
2 CONC ILLIAM The CONQUEROR, or The BASTARD, or WILLIAM of NORMANDY , French G
2 CONC UILLAUME le CONQURANT, or le B(P)TARD, or GUILLAUME de NORMA NDIE (b. c
2 CONC . 1028, Falaise, Normandy--d. Sept. 9, 1087, Rouen), duke o f Normandy (
2 CONC as William II) from 1035 and king of England from 1066, on e of the gre
2 CONC atest soldiers and rulers of the Middle Ages. He made hims elf the migh
2 CONC tiest feudal lord in France and then changed the course o f England's h
2 CONC istory by his conquest of that country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the elder of two children of Robert I of Normandy and hi s c
2 CONC oncubine Herleva, or Arlette, the daughter of a burgher from the to wn o
2 CONC f Falaise. In 1035 Robert died when returning from a pilgrimage t o Jer
2 CONC usalem, and William, his only son, whom he had nominated as his h eir b
2 CONC efore his departure, was accepted as duke by the Norman magnate s and h
2 CONC is feudal overlord, King Henry I of France. William and his fri ends ha
2 CONC d to overcome enormous obstacles. His illegitimacy (he was gene rally k
2 CONC nown as the Bastard) was a handicap, and he had to survive th e collaps
2 CONC e of law and order that accompanied his accession as a child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of William's guardians died violent deaths before he grew up, an d h
2 CONC is tutor was murdered. His father's kin were of little help; most o f t
2 CONC hem thought that they stood to gain by the boy's death. But his mot her m
2 CONC anaged to protect William through the most dangerous period. Thes e ear
2 CONC ly difficulties probably contributed to his strength of purpose a nd hi
2 CONC s dislike of lawlessness and misrule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruler of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1042, when William reached his 15th year, was knighted, and began t o p
2 CONC lay a personal part in the affairs of his duchy, the worst was over . B
2 CONC ut his attempts to recover rights lost during the anarchy and to br ing d
2 CONC isobedient vassals and servants to heel inevitably led to trouble . Fro
2 CONC m 1046 until 1055 he dealt with a series of baronial rebellions , mostl
2 CONC y led by kinsmen. Occasionally he was in great danger and had t o rely o
2 CONC n Henry of France for help. In 1047 Henry and William defeate d a coali
2 CONC tion of Norman rebels at Val-s-Dunes, southeast of Caen. It w as in the
2 CONC se years that William learned to fight and rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT William soon learned to control his youthful recklessness. He was alwa y
2 CONC s ready to take calculated risks on campaign and, most important, t o f
2 CONC ight a battle. But he was not a chivalrous or flamboyant commander . Hi
2 CONC s plans were simple, his methods direct, and he exploited ruthless ly a
2 CONC ny advantage gained. If he found himself at a disadvantage, he wit hdre
2 CONC w immediately. He showed the same
2 CONT qualities in his government. He never lost sight of his aim to recove r l
2 CONC ost ducal rights and revenues, and, although he developed no theor y of g
2 CONC overnment or great interest in administrative techniques, he wa s alway
2 CONC s prepared to improvise and experiment. He seems to have live d a moral l
2 CONC ife by the standards of the time, and he acquired an intere st in the w
2 CONC elfare of the Norman church. He made his half brother, Odo , bishop of B
2 CONC ayeux in 1049 at the age of about 16, and Odo managed t o combine the r
2 CONC oles of nobleman and prelate in a way that did not grea tly shock conte
2 CONC mporaries. But William also welcomed foreign monks an d scholars to Nor
2 CONC mandy. Lanfranc of Pavia, a famous master of the libe ral arts, who ent
2 CONC ered the monastery of Bec about 1042, was made abbo t of Caen in 1063.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a brief description of William's person by an anonymous a u
2 CONC thor, who borrowed extensively from Einhard's Life of Charlemagne, h e w
2 CONC as just above average height and had a robust, thick-set body. Thou gh h
2 CONC e was always sparing of food and drink, he became fat in later lif e. H
2 CONC e had a rough bass voice and was a good and ready speaker. Writer s of t
2 CONC he next generation agree that he was exceptionally strong and vi gorous
2 CONC . William was an out-of-doors man, a hunter and soldier, fierc e and de
2 CONC spotic, generally feared; uneducated, he had few graces but wa s intell
2 CONC igent and shrewd and soon obtained the respect of his rivals.
2 CONT
2 CONT New alliances.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1047 William began to take part in events outside his duchy. I n s
2 CONC upport of his lord, King Henry, and in pursuit of an ambition to st ren
2 CONC gthen his southern frontier and expand into Maine, he fought a seri es o
2 CONC f campaigns against Geoffrey Martel, count of Anjou. But in 1052 H enry a
2 CONC nd Geoffrey made peace, there was a serious rebellion in easter n Norma
2 CONC ndy, and, until 1054 William was again in serious danger. Durin g this p
2 CONC eriod he conducted important negotiations with his cousin Edwa rd the C
2 CONC onfessor, king of England, and took a wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Norman interest in Anglo-Saxon England derived from an alliance made i n 1
2 CONC 002, when King Ethelred II of England married Emma, the sister of C oun
2 CONC t Richard II, William's grandfather. Two of her sons, William's cou sin
2 CONC s once removed, had reigned in turn in England, Hardecanute (1040-4 2) a
2 CONC nd Edward the Confessor (1042-66). William had met Edward during t hat p
2 CONC rince's exile on the Continent and may well have given him some s uppor
2 CONC t when he returned to England in 1041. In that year Edward was ab out 3
2 CONC 6 and William 14. It is clear that William expected some sort o f rewar
2 CONC d from Edward and, when Edward's marriage proved unfruitful, be gan to d
2 CONC evelop an ambition to become his kinsman's heir. Edward probab ly at ti
2 CONC mes encouraged William's hopes. His childlessness was a diplom atic ass
2 CONC et.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1049 William negotiated with Baldwin V of Flanders for the hand o f h
2 CONC is daughter, Matilda. Baldwin, an imperial vassal with a distinguis hed l
2 CONC ineage, was in rebellion against the Western emperor, Henry III , and i
2 CONC n desperate need of allies. The proposed marriage was condemne d as inc
2 CONC estuous (William and Matilda were evidently related in some wa y) by th
2 CONC e Emperor's friend, Pope Leo IX, at the Council of Reims in Oc tober 10
2 CONC 49; but so anxious were the parties for the alliance that befo re the e
2 CONC nd of 1053, possibly in 1052, the wedding took place. In 105 9 William w
2 CONC as reconciled to the papacy, and as penance the disobedien t pair built t
2 CONC wo monasteries at Caen. Four sons were born to William a nd Matilda: Ro
2 CONC bert (the future duke of Normandy), Richard (who died yo ung), William R
2 CONC ufus (the Conqueror's successor in England), and Henr y (Rufus' success
2 CONC or). Among the daughters was Adela, who was the mothe r of Stephen, kin
2 CONC g of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward the Confessor was supporting the Emperor, and it is possible th a
2 CONC t William used his new alliance with Flanders to put pressure on Edwa r
2 CONC d and extort an acknowledgment that he was the English king's heir. A t a
2 CONC ll events, Edward seems to have made some sort of promise to Willia m i
2 CONC n 1051, while Tostig, son of the greatest nobleman in England, Ear l Go
2 CONC dwine, married Baldwin's half sister. The immediate purpose of thi s tr
2 CONC ipartite alliance was to improve the security of each of the parti es. I
2 CONC f William secured a declaration that he was Edward's heir, he wa s also l
2 CONC ooking very far ahead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1054 and 1060 William held his own against an alliance betwee n K
2 CONC ing Henry I and Geoffrey Martel of Anjou. Both men died in 1060 an d we
2 CONC re succeeded by weaker rulers. As a result, in 1063 William was ab le t
2 CONC o conquer Maine. In 1064 or 1065 Edward sent his brother-in-law, H arol
2 CONC d, earl of Wessex, Godwine's son and successor, on an embassy to N orma
2 CONC ndy. William took him on a campaign into Brittany, and in connecti on w
2 CONC ith this Harold swore an oath in which, according to Norman writer s, h
2 CONC e renewed Edward's bequest of the throne to William and promised t o su
2 CONC pport it.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edward died childless on Jan. 5, 1066, Harold was accepted as kin g b
2 CONC y the English magnates, and William decided on war. Others, however , m
2 CONC oved more quickly. In May Tostig, Harold's exiled brother, raided E ngl
2 CONC and, and in September he joined the invasion forces of Harald III H ard
2 CONC raade, king of Norway, off the Northumbrian coast. William assemble d a f
2 CONC leet, recruited an army, and gathered his forces in August at th e mout
2 CONC h of the Dives River. It is likely that he originally intended t o sail d
2 CONC ue north and invade England by way of the Isle of Wight and So uthampto
2 CONC n Water. Such a plan would give him an offshore base and inter ior line
2 CONC s. But adverse winds detained his fleet in harbour for a month , and in S
2 CONC eptember a westerly gale drove his ships up-Channel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT William regrouped his forces at Saint-Valry on the Somme. He had suffe r
2 CONC ed a costly delay, some naval losses, and a drop in the morale of hi s t
2 CONC roops. On September 27, after cold and rainy weather, the wind back ed s
2 CONC outh. William embarked his army and set sail for the southeast coa st o
2 CONC f England. The following morning he landed, took the unresisting t owns o
2 CONC f Pevensey and Hastings, and began to organize a bridgehead wit h betwe
2 CONC en 4,000 and 7,000 cavalry and infantry.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's forces were in a narrow coastal strip, hemmed in by the grea t f
2 CONC orest of Andred, and, although this corridor was easily defensible , it w
2 CONC as not much of a base for the conquest of England. The campaigni ng sea
2 CONC son was almost past, and when William received news of his oppon ent it w
2 CONC as not reassuring. On September 25 Harold had defeated and sla in Tosti
2 CONC g and Harald Hardraade at Stamford Bridge, near York, and wa s retracin
2 CONC g his steps to meet the new invader. On October 13, when Har old emerge
2 CONC d from the forest, William was taken by surprise. But the ho ur was too l
2 CONC ate for Harold to push on to Hastings, and he took up a de fensive posi
2 CONC tion. Early the next day William went out to give battle . He attacked t
2 CONC he English phalanx with archers and cavalry but saw hi s army almost dr
2 CONC iven from the field. He rallied the fugitives, however , and brought th
2 CONC em back into the fight and in the end wore down his op ponents. Harold'
2 CONC s brothers were killed early in the battle. Toward nig htfall the King h
2 CONC imself fell and the English gave up. William's coolne ss and tenacity s
2 CONC ecured him victory in this fateful battle, and he the n moved against p
2 CONC ossible centres of resistance so quickly that he prev ented a new leade
2 CONC r from emerging. On Christmas Day 1066 he was crowne d king in Westmins
2 CONC ter Abbey. In a formal sense the Norman Conquest o f England had taken p
2 CONC lace.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of England
2 CONT
2 CONT William was already an experienced ruler. In Normandy he had replace d d
2 CONC isloyal nobles and ducal servants with his own friends, limited pri vat
2 CONC e warfare, and recovered usurped ducal rights, defining the feuda l dut
2 CONC ies of his vassals. The Norman church flourished under his rule . He wa
2 CONC nted a church free of corruption but subordinate to him. He wou ld not t
2 CONC olerate opposition from bishops and abbots or interference fro m the pa
2 CONC pacy. He presided over church synods and reinforced ecclesiast ical dis
2 CONC cipline with his own. In supporting Lanfranc, prior of Bec, ag ainst Be
2 CONC rengar of Tours in their dispute over the doctrine of the Euch arist, h
2 CONC e found himself on the side of orthodoxy. He was never guilt y of the s
2 CONC elling of church office (simony). He disapproved of clerica l marriage. A
2 CONC t the same time he was a stern and sometimes rough master , swayed by p
2 CONC olitical necessities, and he was not generous to the chur ch with his o
2 CONC wn property. The reformer Lanfranc was one of his adviser s; but perhap
2 CONC s even more to his taste were the worldly and soldierly b ishops Odo of B
2 CONC ayeux and Geoffrey of Coutances.
2 CONT
2 CONT William left England early in 1067 but had to return in December becau s
2 CONC e of English unrest. The English rebellions that began in 1067 reache d t
2 CONC heir peak in 1069 and were finally quelled in 1071. They complete d the r
2 CONC uin of the highest English aristocracy and gave William a dista ste for h
2 CONC is newly conquered kingdom. Since his position on the Contine nt was de
2 CONC teriorating, he wanted to solve English problems as cheaply a s possibl
2 CONC e. To secure England's frontiers, he invaded Scotland in 107 2 and Wale
2 CONC s in 1081 and created special defensive "marcher" counties a long the S
2 CONC cottish and Welsh borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the last 15 years of his life he was more often in Normandy than i n E
2 CONC ngland, and there were five years, possibly seven, in which he di d not v
2 CONC isit the kingdom at all. He retained most of the greatest Anglo -Norman b
2 CONC arons with him in Normandy and confided the government of Eng land to b
2 CONC ishops, trusting especially his old friend Lanfranc, whom h e made arch
2 CONC bishop of Canterbury. Much concerned that the natives shoul d not be un
2 CONC necessarily disturbed, he allowed them to retain their ow n laws and co
2 CONC urts.
2 CONT
2 CONT William returned to England only when it was absolutely necessary: i n 1
2 CONC 075 to deal with the aftermath of a rebellion by Roger, earl of Her efo
2 CONC rd, and Ralf, earl of Norfolk, which was made more dangerous by th e in
2 CONC tervention of a Danish fleet; and in 1082 to arrest and imprison h is h
2 CONC alf brother Odo, bishop of Bayeux and earl of Kent, who was planni ng t
2 CONC o take an army to Italy, perhaps to make himself pope. In the spri ng o
2 CONC f 1082 William had his son Henry knighted, and in August at Salisb ury h
2 CONC e took oaths of fealty from all the important landowners in Engla nd, w
2 CONC hosoever's vassals they might be. In 1085 he returned with a larg e arm
2 CONC y to meet the threat of an invasion by Canute IV (Canute the Holy ) of D
2 CONC enmark. When this came to nothing owing to Canute's death in 108 6, Wil
2 CONC liam ordered an economic and tenurial survey to be made of the k ingdom
2 CONC , the results of which are summarized in the two volumes of Dome sday B
2 CONC ook.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was preoccupied with the frontiers of Normandy. The danger spo t
2 CONC s were in Maine and the Vexin on the Seine, where Normandy bordered o n t
2 CONC he French royal demesne. After 1066 William's continental neighbour s b
2 CONC ecame more powerful and even more hostile. In 1068 Fulk the Surly s ucc
2 CONC eeded to Anjou and in 1071 Robert the Frisian to Flanders. Phili p I of F
2 CONC rance allied with Robert and Robert with the Danish king, Canu te IV. T
2 CONC here was also the problem of William's heir apparent, Robert C urthose, w
2 CONC ho, given no appanage and seemingly kept short of money, lef t Normandy i
2 CONC n 1077 and intrigued with his father's enemies. In 1081 Wi lliam made a c
2 CONC ompromise with Fulk in the treaty of Blancheland: Rober t Curthose was t
2 CONC o be count of Maine but as a vassal of the count of An jou. The eastern p
2 CONC art of the Vexin, the county of Mantes, had fallen c ompletely into Kin
2 CONC g Philip's hands in 1077 when William had been bus y with Maine. In 108
2 CONC 7 William demanded from Philip the return of the t owns of Chaumont, Ma
2 CONC ntes, and Pontoise. In July he entered Mantes by s urprise, but while t
2 CONC he town burned he suffered some injury from whic h he never recovered. H
2 CONC e was thwarted at the very moment when he seeme d about to enforce his l
2 CONC ast outstanding territorial claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT William was taken to a suburb of Rouen, where he lay dying for five we e
2 CONC ks. He had the assistance of some of his bishops and doctors, and i n a
2 CONC ttendance were his half brother Robert, count of Mortain, and his y oun
2 CONC ger sons, William Rufus and Henry. Robert Curthose was with the Kin g o
2 CONC f France. It had probably been his intention that Robert, as was th e c
2 CONC ustom, should succeed to the whole inheritance. In the circumstance s h
2 CONC e was tempted to make the loyal Rufus his sole heir. In the end h e com
2 CONC promised: Normandy and Maine went to Robert and England to Rufus . Henr
2 CONC y was given great treasure with which he could purchase an appan age. W
2 CONC illiam died at daybreak on September 9, in his 60th year, and wa s buri
2 CONC ed in rather unseemly fashion in St. Stephen's Church, which h e had bu
2 CONC ilt at Caen. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD, 1996, WILLIAM I]Wil liam I, b
