2 CONC wo monasteries at Caen. Four sons were born to William a nd Matilda: Ro
2 CONC bert (the future duke of Normandy), Richard (who died yo ung), William R
2 CONC ufus (the Conqueror's successor in England), and Henr y (Rufus' success
2 CONC or). Among the daughters was Adela, who was the mothe r of Stephen, kin
2 CONC g of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward the Confessor was supporting the Emperor, and it is possible th a
2 CONC t William used his new alliance with Flanders to put pressure on Edwa r
2 CONC d and extort an acknowledgment that he was the English king's heir. A t a
2 CONC ll events, Edward seems to have made some sort of promise to Willia m i
2 CONC n 1051, while Tostig, son of the greatest nobleman in England, Ear l Go
2 CONC dwine, married Baldwin's half sister. The immediate purpose of thi s tr
2 CONC ipartite alliance was to improve the security of each of the parti es. I
2 CONC f William secured a declaration that he was Edward's heir, he wa s also l
2 CONC ooking very far ahead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1054 and 1060 William held his own against an alliance betwee n K
2 CONC ing Henry I and Geoffrey Martel of Anjou. Both men died in 1060 an d we
2 CONC re succeeded by weaker rulers. As a result, in 1063 William was ab le t
2 CONC o conquer Maine. In 1064 or 1065 Edward sent his brother-in-law, H arol
2 CONC d, earl of Wessex, Godwine's son and successor, on an embassy to N orma
2 CONC ndy. William took him on a campaign into Brittany, and in connecti on w
2 CONC ith this Harold swore an oath in which, according to Norman writer s, h
2 CONC e renewed Edward's bequest of the throne to William and promised t o su
2 CONC pport it.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edward died childless on Jan. 5, 1066, Harold was accepted as kin g b
2 CONC y the English magnates, and William decided on war. Others, however , m
2 CONC oved more quickly. In May Tostig, Harold's exiled brother, raided E ngl
2 CONC and, and in September he joined the invasion forces of Harald III H ard
2 CONC raade, king of Norway, off the Northumbrian coast. William assemble d a f
2 CONC leet, recruited an army, and gathered his forces in August at th e mout
2 CONC h of the Dives River. It is likely that he originally intended t o sail d
2 CONC ue north and invade England by way of the Isle of Wight and So uthampto
2 CONC n Water. Such a plan would give him an offshore base and inter ior line
2 CONC s. But adverse winds detained his fleet in harbour for a month , and in S
2 CONC eptember a westerly gale drove his ships up-Channel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT William regrouped his forces at Saint-Valry on the Somme. He had suffe r
2 CONC ed a costly delay, some naval losses, and a drop in the morale of hi s t
2 CONC roops. On September 27, after cold and rainy weather, the wind back ed s
2 CONC outh. William embarked his army and set sail for the southeast coa st o
2 CONC f England. The following morning he landed, took the unresisting t owns o
2 CONC f Pevensey and Hastings, and began to organize a bridgehead wit h betwe
2 CONC en 4,000 and 7,000 cavalry and infantry.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's forces were in a narrow coastal strip, hemmed in by the grea t f
2 CONC orest of Andred, and, although this corridor was easily defensible , it w
2 CONC as not much of a base for the conquest of England. The campaigni ng sea
2 CONC son was almost past, and when William received news of his oppon ent it w
2 CONC as not reassuring. On September 25 Harold had defeated and sla in Tosti
2 CONC g and Harald Hardraade at Stamford Bridge, near York, and wa s retracin
2 CONC g his steps to meet the new invader. On October 13, when Har old emerge
2 CONC d from the forest, William was taken by surprise. But the ho ur was too l
2 CONC ate for Harold to push on to Hastings, and he took up a de fensive posi
2 CONC tion. Early the next day William went out to give battle . He attacked t
2 CONC he English phalanx with archers and cavalry but saw hi s army almost dr
2 CONC iven from the field. He rallied the fugitives, however , and brought th
2 CONC em back into the fight and in the end wore down his op ponents. Harold'
2 CONC s brothers were killed early in the battle. Toward nig htfall the King h
2 CONC imself fell and the English gave up. William's coolne ss and tenacity s
2 CONC ecured him victory in this fateful battle, and he the n moved against p
2 CONC ossible centres of resistance so quickly that he prev ented a new leade
2 CONC r from emerging. On Christmas Day 1066 he was crowne d king in Westmins
2 CONC ter Abbey. In a formal sense the Norman Conquest o f England had taken p
2 CONC lace.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of England
2 CONT
2 CONT William was already an experienced ruler. In Normandy he had replace d d
2 CONC isloyal nobles and ducal servants with his own friends, limited pri vat
2 CONC e warfare, and recovered usurped ducal rights, defining the feuda l dut
2 CONC ies of his vassals. The Norman church flourished under his rule . He wa
2 CONC nted a church free of corruption but subordinate to him. He wou ld not t
2 CONC olerate opposition from bishops and abbots or interference fro m the pa
2 CONC pacy. He presided over church synods and reinforced ecclesiast ical dis
2 CONC cipline with his own. In supporting Lanfranc, prior of Bec, ag ainst Be
2 CONC rengar of Tours in their dispute over the doctrine of the Euch arist, h
2 CONC e found himself on the side of orthodoxy. He was never guilt y of the s
2 CONC elling of church office (simony). He disapproved of clerica l marriage. A
2 CONC t the same time he was a stern and sometimes rough master , swayed by p
2 CONC olitical necessities, and he was not generous to the chur ch with his o
2 CONC wn property. The reformer Lanfranc was one of his adviser s; but perhap
2 CONC s even more to his taste were the worldly and soldierly b ishops Odo of B
2 CONC ayeux and Geoffrey of Coutances.
2 CONT
2 CONT William left England early in 1067 but had to return in December becau s
2 CONC e of English unrest. The English rebellions that began in 1067 reache d t
2 CONC heir peak in 1069 and were finally quelled in 1071. They complete d the r
2 CONC uin of the highest English aristocracy and gave William a dista ste for h
2 CONC is newly conquered kingdom. Since his position on the Contine nt was de
2 CONC teriorating, he wanted to solve English problems as cheaply a s possibl
2 CONC e. To secure England's frontiers, he invaded Scotland in 107 2 and Wale
2 CONC s in 1081 and created special defensive "marcher" counties a long the S
2 CONC cottish and Welsh borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the last 15 years of his life he was more often in Normandy than i n E
2 CONC ngland, and there were five years, possibly seven, in which he di d not v
2 CONC isit the kingdom at all. He retained most of the greatest Anglo -Norman b
2 CONC arons with him in Normandy and confided the government of Eng land to b
2 CONC ishops, trusting especially his old friend Lanfranc, whom h e made arch
2 CONC bishop of Canterbury. Much concerned that the natives shoul d not be un
2 CONC necessarily disturbed, he allowed them to retain their ow n laws and co
2 CONC urts.
2 CONT
2 CONT William returned to England only when it was absolutely necessary: i n 1
2 CONC 075 to deal with the aftermath of a rebellion by Roger, earl of Her efo
2 CONC rd, and Ralf, earl of Norfolk, which was made more dangerous by th e in
2 CONC tervention of a Danish fleet; and in 1082 to arrest and imprison h is h
2 CONC alf brother Odo, bishop of Bayeux and earl of Kent, who was planni ng t
2 CONC o take an army to Italy, perhaps to make himself pope. In the spri ng o
2 CONC f 1082 William had his son Henry knighted, and in August at Salisb ury h
2 CONC e took oaths of fealty from all the important landowners in Engla nd, w
2 CONC hosoever's vassals they might be. In 1085 he returned with a larg e arm
2 CONC y to meet the threat of an invasion by Canute IV (Canute the Holy ) of D
2 CONC enmark. When this came to nothing owing to Canute's death in 108 6, Wil
2 CONC liam ordered an economic and tenurial survey to be made of the k ingdom
2 CONC , the results of which are summarized in the two volumes of Dome sday B
2 CONC ook.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was preoccupied with the frontiers of Normandy. The danger spo t
2 CONC s were in Maine and the Vexin on the Seine, where Normandy bordered o n t
2 CONC he French royal demesne. After 1066 William's continental neighbour s b
2 CONC ecame more powerful and even more hostile. In 1068 Fulk the Surly s ucc
2 CONC eeded to Anjou and in 1071 Robert the Frisian to Flanders. Phili p I of F
2 CONC rance allied with Robert and Robert with the Danish king, Canu te IV. T
2 CONC here was also th
