2 CONC hen was, persuaded his reluctant barons to accept her, on oa th, as his s
2 CONC uccessor (Jan. 1, 1127). The novel prospect of a female ru ler was itse
2 CONC lf unwelcome; Matilda's 17-year absence in Germany (wher e she was not u
2 CONC npopular) and her apparent arrogrance estranged her fro m her father's s
2 CONC ubjects. Difficulties also might result from her remar riage to provide f
2 CONC or the succession. Her marriage in 1128 to Geoffre y Plantagenet, heir t
2 CONC o Anjou and Maine (designed by Henry I, like he r first marriage, for p
2 CONC olitical ends), whose father, CountFulk, depart ed immediately after th
2 CONC e ceremony to become the consort of Melisende o f Jerusalem, flouted th
2 CONC e barons' stipulation that she should not marr y outside England withou
2 CONC t their consent, and was unpopular in Normand y and England. On Henry I
2 CONC 's death, his nephew Stephen by prompt actio n secured England and was r
2 CONC ecognized by Pope Innocent II. Matilda an d Geoffrey, however, made som
2 CONC e headway in Normandy. Matilda's subseque nt challenge to Stephen's pos
2 CONC ition in England mainly depended on the s upport of her half-brother Ea
2 CONC rl Robert of Gloucester. After the defea t and capture of Stephen at Li
2 CONC ncoln (Feb. 1141), Matilda was elected " lady of the English" and would h
2 CONC ave been queen could she have proceede d to coronation, but active supp
2 CONC ort for her cause still came mainly fr om the western counties. Her cha
2 CONC nce of consolidating her precarious vi ctory was swiftly destroyed by a r
2 CONC eaction initated by her tactless han dling of London. After her defeat a
2 CONC t Winchester in Sept. 1141, her sup porters, slowly reduced by death an
2 CONC d defection, maintained a stubbor n defense until Earl Robert died (114
2 CONC 7) and Matilda retired (1148) t o Normandy, of which her husband had ga
2 CONC ined possession. She continue d to interest herself in the government o
2 CONC f the territories of her elde st son, the future Henry II of England. H
2 CONC er career was not entirely un successful: all the subsequent monarchs o
2 CONC f England have been her desce ndants, not Stephen's. She died in Norman
2 CONC dy on Sept. 10, 1167.
2 CONT
2 CONT ***********
