1 NOTE  OCCUPATION:  Matilda of Scotland; b. 1079; d. 1 may 118; m. 11 nov 110 0
2 CONC , HENRY I, Beauclerc, King of England, 1100-1135; b. 1070; d. 1 Dec . 1
2 CONC 135 (He m. (2) 29 Jan. 1121, ADELIZA of Louvain, b. ca. 1103; d. 2 3 Ap
2 CONC r. 1151 (s.p. by this marriage); she m. (2) 1138, William d'Aubign y, E
2 CONC arl of Arundel, d. 12 Oct. 1176).Christened Edith, but adopted th e nam
2 CONC e Matilda upon her marriage to Henry I.  It was thought the Norma n bar
2 CONC ons might not respect a queen with a Saxon name.  The marriage t o Henr
2 CONC y represented the union of the Norman & Saxon royal lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The following information was provided in a post-em by Curt Hofemann , c
2 CONC urt_hofemann@yahoo.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT name at birth Eadgyth, changed to Mathilda in England [Ref: DeVajay p8 5
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda of Scotland (d. 1118).  Queen consort of Henry I from 1100;  d a
2 CONC ughter of Malcolm III of Scots and granddaughter of Edmund Ironside .  K
2 CONC nown as Good Queen Maud, she was a strong supporter of Archbisho p Anse
2 CONC lm and the church. [Ref: Dict of Brit History p237]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Matilda 'Atheling', Princess of SCOTLAND.  Born about 1079/108 0 D
2 CONC unfermline, Fifeshire, Scotland.  Died 1 May 1118 Westminster, Midd les
2 CONC ex, England.  Buried Jun 1118 Church of St Peter, Westminster, Midd les
2 CONC ex, England.  To please the English subjects of her husband, the ki ng, s
2 CONC he changed her Saxon name of Edith to the Norman name of Matilda .  She i
2 CONC s also known by the diminutive of that name - Maud (which ha d been the n
2 CONC ame of Henry's mother).  She was the sister of Edgar, Kin g of Scotland 1
2 CONC 098-1107. [Ref: Unk]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith - Margaret (Matilda) of Scotland, born in 1080 and died in 1118 , m
2 CONC arried Henry I. Beauclerc, King of England, son of William I The Co nqu
2 CONC eror (ruler from 1066 to 1087) and his wife, Matilda of Flanders, w ho d
2 CONC ied in 1083... Matilda was educated at Wilton and Romsey Abbey whe re s
2 CONC he said that her aunt, Christina, forced her to wear a black veil . She t
2 CONC hrew it on the ground whenever left alone, in spite of beatings . When h
2 CONC er mother died she came to England to Edgar Atheling, her uncl e. She w
2 CONC as a sister of King David of Scotland; she was a corresponden t of Anse
2 CONC lm and Hildebert, Bishop of Le Mans, who wrote poetry about h er. She w
2 CONC as a symbol of the union of Saxon and Norman. She was Henry' s Queen fo
2 CONC r seventeen years and six months, and died in her prime lik e most of h
2 CONC er family. Henry and Matilda had a son and a daughter... [R ef: McBride
2 CONC 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda (Edith) of Scotland (1079-1118) daughter of Malcolm III Canmor e
2 CONC , King of Scots 1058-1093 and St. Margaret of Wessex.  Born 1079 Dunf e
2 CONC rmline.  Died 1 May 1118 Westminster Palace.  Married 11 November 110 0 W
2 CONC estminster Abbey Henry I, King of England 1100-1135.  Born 1068 Sel by, Y
2 CONC orkshire.  Died 1 December 1135 St.Denis-le-Fermont, nr Rouen.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in 1079 or 1080 and, as she was destined to become a nun , s
2 CONC he went to Romsey where her Aunt Christina was the Abbess. However , in 1
2 CONC 100 Henry I, the new king of England, demanded her hand in marri age; a
2 CONC nd on 11 November 1100 at Westminster she became his queen. Upo n her m
2 CONC arriage she adopted the name Matilda in honour of the king's mo ther. T
2 CONC hey had two, possibly three children. Queen Matilda built a lep er hosp
2 CONC ital at St. Giles-in-the-Fields, London, and founded the August inian P
2 CONC riory at Aldgate. She was aged only about thirty-eight when sh e died. [
2 CONC Ref: Leo van de Pas]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regards,
2 CONT Curt
2 CONT
2 CONT *********Alternate name found in file: Matilda Scotland, Atheling
2 CONT
2 CONT Brian Tompsett:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Some say she is buried at Winchester."
2 CONT Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (1:23), (121:25).
2 CONT
