1 NOTE  CONFLICT: Benjamin Maulsby and Rhoda WILLIAMS Backward and Forward, G . A
2 CONC nn Roberts, 1990 (a private publishing) [states Robert of Caen, Ear l o
2 CONC f Gloucester]
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH:  Mother maybe Concubine of Eng
2 CONT
2 CONT MISC:  Robert, Earl of Gloucester, 1122-47, d. 1147, and Mabel, dau. o f R
2 CONC obert Fitz Hamond, and grandson of King Henry I of England.MISC:  [ Fro
2 CONC m Burke's Peerage-see source for details]
2 CONT
2 CONT An undoubted Earl of Gloucester, perhaps the first authentic one, at a n
2 CONC y rate after the Conquest, is Robert FitzHamon's son-in-law, anothe r R
2 CONC obert, who was an illegitimate son of Henry I and was so created 11 22. T
2 CONC he Earldom passed to his eldest son, William FitzRobert, and fro m him t
2 CONC o John, later King John and husband from 1189 to 1199 (when h e divorce
2 CONC d her) of Isabel, the youngest of William FitzRobert's thre e daughters
2 CONC .  On John's coming to the throne the title did not merge i n the Crown f
2 CONC or it was not his in his own right but in right of his wi fe.[From Burk
2 CONC e's Peerage-see source for details]
2 CONT
2 CONT ALIAS: Robert FitzRoy, 1st Earl of Gloucester
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, EARL OF GLOUCESTER, by Mabel, daughter and heir of Robert FITZ -
2 CONC HAMOND, lord of Tewkesbury.  [Complete Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [From Burke's Peerage-see source for details]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, provided the following informa t
2 CONC ion in a post-em, including an indication that he was a son of Sybi l C
2 CONC orbet.  I believe that Robert was born too early to be a son of Syb il.  C
2 CONC ertainly The Complete Peerage did not indicate he was her son (a lthoug
2 CONC h there was a confused reference to Sybil daughter of Robert Cor bet, b
2 CONC urgess of Caen, which has been refuted):
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1090 in Caen, Calvados, Normandy, France
2 CONT
2 CONT born: about 1090 [Ref: ES III:354, Paget p11, Watney p404 & 494, Wei s A
2 CONC R7 124:26], father: [Ref: CMH p600, Paget p11, Sheppard Apr65 p96 , Wei
2 CONC s AR7 124:26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research note 1: mother Sibyl Corbet [Ref: Weir RoyalFam p48]
2 CONT Research note 2: illegitimate son of Henry I, probably by Sibil, dau o f R
2 CONC obert Fitz Corbet, burgess of Caen [Ref: Watney p404]
2 CONT Research note 3: shown as son of Sibilla Corbet, but "There is no cert a
2 CONC in evidence to show that Sybilla really was Robert's mother, who ma y h
2 CONC ave been an unknown woman of Caen" [Ref: Weir RoyalFam p48]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 31 OCT 1147 in Bristol, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT died 31 Oct 1147 [Ref: ES III:354, Paget p11, Watney p404 & 494] 114 7 [
2 CONC Ref: CMH p600, Weis AR7 124:26, Weis AR7 63:26], place: [Ref: Page t p1
2 CONC 1, Watney p404 & 494, Weis AR7 124:26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Priory of St. James, Bristol, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Research note: Buried Tewkesbury [Ref: Paget p11]
2 CONT
2 CONT married 1119 Maud (Mabel) FitzHammon
2 CONT date: [Ref: Watney p404 & 494], names: [Ref: Paget p11, Turton,  Wei s A
2 CONC R7 63:26 & 124:26]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1122-1147 Earl of Gloucester [Ref: Weis AR7 124:26]
2 CONT Earl of Gloucester, created between Jun and Sep 1122 [Ref: Paget p11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, earl of Gloucester (d. 1147).  An illegitimate son of Henry I , w
2 CONC ho supported the claim to the throne of his half-sister Matilda i n 113
2 CONC 5.  He invaded England with her in 1139 and captured King Stephe n at L
2 CONC incoln in feb 1141.  A few months later he was himself taken pri soner a
2 CONC nd exchanged for the king.  His death from fever at Bristol eff ectivel
2 CONC y ended Matilda's bid for the throne. [Ref: Dict of Brit Histor y, pg 3
2 CONC 05]
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is a (not so) brief biography of Robert, Earl of Gloucester, prob a
2 CONC bly the best-known of Henry's spectacular brood of bastards. It is la r
2 CONC gely a paraphrase of the information in CP.
2 CONT Robert 'of Caen', Earl of Gloucester, born circa 1090 in Caen, Normand y
2 CONC ; married 1107 Mabel fitz Robert FitzHamon (died 1157). Robert died 3 1 O
2 CONC ct 1147 in Bristol, England; buried at the Priory of St. James, Bri sto
2 CONC l. [children are at foot of bio] Sources: Given-Wilson & Curteis 'T he R
2 CONC oyal Bastards of Medieval England' pp 63, 75 Cokayne 'Complete Pee rage
2 CONC ' (Gloucester) Sanders, 'English Baronies: A study of their origin s an
2 CONC d descent'.
2 CONT Robert was born about 1090, probably in Caen, Normandy (hence he was k n
2 CONC own as 'Robert of Caen'). His mother was probably an unknown woman o f C
2 CONC aen and not Sibyl Corbet, as is stated by CP and others. He was ack now
2 CONC ledged by his father King Henry I from his birth, and was raised a t co
2 CONC urt from the time of his father's accession to the throne and educ ated u
2 CONC nder his direct supervision. Robert had a reputation for learnin g and l
2 CONC iterary appreciation, rare attributes at this time, which wer e undoube
2 CONC dly fostered by his scholarly father. By the time he was in h is early t
2 CONC wenties he was one of his father's leading military captain s and advis
2 CONC ers.
2 CONT In 1107 he received from Henry the hand of a wealthy heiress, Mabel o f G
2 CONC loucester, daughter of Robert FitzHamon, who brought with her the b aro
2 CONC ny of Gloucester and lordship of Glamorgan. They had a mansion at T ewk
2 CONC esbury.
2 CONT Robert fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119, where Henry defeated K i
2 CONC ng Louis VI of France.  In 1122, after the death of his legitimate ha l
2 CONC f-brothers, the earldom of Gloucester was created for his benefit.
2 CONT In 1123, he led a force to assist in the capture of the castle of Brio n
2 CONC ne, which was held by rebel Norman barons.  In 1126 he had custody o f h
2 CONC is uncle, the imprisoned rebel, Robert, Duke of Normandy.
2 CONT In 1127 he did homage to his half-sister the Empress Matilda, recogniz i
2 CONC ng her as their father&#00063;s successor to the throne. Henry looke d t
2 CONC o him to protect Matilda's interests after his death. Robert was wi th h
2 CONC is father when he died in 1135, and it was Robert who made the fun eral a
2 CONC rrangements.
2 CONT Robert was a significant figure in the struggle for succession betwee n S
2 CONC tephen (his cousin) and Matilda. Robert and Stephen had been fierc e ri
2 CONC vals at the English court since 1127, and some barons encouraged R ober
2 CONC t to claim the throne himself (after all, his grandfather Willia m the C
2 CONC onqueror had also been illegitimate), but when Stephen was chos en as k
2 CONC ing, Robert eventually did conditional homage to him for his En glish l
2 CONC ands.
2 CONT In 1137 he accompanied King Stephen to Normandy, where Matilda was rai s
2 CONC ing support, and a quarrel ensued between the men when Stephen trie d t
2 CONC o ambush Robert.  Robert then threw his support behind Matilda, wh o wa
2 CONC s in Normandy, and obtained the surrender of Caen and Bayeux to he r hu
2 CONC sband Geoffrey, Count of Anjou.  In September 1139 Robert landed i n En
2 CONC gland with Matilda and took her to Arundel Castle. He then made hi s wa
2 CONC y to Bristol Castle, which had remained loyal to him, and was late r jo
2 CONC ined there by Matilda.
2 CONT Robert became her commander-in-chief in the civil war which followed . U
2 CONC nlike most barons of the time, he fought his battles within the rul es o
2 CONC f warfare and was not unnecessarily violent or cruel, but was als o reg
2 CONC arded as brave and a good commander. He was also known for his re spect f
2 CONC or law and justice, and his integrity and chivalry. Between 113 9 and 1
2 CONC 141, he progressively took control of most of the south-west.
2 CONT In early 1141, Robert obtained word that Stephen was besieging Lincol n C
2 CONC astle. Robert quickly moved there and forced battle by personally s wim
2 CONC ming across the River Trent and requiring his troops to follow. I n the b
2 CONC attle, many of Stephen's knights fled and Stephen was capture d by Robe
2 CONC rt; he was imprisoned at Bristol in the care of Robert's wif e Mabel.
2 CONT Matilda went to London to be crowned, but she made herself unpopular w i
2 CONC th the Londoners and they were forced to flee. While beseiging Winche s
2 CONC ter, they themselves were surrounded. Robert engaged Stephen's queen' s a
2 CONC rmy for long enough to allow Matilda to escape, but he himself wa s cap
2 CONC tured and place in the custody of Stephen's queen at Rochester. S tephe
2 CONC n and Robert were then returned to their respective camps in an e ven e
2 CONC xchange, although Robert had wanted some of his supporters releas ed to c
2 CONC ompensate for his lower status. The even exchange reflected Rob ert&#00
2 CONC 063;s value to Matilda as her main supporter and battle leader.
2 CONT In 1142 Matilda sent Robert to Anjou to attempt to convince her husban d G
2 CONC eoffrey to come to her aid.  Geoffrey declined to help until he ha d co
2 CONC nquered Normandy, so Robert joined in his campaign.  However, hear ing t
2 CONC hat Matilda was besieged at Oxford, he hurried back to her assist ance.  H
2 CONC e took Matilda and Geoffrey's son, nine year old Henry (the fu ture Kin
2 CONC g Henry II), with him.  Despite some more victories, Matilda' s support h
2 CONC ad gradually dwindled; Robert was unable to continue to pre ss her caus
2 CONC e, although he continued to support his nephew Henry. He di d however r
2 CONC etain control of most of the West Country, imposing law an d order ther
2 CONC e. In 1144 one of Robert's sons, Philip, rebelled agains t his father i
2 CONC n support of Stephen.
2 CONT In 1147 thirteen year old Henry arrived in England with mercenary troo p
2 CONC s, meaning to conquer England. After a couple of weeks he turned up o n R
2 CONC obert's doorstep in Bristol asking for money to pay the troops. Rob ert m
2 CONC arched him straight to Wareham and put him on a ship, paying th e capta
2 CONC in to make sure he got back to his father in Normandy. Henry la ter reg
2 CONC arded Robert as one of the formative influences on his life, th e man w
2 CONC ho had made it possible for him to become king of England.
2 CONT Later in 1147 Robert died of a rapid fever at Bristol, even though h e w
2 CONC as still very fit and able to lead an army himself. After his deat h Ma
2 CONC tilda's cause collapsed completely, a measure of his indispensabil ity t
2 CONC o her.
2 CONT Robert was a patron of scholars, including some of the best-known medi e
2 CONC val chroniclers such as William of Malmesbury and Geoffrey of Monmout h
2 CONC , and was known for his own cleverness and literary ability. One of h i
2 CONC s enemies, Baldwin fitz Gilbert, described him in terms which suggest e
2 CONC d he was all bark and no bite - a man who threatened much but did lit t
2 CONC le, eloquent but lazy. His record as a soldier would tend to refute t h
2 CONC is last point, at the very least. He was also a generous benefactor o f t
2 CONC he Church, founding the Benedictine priory of St. James just outsid e B
2 CONC ristol and a Cistercian abbey at Margam, South Wales, and suppporti ng a
2 CONC bbeys at Tewkesbury, Gloucester, and Neath. He was buried in a mag nifi
2 CONC cent green jasper tomb at his foundation of St. James [CP querie s the s
2 CONC ite of his burial, also claimed for Tewkesbury]. [Ref: Suzann e Doig <s
2 CONC md49@csc.canterbury.ac.nz> 14 Sep 1998 messaage to soc.geneal ogy.medie
2 CONC val]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regards,
2 CONT Curt
2 CONT
2 CONT **********Alternate name found in file: Robert Fitzroy,  Robert (Ear l G
2 CONC loucester) De Caen
2 CONT
2 CONT [From Burke's Peerage-see source for details]
2 CONT
2 CONT An undoubted Earl of Gloucester, perhaps the first authentic one, at a n
2 CONC y rate after the Conquest, is Robert FitzHamon's son-in-law, anothe r R
2 CONC obert, who was an illegitimate son of Henry I and was so created 11 22. T
2 CONC he Earldom passed to his eldest son, William FitzRobert, and fro m him t
2 CONC o John, later King John and husband from 1189 to 1199 (when h e divorce
2 CONC d her) of Isabel, the youngest of William FitzRobert's thre e daughters
2 CONC .  On John's coming to the throne the title did not merge i n the Crown f
2 CONC or it was not his in his own right but in right of his wi fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT *********(4) ROBERT the King's son, whose mother Ede or Edith was appa r
2 CONC ently da. of Forn, probably identical with Forn Sigulfson, lord of Gr e
2 CONC ystoke (Cumberland) and a tenant-in-chief in County York; which Edith , a
2 CONC fter her liaison with Henry I, m. Robert de Oilli, a royal Constabl e a
2 CONC nd constable of Oxford Castle. Robert held land in Devonshire in 11 30. H
2 CONC e supported his half-sister, the EMPRESS Maud, in the Civil War . He wa
2 CONC s a great tenant-in-chief, his servitium debitum being 100 knig hts. He m
2 CONC . Maud, dame du Sap in Normandy, widow of William de Courcy , and da. a
2 CONC nd h. of Robert de Avranches, by whom he had an only daught er.  He d. 3
2 CONC 1 May 1172.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Complete Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT ALIAS: Robert FitzRoy
2 CONT
2 CONT *******
