1 NOTE  (1) MAUD, whose mother was Edith, of whom nothing is known (b).  She m . i
2 CONC n 1103, Rotrou, COUNT of Perche, styled the Great, s. and h. of Geo ffr
2 CONC ey, Count of Perche, by Beatrice, da. of Hilduin, Count of Montdidi er a
2 CONC nd  (jure uxoris) Count of Roucy.  Rotrou had gone on the 1st Crus ade i
2 CONC n 1096.  In 1105 and 1114 he went to Spain, to help his cousin Al fonso I
2 CONC , King of Navarre and Aragon, against the Moors.  In 1114 he as sisted H
2 CONC enry I at the siege of Belleme, which he had long before claim ed as hi
2 CONC s hereditary right. The King granted him the Belleme fiefs.  H e was pr
2 CONC esent at the death of his royal father-in-law in 1135. In 113 7 Stephen g
2 CONC ave him Moulins; but in 1141 he made terms with Geoffrey Pl antagenet.  M
2 CONC aud was drowned in the wreck of the White Ship, 25 Nov. 1 120, leaving 2 d
2 CONC aughters. Rotrou m., 2ndly, before 1127, Hawise, da. o f Walter de Sali
2 CONC sbury, and sister of Patrick, 1st EARL OF Salisbury. H e d. in 1144 at t
2 CONC he siege of the Tower of Rouen (20 Jan. to 23 Apr.) b y Geoffrey Planta
2 CONC genet, and his widow m., as his 2nd wife, Robert, 1s t Count of Dreux, 3
2 CONC rd s. of Louis VI (Le Gros), King of France; whic h Robert styled himse
2 CONC lf Count of Perche and lord of Belleme during th e minority of his step
2 CONC son.
2 CONT
2 CONT (b) As her daughter married in 1103, she cannot be the daughter of For n
2 CONC .
2 CONT
