1 NAME Godfrey Fitz Richard Ct EuBrion //
2 GIVN Godfrey Fitz Richard Ct EuBrion
1 SEX M
1 _UID 62BF79C231684D589831C58CB038732A3990
1 _AMTID 162143283795:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  Godfrey, Earl of Ewe and Brion, natural son of Richard I, Duke of Norm a
2 CONC ndy, was father of Gilbert, Earl of Brion.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Lon d
2 CONC on, 1883, p. 139, Courtenay, Barons Courtenay, Earls of Devon]ALIAS : G
2 CONC eoffrey of BrionneALIAS: Geoffrey of Brionne Comte d'Eu
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, Count of Brionne, who was son of Godfrey, Count of Brionne, i l
2 CONC legitimate son of Richard I, the Fearless, Duke of Normandy.  [Magn a C
2 CONC harta Sureties, line 157-1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Turton has Geoffrey (Godfrey Comte d'Eu as a legitimate son of Richar d & G
2 CONC unnora, which confused me for awhile and I had him as a 2nd perso n (fa
2 CONC ther of Adele only).  However I believe Turton is wrong, and Godf rey w
2 CONC as an illegitimate son (in fact the eldest illegitimate son).
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is the first part of a post to SGM, 29 Aug 1996, by Dav e U
2 CONC tzinger:
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Dave Utzinger (UTZ@AOL.COM)
2 CONT Subject: CLARE FAMILY
2 CONT Newsgroups: soc.genealogy.medieval
2 CONT Date: 1996/08/29
2 CONT
2 CONT From "A Baronial Family in Medieval England: The Clares, 1217-1314", b y M
2 CONC ichael Altschul, Baltimore, The Johns Hopkins press, 1965.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Clares came to England with the Conqueror.  Like many other grea t f
2 CONC amilies settled in England after the Conquest, they were related t o th
2 CONC e dukes of Normandy and had established themselves as important me mber
2 CONC s of the Norman feudal aristocracy in the late tenth and early ele vent
2 CONC h centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of the family can be traced to Godfrey, eldest of the illeg i
2 CONC timate children of Duke Richard I (the Fearless), the Conqueror's gre a
2 CONC t-grandfather. While the Duke granted Godfrey Brionne, he did not mak e h
2 CONC im a count. Godfrey's comital title derives from the grant of the c oun
2 CONC ty of Eu made to him after 996 by his half-brother, Duke Richard II .  A
2 CONC fter Godfrey's death, Eu was given to William, another of Duke Ric hard I
2 CONC 's bastard sons, and Gilbert, Godfrey's son, was left with onl y the lo
2 CONC rdship of Brionne.  However, under Duke Robert I, father of Wi lliam th
2 CONC e Conqueror, Gilbert assumed the title of count of Brionne whi le not r
2 CONC elinquishing his claim to Eu.  When Count William of Eu died s hortly b
2 CONC efore 1040, Gilbert assumed the land and title, but he was ass assinate
2 CONC d in 1040 and his young sons, Richard and Baldwin, were force d to flee N
2 CONC ormandy, finding safety at the court of Baldwin V, count o f Flanders.  W
2 CONC hen William the Conqueror married Count Baldwin's daught er, he restore
2 CONC d Gilbert's sons to Normandy, although he did not inves t them with eit
2 CONC her Brionne or Eu or a comital title.  William grante d the lordships o
2 CONC f Bienfaite and Orbec to Richard fitz Gilbert, and L e Sap and Meules t
2 CONC o Baldwin.  While Gilbert's descendants later presse d a claim for Brio
2 CONC nne, it was never restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT **********ALIAS: Geoffrey of Brionne Comte d' Eu
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, Count of Brionne, who was son of Godfrey, Count of Brionne, i l
2 CONC legitimate son of Richard I, the Fearless, Duke of Normandy.  [Magn a C
2 CONC harta Sureties, line 157-1]
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT Turton has Geoffrey (Godfrey Comte d'Eu as a legitimate son of Richar d & G
2 CONC unnora, which confused me for awhile and I had him as a 2nd perso n (fa
2 CONC ther of Adele only).  However I believe Turton is wrong, and Godf rey w
2 CONC as an illegitimate son (in fact the eldest illegitimate son).
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT The following is the first part of a post to SGM, 29 Aug 1996, by Dav e U
2 CONC tzinger:
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Dave Utzinger (UTZ@AOL.COM)
2 CONT Subject: CLARE FAMILY
2 CONT Newsgroups: soc.genealogy.medieval
2 CONT Date: 1996/08/29
2 CONT
2 CONT From "A Baronial Family in Medieval England: The Clares, 1217-1314", b y M
2 CONC ichael Altschul, Baltimore, The Johns Hopkins press, 1965.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Clares came to England with the Conqueror.  Like many other grea t f
2 CONC amilies settled in England after the Conquest, they were related t o th
2 CONC e dukes of Normandy and had established themselves as important me mber
2 CONC s of the Norman feudal aristocracy in the late tenth and early ele vent
2 CONC h centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of the family can be traced to Godfrey, eldest of the illeg i
2 CONC timate children of Duke Richard I (the Fearless), the Conqueror's gre a
2 CONC t-grandfather.  While the Duke granted Godfrey Brionne, he did not ma k
2 CONC e him a count. Godfrey's comital title derives from the grant of th e c
2 CONC ounty of Eu made to him after 996 by his half-brother, Duke Richar d II
2 CONC . After Godfrey's death, Eu was given to William, another of Duk e Rich
2 CONC ard I's bastard sons, and Gilbert, Godfrey's son, was left wit h only t
2 CONC he lordship of Brionne.  However, under Duke Robert I, fathe r of Willi
2 CONC am the Conqueror, Gilbert assumed the title of count of Brio nne while n
2 CONC ot relinquishing his claim to Eu.  When Count William of E u died short
2 CONC ly before 1040, Gilbert assumed the land and title, but h e was assassi
2 CONC nated in 1040 and his young sons, Richard and Baldwin, we re forced to f
2 CONC lee Normandy, finding safety at the court of Baldwin V , count of Fland
2 CONC ers. When William the Conqueror married Count Baldwin' s daughter, he r
2 CONC estored Gilbert's sons to Normandy, although he did no t invest them wi
2 CONC th either Brionne or Eu or a comital title.  William g ranted the lords
2 CONC hips of Bienfaite and Orbec to Richard fitz Gilbert, a nd Le Sap and Me
2 CONC ules to Baldwin.  While Gilbert's descendants later pr essed a claim fo
2 CONC r Brionne, it was never restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT *************
