2 CONC 028-1035.Robert I, byname ROBERT The MAGNIFICENT, or The DEVIL, Fre nch R
2 CONC OBERT le MAGNIFIQUE, or le DIABLE (d. July 1035, Nicaea), duke o f Norm
2 CONC andy (1027-35), the younger son of Richard II of Normandy and th e fath
2 CONC er, by his mistress Arlette, of William the Conqueror of England . On t
2 CONC he death of his father (1026/27), Robert contested the duchy wit h his e
2 CONC lder brother Richard III, legally the heir, until the latter' s opportu
2 CONC ne death a few years later. A strong ruler, Robert succeede d in exacti
2 CONC ng the obedience of his vassals. On the death of Robert I I the Pious, k
2 CONC ing of France (1031), a crisis arose over the successio n to the French t
2 CONC hrone. The Duke gave his support to Henry I against t he party favourin
2 CONC g his younger brother; in reward for his services h e demanded and rece
2 CONC ived the Vexin Franais, a territory not far north o f Paris. A patron o
2 CONC f the monastic reform movement, he died while retur ning from a pilgrim
2 CONC age to Jerusalem. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97] Robert I, by name R
2 CONC OBERT The MAGNIFICENT, or The DEVIL, French ROBER T le MAGNIFIQUE, or l
2 CONC e DIABLE (d. July 1035, Nicaea), duke of Normand y (1027-35), the young
2 CONC er son of Richard II of Normandy and the father , by his mistress Arlet
2 CONC te, of William the Conqueror of England. On th e death of his father (1
2 CONC 026/27), Robert contested the duchy with his e lder brother Richard III
2 CONC , legally the heir, until the latter's opportu ne death a few years lat
2 CONC er. A strong ruler, Robert succeeded in exacti ng the obedience of his v
