1 NOTE  OCCUPATION:  Warred against the last of the Carolingians; supported HU G
2 CONC H CAPET and Capetian dynasty.  Duke of Normandy (942-996).Richard I , b
2 CONC yname RICHARD THE FEARLESS, French RICHARD SANS PEUR (b. c. 932--d . 99
2 CONC 6), duke of Normandy (942-996), son of William I Longsword.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IV of France took the boy-duke into his protective custody, appa r
2 CONC ently intent upon reuniting Normandy to the crown's domains; but in 9 4
2 CONC 5 Louis was captured by the Normans, and Richard was returned to hi s p
2 CONC eople. Richard withstood further Carolingian attempts to subdue hi s du
2 CONC chy and, in 987, was instrumental in securing the French crown fo r his b
2 CONC rother-in-law, the Robertian Hugh Capet.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]Richard I, byname RICHARD The FEARLES S
2 CONC , French RICHARD SANS PEUR (b. c. 932--d. 996), duke of Normandy (942 -
2 CONC 996), son of William I Longsword.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IV of France took the boy-duke into his protective custody, appa r
2 CONC ently intent upon reuniting Normandy to the crown's domains; but in 9 4
2 CONC 5 Louis was captured by the Normans, and Richard was returned to hi s p
2 CONC eople. Richard withstood further Carolingian attempts to subdue hi s du
2 CONC chy and, in 987, was instrumental in securing the French crown fo r his b
2 CONC rother-in-law, the Robertian Hugh Capet. [Encyclopaedia Britann ica CD '
2 CONC 97]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT On the ancestry of the Grenville's and parents of Mauger, Count of Cor b
2 CONC eil, their ancestor:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard I, Duke of Normandy (ggf through his eldest son of Robert II ( T
2 CONC he Devil), Duke of Normandy, who was in turn father by his mistress H a
2 CONC rlette/Harlot of William I (The Conqueror); b 933; married 2nd his fo r
2 CONC mer mistress Gunnor and died 20 Nov 996, leaving [Mauger].  [Burke' s P
2 CONC eerage, p. 1603]Richard I, byname RICHARD The FEARLESS, French RICH ARD S
2 CONC ANS PEUR (b. c. 932--d. 996), duke of Normandy (942-996), son o f Willi
2 CONC am I Longsword.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IV of France took the boy-duke into his protective custody, appa r
2 CONC ently intent upon reuniting Normandy to the crown's domains; but in 9 4
2 CONC 5 Louis was captured by the Normans, and Richard was returned to hi s p
2 CONC eople. Richard withstood further Carolingian attempts to subdue hi s du
2 CONC chy and, in 987, was instrumental in securing the French crown fo r his b
2 CONC rother-in-law, the Robertian Hugh Capet. [Encyclopaedia Britann ica CD '
2 CONC 97]
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, in a post-em, wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT Not that you probably don't already have a ton of material on him but:
2 CONT
2 CONT 942-996: Duke of Normandy [Ref: Monarchs, Rulers, Dynasties and Kingdo m
2 CONC s of the World by R.F. Tapsell 1983 p202]
2 CONT
2 CONT Title of Duke, again, was not likely generally used by Richard I or hi s s
2 CONC on Richard II, whose official documents style themselves "count o f Rou
2 CONC en."  Later eleventh century documents use the term Duke - and ad opted f
2 CONC or historical record.  [Ref: William The Conqueror, The Norma n Impact U
2 CONC pon England by David C. Douglas 1964]
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Richard I and his new settler in-laws of the 960s were the winners wh o l
2 CONC asted. In becoming so they learned (and taught) two principles of s ucc
2 CONC ess that marked them off from the Franks. They learned the value o f a s
2 CONC trong centralizing chieftain who could at least freeze the statu s quo o
2 CONC nce his own local chieftains had taken what they wanted. The mo re succ
2 CONC essful he was, the more chiefs attached themselves to him for j ust thi
2 CONC s: with his warranty, backed by his chieftains, their defeate d enemies c
2 CONC ould not recover by violence what had been taken from the m by violence
2 CONC . Thus were the Norman dukes 'settlers of quarrels.' Fear lessness was t
2 CONC he necessary quality in such a coordinating chieftain, a nd Richard I, w
2 CONC ho has no encomiast of his deeds, has at least this sob riquet, 'the Fe
2 CONC arless' Those who were great fighters and the ruthlessl y, selectively v
2 CONC iolent, were the great centralizers among the threaten ed and rapacious N
2 CONC orse.'  [Ref: Predatory Kinship and the Creation o f Norman Power, 840-
2 CONC 1066 by Eleanor Searle, University of California P ress, 1988 -Charlott
2 CONC e's Web Geneology http://www.charweb.org/gen/rjone s/d0042/g0000019.htm
2 CONC #I238]
2 CONT
2 CONT "RICHARD I, "the Fearless", Duke of Normandy, b. Fecamp ca. 933, name d f
2 CONC ather's h. 29 May 942, d. 20 Nov. 996; m. (1) (Danish wife) Gunnor , d. 1
2 CONC 027 or 1031, dau. of the forester of Arques, but betrothed ca. 9 45 & e
2 CONC vent. m. (2) 960 to Emma, d. ca. 968, dau. Hugh Capet ..., Coun t of Pa
2 CONC urs.  After Emma's death, m. (Christian marriage) Gunnor to leg it. the
2 CONC ir children. ... By Gunnor, Richard had [RICHARD II]." [Ref: We is AR7:
2 CONC 110-111]
2 CONT
2 CONT "When in 942 William was murdered at the instigation of Count Arnulf o f F
2 CONC landers, his son Richard, still a minor, succeeded him.  Louis IV a nd H
2 CONC ugh the Great each tried to sieze Normandy, and Louis took charg e of R
2 CONC ichard. He then ensconced himself at Rouen and Hugh took Bayeux , which s
2 CONC till had a Scandinavian leader called Sictric.  Richard escap ed from h
2 CONC is custody at Laon, retook Rouen, and called on another Vikin g leader, H
2 CONC arald of the Bassin, for help.  The Normans under Richard w ere able to r
2 CONC e-establish their autonomy and from 947 Richard governe d in relative p
2 CONC eace.  In 965 he swore allegiance to the Carolingian ki ng Lothar at Gi
2 CONC sors.  Richard's official marriage was to Emma, daughte r of Hugh the G
2 CONC reat; they had no children, but by his common-law wif e Gunnor, a Dane, h
2 CONC e had many.  Richard II, son of Gunnor and Richar d I, succeeded his fa
2 CONC ther in 996, another son Robert was archbishop o f Rouen from 989 to 10
2 CONC 37 and Emma their daughter became queen of Engla nd on her marriage to A
2 CONC ethelred, a position she maintained after his d eath in 1016 by marryin
2 CONC g Cnot (sic: Cnut/Knut...Curt).  Gunnor's nephe ws and other relatives f
2 CONC urthermore formed the core of the new aristocr acy which developed in t
2 CONC he course of the eleventh century.  Unfortunat ely we know little about t
2 CONC he internal organization and history of Norm andy until the reign of Ri
2 CONC chard II, and this falls outside our period. " [Ref: The Frankish Kingd
2 CONC oms under the Carolingians 751-987, by Rosam ond McKitterick, London & N
2 CONC Y (Longman) 1983 p238-239]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the minority of his (William Long-Sword) successor, Duke Richar d
2 CONC , King Louis IV -- who was making an expedition into Normandy -- wa s c
2 CONC aptured by the inhabitants of Rouen and handed over to Hugh the Gre at. F
2 CONC rom this time onwards the dukes of Normandy began to enter into r elati
2 CONC ons with the dukes of France; and in 958 Duke Richard married Hug h the G
2 CONC reat's daughter. He died in 996.  (Succeeded by Richard II.) [R ef: Gor
2 CONC don Fisher <gfisher@SHENTEL.NET> message to soc.genealogy.medie val 6 N
2 CONC ov 1996]
2 CONT
2 CONT One more minor item, ES II:11 indicates he was also buried at Fcamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT BTW,where did you get the day & month for his birth & death&#00063;  [ N
2 CONC ote: The birth date is an unsourced item I picked up on World Connect , i
2 CONC t has at least a 1/365 chance of being right.  The death date is fr om A
2 CONC R, as the source indicates (AR refers to Moriarty's Plantagenet An cest
2 CONC ry, p. 10-11, 13).  JW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regards,
2 CONT Curt
2 CONT
2 CONT ********
