2 CONT MISC:  He visited Rome and founded two abbeys:  Beaulieu-les-Loches ne a
2 CONC r Tours and St. Nicholas at Angers.  Bones were found at abbey-churc h o
2 CONC f Beaulieu-les-Loches in 1870 were probably his.  Led under escor t to J
2 CONC erusalem.  At first, however, he was not allowed to go through t he cit
2 CONC y gate where pilgrims were being forced to pay an entrance fee . But by p
2 CONC aying for himself and for the other Chddristains delayed a t the forbid
2 CONC den gate, he was able to go in with all of them.  But the n doors barre
2 CONC d them from the Lord's tomb.  Once it was known that he w as a high-bor
2 CONC n man, the guards said he could have access to the tomb b ut only, they a
2 CONC dded deceitfully, by urinating upon it and upon the hol d cross.  Fulk b
2 CONC eing a wise man, assented, albeit unwillingly.  He obt ained a ram's bl
2 CONC adder, cleansed it of impurities, filled it with the b est white wine a
2 CONC nd placed it in a convincing place between his thighs .  Then, after re
2 CONC moving his shoes, he approached the Lord's tomb, pour ed the wine over i
2 CONC t and was thus allowed to enter freely with all hi s companions.  In th
2 CONC e tomb he prayed, shedding many tears, and soon fe lt divine power when t
2 CONC he hard stone became soft.  After this, he tor e himself away from the t
2 CONC omb, which he had smothered with kisses, an d departed enraged because m
2 CONC acking and ignorant infidels had accompain ed him.  But giving generous a
2 CONC lms to the needy, he earned a piece of t he holy corss from the Syrians g
2 CONC uarding the tomb.  In the church of S t Mary the Virgin at Amboise, he p
2 CONC laced a piece of the Saviour's cros s and fragment of the thongs by whi
2 CONC ch the hands of Christ had been bou nd.  To this church, the body of St F
2 CONC lorentine was transferred in Fulk 's time from Poitou and there, too, h
2 CONC e and Sulpice, the treasurer of S t. Marin, established canons.
