1 NOTE  Parents: William the Conqueror and Mathilda of FlandersSignificant Sib l
2 CONC ings: Robert, William RufusSpouse: (1st) Eadgyth, daughter of Malcol m I
2 CONC II of Scotland; (2nd) Adelaide of LouvainSignificant Offspring: Wil lia
2 CONC m, Matilda, Robert de Mellent (Earl of Gloucester), SibyllaContempo rar
2 CONC ies: Louis VI ("Louis the Fat", King of France, 1108-1137), Roger o f S
2 CONC alisbury, Anselm (Archbishop of Canterbury), Pope Pascal IIHenry I , th
2 CONC e most resilient of the Norman kings (his reign lasted thirty-fiv e yea
2 CONC rs), was nicknamed "Beauclerc" (fine scholar) for his above avera ge ed
2 CONC ucation. During his reign, the differences between English and No rman s
2 CONC ociety began to slowly evaporate. Reforms in the royal treasur y system b
2 CONC ecame the foundation upon which later kings built. The stabi lity Henry a
2 CONC fforded the throne was offset by problems in succession: h is only surv
2 CONC iving son, William, was lost in the wreck of the White Shi p in Novembe
2 CONC r 1120.The first years of Henry's reign were concerned wit h subduing N
2 CONC ormandie. William the Conqueror divided his kingdoms betwe en Henry's o
2 CONC lder brothers, leaving England to William Rufus and Norman die to Rober
2 CONC t. Henry inherited no land but received £5000 in silver. H e played eac
2 CONC h brother off of the other during their quarrels; both dis trusted Henr
2 CONC y and subsequently signed a mutual accession treaty barrin g Henry from t
2 CONC he crown. Henry's hope arose when Robert departed for th e Holy Land on t
2 CONC he First Crusade; should William die, Henry was the ob vious heir. Henr
2 CONC y was in the woods hunting on the morning of August 2 , 1100 when Willi
2 CONC am Rufus was killed by an arrow. His quick movement i n securing the cr
2 CONC own on August 5 led many to believe he was responsibl e for his brother
2 CONC 's death. In his coronation charter, Henry denounce d William's oppress
2 CONC ive policies and promising good government in an ef fort to appease his b
2 CONC arons. Robert returned to Normandie a few weeks l ater but escaped fina
2 CONC l defeat until the Battle of Tinchebrai in 1106 ; Robert was captured a
2 CONC nd lived the remaining twenty-eight years of hi s life as Henry's priso
2 CONC ner.Henry was drawn into controversy with a rap idly expanding Church. L
2 CONC ay investiture, the king's selling of clergy a ppointments, was heavily o
2 CONC pposed by Gregorian reformers in the Churc h but was a cornerstone of N
2 CONC orman government. Henry recalled Anselm o f Bec to the archbishopric of C
2 CONC anterbury to gain baronial support, bu t the stubborn Anselm refused to d
2 CONC o homage to Henry for his lands. Th e situation remained unresolved unt
2 CONC il Pope Paschal II threatened Henr y with excommunication in 1105. He r
2 CONC eached a compromise with the papac y: Henry rescinded the king's divine a
2 CONC uthority in conferring sacred of fices but appointees continued to do h
2 CONC omage for their fiefs. In practi ce, it changed little - the king maint
2 CONC ained the deciding voice in appo inting ecclesiastical offices - but it a m
2 CONC arked a point where kingshi p became purely secular and subservient in t
2 CONC he eyes of the Church.By 1 106, both the quarrels with the church and t
2 CONC he conquest of Normandie w ere settled and Henry concentrated on expand
2 CONC ing royal power. He mixe d generosity with violence in motivating alleg
2 CONC iance to the crown and a ppointing loyal and gifted men to administrati
2 CONC ve positions. By raisin g men out of obscurity for such appointments, H
2 CONC enry began to rely les s on landed barons as ministers and created a lo
2 CONC yal bureaucracy. He wa s deeply involved in continental affairs and the
2 CONC refore spent almost ha lf of his time in Normandie, prompting him to cr
2 CONC eate the position of j usticiar - the most trusted of all the king's of
2 CONC ficials, the justicia r literally ruled in the king's stead. Roger of S
2 CONC alisbury, the first j usticiar, was instrumental in organizing an effic
2 CONC ient department for c ollection of royal revenues, the Exchequer. The E
2 CONC xchequer held session s twice a year for sheriffs and other revenue-col
2 CONC lecting officials; th ese officials appeared before the justiciar, the c
2 CONC hancellor, and sever al clerks and rendered an account of their finance
2 CONC s. The Exchequer wa s an ingenious device for balancing amounts owed ve
2 CONC rsus amounts paid . Henry gained notoriety for sending out court offici
2 CONC als to judge loca l financial disputes (weakening the feudal courts con
2 CONC trolled by loca l lords) and curb errant sheriffs (weakening the power b
2 CONC estowed upon t he sheriffs by his father).The final years of his reign w
2 CONC ere consume d in war with France and difficulties ensuring the successi
2 CONC on. The Fre nch King Louis VI began consolidating his kingdom and attac
2 CONC ked Normand ie unsuccessfully on three separate occasions. The successi
2 CONC on becam e a concern upon the death of his son William in 1120: Henry's m
2 CONC arriag e to Adelaide was fruitless, leaving his daughter Matilda as the o
2 CONC nl y surviving legitimate heir. She was recalled to Henry's court in 11
2 CONC 2 5 after the death of her husband, Emperor Henry V of Germany. Henry f
2 CONC o rced his barons to swear an oath of allegiance to Matilda in 1127 aft
2 CONC e r he arranged her marriage to the sixteen-year-old Geoffrey of Anjou t o c
2 CONC ement an Angevin alliance on the continent. The marriage, unpopula r wi
2 CONC th the Norman barons, produced a male heir in 1133, which prompte d yet a
2 CONC nother reluctant oath of loyalty from the aggravated barons. I n the su
2 CONC mmer of 1135, Geoffrey demanded custody of certain key Norma n castles a
2 CONC s a show of good will from Henry; Henry refused and the pai r entered i
2 CONC nto war. Henry's life ended in this sorry state of affair s - war with h
2 CONC is son-in-law and rebellion on the horizon - in Decembe r 1135.Third su
2 CONC rviving son of WIlliam the Conqueror.Henry had at leas t 20 illegimate c
