2 CONT Henry II
2 CONT 1154-1189
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was born in 1133 and was married to Eleanor, Duchess of
2 CONT Aquitaine immediately after her divorce from Louis VII, Kingof France.
2 CONT They had nine children: William, Henry, Matilda, Richard, Geoffrey,
2 CONT Philip, Eleanor, Joan and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was the first of fourteen hereditary kings, who were later refer e
2 CONC d to
2 CONT in the history oracles as Plantagenets. Henry was the son of the Coun t o
2 CONC f
2 CONT Anjou, whose family emblem was the 'plante genet', a yellow flowering
2 CONT broom.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was with the land bequeathed by the Count to Henry and his auspicio u
2 CONC s
2 CONT marriage to Eleanor, which gained him a vast amountof lands in France.
2 CONT These lands exceeded the lands owned in France by the King of France,
2 CONT himself. In those times, the King of France ruled from Paris andits su r
2 CONC rounding areas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry had lands reaching for 1000 miles, and it was this vast domain , w
2 CONC hich was called the Angevin Empire. Henry was the first king to dem ons
2 CONC trate that he was truly a sovereign, and he ended all the anarchy a nd d
2 CONC emonstration of strengths throughout his lands. He devoted himsel f to t
2 CONC he internal security of his land and promoted domestic and forei gn tra
2 CONC de. Productivity doubled during his reign. He revolutionised th e law s
2 CONC ystem, and even sat overcases himself. He was a notable lawyer , and he b
2 CONC uilt up the system of English Common Law, and began to devel op the tra
2 CONC ditional jury system. He was a gifted administrator.
2 CONT Henry's notable failure was his attempt to curb the power and strengt h o
2 CONC f the Church, particularly
2 CONT in the case of Thomas Beckett, who had beenhis wild pal until he was m a
2 CONC de Archbishop of Canterbury. His death did little for Henry's popular i
2 CONC ty.
2 CONT He was unfortunate in love, relentlessly and romantically pursuing th e h
2 CONC and of his wife, Eleanor,
2 CONT who became a selfish spoilt lady, and who turned her sons against thei r o
2 CONC wn father. A rebellion by the eldest son,Henry was crushed, and Ele ano
2 CONC r was placed under house arrest for fifteen years. The other brothe rs p
2 CONC laced continual pressure on their father, in alliances with the Ki ng o
2 CONC f France. Henry died a lonely and grief stricken man deserted by a ll o
2 CONC f those he had loved and honoured.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II was born at Le Mans in 1133. He was the eldest son of the Emp r
2 CONC ess Matilda, daughter of Henry I, by her second marriage to Geoffre y t
2 CONC he Fair of Anjou. His parents' marriage was tempestous, and both pa rti
2 CONC es were glad when politics brought a separation, with Matilda goin g to E
2 CONC ngland to fight King Stephen, and Geoffrey of Normandy to wi n a herita
2 CONC ge for young Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT He first came to England at the age of nine when his mother made her d r
2 CONC amatic escape from Oxford where she was besieged by Stephen, across t h
2 CONC e ice and snow, dressed all in white, to welcome him at Wallingford . H
2 CONC is next visit, when he was fourteen, showed his character: he recru ite
2 CONC d a small army of mercenaries to cross over and fight Stephen in En gla
2 CONC nd, but failed so miserably in the execution of his plans that he e nde
2 CONC d up borrowing money from Stephen to get back home. A third expedit ion
2 CONC , two years later, was almost as great a failure. Henry was not a s old
2 CONC ier, his were skills of administration and diplomacy; warfare bore d an
2 CONC d sometimes frightened him. For the meanwhile he now concentrate d on N
2 CONC ormandy, of which his father had made him joint ruler. In 1151 , the ye
2 CONC ar of his father's death, he went to Paris to do homage to Lou is VII f
2 CONC or his duchy. There he met Queen Eleanor, and she fell in lov e with hi
2 CONC m.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was by no means averse. To steal a king's wife does a great dea l f
2 CONC or the ego of a young duke; he was as lusty as she, and late in the ir l
2 CONC ives he was still ardently wenching with 'the fair Rosamund' Cliff ord,
2 CONC and less salubrious girls with names like 'Bellebelle'; finally, s he w
2 CONC ould bring with her the rich Duchy of Aquitaine, which she held i n her o
2 CONC wn right. With this territory added to those he hoped to inheri t and w
2 CONC in, his boundaries would be Scotland in the north, and the Pyre nees in t
2 CONC he south.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was, apart from his prospects, a 'catch' for any woman. He was i n
2 CONC telligent, had learned Latin and could read and possibly write; immen s
2 CONC ely strong and vigorous, a sportsman and hard rider who loved travel ; e
2 CONC motional and passionate, prone to tears and incredible rages; carel ess
2 CONC ly but richly dressed, worried enough in later life to conceal hi s bal
2 CONC dness by careful arrangement of his hair, and very concerned no t to gr
2 CONC ow fat.
2 CONT
2 CONT But now he was in the prime of youth, and in 1153, when he landed wit h a l
2 CONC arge force in Bristol, the world was ready to be won. He quickl y gaine
2 CONC d control of the West Country and moved up to Wallingford fo r a crucia
2 CONC l battle with Stephen. This was avoided, however, because i n the prepa
2 CONC rationsfor the battle Henry fell from his horse three times , a bad ome
2 CONC n. Henry himself was not superstitious -- he was the revers e, a cheerf
2 CONC ul blasphemer -- but he disliked battles and when his anxio us advisers u
2 CONC rged him to heed the omen, he willingly agreed to parle y privately wit
2 CONC h Stephen.The conference was a strange occasion: ther e were only two o
2 CONC f them there, at the narowest point of the Thames, wi th Henry on one b
2 CONC ank and Stephenon the other. None the less, they see m to have come to a
2 CONC n agreement to take negotiations further.
2 CONT
2 CONT That summer Stephen's son died mysteriously, and Eleanor bore Henry a n h
2 CONC eir (about the same time as an English whore Hikenai produced his f ait
2 CONC hful bastard Geoffrey). The omens clearly showed what was soon conf irm
2 CONC ed between the two -- that when Stephen died, Henry should rule i n his p
2 CONC lace. A year later Stephen did die, and in December 1154, Henr y and El
2 CONC eanor were crowned in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was only 21, but he soon showed his worth, destroying unlicense d c
2 CONC astles, and dispersing the foreign mercenaries. He gave even-hande d ju
2 CONC stice, showing himself firm, but not unduly harsh. A country racke d by c
2 CONC ivil war sighed with relief. Only two major difficulties appeare d: fir
2 CONC st Henry's failure in his two Welsh campaigns in 1157 and 1165 , when g
2 CONC uerilla tactics utterly defeated and on the first occasion nea rly kill
2 CONC ed him; second was the reversal of his friendship for Becket
