1 NOTE  Name Prefix:<NPFX> King
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8XJ2-7B
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the call of Pope Eugnius IV for a crusade, at Christmas tim e 1
2 CONC 145, the French king, Louis VII, revealed to his courtiers his desi gns t
2 CONC o go to the aid of the Latins in the East. The King met,however , with c
2 CONC onsiderable opposition from his advisors, who believed that th e welfar
2 CONC e of the kingdom required that the King remain at home. It wa s agreed, t
2 CONC herefore, to defer any action on the project until the foll owing Easte
2 CONC r. In the meantime, the King sought the advice of the power ful and ren
2 CONC owned Bernard of Clairvaux, who agreed to preach on behal f of the Crus
2 CONC ade to the King's court during Easter time at Vezelay . A German army u
2 CONC nder Conrad II set out through Hungary, but met a dis astrous end at th
2 CONC e hands of the Slejuqs in Anatolia. While the German s were marching he
2 CONC edlessly toward defeat, the French army, led by Kin g Louis VII, was fo
2 CONC llowing in their tracks, about a month behind. Th e story of their jour
2 CONC ney is related by the French King's chaplain, Od o of Deuil. The French f
2 CONC orces arrived at Constantinople on October 4 , 1147. There they were bo
2 CONC th impressed by the splendor of the city an d alarmed by the suspicious a
2 CONC ctions of the Greeks.  The French force s crossed the straits into Asia M
2 CONC inor about October 16, 1147, and the n headed straightway into the hint
2 CONC erland of Anatolia or, as Odo call s it, Romania. Though they were more f
2 CONC ortunate than the other forces w hich had preceded them into Anatolia, t
2 CONC he French expedition's journe y through the peninsula was difficult, sl
2 CONC ow, and painful. The rugged c ountryside, the continual harassment of t
2 CONC he troops by the Turks, the p ersistent difficulties with supplies and c
2 CONC ommunications, all combine d to discourage the leaders and to make inro
2 CONC ads upon the army's streng th. As the French forces pushed further duri
2 CONC ng the winter of 1147-1148 , their despair deepened. Turkish raids took a m
2 CONC ounting toll, while th e weather impeded progress and did its own share i
2 CONC n weakening the mora le of the men. By the time the Crusaders reached A
2 CONC dalia, King Louis an d his advisors had had their fill. Despairing of t
2 CONC he prospect of conti nuing to fight their way toward Jerusalem, the Kin
2 CONC g and his advisors d ecided to continue the rest of the way by sea. Unf
2 CONC ortunately for thes e plans, however, the available Byzantine shipping w
2 CONC as insufficient t o transport the whole army and they could not wait in
2 CONC definitely in Ada lia for the arrival of further ships.As a result, Kin
2 CONC g Louis with hi s household and a scattering of knights from the army w
2 CONC ere taken aboar d the available ships and sailed to St. Simeon, the por
2 CONC t city of Antio ch, leaving the rest of the Crusading army to continue t
2 CONC he journey a s best it could. Many of the troops thus left behind at Ad
2 CONC alia were ki lled in combat with the Turks in the vicinity of the town w
2 CONC hen they at tempted to continue their journey by land. Those who manage
2 CONC d to brea k through the Turkish cord on around the city were decimated b
2 CONC y furthe r Turkish and Arab attacks and only a handful remained alive t
2 CONC o comple te their journey t
2 CONT o Jerusalem.
