2 CONT King Edward III
2 CONT Born at Windsor Castle on 13 November 1312 at 5.40 a.m., the very firs t E
2 CONC nglish King to have the exact time of his birth noted. He succeede d hi
2 CONC s father, KingEdward II , who was deposed on 20 January 1327. Th e coro
2 CONC nation took place at Westminster Abbey on 2 February. During hi s reign
2 CONC , the parliament was divided into two houses which still exis t today, t
2 CONC he office of "Justice of Peace" was created, and the Frenc h language w
2 CONC as replaced by the English in the law courts. In1348, he f ounded the "
2 CONC Order of the Garter", still one of the most prestigiousord ers of chiva
2 CONC lry in Europe. On 21 June 1377, he died following a strok e at Sheen Pa
2 CONC lace.
2 CONT Below is a part of a document (1350); the final line is attributed t o t
2 CONC he King. This is the oldest surviving example of a sovereign s auto gra
2 CONC ph(not signature).
2 CONT Father: Edward II <edwardii.htm> of Caernarvon, King of EnglandMother : d
2 CONC e France, IsabellaAcceded: 1 February 1327, Westminster Abbey, Lond on, E
2 CONC nglandDied: 21 June 1377, Sheen Palace, SurreyInterred: Westminst er Ab
2 CONC bey, London, England
2 CONT Reigned 1327-1377. Edward assumed effective power in 1330 after impris o
2 CONC ning his mother and executing her lover Roger de Mortimer who had mur d
2 CONC ered his father; thereafter his reign was dominated by military adven t
2 CONC ures. His victory in Scotland, especially at Haildon Hill 1333 encour a
2 CONC ged him to plan (1363) the union of England and Scotland. Through hi s m
2 CONC other he claimed theFrench throne thus starting (1337) the Hundre d yea
2 CONC rs war. His son John of Gaunt dominated the government during hi s last y
2 CONC ears. Died of a Stroke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Custom Field:<_FA#> 1327Became King of England
