1 NOTE  Name Suffix:<NSFX> III
2 CONT Name Prefix:<NPFX> King
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8XJ5-ZJ
2 CONT [janet skelton.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III, who succeeded [his father, King John]when he was only niney e
2 CONC ars old,...played a large part in an important event connected with t h
2 CONC ehistory of Lewes which had a far-reaching effect on the developmen t o
2 CONC fEngland.
2 CONT King Henry appears to have been a man of artistic tastes as well aspos s
2 CONC essing a fondness for magnificence. For years during his reign therew a
2 CONC smisgovernment, for Henry had no sense of political justice. Hisextra v
2 CONC agance, coupled with several bad harvests, brought grave discontentan d h
2 CONC astened mattersto a climax, and to a conflict between King andbaron s. S
2 CONC imon de Montfort, Dukeof Leicester, a nephew of the King,became th eir l
2 CONC eader, and in the spring of 1264 gather together a force of15,00 0 men a
2 CONC nd began to march to the relief of theCinque Ports on thecoas t of Suss
2 CONC ex, which the King was threatening.
2 CONT The Kingand his army arrived in the neighbourhood of Lewes in May. DeM o
2 CONC ntfort's men hadalso entered the county and were encamped at thevilla g
2 CONC e of Fletching in the Weald, eight miles away. The King was stayingwi t
2 CONC h his brother,... known as the King of the Romans, at the Priory ofSt . P
2 CONC ancras, at Southover. His son, Prince Edward, was the guest of John de W
2 CONC arenne, Earl of Surrey, at Lewes Castle....
2 CONT Simon de Montfort seems to have made a last-minute attempt to come tot e
2 CONC rms with the King...but Henry refused to listen [to the bishops thatw e
2 CONC re sent to him]. [Onthe break of day, Wednesday, 13 May 1264, deMontf o
2 CONC rt's men set out for field of battle, on a long march. They hadappare n
2 CONC tly spent the night before in prayer.But the King and his companyha d s
2 CONC pent the night in feasting at the Priory andwere] in such a livelya nd r
2 CONC iotous state that the Prior and his monks were shocked. [The Kingw as s
2 CONC urprised that the other force was so near, and hastily collected h isfo
2 CONC rces to meet it.] The King himself led the centre of his army wit h his
2 CONC banner bearing a white dragon fluttering in the breeze, giving a s hisb
2 CONC attlecry,'Symon, je vous defie'.
2 CONT The clash between the two armies took place on MountHarry, which is no t
2 CONC far from the present racecourse. The name of that point of the downs , i
2 CONC s,however, believed to have no connexion with the king. [At first t heb
2 CONC attle went in the King's favour, and Prince Edward, commanding th e arm
2 CONC yon theleft, drove some of de Montfort's men down the hillside t o theW
2 CONC eald by Offham,probably close to where there is now a disused ch alkpit
2 CONC . So sure was he that the day was theirs, that he followed there treati
2 CONC ng men for miles, eventually returning to the top of the downs t ofind t
2 CONC hat the King's army had been beaten andhis soldiers were fleein gin all d
2 CONC irections.]
2 CONT Simon de Montfort strangely enough brought along with his army threeun f
2 CONC ortunate aldermen of London who had recenty shut the drawbridge ofLon d
2 CONC on Bridge in his face. [He imprisoned them in a kind of coachprotecte d w
2 CONC ith metal plates. The Kings' men had not been able todistinguish d e Mo
2 CONC ntfort himself during the battle; but thought the coachprobably co ntai
2 CONC ned him. They thrust spears through the iron grating atthe side an d th
2 CONC e aldermen were killed. The story used to be told thatKing Henry s urre
2 CONC ndered to Simon de Montfort on the field of battle, butthe King ac tual
2 CONC ly fell back to the Priory and fortified himself and someof his me n in
2 CONC side. His brother, King of the Romans, also fled and oldballads sa y he t
2 CONC ook refuge in a windmill, where he wasafterwardsdiscovered and t aken p
2 CONC risoner. But all mills in Henry's day werewatermills. The mill i n ques
2 CONC tion may have been a very small watermillknown to have stood o n the ba
2 CONC nks of the Winterbourne in Southover.]
2 CONT [The beaten army was pursued through Lewes and there was fighting in t h
2 CONC enarrow streets, continuing downthe slopes through the East Gate an d o
2 CONC nto the narrow bridge over the river, where there was a frightful m ele
2 CONC e,and on toward the marshes beyond where many menwere lost. 5,000 m en w
2 CONC eresaid to have perished in
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III was born in 1207 and succeeded his father John on the thron e o
2 CONC f England in 1216. It was a ravaged inheritance, the scene of civi l wa
2 CONC r and anarchy, and much of the east and south eastern England wa s unde
2 CONC r the control of the French Dauphin Louis. But Henry had two gre at pro
2 CONC tectors---his liege lord the Pope, and the aged William Marshal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marshal, by a combination of military skill and diplomatic ability ,
2 CONC saw off the Dauphin by September, 1217, but less than two years late r h
2 CONC e was dead, and a triumvirate ruled in his place: the papal legal P and
2 CONC ulf; the Poitevin Bishop of Winchester Peter des Roches; and the Ju sti
2 CONC ciar Hubert de Burgh. The legate departed in 1221; two years late r Hen
2 CONC ry became of age and, rejecting Peter, chose Hubert to be his chi ef co
2 CONC unsellor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trouble soon came, as Hubert attempted to re-asert royal authority. Ba r
2 CONC ons, who had kept their castles undistrubed and exercised their power s w
2 CONC ithout supervison, were now called to account to the haughty justic iar
2 CONC , and the party of Peter des Roches did not fail to underline the a nno
2 CONC yances involved. The years 1223-4 were taken up with quelling rebel lio
2 CONC ns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile the situation abroad was even more disturbing: the French ki n
2 CONC g Philip Augustus was eating up English lands in Gascony, and Henry' s m
2 CONC other Isabella made a bad situation worse by her marriage with Coun t H
2 CONC ugh of Lusignan. It was only in 1230 that a badly prepared Englis h for
2 CONC ce setout for France and, after much squabbling, all it was abl e to do w
2 CONC as makea demonstration march through Gascony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hubert had already had one dismal failure in Wales in 1228, and his ar r
2 CONC ogant attempts to build up a personal base in the Marches provoke d a W
2 CONC elsh raid in 1231 which did more harm to his good name. Hubert wa s thr
2 CONC ust out of power, to be replaced by Peter des Roches' Poitevins . But b
2 CONC y 1234 they had upset the baronage of England, who had never ta ken kin
2 CONC dly to foreigners other than the Normans, and Richard Marshal c ombined w
2 CONC ith Edmund of Abington, Archbishop of Canterbury, to force th e King to r
2 CONC eplace them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry now began his period of personal rule, and the world was to se e w
2 CONC hat sort of king he would make. He was a simple, direct man, trustf ul o
2 CONC n first impression, but bearing a life-long grudge when people le t him d
2 CONC own. At times lavish and life-loving, he could show another sid e of hi
2 CONC s nature, that wicked Angevin temper and streak of vindictive c ruelty. H
2 CONC ehad a very refined taste, and enjoyed building and restorati on work m
2 CONC ore than anything else. Surrounded by barons who had been pro ved in th
2 CONC e hardest schools of war, the King had the spirit of an inter ior decor
2 CONC ator; the nation could have born the expense of his artisti c tastes, c
2 CONC ould have forgiven the eccentricity of it all, but Henry sh owed time a
2 CONC nd again that he was timorous as well as artistic. He feare d thunderst
2 CONC orms, and battle was beyond him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Crown had some 60 castles in England, and these were in a bad stat e a
2 CONC fter the troubles of John's reign and the minority. Henry travelle d ab
2 CONC out tirelessly rebuilding them and making them more comfortable, s pend
2 CONC ing at least ten percent of his income on building works. He perso nall
2 CONC y instructed his architects in great detail, and could not wait fo r th
2 CONC em to finish---it must be ready for his return 'even if a thousan d wor
2 CONC kmen are required every day' and the job must be 'properly done , beaut
2 CONC iful and fine.' In addition he built or restored twenty royal h ouses, d
2 CONC ecorating them sumptiously. The painted chamber at Westminste r was 80 f
2 CONC t. long, 26ft. wide, and 31 ft. high. The walls were all wai nscotted (
2 CONC at Winchestereven the pantry and cellar were wainscotted) an d painted w
2 CONC ith pictures and proverbs. The subjects of the pictures var ied accordi
2 CONC ng to the royal moods---in May 1250 the Queen borrowed a bo ok about th
2 CONC e crusades, and a year later the walls at Clarendon showe d Richard the L
2 CONC ion heart duelling with Saladin. Wherever there were n o pictures, ther
2 CONC e was the King's favourite
