2 CONT Count of Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT FHL The Magna Charta Barons, pg 204
2 CONT
2 CONT !Sources: Norr, Kraentzler 1146, 1333; AF; Ancestral Roots 83, 123. Ro o
2 CONC ts: Hamelin Plantagenet, Earl of Surrey, natural son of Geoffrey V Pl a
2 CONC ntagenet. K: Hamel(in) Plantagenet, Earl of Surrey. Natural son. Norr : H
2 CONC ameline (of Warenne after marriage) 5th Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hameline Plantagenet, natural brother to King Henry II, likewiseobtain e
2 CONC d, jure uxoris, the Earldom of Surrey, and assumed the surname andarm s o
2 CONC f de Warren. This nobleman bore one of the three swords at thesecon d c
2 CONC oronation of Richard I, and in the 6th of the same reign [1195],h e was w
2 CONC ith that king in his army in Normandy. Hed. 7 May, 1202, fourye ars aft
2 CONC er the countess, having had issue, William, Adela, Maud, anothe rdau. w
2 CONC ho m. Gilbert de Aquila, Isabel, and Margaret. [Sir Bernard Bur ke,Dorm
2 CONC ant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage , Ltd.,L
2 CONC ondon, 1883, p. 569, Warren, Earls of Surrey]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT EARLDOM OF SURREY (V)
2 CONT
2 CONT HAMELIN illegitimate son of GEOFFREY V, styled "PLANTAGENET," COUNT OF A
2 CONC NJOU, TOURAINE and MAINE, sometime DUKE OF NORMANDY, by an unknownwom a
2 CONC n, held lands in Touraine, presumably the gift of his half-brotherHen r
2 CONC y II, and appears to have been styled vicomte of Touraine. He becameE A
2 CONC RL OF SURREY in consequenceof his marriage to the Countess Isabel in1 1
2 CONC 64; in which year he attended the Council of Northampton. In 1166 her e
2 CONC turned his carta, as holding 60 knights' fees.He supported the Kingag a
2 CONC inst his rebellious sons in 1173; and in 1176 he was one of the noble s
2 CONC who escorted Henry's daughter Joan for her marriage to the King ofSic i
2 CONC ly. He was present at the 1st Coronation of Richard I, 3 September118 9
2 CONC . In the King's absence he supported the Chancellor against John; in1 1
2 CONC 93he was one of the Treasurers for the King's ransom; and in 1194 hew a
2 CONC s at the Council of Nottingham. At Richard's 2nd Coronation, 17 April 1
2 CONC 194, he bore one of the 3 swords; and he was present at the Coronatio n
2 CONC of King John, 27 May 1199. He was a benefactor, or confirmed previous g
2 CONC ifts, to the abbeys of St. Mary's(York), St. Victor-en-Caux, WestDere h
2 CONC am and Foucarmont, the priories of Lewes, Nostell, St. Katherine(Linc o
2 CONC ln), Southwark, Thetford and Castle Acre, the Hospitallers, and thech a
2 CONC pel of St. Philip and St. James in Conisborough Castle. He married, i n
2 CONC 1164 (probably in April), Isabel, widow of William (OF BLOIS) 4th EAR L
2 CONC OFSURREY, and only daughter and heir of William (DE WARENNE), 3rd EAR L O
2 CONC FSURREY, all above-named. He died 7 May 1202 and was buried in th e Cha
2 CONC pterHouse atLewes. Isabel survived him and made gifts for the sou l of h
2 CONC erlate husband to the priory of St. Katherine, Lincoln, and to L ewesPr
2 CONC iory. She was livingin April 1203 but died probably soon afterwa rds,po
2 CONC ssibly 12 July 1203, and was buried in the Chapter House at Lewe s (g).
