2 CONT Moderately popular. He was succeeded by his niece VictoriaWilliam I, W i
2 CONC lliam the Conqueror (c.1028 - 1087)William I After successfully invad i
2 CONC ng England, William the Conqueror changed the course of English histo r
2 CONC y. The illegitimate son of Robert I of Normandie, William became Duk e o
2 CONC f Normandie on his father's death in 1035. With many in his famil y eag
2 CONC er to profit from his death, his childhood was dangerous: three o f his g
2 CONC uardians died violently and his tutor was murdered.In 1042 he b egan to t
2 CONC ake more personal control, but his attempts to bring his subj ects into l
2 CONC ine caused problems. From 1046 until 1055 he dealt with a s eries of ba
2 CONC ronial rebellions. In 1052, facing rebellions in eastern No rmandie, he b
2 CONC egan conducting negotiations with his cousin Edward the C onfessor, Kin
2 CONC g of England. William developed an ambition to become hi s kinsman's he
2 CONC ir, encouraged by Edward, who possibly even promised hi m the throne in 1
2 CONC 051.In around 1064 Edward sent Harold, Earl of Wessex , on an embassy t
2 CONC o Normandie. During this trip Norman writers maintai n that he swore to s
2 CONC upport William's claim to the English throne. Ye t when Edward died chi
2 CONC ldless in January 1066, Harold was himself crown ed king. Furious, Will
2 CONC iam decided on war. He landed in England on 28t h September, establishi
2 CONC ng a bridgehead near Hastings.Harold met him fr om Stamford Bridge, whe
2 CONC re he had just defeated Harald Hardraade. He ar rived at Hastings late o
2 CONC n 13th October, his troops tired. Early the ne xt day William attacked. A
2 CONC fter a poor start, he rallied his troops. Ha rold's brothers were kille
2 CONC d early in the battle; Harold fell towards d usk. On Christmas Day 1066 W
2 CONC illiam was crowned.The first years of hi s reign were spent quashing re
2 CONC bellions and securing his borders. He in vaded Scotland in 1072 and Wal
2 CONC es in 1081 and created special defensiv e 'marcher' counties along the b
2 CONC orders. The kingdom safe, he spent mos t of his last 15 years in Norman
2 CONC die. He left the government of Englan d to bishops, returning only when a
2 CONC bsolutely necessary. While in Engla nd to face a threatened Danish inva
2 CONC sion, in 1086 he ordered a survey t o be made of the kingdom: this was t
2 CONC o be Domesday Book.Abroad, Willia m was threatened by an alliance of Ph
2 CONC ilip I of France and William's so n, Robert Curthose. In July 1087, whi
2 CONC le in Mantes, he was mortally wou nded. He spent five weeks dying, atte
2 CONC nded by his younger sons, Willia m Rufus and Henry. Robert was with Phi
2 CONC lip and William's anger resulte d in his splitting his inheritance. In l
2 CONC ine with custom, Robert receiv ed William's French lands and titles, bu
2 CONC t England went to William Rufu s.[FAVthomas.FTW]Byname William The Conq
2 CONC ueror, or The Bastard, or Will iam Normandie, French Guillaume Le Conqu
2 CONC érant, or Le Bâtard, or Guilla ume De Normandie duke of Normandie (as W
2 CONC illiam II) from 1035 and kin g of England from 1066, one of the greates
2 CONC t soldiers and rulers of th e Middle Ages. He made himself the mighties
2 CONC t feudal lord in France an d then changed the course of England's histo
2 CONC ry by his conquest of tha t country.William was the elder of two childr
2 CONC en of Robert I of Normand ie and his concubine Herleva, or Arlette, the d
2 CONC aughter of a burgher fr om the town of Falaise. In 1035 Robert died whe
2 CONC n returning from a pilg rimage to Jerusalem, and William, his only son, w
2 CONC hom he had nominate d as his heir before his departure, was accepted as d
2 CONC uke by the Norma n magnates and his feudal overlord, King Henry I of Fr
2 CONC ance. William an d his friends had to overcome enormous obstacles. His i
2 CONC llegitimacy (h e was generally known as the Bastard) was a handicap, an
2 CONC d he had to su rvive the collapse of law and orderthat accompanied his a
2 CONC ccession a s a child.Three of William's guardians died violent deaths b
2 CONC efore he g rew up, and his tutor was murdered. His father's kin were of l
2 CONC ittle he lp; most of them thought that they stood to gain by the boy's d
2 CONC eath. B ut his mother managed to protect William through the most dange
2 CONC rous pe riod. These early difficulties probably contributed to his stre
2 CONC ngth o f purpose and his dislike of lawlessness and misrule.By 1042, wh
2 CONC en Wil liam reached his 15th year, was knighted, and began to play a pe
2 CONC rsona l part in the affairs of his duchy, the worst was over. But his a
2 CONC ttemp ts to recover rights lost during the anarchy and to bring disobed
2 CONC ien t vassals and servants to heel inevitably led to trouble. From 1046 u
2 CONC n til 1055 he dealt with a series of baronial rebellions, mostly led b y k
2 CONC insmen. Occasionally he was in great danger and had to rely on Henr y o
2 CONC f France for help. In 1047 Henry and William defeated a coalition o f N
2 CONC ormanrebels at Val-ès-Dunes, southeast of Caen. It was in these yea rs t
2 CONC hat William learned to fight and rule.William soon learned to cont rol h
2 CONC is youthful recklessness. He was always ready to take calculate d risks o
2 CONC n campaign and, most important, to fight a battle. But he wa s not a ch
2 CONC ivalrous or flamboyant commander. His plans were simple, hi s methods d
2 CONC irect, and he exploited ruthlessly any advantage gained. I f he found h
2 CONC imself at a disadvantage, he withdrew immediately. He show ed the same q
2 CONC ualities in his government. He never lost sight of his ai m to recover l
2 CONC ost ducal rights and revenues, and, although he develope d no theory of g
2 CONC overnment or great interest in administrative techniqu es, hewas always p
2 CONC repared to improvise and experiment. He seems to hav e lived a moral li
2 CONC fe by the standards of the time, and he acquired a n interest in the we
2 CONC lfare of the Norman church. He made his half broth er, Odo, bishop of B
2 CONC ayeux in 1049 at the age of about 16, and Odo mana ged to combine the r
2 CONC oles of nobleman and prelate in a way that did no t greatly shock conte
2 CONC mporaries. But William also welcomed foreign monk s and scholars to Nor
2 CONC mandie. Lanfranc of Pavia, a famous master of th e liberal arts, whoent
2 CONC ered the monastery of Bec about 1042, was made a bbot of Caen in 1063.A
2 CONC ccording to a brief description of William's per son by an anonymous au
2 CONC thor, who borrowed extensively from Einhard's Li fe of Charlemagne, he w
2 CONC as just above average height and had a robust , thick-set body. Though h
2 CONC e was always sparing of food and drink, he b ecame fat in later life. H
2 CONC e had a rough bass voice and was a good an d ready speaker. Writers of t
2 CONC he next generation agree that he was exce ptionally strong and vigorous
2 CONC . William was an out-of-doors man, a hunt er and soldier, fierce and de
2 CONC spotic, generally feared; uneducated, h e had few graces but was intell
2 CONC igent and shrewd and soon obtained th e respect of his rivals.After 104
2 CONC 7 William began to take part in event s outside his duchy. In support o
2 CONC f his lord, King Henry, and in pursui t of an ambition to strengthen hi
2 CONC s southern frontier and expand into M aine, he fought a series of campa
2 CONC igns against Geoffrey Martel, count o f Anjou. But in 1052 Henry and Ge
2 CONC offrey made peace, there was a seriou s rebellion in eastern Normandie, a
2 CONC nd, until 1054 William was again i n serious danger. During this period h
2 CONC e conducted important negotiatio ns with his cousin Edward the Confesso
2 CONC r, king of England, and took a w ife.Norman interest in Anglo-Saxon Eng
2 CONC land derived from an alliance ma de in 1002, when King Ethelred II of E
2 CONC ngland married Emma, the siste r of Count Richard II, William's grandfa
2 CONC ther. Two of her sons, William 's cousins once removed, had reigned in t
2 CONC urn in England, Hardecanute ( 104042) and Edward the Confessor (10426
2 CONC 6). William had met Edward du ring that prince's exile on the Continent a
2 CONC nd may well have given hi m some support when he returned to England in 1
2 CONC 041. In that year Edwar d was about 36 and William 14. It is clear that W
2 CONC illiam expected som e sort of reward from Edward and, when Edward's mar
2 CONC riage proved unfrui tful, began to develop an ambition to become his ki
2 CONC nsman's heir. Edwar d probably at times encouraged William's hopes. His c
2 CONC hildlessness wa s a diplomatic asset.In 1049 William negotiated with Ba
2 CONC ldwin V of Flan ders for the hand of his daughter, Matilda. Baldwin, an i
2 CONC mperial vassa l with a distinguished lineage, was in rebellion against t
2 CONC he Western e mperor, Henry III, and in desperate need of allies. The pr
2 CONC oposed marri age was condemned as incestuous (William and Matilda were e
2 CONC vidently re lated in some way) by the Emperor's friend, Pope Leo IX, at t
2 CONC he Counci l of Reims in October 1049; but so anxious were the parties f
2 CONC or the al liance that before the end of 1053, possibly in 1052, the wed
2 CONC ding too k place. In 1059 William was reconciled to the papacy, and as p
2 CONC enanc e the disobedient pair built two monasteries at Caen. Four sons w
2 CONC ere b orn to William and Matilda: Robert (the future duke of Normandie)
2 CONC , Ric hard (who died young), William Rufus (the Conqueror's successor i
2 CONC n Eng land), and Henry (Rufus' successor). Among the daughters was Adel
2 CONC a, wh o was the mother of Stephen, king of England.Edward the Confessor w
2 CONC a s supporting the Emperor, and it is possible that William used his ne w a
2 CONC lliance with Flanders to put pressure on Edward and extort an ackno wle
2 CONC dgment that he was the English king's heir. At all events, Edward s eem
2 CONC s to have made some sort of promise to William in 1051, while Tosti g, s
2 CONC on of the greatest nobleman in England, Earl Godwine, married Bald win'
2 CONC s half sister. The immediate purpose of this tripartite alliance w as t
2 CONC o improve the security of each of the parties. IfWilliam secure d a dec
2 CONC laration that he was Edward's heir, he was also looking very fa r ahead
2 CONC .Between 1054 and 1060 William held his own against an allianc e betwee
2 CONC n King Henry I and Geoffrey Martel of Anjou. Both men died i n 1060 and w
2 CONC ere succeeded by weaker rulers. As a result, in 1063 Willi am was able t
2 CONC o conquer Maine. In 1064 or 1065 Edward sent his brother- in-law, Harol
2 CONC d, earl of Wessex, Godwine's son and successor, on an emb assy to Norma
2 CONC ndie. William took him on a campaign into Brittany, and i n connection w
2 CONC ith this Harold swore an oath in which, according to Nor man writers, h
2 CONC erenewed Edward's bequest of the throne to William and p romised to sup
2 CONC port it.When Edward died childless on Jan. 5, 1066, Haro ld was accepte
2 CONC d as king by the English magnates, and William decided o n war. Others, h
2 CONC owever, moved more quickly. In May Tostig, Harold's ex iled brother, ra
2 CONC ided England, and in September he joined the invasio n forces of Harald I
2 CONC II Hardraade, king of Norway, off the Northumbria n coast. William asse
2 CONC mbled a fleet, recruited an army, and gathered hi s forces in August at t
2 CONC he mouth of the Dives River. It is likely tha t he originally intended t
2 CONC o sail due north and invade England by way o f the Isle of Wight and So
2 CONC uthampton Water. Such a plan would give hi m an offshore base and inter
2 CONC ior lines. But adverse winds detained hi s fleet in harbour for a month
2 CONC , and in September a westerly gale drov e his ships up-Channel.William r
2 CONC egrouped his forces at Saint-Valéry o n the Somme. He had suffered a co
2 CONC stly delay, some naval losses, an d a drop in the morale of his troops. O
2 CONC n September 27, after cold an d rainy weather, the wind backed south. W
2 CONC illiam embarked his army an d set sail for the southeast coast of Engla
2 CONC nd. The following morning h e landed, took the unresisting towns of Pev
2 CONC ensey and Hastings, and beg an to organize a bridgehead with between 4,
2 CONC 000 and 7,000 cavalry and i nfantry.William's forces were in a narrow c
2 CONC oastal strip, hemmed in b y the great forest of Andred, and, although t
2 CONC his corridor was easily d efensible, it was not much of a base for the c
2 CONC onquest of England. Th e campaigning season was almost past, and when W
2 CONC illiam received news o f his opponent it was not reassuring. On Septemb
2 CONC er 25 Harold had defea ted and slain Tostig and Harald Hardraade at Sta
2 CONC mford Bridge, near Yor k, and was retracing his steps to meet the new i
2 CONC nvader. On October 13 , when Harold emerged from the forest, William wa
2 CONC s taken by surprise . But the hour was too late for Harold to push on t
2 CONC o Hastings, and h e took up a defensive position. Early the next day Wi
2 CONC lliam went out t o give battle. He attacked the English phalanx with ar
2 CONC chers and cavalr y but saw his army almost driven from the field. He ra
2 CONC llied the fugiti ves, however, and brought them back into the fight and i
2 CONC n the end wor e down his opponents. Harold's brothers were killed early i
2 CONC n the battl e. Toward nightfall the King himself fell and the English g
2 CONC ave up. Wil liam's coolness and tenacity secured him victory in this fa
2 CONC teful battl e, and he then moved against possible centres of resistance s
2 CONC o quickl y that he prevented a new leader from emerging. On Christmas D
2 CONC ay 106 6 he was crowned king in Westminster Abbey. In a formal sense th
2 CONC e Norm an Conquest of England had taken place.William was already an ex
2 CONC perien ced ruler. In Normandie he had replaced disloyal nobles and duca
2 CONC l serv ants with his own friends, limited private warfare, and recovere
2 CONC d usur ped ducal rights, defining the feudal duties of his vassals. The N
2 CONC orma n church flourished under his rule. He wanted a church free of cor
2 CONC rupt ion but subordinate to him. He would not tolerate opposition from b
2 CONC ish ops and abbots or interference from the papacy. He presided over ch
2 CONC urc h synods and reinforced ecclesiastical discipline with his own. In s
2 CONC up porting Lanfranc, prior of Bec, against Berengar of Tours in their d
2 CONC is puteover the doctrine of the Eucharist, he found himself on the side o f o
2 CONC rthodoxy. He was never guilty of the selling of church office (simo ny)
2 CONC . He disapproved of clerical marriage. At the same time he was a st ern a
2 CONC nd sometimes rough master, swayed by political necessities, and h e was n
2 CONC ot generous to the church with his own property. The reformer L anfranc w
2 CONC as one of his advisers; but perhaps even more to his taste we re the wo
2 CONC rldly and soldierly bishops Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey of Cou tances.Wi
2 CONC lliam left England early in 1067 but had to return in Decembe r because o
2 CONC f English unrest. The English rebellions that began in 106 7 reached th
2 CONC eir peak in 1069 and were finally quelled in 1071. They co mpleted the r
2 CONC uin of the highest English aristocracy and gave Willia m a distaste for h
2 CONC is newly conquered kingdom. Since his position on th e Continent was de
2 CONC teriorating, he wanted to solve English problems a s cheaply as possibl
2 CONC e. To secure England's frontiers, he invaded Scotl and in 1072 and Wale
2 CONC s in 1081 and created special defensive marcher co unties along the Sco
2 CONC ttish and Welsh borders.In the last 15 years of hi s life he was more o
2 CONC ften in Normandie than in England, and there wer e five years, possibly s
2 CONC even, in which he did not visit the kingdom a t all. He retained most o
2 CONC f the greatest Anglo-Norman barons with him i n Normandie and confided t
2 CONC he government of England to bishops, trustin g especially his old frien
2 CONC d Lanfranc, whom he made archbishop of Cante rbury. Much concerned that t
2 CONC he natives should not be unnecessarily dis turbed, he allowed them to r
2 CONC etain their own laws and courts.William re turned to England only when i
2 CONC t was absolutely necessary: in 1075 to de al with the aftermath of a re
2 CONC bellion by Roger, earl of Hereford, and R alf, earl of Norfolk, which w
2 CONC as made more dangerous by the interventio n of a Danish fleet; and in 1
2 CONC 082 to arrest and imprison his half broth er Odo, bishop of Bayeux and e
2 CONC arl of Kent, who was planning to take a n army to Italy, perhaps to mak
2 CONC e himself pope. In the spring of 1082 W illiam had his son Henry knight
2 CONC ed, and in August at Salisbury he too k oaths of fealty from all the im
2 CONC portant landowners in England, whosoe ver's vassals they might be. In 1
2 CONC 085 he returned with a large army t o meet the threat of an invasion by C
2 CONC anute IV (Canute the Holy) of Den mark. When this came to nothing owing t
2 CONC o Canute's death in 1086, Willi am ordered an economic and tenurial sur
2 CONC vey to be made of the kingdom , the results of which are summarized in t
2 CONC he two volumes of Domesday B ook.William was preoccupied with the front
2 CONC iers of Normandie. The dange r spots were in Maine and the Vexin on the S
2 CONC eine, where Normandie bord ered on the French royal demesne. After 1066 W
2 CONC illiam's continental nei ghbours became more powerful and even more hos
2 CONC tile. In 1068 Fulk the S urly succeeded to Anjou and in 1071 Robert the F
2 CONC risian to Flanders. Ph ilip I of France allied with Robert and Robert w
2 CONC ith the Danish king, C anute IV. There was also the problem of William'
2 CONC s heir apparent, Rober t Curthose, who, given no appanage and seemingly k
2 CONC ept short of money , left Normandie in 1077 and intrigued with his fath
2 CONC er's enemies. In 1 081 William made a compromise with Fulk in the treat
2 CONC y of Blancheland : Robert Curthose was to be count of Maine but as a va
2 CONC ssal of the coun t of Anjou. The eastern part of the Vexin, the county o
2 CONC f Mantes, had f allen completely into King Philip's hands in 1077 when W
2 CONC illiam had bee n busy with Maine. In 1087 William demanded from Philip t
2 CONC he return o f the towns of Chaumont, Mantes,and Pontoise. In July he en
2 CONC tered Mante s by surprise, but while the town burned he suffered some i
2 CONC njury fro m which he never recovered. He was thwarted at the very momen
2 CONC t when h e seemed about to enforce his last outstanding territorial cla
2 CONC im.Willi am was taken to a suburb of Rouen, where he lay dying for five w
2 CONC eeks . He had theassistance of some of his bishops and doctors, and in a
2 CONC tte ndance were his half brother Robert, count of Mortain, and his youn
2 CONC ge r sons, William Rufus and Henry. Robert Curthose was with the King o f F
2 CONC rance. It had probably been his intention that Robert, as was the c ust
2 CONC om, should succeed to the whole inheritance. In the circumstances h e w
2 CONC as tempted to make the loyal Rufus his sole heir. In the end he com pro
2 CONC mised: Normandie and Maine went to Robert and England to Rufus. Hen ry w
2 CONC as given great treasure with which he could purchase an appanage . Will
2 CONC iam died at daybreak on September 9, in his 60th year, and was b uried i
2 CONC n rather unseemly fashion in St. Stephen's Church, which he ha d built a
2 CONC t Caen.To cite this page: "William I" Encyclopædia Britannica <http://w
2 CONC ww.britannica.com/eb/article&#00063;query=william+the+conquer er&eu=790
2 CONC 80 >Reigned 1066-1087. Duc de Normandie 1035-1087. Invaded En gland def
2 CONC eated and killed his rival Harold at the Battle of Hastings a nd became K
2 CONC ing. The Norman conquest of England was completed by 1072 a ided by the e
2 CONC stablishment of feudalism under which his followers wer e granted land i
2 CONC n return for pledges of service and loyalty. King Will iam was noted fo
2 CONC r his efficient if harsh rule. His administration reli ed upon Norman a
2 CONC nd other foreign personnel especially Lanfranc Archbis hop of Canterbur
2 CONC y. In 1085 started the Domesday Book.NOTES: William o f the House of No
2 CONC rmandie; The first Norman King; On 28 Sep 1066 Willia m secured the san
2 CONC ction of Pope Alexander II for a Norman invasion of E ngland. By 1070 t
2 CONC he Norman conquest of England was complete. William i ntroduced the Con
2 CONC tinental system of feudalism; by the Oath of Salisbur y of 1086 all lan
2 CONC dlords swore allegiance to William, thus establishin g the precedent th
2 CONC at a vassal's loyalty to the king overrode his fealt y to his immediate l
2 CONC ord. During a campaign against King Philip I of Fr ance, William fell f
2 CONC rom a horse and was fatally injured. William was t he illegitimate son o
2 CONC f Robert I, Duc de Normandie and Arletta, a tanne r's daughter. He is s
2 CONC ometimes called "William the Bastard".REF: "Fall s the Shadow" Sharon K
2 CONC ay Penman: William requested a large number of J ews to move to England a
2 CONC fter his conquest. They spoke Norman & did wel l under his reign.REF: B
2 CONC ritish Monarchy Official Website: The victor y of William I, 'the Conqu
2 CONC eror' (reigned 1066-1087) at Hastings and hi s subsequent coronation in W
2 CONC estminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066 di d not give him complete contr
2 CONC ol of England. Remaining resistance was , however, severely crushed and c
2 CONC astles were built to control the coun try (including a fortress on the s
2 CONC ite of Windsor Castle, and the Whit e Tower at the Tower of London). Th
2 CONC e lands of defeated Saxon nobles we re given to William's followers in r
2 CONC eturn for military service by a ce rtain number of knights, so that the t
2 CONC enants' foremost obligation wa s allegiance to the king. This firmly es
2 CONC tablished the feudal system. I n 1086, William commissioned the Domesda
2 CONC y Book, to record land holding s for theassessment of taxes and other d
2 CONC ues. William spent long period s in Normandie to maintain his authority t
2 CONC here, dealing with rebellion s and French invasions.William died in 108
2 CONC 7 leaving Normandie to his e ldest son, Robert, and England to his seco
2 CONC nd son, William II Rufus (re igned 1087-1100).REF: "Royal Descents of F
2 CONC amous People" Mark Humphreys : Steve Jones' book "In the Blood: God, Ge
2 CONC nes, & Destiny" 1996, estima tes that 25% of the population of Britain i
2 CONC s descended from William th e Conqueror. Consider you need two parents, f
2 CONC our grandparents, etc. As suming an average of abt 25 years per generat
2 CONC ion, you only need go bac k to 1200, quite within historical times, to n
2 CONC eed more separate ancest ors than the population of the world. Therefor w
2 CONC e all must descend fro m cousin marriages, many times over, even within t
2 CONC he last few hundre d years. Davenport claimed "no people of English des
2 CONC cent are more dist antly related than 30th cousins".William I, byname W
2 CONC ILLIAM THE CONQUER OR, or THE BASTARD, or WILLIAM OF Normandie, French G
2 CONC UILLAUME LE CONQU ÉRA
