1 NOTE  Note: King of Italy 817-855, Emperor of the West 840-855. Frankishempe r
2 CONC or whose attempt to gain sole rule over the Frankishterritories was c h
2 CONC ecked by his brothers. The eldest son of the emperor Louis I the Piou s a
2 CONC nd a grandsonof Charlemagne, Lothair was made King in Bavaria afte r Lo
2 CONC uissucceeded Charlemagne in 814, and in 817 he was made jointemper or. U
2 CONC nder the Ordinatio imperii, a decree issued by Louisin 817 to pro vide f
2 CONC or the unity of the empire after his death,Lothair's younger bro thers, P
2 CONC epin and Louis (later called theGerman), were to receive thei r own kin
2 CONC gdoms, Aquitaine andBavaria, but were to remain under the gen eral suze
2 CONC rainty ofLothair.Ruler in Italy from 822, Lothair was crowne d emperor b
2 CONC y PopePaschal I in 823. He issued the Constitutio Romana (8 24),affirmi
2 CONC ng imperial sovereignty over Rome and demanding an oathof f ealty from t
2 CONC he pope. When in 829 Louis I, under theinfluence of his se cond wife, J
2 CONC udith, revised the Ordinatioimperii to grant part of the e mpire previo
2 CONC usly granted toLothair to his son by Judith, Charles (late r called the B
2 CONC ald),Lothair broke with the imperial government. A palac e revolutionfo
2 CONC rced his reappointment as co-emperor in 830, but he wa s againdeposed s
2 CONC hortly afterward.In 833 discontent with the rule of Lo uis the Pious en
2 CONC ded in arevolt of the three elder sons, led by Lothair , and Lothairrep
2 CONC laced the deposed Louis. Louis was restored to power t hefollowing year
2 CONC , however, and Lothair's rule was restricted toItaly.W hen Pepin died i
2 CONC n December 838, Louis I drew up a new partitionscheme , dividing the em
2 CONC pire, aside from Bavaria and neighbouringareas, whic h were left to Lou
2 CONC is the German, between Lothair andCharles the Bald , with Lothair takin
2 CONC g the eastern portion.Lothair was to have the titl e of emperor, but wi
2 CONC thout thesuzerainty over the other princes that ha d been granted by th
2 CONC eOrdinatio imperii of 817.On Louis I's death (840) , Lothair again clai
2 CONC med his rights underthe Ordinatio of 817, but hi s brothers, Louis the G
2 CONC erman andCharles the Bald, defeated him at th e Battle of Fontenoy (841
2 CONC ).The Treaty of Verdun (August 843) left Loth air the Middle Realmof th
2 CONC e Frankish dominions, from the North Sea to I taly, whileLouis received t
2 CONC he eastern and Charles the western territor y.The imperial title fell t
2 CONC o Lothair.After granting the government o f Italy to his eldest son, Lo
2 CONC uisII, as early as 844, Lothair partition ed his realm between Louis(em
2 CONC peror from 850) and his two other sons, L othair and Charles,in 855. Th
2 CONC en he abdicated and became a monk. From W ikipedia [http://www.wikipedi
2 CONC a.org/wiki/Lothair]: "Lothair(795 - Marc h 2, 855), Holy Roman Emperor, w
2 CONC as the eldest son ofthe emperor Loui s the Pious, and his wife Irmengar
2 CONC de(Ermengarde), daughter of Ingram m (Ingerman), the Duke ofHesbaye. Li
2 CONC ttle is known of his early life, w hich was probablypassed at the court o
2 CONC f his grandfather Charlemagne, u ntil 815when he became ruler of Bavari
2 CONC a. "When Louis in 817 divided th e Empire between his sons, Lothairwas c
2 CONC rowned joint emperor at Aix-la- Chapelle (Aachen) and givena certain su
2 CONC periority over his brothers. I n 821 he marriedIrmengarde (d. 851), dau
2 CONC ghter of Hugo (Hugues), coun t of Tours;in 822 undertook the government o
2 CONC f Italy; and, on the 5th o fApril 823, was crowned emperor by Pope Pasc
2 CONC hal I at Rome, Italy."In N ovember 824 he promulgated a statute concern
2 CONC ing therelations of pope a nd emperor which reserved the supreme powert
2 CONC o the secular potentate, a nd he afterwards issued variousordinances fo
2 CONC r the good government of I taly. "On his return to his father's court h
2 CONC is step-mother Judith wonh is consent to her plan for securing a kingdo
2 CONC m for her sonCharles the B ald, a scheme which was carried out in 829.L
2 CONC othair, however, soon chan ged his attitude, and spent thesucceeding de
2 CONC cade in constant strife ov er the division of theEmpire with his father
2 CONC . He was alternately maste r of the Empire,and banished and confined to I
2 CONC taly; at one time takin g up armsin alliance with his brothers and at a
2 CONC nother fighting against them; whilst the bounds of his appointed kingdo
2 CONC m were in turnextende d and reduced. "When Louis I was dying in 840, he s
2 CONC ent the imperial in signia toLothair, who, disregarding the various par
2 CONC titions, claimed th ewhole of the Empire. Negotiations with his brother L
2 CONC ouis theGerman an d his half-brother Charles the Bald, both of whom arm
2 CONC edto resist thi s claim, were followed by an alliance of theyounger bro
2 CONC thers against L othair. A decisive battle was foughtat Fontenay on the 2
2 CONC 5th of June 84 1, when, in spite of hispersonal gallantry, Lothair was d
2 CONC efeated and f led to Aix. Withfresh troops he entered upon a war of plu
2 CONC nder, but th e forces ofhis brothers were too strong for him, and takin
2 CONC g with him s uchtreasure as he could collect, he abandoned to them his c
2 CONC apital. "Ef forts to make peace were begun, and in June 842 the brother
2 CONC smet on a n island in the Saone, and agreed to an arrangementwhich deve
2 CONC loped, af ter much difficulty and delay, into theTreaty of Verdun signe
2 CONC d in Augu st 843. By this Lothair receivedItaly and the imperial title, t
2 CONC ogethe r with a stretch of landbetween the North and Mediterranean Seas l
2 CONC yin g along the valleysof the Rhine and the Rhone. He soon abandoned It
2 CONC al y to hiseldest son, Louis, and remained in his new kingdom, engaged i
2 CONC n alternate quarrels and reconciliations with his brothers, and infutil e e
2 CONC fforts to defend his lands from the attacks of theNormans and the S ara
2 CONC cens. "In 855 he became seriously ill, and despairing of recoveryre nou
2 CONC nced the throne, divided his lands between his three sons,and on th e 2
2 CONC 3rd of September entered the monastery of Pruem, inRheinland, Pruss ia, w
2 CONC here he died six days later. He was buriedat Pr&#00129;m, where h is re
2 CONC mains were found in 1860. "His kingdom was divided among his thre e son
2 CONC s - the eldest,Louis II, received Italy and the title of Emperor ; the s
2 CONC econd,Lothair II, received Lotharingia, while the youngest, Cha rles,re
2 CONC ceived Burgundy. "See "Annales Fuldenses"; Nithard, "Historiaru m Libri
2 CONC ," both inthe Monumenta Germaniae historica. Scriptores, Bnd e i. and i
2 CONC i.(Hanover and Berlin, 1826 fol.); E. M&#00129;hlbacher, Di e Regesten d
2 CONC esKaiserreichs unter den Karolingern (Innsbruck, 1881); E. Dummler, Ges
2 CONC chichte des ostfrnkischen Reichs (Leipzig,1887-1888); B . Simson, Jahr
2 CONC buecher des deutschen Reiches unterLudwig dem Frommen (L eipzig, 1874-1
2 CONC 876). "Adapted from the 1911 Encyclopedia"From http://ww w.mathematical
2 CONC .com/emporerlothaire1.html: "Lothairbecame co-ruler wit h his father in 8
2 CONC 17 and was crowned by thepope six years later. He twi ce conspired with h
2 CONC is brothers inrevolts against their father. In 83 9 Lothair received th
2 CONC eeastern part of the empire in addition to Italy , which he hadreceived i
2 CONC n 822. After the Death of Louis I, Lothair att emptedto assert his powe
2 CONC r over his brothers, but he was defeated bythe m at Fontenoy, France, o
2 CONC n June 25, 841. By the Treaty ofVerdun (843) , the title of Holy Roman e
2 CONC mperor was guaranteed toLothair, together w ith sovereignty over Italy, B
2 CONC urgundy, Alsace,Lorraine, and the Low Cou ntries. After having divided h
2 CONC iskingdom among his sons, Lothair retire d to a monastery. Hissecond so
2 CONC n, sometimes called Lothair II, reigne d from 855 to 869over the Kingdo
2 CONC m of Lotharingia. He is buried in Mona stery ofPruem."References:1 Fred
