2 CONC EMAGNE (742&#00063;-814). "By the sword an d the cross,"Charlemagne (Ch
2 CONC arles the Great) became master of Wester n Europe.It was falling into d
2 CONC ecay when Charlemagne became joint kin g ofthe Franks in 768. Except in t
2 CONC he monasteries, people had all butfo rgotten education and the arts. Bo
2 CONC ldly Charlemagne conquered barbarian s and kings alike. Byrestoring the r
2 CONC oots of learning and order, he pre served manypolitical rights and revi
2 CONC ved culture.Charlemagne's grandfat her was Charles Martel, the warrior w
2 CONC hocrushed the Saracens (see Charl es Martel). Charlemagne was theelder s
2 CONC on of Bertrade ("Bertha Greatfoo t") and Pepin the Short,first "mayor o
2 CONC f the palace" to become king o f the Franks.Although schools had almost d
2 CONC isappeared in the 8th centur y,historians believe that Bertrade gave yo
2 CONC ung Charles someeducation an d that he learned to read. His devotion to t
2 CONC hechurch motivated him thr oughout life.Charlemagne was tall, powerful, a
2 CONC nd tireless. His secreta ry,Eginhard, wrote that Charlemagne had fair h
2 CONC air and a "facelaughin g and merry . . . his appearance was always stat
2 CONC ely anddignified." H e had a ready wit, but could be stern. His tastesw
2 CONC ere simple and moder ate. He delighted in hunting, riding, andswimming. H
2 CONC e wore the Frankis h dress linen shirt and breeches, asilk-fringed tuni
2 CONC c, hose wrapped wi th bands, and, in winter, atight coat of otter or ma
2 CONC rten skins. Over a ll these garments "heflung a blue cloak, and he alwa
2 CONC ys had a sword gir t about him."Charlemagne's character was contradicto
2 CONC ry. In an age whe n theusual penalty for defeat was death, Charlemagne s
2 CONC everal timesspar ed the lives of his defeated foes; yet in 782 at Verde
2 CONC n,after a Saxo n uprising, he ordered 4,500 Saxons beheaded. Hecompelle
