2 CONC s without an y cause. He forced kings and princesto kneel at his feet, y
2 CONC et his moth er and his two favorite wivesoften overruled him in his own h
2 CONC ousehold. Charlemagne Begins His ReignIn 768, when Charlemagne was 26, h
2 CONC e and hi s brother Carlomaninherited the kingdom of the Franks. In 771 C
2 CONC arloma n died, andCharlemagne became sole ruler of the kingdom. At that t
2 CONC im e thenorthern half of Europe was still pagan and lawless. In thesout
2 CONC h , the Roman Catholic church was striving to assert itspower against t
2 CONC h e Lombard kingdom in Italy. In Charlemagne's ownrealm, the Franks wer e f
2 CONC alling back into barbarian ways,neglecting their education and reli gio
2 CONC n.Charlemagne was determined to strengthen his realm and to bringor der t
2 CONC o Europe. In 772 he launched a 30-year campaign thatconquered an d Chri
2 CONC stianized the powerful pagan Saxons in thenorth. He subdued th e Avars, a h
2 CONC uge Tatar tribe on the Danube.He compelled the rebelliou s Bavarian duk
2 CONC es to submit to him.When possible he preferred to settl e matters peace
2 CONC fully,however. For example, Charlemagne offered to pa y the Lombardking D
2 CONC esiderius for return of lands to the pope, but, whe nDesiderius refused
2 CONC , Charlemagne seized his kingdom in 773 to 774and r estored the Papal S
2 CONC tates.The key to Charlemagne's amazing conquests wa s his ability toorg
2 CONC anize. During his reign he sent out more than 50 mi litaryexpeditions. H
2 CONC e rode as commander at the head of at least halfo f them. He moved his a
2 CONC rmies over wide reaches of country withunbelieva ble speed, but every m
2 CONC ove was planned in advance.Before a campaign h e told the counts, princ
2 CONC es, and bishopsthroughout his realm how many m en they should bring, wh
2 CONC at armsthey were to carry, and even what to lo ad in the supply wagons.
2 CONC These feats of organization and the swift marc hes later ledNapoleon to s
2 CONC tudy his tactics.One of Charlemagne's mino r campaigns has become the m
2 CONC ost famous.In 778 he led his army into Spa in, where they laid siege to
2 CONC Saragossa. They failed to take the city, a nd during theirretreat a gro
2 CONC up of Basques ambushed the rear guard atRo ncesvalles and killed "Count R
2 CONC oland." Roland became a greathero of med ieval songs and romances (see R
2 CONC oland).By 800 Charlemagne was the undis puted ruler of Western Europe.H
2 CONC is vast realm covered what are now Fran ce, Switzerland,Belgium, and th
