2 CONC hools , opening them to peasant boys as well as nobles.Charlemagne neve
2 CONC r sto pped studying. He brought an English monk,Alcuin, and other schol
2 CONC ars t o his court. He learned to readLatin and some Greek but apparentl
2 CONC y di d not master writing. Atmeals, instead of having jesters perform, h
2 CONC e l istened to menreading from learned works.To revive church music, Ch
2 CONC arl emagne had monks sent from Rome totrain his Frankish singers. To re
2 CONC sto re some appreciation of art,he brought valuable pieces from Italy. A n i
2 CONC mpressive monument tohis religious devotion is the cathedral at Aac hen
2 CONC , which hebuilt and where he was buried (see Aachen).At Charlemagne 's d
2 CONC eath in 814 only one of his three sons, Louis,was living. Louis' s weak r
2 CONC ule brought on the rise of civil warsand revolts. After his de ath his t
