2 CONC 4 Compton's NewMedia, Inc.HOLY ROMAN EMPIRE. From Christm as Day in AD 8
2 CONC 00 until Aug. 6,1806, there existed in Europe a peculia r political ins
2 CONC titutioncalled the Holy Roman Empire. The name of the e mpire as it isk
2 CONC nown today did not come into general use until 1254. I t hastruly been s
2 CONC aid that this political arrangement was not holy, orR oman, or an empir
2 CONC e. Any holiness attached to it came from theclaims o f the popes in the
2 CONC ir attempts to assert religiouscontrol in Europe. I t was Roman to the e
2 CONC xtent that it tried torevive, without success, th e political authority o
2 CONC f the RomanEmpire in the West as a countermeasu re to the Byzantine Emp
2 CONC irein the East. It was an empire in the looses t sense of the wordat no t
2 CONC ime was it able to consolidate unchallenge d politicalcontrol over the v
2 CONC ast territories it pretended to rule. The rewas no central government, n
2 CONC o unity of language, no commonsystem o f law, no sense of common loyalt
2 CONC y among the many stateswithin it. Ove r the centuries the empire's boun
2 CONC daries shiftedand shrank drastically. OriginsThe original Roman Empire e
2 CONC nded in Italy and Western Europe inA D 476, when the last emperor Romul
2 CONC us Augustulus was deposed.Politica l power passed to Constantinople (no
2 CONC w Istanbul), thecapital of the Byz antine Empire. Theoretically Constan
2 CONC tinopleincluded all of Europe in i ts domain. Realistically, however,th
2 CONC is proved impossible, as barbaria n kingdoms were establishedthroughout W
2 CONC estern Europe. The only figur e in the West who hadany claim to univers
2 CONC al authority was the pope i n Rome, and hewas legally bishop of Rome, c
2 CONC onfirmed in his position b y theByzantine emperor.By the 8th century, B
2 CONC yzantine control of Ital y had vanished. TheLombard kingdom of northern I
2 CONC taly had driven out th e emperor'srepresentative in Ravenna in 751. The
2 CONC re were also strongrel igious differences between the pope and the chur
2 CONC ch inConstantinople di fferences that would lead to a complete breakin 1
2 CONC 054. Confronted wit h this situation, the Roman popes soughtpolitical p
2 CONC rotection from th e only people who would give it thekings of the Frank
2 CONC s, the stronges t power north of the Alps. In754 the Frankish king Pepi
2 CONC n the Short inv aded Italy andconquered the Lombard kingdom. Two years l
2 CONC ater he assign ed theformer Byzantine territory around Ravenna to the p
2 CONC ope. This wast he birth of the Papal States of Italy, which would endur
2 CONC e untilthe uni fication of Italy in the 19th century.This close coopera
2 CONC tion between p opes and the Frankish kingswould have far-reaching conse
2 CONC quences. It la id the basis forcenturies of conflict between emperors a
2 CONC nd popes ove r who hadthe supreme authority in Europe. According to the p
2 CONC opes, thee mpire was the political arm of the church. The emperors, on t
2 CONC heother h and, saw themselves as directly responsible to God, andthey r
2 CONC elied o n conquest and control for their power.There is little doubt th
2 CONC at th e popes hoped to become thesuccessors of emperors in the West. Si
2 CONC nce t his was politicallyimpossible, the next best solution was to asse
2 CONC rt re ligiouscontrol by means of political institutions. On Dec. 25, 80
2 CONC 0,Pop e Leo III crowned Charlemagne emperor during a service at St.Pete
2 CONC r's B asilica in Rome (see Charlemagne). The act was illegal,because po
2 CONC pes n ever had the right to crown emperors. Thecrowning did nothing for C
2 CONC har lemagne. He was as before king ofthe Franks and Lombards and the mo
2 CONC s t powerful monarch in Europe.The main practical outcome of Leo's act w a
2 CONC s to complete the separation between Eastand West. It thereby set u p a r
2 CONC ivalry with Constantinople, arivalry in which neither side ha d a real a
2 CONC dvantage. Mostsignificantly the coronation involved the ne w emperor an
2 CONC d hissuccessors in the political pretensions of the papacy .Charlemagne
2 CONC 's EmpireThe empire lasted as long as it did because the i dea waspolit
2 CONC ically and religiously appealing to the peoples and rulers of Europe. I
2 CONC t did not endure unbroken, however. Charlemagne'skingdom d id not remai
2 CONC n whole very long after his death. Hisdomains were fragmen ted by his s
2 CONC uccessors. The last of hisdescendants to hold the title o f emperor was C
2 CONC harles III the Fat(881-87). From 888 France, Germany, a nd Italy were s
2 CONC eparatestates (though not unified nations by any means) . A successiono
