2 CONT
2 CONT This nobleman, upon the return of King Richard from captivity, took ar m
2 CONC s in his behalf and, joining the Earl of Chester, besieged Nottingha m C
2 CONC astle, which, after a brief resistance, surrendered. For this and o the
2 CONC r acts of fidelity, he was chosen by the king to sit with the res t of t
2 CONC he peers in the great council held at the said castle in Notting ham in t
2 CONC he ensuing March. Moreover, at Richard's second coronation h e was one o
2 CONC f the four that carried the canopy over the king's head. Up on the acce
2 CONC ssion of King John, his lordship, with the Earls of Clare a nd Chester, a
2 CONC nd other great men, swore fealty to the new monarch but u pon the condi
2 CONC tion that each person should have his right. His lordshi p was present a
2 CONC t the coronation of King John and 7 June following, bei ng solemnly cre
2 CONC ated Earl of Derby by special charter dated at Northamp ton, he was gir
2 CONC t with a sword by the king's own hands (being the firs t of whom in any c
2 CONC harter that expression was used). He had also a gran t of the third pen
2 CONC ny of all the pleas before the sheriff throughout th e whole country wh
2 CONC ereof he was earl, to hold to him and his heirs as a mply as any of his a
2 CONC ncestors had enjoyed the same. Moreover, in consid eration of 4,000 mar
2 CONC ks, he obtained another charter from the king of t he manor of Higham-F
2 CONC errers, co. Northampton, with the hundred and park ; as also of the man
2 CONC ors of Bliseworth and Newbottle, in the same shire ; which were part of t
2 CONC he lands of his great grandfather, William Pever el of Nottingham. King J
2 CONC ohn also conferred upon him a mansion-house si tuated in the parish of S
2 CONC t. Margaret within the city of London, whic h had belonged to Isaac, a J
2 CONC ew, at Norwich, to hold by the service o f waiting upon the king (the e
2 CONC arl and his heirs), at all festivals yea rly without any cap, but with a g
2 CONC arland of the breadth of his little f inger upon his head. These libera
2 CONC l marks of royal favour were felt s o gratefully by the earl that in al
2 CONC l the subsequent struggles betwee n the king and the refractory barons, h
2 CONC is lordship never once swerve d from his allegiance, but remained true t
2 CONC o the monarch; and loyalty t o the interests of his son, King henry III
2 CONC . His lordship assisted at t he coronation of the new monarch and immed
2 CONC iately after the ensuing Eas ter, he took part with the famous William M
2 CONC arshall (governor of the ki ng and kingdom), the Earls of Chester and A
2 CONC lbemarle, and many other gr eat men in the siege of Mountsorell Castle i
2 CONC n Leicestershire, then hel d by Henry de Braybroke and ten other stout k
2 CONC nights. And the same yea r was likewise with those noble persons at rai
2 CONC sing the siege of Lincol n, which place the rebellious barons with Lewi
2 CONC s, King of France, had i nvested. His lordship m. Agnes, sister and one o
2 CONC f the co-heirs of Ranu lph, Earl of Chester, by whom he had two sons, W
2 CONC illiam and Thomas. H e died of the gout in 1246 and his countess d. in t
2 CONC he same year afte r a union, according to some authorities, of seventy-
2 CONC five, and by othe rs, of fifty-five years. His lordship was s. by his e
2 CONC lder son, Willia m de Ferrers, 5th Earl of Derby. [Sir Bernard Burke, D
2 CONC ormant, Abeyant , Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd
2 CONC ., London, 1883 , p. 196, Ferrers, Earls of Derby]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------There is substantial confusion over his name. See The Comple t
2 CONC e Peerage Vol. 4, p 193 for an account. Personally, I feel there coul d h
2 CONC ave been two brothers, William and Robert, Robert being the Earl an d w
2 CONC hen he died at Acre his nephew William son of his brother William s ucc
2 CONC eeded, but no documents support this theory either! In The Complet e Pe
2 CONC erage vol. XIV ,p .250 it is suggested that Robert is a fabricatio n by V
2 CONC incent, Earl of Ferrieres. [Brian Tompsett, Directory of Royal G enealo
2 CONC gical Data]
