1 NOTE  James de Alditheley was a great favourite of Richard, Earl of Cornwall , a
2 CONC t whose coronation as king of Almaigne he assisted. This nobleman h ad l
2 CONC ivery of his lands in the 31st Henry III [1247], and was constitut ed i
2 CONC n two years afterwards constable of Newcastle-under-Lyne. Being on e of t
2 CONC he lords-marchers he was actively employed for some years agains t the W
2 CONC elsh and was appointed governor of the castles of Salop and Bri dgenort
2 CONC h and sheriff of the counties of Salop and Stafford. In the 47t h of He
2 CONC nry III [1263] he was made justice of Ireland; and in the sam e year, u
2 CONC pon the misunderstanding between the king and the barons rega rding the p
2 CONC rovisions of Oxford, being referred to arbitration of the m onarch of F
2 CONC rance, he was one of the noblemen who undertook for the kin g therein. T
2 CONC he next year we find him with Roger de Mortimer and the ot her barons-m
2 CONC archers giving battle to Llewelyn, Prince of Wales, and af terwards joi
2 CONC ning the Earl of Gloucester at Evesham in rescuing the kin g, who had b
2 CONC ecome captive to the Earl of Leicester at the battle of Le wes. In the 5
2 CONC 2nd of Henry III [1268], his lordship performed a pilgrim age to the sh
2 CONC rine of St. James in Galicia, and the following year emba rked in the C
2 CONC rusade. His death, occasioned by breaking his neck, occur red soon afte
2 CONC rwards (1271). He had a dau. Joan, who m. John, son of Ro bert de Beauc
2 CONC hamp, to whose child, prior to its birth, the said John t hen being dec
2 CONC eased, his lordship was appointed guardian. He had also f ive sons, the y
2 CONC oungest of whom, Hugh, is supposed to have been the Hug h Alditheley wh
2 CONC o had summons to parliament 15 May 1321, and whose so n became Earl of G
2 CONC loucester. His lordship was s. by his eldest son, Ja mes de Alditheley, w
2 CONC ho d. s. p. in 1272, and was s. by his brother, He nry, who d. without i
2 CONC ssue in 1275, and was s. by his brother, William , who d. without issue i
2 CONC s 1275, was s. by his brother, Nicholas, who d ied in 1299. [Sir Bernar
2 CONC d Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extin ct Peerages, Burke's Pee
2 CONC rage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 15, Audl ey, Barons Audley, of He
2 CONC leigh]
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: John Burke, History of the Commoners of Great Britain and Irelan d
2 CONC , Vol. IV, R. Bentley, London, 1834, p. 757, Stansfeld, of Burley Par k
2 CONC , names two additional sons, William, Lord of Blore and Grindon, co . S
2 CONC tafford, and Sir Adam, surnamed FitzWolfric.
2 CONT
