2 CONT Summary (revised Aug 1999):  An article in "The American Genealogist"
2 CONT vol 41 pg 168-175, by John G. Hunt, B.S.C., of Arlington, VA., says th e
2 CONT 1607 will of Kenelm Winslow of St. Andrew in Worcester, named his
2 CONT wife Katherine executrix and referred to his children and grandchildre n
2 CONT without naming them.  It also says that the year 1551 for the birth of
2 CONT Kenelm was taken from a record in Great Britain Court of Requests, bdl e
2 CONT IX p. 3, bo. 405/134 which records a suit brought in 1605 in which
2 CONT Kenelme Wincelowe of Worcester, yeoman, aged 54 years, deposed.
2 CONT Ancestry has been researched by D. Kenelm Winslow of London back to
2 CONT 1425, from Kenelm to Thomas, Richard, William, Richard, Galfird all of
2 CONT Earls Crome, Worcestershire.  Thomas, Richard and William and wives
2 CONT are in the IGI but the 2nd Richard and Galfird are not there.
2 CONT Mr. Hunt, in TAG 42:186, says "To go back to Richard Winslow's will,
2 CONT dated 7 Oct. Hen. VIII and probated at Worcester 21 Jan. 1546/7, in 19 5
2 CONC 7
2 CONT D. Kenelm Winslow of London, in his 'Mayflower Heritage', asserted (pp .
2 CONT 22 f.): 'Through this will and manorial records we can trace how his
2 CONT youngest son, Thomas, moved to nearby Kerswell, a hamlet within the
2 CONT parish of Kempsey . . . . Kenelm Winslow, son of Thomas of Kerswell, s o
2 CONC ld
2 CONT the farm and moved north to Worcester City.   Kenelm's son Edward
2 CONT moving farther north where he set up in business extracting the famous
2 CONT Droitwich salt . . .'  In other words, from a study of manorial record s
2 CONC , Mr.
2 CONT D. Kenelm Winslow was satisfied that Kenelm Winslow who lived in
2 CONT Worcester was, indeed, grandfather of Gov. Edward Winslow.  We think
2 CONT this reasonable and must reject the supposed 'family record' of a birt h
2 CONT in 1560 for Edward Winslow Sr.  If, as and when the original of this
2 CONT so-called record can be produced for examination, or if it can be
2 CONT substantiated by any parish entry or bishop's transcript entry, we may
2 CONT change our suspicion about the date 1560 in connection with Edward
2 CONT Winslow Sr., but until such time, we view this as an invented record o f
2 CONT no value whatever."
2 CONT It looks as if Mr. Winslow of London has also failed to substantiate d h
2 CONC is
2 CONT statement with any specific record.  It would seem that until the
2 CONT manorial record can be produced for examination, we should not accept
2 CONT this as proven.
2 CONT There seems to be doubt that Katherine was the mother of Edward who
2 CONT married Magdalene Ollyver.  There is probably the assumption that she
2 CONT was not first wife.  A series of articles in "New Eng Hist  & Gen Reg" ,
2 CONT vol. 121-124, by John G. Hunt, B.S.C. of Arlington, Va.,  discusses th e
2 CONT various possibilities, the most promising being that she was Elizabeth ,
2 CONT born in 1540 daughter of John Foliott by his first wife, Eleanor Moore . A
2 CONC n
2 CONT article by Mahomet G. Jones, Ogden, Utah, found in pamphlet form in th e
2 CONT Family History Library in Salt Lake City, reviews the various theories
2 CONT and comes to the conclusion that none of the congectures about the wif e
2 CONT of Kenelm Winslow of Droitwich are probable enough to accept.  He also
2 CONT has questions about the paternal pedigree saying that it needs to be
2 CONT substantiated.
2 CONT
