1 NAME Edward /Winslow/
2 GIVN Edward
2 SURN Winslow
1 SEX M
1 _UID DC8FF18088AB4945967BAF1B504342004D0A
1 _AMTID 162143285131:1030:164657654
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 NOV 2019
1 NOTE  !Initial source:  Family group sheet in the FGRA collection of the Fam i
2 CONC ly
2 CONT History Library, Salt Lake City, Utah, submitted by Gerald R. Fuller , 2
2 CONC 624
2 CONT Battery, Little Rock, Ark.  His sources:  The Hamlin Fam note 96 p 126 ,
2 CONT Savage Gen Dict Vol 4 p 600.
2 CONT BIRTH:  Not clearly established.  (see below)
2 CONT MARRIAGE:  Not clearly established that there was a marriage before th e
2 CONT one to Magdalene Ollyver, the latter from film FHL 801595 which is the
2 CONT parish register of St. Peter de Witton Church, Droitwich.  Another rec o
2 CONC rd
2 CONT with same date is in St. Bride Church, London, London, England.  (see
2 CONT below)
2 CONT DEATH:  Estimated date from pg 172 of "The American Genealogist", Vol
2 CONT 41, in an article "Clues to the Ancestry of Winslow of Droitwich" by
2 CONT John G. Hunt.  Probable place suggested by a statement on pg 23 of vol .
2 CONT 121 of "New Eng. Hist. and Gen. Reg.", in another article by John G. H u
2 CONC nt,
2 CONT "The Mayflower Winslows".  He gives "Mayflower Heritage", by D. Kenelm
2 CONT Winslow of London, as the source.
2 CONT The FGRA has LDS proxy sealing of his marriage to Magdalene Ollyver 26
2 CONT Apr 1966 ARIZO.  The 1997 IGI has several entries, one is extracted, t o
2 CONT "Magdalen" (no surname) proxy sealing 7 Dec 1982 SEATT, another to
2 CONT "Magdalene" (no surname), 1 Nov 1946 SLAKE.  Others with Magdalene
2 CONT Ollyver with various sealing dates, some from temple films and others
2 CONT from form submissions.  It has 95 entries of his marriage to Eleanor
2 CONT Pelham in the British section!!  A few of these may be duplicates but
2 CONT most of them were done since 1992.
2 CONT The FGRA sheet has Edward's birthdate as 17 Oct 1560 and shows a first
2 CONT marriage to Eleanor Pelham. However, a series of articles by John G.
2 CONT Hunt, B.S.C., of Arlington, Va., appeared in "The American Genealogist "
2 CONC ,
2 CONT vol 41-43, in which he says (Vol 42 pg 186) the date of birth for Edwa r
2 CONC d
2 CONT and the first marriage to Eleanor Pelham have never been documented. H e
2 CONT says the birthdate originated, with no source given, in Holton's "Wins l
2 CONC ow
2 CONT Memorial", published in 1887-1888.  It may be that he doubts that date
2 CONT because evidence has been uncovered to establish the probable birthdat e
2 CONT of his purported father, Kenelm, as 1551, (see notes for Kenelm) makin g
2 CONT it impossible for him to have had a son born 1560.  Mr. Hunt guesses
2 CONT Edward was born nearer 1572.
2 CONT There was an article in Vol 42 pg 86 of "American Genealogist", by
2 CONT Meredith B Colket, Jr., suggesting that the 1560 birthdate for Edward
2 CONT may be right and that the problem with it is in assuming that Edward
2 CONT was son of Kenelm when he may be brother instead.  Mr. Hunt, in answer ,
2 CONT (pg 186) said he did not think it likely.
2 CONT It is not clear in Mr. Hunt's article what was the source of the purpo r
2 CONC ted
2 CONT first marriage.  He said it appeared in "Americana Illustrated", also
2 CONT without a source.  He said further that both statements, of birth an d o
2 CONC f
2 CONT first marriage, have been accepted by some and not by others ever sinc e
2 CONC .
2 CONT He said further "Indeed, we can find no record attesting the existenc e i
2 CONC n
2 CONT Worcestershire of any Pelham family at that or any related era."
2 CONT A family group sheet showing this first marriage is in the FGRA
2 CONT collection, also submitted by Gerald R. Fuller, with sources:  Tilso n G
2 CONC en.
2 CONT and N.Y. Gen and Bio Rec V46 pg 306.  It shows Eleanor born 1564 of
2 CONT Droitwich, Worcester, Eng., daughter of Sir Herbert Pelham, and lists
2 CONT two children, Richard, born abt 1585, Droitwich, married 1605 Alice
2 CONT Hurdman, and died 20 May 1659; and Margaret, born 1589, Droitwich,
2 CONT married Robert Hicks and died 1665. There was a note on the sheet, "It
2 CONT has been noted that discrepancies appear on this record....."  It didn '
2 CONC t
2 CONT specify what they were and they are not apparent.   The 1997 IGI has 9 5
2 CONT entries of his marriage to Eleanor Pelham in the British section! !  A f
2 CONC ew
2 CONT of these may be duplicates but most of them were submitted since 1992.
2 CONT About 12 are with date before 1594, the rest with only the year 1620.
2 CONT The 1997 IGI has an extracted marriage to Magdelen (no "e" on the end
2 CONT and no surname) with date 3 Nov 1594, Saint Peter, Droitwich,
2 CONT Worcester, England, from film FHL 801595 which is the parish register
2 CONT of St. Peter de Witton Church, Droitwich.   It also has a marriage ent r
2 CONC y
2 CONT with wife's name Magdalene (no surname) the same date but St. Bride
2 CONT Church, London, London, England, from temple film FHL184772. Others
2 CONT with Magdalene Ollyver, same date (or 4 Nov) and place, some from
2 CONT temple films and others from form submissions.
2 CONT One of Mr. Hunt's articles, found in "The American Genealogist", vol 4 1
2 CONT pg 168, says Edward Winslow and Magdalene Oliver were married in 1594
2 CONT at Saint Brides, Fleet Street, London.  It gives the source as "Dictio n
2 CONC ary
2 CONT of National Biography", sub "Winslow, Edward"; cf. Charles Edward
2 CONT Banks, "The English Ancestry and Homes of the Pilgrim Fathers" (1929),
2 CONT 98-99.  There is a note in "Notes and Queries" published in "NEHGR" vo l
2 CONT 24 (1870) pg 329, sent in by Joseph L. Chester, London, which says he
2 CONT found the following marriage entry in the parish register of St. Bride '
2 CONC s,
2 CONT Fleet Street, London:  "1594, Nov. 4.  Edward Winslowe and Magdalene
2 CONT Ollyver."  It looks like two authentic records exist of the marriage , o
2 CONC ne
2 CONT in Droitwich, Worcester, and the other in London.  The two places ar e a
2 CONC t
2 CONT least 100 miles apart.  Perhaps the one in Droitwich was made only for
2 CONT information and not at time of the actual performing of the marriage.
2 CONT An article by Mahomet G. Jones, Ogden, Utah, titled "The Winslow Engli s
2 CONC h
2 CONT Pedigree Critically Examined", (FHL 990094 it 9).found in pamphlet for m
2 CONT in the Family History Library in Salt Lake City, reviews the various
2 CONT theories and comes to the conclusion that none of the conjectures abou t
2 CONT the wife of Kenelm Winslow of Droitwich are probable enough to accept.
2 CONT He also has questions about the paternal pedigree saying that it need s t
2 CONC o
2 CONT be substantiated.
2 CONT A more recent article in "NEHGR" vol. 154 (2000) pg 78, by Kenneth W.
2 CONT Kirkpatrick, reviews the Hunt articles and other sources in depth and
2 CONT comes to the conclusion that the most likely, but by no means proven,
2 CONT theory is that the mother or Edward Winslow of Droitwich may be an
2 CONT un-named daughter, (or even Blanche, one that is named but for which n o
2 CONT marriage is found), of Fulke and Elizabeth (Willoughby) Greville.  H e a
2 CONC lso
2 CONT shows familial connections of the Greville family to the Hesilridge an d
2 CONT Foliott families which could explain why the cousin connection with
2 CONT them.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Mayflower Winslows" in NEHGR V. 121 pg 26:  Edward Winslow
2 CONT was a salt extractor of Droitwich, Worcs, England.  By his wife
2 CONT Magdalene he had five sons who were early settlers of New England.  "O f
2 CONT these the eldest was Edward, 1595-1655, sometime governor of
2 CONT Plymouth Colony.  ... Not long before the outbreak of the American
2 CONT Revolution, Governor Thomas Hutchinson, a well informed political
2 CONT figure, writing of Gov. Edward Winslow, asserted that he was 'a
2 CONT gentleman of the best family of any of the Plymouth planters.'
2 CONT On the other hand, in 1951, Bradford Smith, wirting apropos of the
2 CONT humble status of the generality of the Mayflower passengers of 1620
2 CONT asserted:  'Efforts have been made to prove that Winslow came from a
2 CONT higher step in the English stairway of classes.  There is nothing to
2 CONT sustain this'.
2 CONT Taken together, however, there are several records that clearly suppor t
2 CONT the statement that Edward Winslow was a gentleman. . . .  Specifically : 1
2 CONC .
2 CONT In 1607 at Droitwich (Edward, Sr.) was styled 'Mr.', a prefix of respe c
2 CONC t
2 CONT not casually bestowed in that era.  2.  By 1611 he had become surety f o
2 CONC r
2 CONT Simon Clarke, Esq., who in 1617 was to be created baronet.  3. By 1620
2 CONT Winslow was under-sheriff of Worcestershire; at that period such a
2 CONT position would rarely have been  given a man who had no important
2 CONT families to call cousins.  4.  Before 1635 he was well liked by Thomas ,
2 CONT Lord Coventry, who in 1620 had been M.P. for Droitwich, a native of
2 CONT Crome Dabitot, near the towns in which Winslow's ancestors had long
2 CONT lived." (This statement was made in a letter concerning the son Edward ,
2 CONT so probably was after the death of Edward, Sr.)  "5.  In 1647 Elizabet h
2 CONT Bosville, a lady of an aristocratic ancestry, was wife to Herbert Pelh a
2 CONC m
2 CONT of Bures, Essex, Esq., who in writing that year to John Winthrop,
2 CONT governor of Massachusetts Bay called Edward Winslow 'my cosen.'  6.  I n
2 CONT 1650 Lady Dorothy Hesilrige (Mrs. Pelham's aunt and cousin) styled
2 CONT herself Winslow's 'loving cosen.'  In or before 1705, Archbishop Wake,
2 CONT 1657-1737, stated that Edward Winslow was 'a Gentleman of a Good
2 CONT Family in Worcestershire.'
2 CONT Considering the foregoing factors, it seems fair to state that while
2 CONT Kenelm Winslow was a yeoman, his son Edward was a gentleman.  It
2 CONT appears that Edward's status may have derived from his mother."
2 CONT (Further conjecture as to the identity of his mother has not been
2 CONT substantiated.)
2 CONT Further in the article, "The will of Thomas Harton, dated 14 Sept 1620
2 CONT and proved Dec. following includes under the heading 'Desperate Debts, '
2 CONT the following: 'Edward Winslowe some time under sheriff of co.
2 CONT Worcester, owes money for which I can get no payment as he has fled to
2 CONT Ireland.  Sir Henry Follett has my bonds for same.  ...
2 CONT Possibly the indebtedness of Winslow derived from his having helped
2 CONT Simon Clarke and Charles Stamford, for Chancery Proceedings of 24 Oct
2 CONT 1611 show this answer to a bill of complaint of Richard Moore, gent.:
2 CONT 'Answer (only) of Edward Winslow: Subject Winslow was surety on a
2 CONT bond.  Simon Clarke and Charles Stamford were principals'  (Banks MSS,
2 CONT Rare Book Room, Library of Congress, red bound series, fo. 749)."
2 CONT In NEHGR V. 122 pg 176 is the following:  Edward Winslow "was first
2 CONT recorded as styled 'gent.' in 1600; he was probably undersheriff by 16 0
2 CONC 7,
2 CONT when called upon concerning 'writings' in a lawsuit concerning the
2 CONT grammar school at Hartlebury, Worcs.  From these clues it seems
2 CONT possible the elder Edward Winslow may have been undersheriff by 1600
2 CONT and that he was chosen for that office by William Child, brother-in-la w
2 CONT of Elizabeth Foliott, ... ; it was usual at that time for sheriffs t o s
2 CONC elect
2 CONT undersheriffs from among their kinfolk."
2 CONT Further on in the article it states that Edward had holdings in St. An d
2 CONC rew
2 CONT Holborn, Middlesex, in 1602, and still further that Thomas Gower,
2 CONT nephew of Elizabeth Foliott, ... bought in 1605 a salt boilery in Droi t
2 CONC wich
2 CONT which he sublet to Edward Winslow.
2 CONT Mr. Hunt estimates that Edward of Droitwich died about 1630.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information is most accurate on direct line individuals.  Generally, m y s
2 CONC ources are listed only on direct line individuals, even though the y ap
2 CONC ply to others.  Since my database is not 100 percent accurate, I a ppre
2 CONC ciate corrections with sources.
