2 CONT MATILDA (1102-1167), empress, was the daughter of Henry I of England b y h
2 CONC is first marriage. She was betrothed in 1109 and married in 1114 t o th
2 CONC e German emperor Henry V. When her husband died (1125) leaving he r chi
2 CONC ldless, her father, whose only surviving legitimate child she the n was
2 CONC , persuaded his reluctant barons to accept her, on oath, as his s ucces
2 CONC sor (Jan. 1, 1127). The novel prospect of a female ruler was itse lf un
2 CONC welcome; Matilda's 17-year absence in Germany (where she was no t unpop
2 CONC ular) and her apparent arrogrance estranged her from her father 's subj
2 CONC ects. Difficulties also might result from her remarriage to pro videfor t
2 CONC he succession. Her marriage in 1128 to Geoffrey Plantagenet , heir to A
2 CONC njou and Maine (designed by Henry I, like her first marriag e, for poli
2 CONC tical ends), whose father, Count Fulk, departed immediatel y after the c
2 CONC eremony to become the consort of Melisende of Jerusalem , flouted the b
2 CONC arons' stipulation that she should not marry outside En gland without t
2 CONC heir consent, and was unpopular in Normandy and England . On Henry I's d
2 CONC eath, his nephew Stephen by prompt action secured Engl and and was reco
2 CONC gnized by Pope Innocent II. Matilda and Geoffrey, howe ver, made some h
2 CONC eadway in Normandy. Matilda's subsequent challenge t o Stephen's positi
2 CONC onin England mainly depended on the support of her h alf-brother Earl R
2 CONC obert of Gloucester. After the defeat and capture o f Stephen at Lincol
2 CONC n (Feb.1141), Matilda was elected "lady of the Engl ish" and would have b
2 CONC een queen could she have proceeded to coronation , but active support f
2 CONC or her cause still came mainly from the wester n counties. Her chance o
2 CONC f consolidating her precarious victory was swi ftly destroyed by a reac
2 CONC tion initated by her tactless handling of Lond on. After her defeat at W
2 CONC inchester in Sept. 1141, her supporters, slow ly reduced by death and d
2 CONC efection, maintained a stubborn defense unti l Earl Robert died (1147) a
2 CONC nd Matilda retired (1148) to Normandy, of w hich her husband had gained p
2 CONC ossession. She continued to interest hers elf in the government of the t
2 CONC erritories of her eldest son, the futur e Henry II of England. Her care
2 CONC er was not entirely unsuccessful: all t he subsequent monarchs of Engla
2 CONC nd have been her descendants, not Steph en's. She died in Normandy on S
2 CONC ept.10, 1167.
