1 NOTE  The following post by Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, indicate s t
2 CONC he difficulty in "proving" anything about these early Scandinavia n peo
2 CONC pe.  Their ancestry is taken mostly from 12th century writers an d old S
2 CONC agas passed down through the centuries.  The sagas, which proba bly con
2 CONC tain some "truths", also contain a lot of fabulous stories abou t the l
2 CONC ives of the early rulers.  As the people of the period did no t have wr
2 CONC iting, it is difficult to "prove" anything.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, after just a few years of medieval genealogy, I ca n p
2 CONC robably name all of the kings of England, from memory, starting wit h E
2 CONC dward the Confessor, which is almost a thousand year period; and th at i
2 CONC s without any concerted effort to memorize them; I could probabl y go b
2 CONC ack further if I tried.  There is no reason to assume that the p eople o
2 CONC f the 12th century were any less inaccurate in their oral tradi tion of k
2 CONC eeping track of such things as the ancestry of the Scandinavi an ruling f
2 CONC amilies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus I don't believe in throwing such people on the trash heap because , b
2 CONC y circumstance, they had nothing written about them by themselves o r b
2 CONC y their contemporaries.  I think that such people should be include d, b
2 CONC ut with an understanding that their information is, by necessity , obta
2 CONC ined in a different and less precise manner than other people i n one's f
2 CONC amily pedigree.
2 CONT
2 CONT By that token, you can divide them into "historical" figures proven b y s
2 CONC omething written during their lifetime or soon after, and "legendar y" f
2 CONC igures, who I think probably did exist, but are only proven by sag as a
2 CONC nd oral traditions written down in the 12th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of More [Ref: Wurts p421]
2 CONT Jarl of Hademerken [Ref: Moriarty Plantagenet p170]
2 CONT Eynstein the Eloquent, or Noisy [Ref: Watney #740]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research note: Eysteinn Glumra (of dubious historicity). Genealogies i n l
2 CONC ate Icelandic sources make Rognvaldr a son of Eysteinn Glumra, so n of Í
2 CONC varr, son of Hálfdan the Old [OI 1: 187 (from Landnáamabók iv.14 .1); O
2 CONC S 3]. These sources are not independent, but depend on each othe r in a w
2 CONC ay that is not entirely clear. Given the general unreliabilit y of the S
2 CONC candinavian saga sources for such an early period, there i s no good re
2 CONC ason to believe that Eysteinn or the earlier generations a re historica
2 CONC l. [Ref: Henry Project citing:
2 CONT
2 CONT OI = Gudbrand Vigfusson and F. York Powell, ed. & trans., Origines Isl a
2 CONC ndicae, 2 vols. (Oxford, 1905).
2 CONT OS = OrkS = Hermann Pálsson and Paul Edwards, ed. & trans., Orkneying a S
2 CONC aga (London, 1978). Citation is by chapter, with the page number i n pa
2 CONC rentheses]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regards,
2 CONT Curt
