2 CONT Note: From Wikipedia [http://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_the_Bald]: "
2 CONC Charles the Bald (Charles I of France and Holy Roman EmperorCharles I I
2 CONC ) (823-877), Roman emperor and king of the WestFranks, was the son o f t
2 CONC he emperor Louis the Pious and hissecond wife Judith. He was born w hen h
2 CONC is elder brothers werealready adults who had been assigned thei r own r
2 CONC egna, orsubkingdoms, by their father. The attempts made by Loui s thePi
2 CONC ous to assign Charles a kingdom, first Alemannia (829), thenth e countr
2 CONC y between the Meuse and the Pyrenees (839), at theexpense of h is half-
2 CONC brothers Lothair and Louis led to a risingon the part of thes e two aga
2 CONC inst the emperor. "The death of the emperor in 840 was the si gnal for t
2 CONC he outbreakof war between his sons. Charles allied himself w ith his br
2 CONC otherLouis the German to resist the pretensions of the emper orLothar, a
2 CONC nd the two allies conquered him at Fontenoy-en-Puisayeon 2 5 June 841. I
2 CONC n the following year, the two brothersconfirmed their all iance by the c
2 CONC elebrated oaths of Strassburg.The war was brought to a n end by the tre
2 CONC aty of Verdun in August843. The settlement gave Charle s the Bald the k
2 CONC ingdom of thewestern Franks, which practically corresp onded with what i
2 CONC s nowFrance, as far as the Meuse, the Sane and the R hone, with theadd
2 CONC ition of the Spanish March as far as the Ebro. "The f irst years of Cha
2 CONC rles' reign, up to the death of Lothar Iin 855, wer e comparatively pea
2 CONC ceful, and during them wascontinued the system of " confraternal govern
2 CONC ment" of the sons ofLouis the Pious, who had variou s meetings with one a
2 CONC nother, atCoblenz (848), at Meersen (851), and a t Attigny (854). In 85
2 CONC 8,Louis the German, summoned by disaffected nobl es to oustCharles, inv
2 CONC aded the western Frankish kingdom. Charles fle d toBurgundy, and was on
2 CONC ly saved by the help of the bishops and byth e fidelity of the Welfs, w
2 CONC ho were related to his mother,Judith. In 86 0 he in his turn tried to s
2 CONC eize the kingdom of hisnephew, Charles of P rovence, but met with a rep
2 CONC ulse. On thedeath of his nephew Lothar II i n 869, Charles tried to sei
2 CONC zeLothar's dominions, but by the treaty o f Mersen (870) wascompelled t
2 CONC o share them with Louis the German. "Besi des these family disputes, Ch
2 CONC arles had to struggle againstthe incessan t rebellions in Aquitaine and a
2 CONC gainst the Bretons.Led by their chief s Nomeno and Erispo, who inflic
2 CONC ted on theking the defeats of Ballo n (845) and Juvardeil (851), theBre
2 CONC tons were somewhat successful. Char les also fought againstthe Normans, w
2 CONC ho devastated the country in th e north of Gaul,the valleys of the Sein
2 CONC e and Loire, and even up to th e bordersof Aquitaine. Charles was sever
2 CONC al times compelled to purchase their retreat at a heavy price. Charles l
2 CONC ed various expeditionsagains t the invaders, and tried to put a barrier i
2 CONC n their way byhaving forti fied bridges built over all the rivers. "In 8
2 CONC 75, after the death of th e emperor Louis II, Charles theBald, supporte
2 CONC d by Pope John VIII, desc ended into Italy,receiving the royal crown at P
2 CONC avia and the imperial c rown atRome (29th December). Louis the German, w
2 CONC ho was also a candidat efor the succession of Louis II, revenged himsel
2 CONC f for Charles1ssucces s by invading and devastating his dominions. Char
2 CONC les wasrecalled to Fr ancia, and after the death of Louis the German(28
2 CONC th August 876), in hi s turn made an attempt to seize hiskingdom, but a
2 CONC t Andernach met wit h a shameful defeat (8thOctober 876). In the meanti
2 CONC me, John VIII, wh o was menaced by theSaracens, Continued to urge Charl
2 CONC es to come to Ita ly. Afterhaving taken at Quierzy the necessary measur
2 CONC es for safeguardi ngthe government of his dominions in his absence, Cha
2 CONC rles againcrosse d the Alps, but this expedition had been received with
2 CONC small enthusias m by the nobles, and even by Boso, Charles'sbrother-in-
2 CONC law, who had be en entrusted by him with thegovernment of Lombardy, and t
2 CONC hey refused t o come with their mento join the imperial army. At the sa
2 CONC me time Carlo man, son ofLouis the German, entered northern Italy. Char
2 CONC les, ill an d ingreat distress, started on his way back to Gaul, and di
2 CONC ed whilecro ssing the pass of the Mont Cenis on the 5th or 6th of Octob
2 CONC er877. "Cha rles was succeeded by his son, Louis, the child ofErmentrud
2 CONC e, daughte r of a count of Orleans, whom he had marriedin 842, and who h
2 CONC ad died i n 869. In 870 Charles had marriedRichilde, who was descended f
2 CONC rom a no ble family of Lorraine, butnone of the children whom he had by h
2 CONC er pla yed a part of anyimportance. Charles seems to have been a prince o
2 CONC f ed ucation andletters, a friend of the church, and conscious of the s
2 CONC uppo rt hecould find in the episcopate against his unruly nobles, for h
2 CONC echo se his councillors for preference from among the higherclergy, as i
2 CONC n t he case of Guenelon of Sens, who betrayed him, orof Hincmar of Reim
2 CONC s . "Liberally adapted and edited from a 1911 Encyclopedia."From Micros
2 CONC o ft's Funk & Wagnall's: "Also known as CHARLES THEBALD, French CHARLE S L
2 CONC E CHAUVE, German KARL DER KAHLE, king ofFrance (i.e., Francis Occid ent
2 CONC alis, the West Frankish kingdom)from 843 to 877 and Western empero r fr
2 CONC om 875 to 877. (He isreckoned as Charles II both of the Holy Roma n Emp
2 CONC ire and ofFrance.) "Charles II (Holy Roman Empire), called The Ba ld (8
2 CONC 23-877), HolyRoman emperor (875-877), and, as Charles I, king o f Franc
2 CONC e, bornin Frankfurt am Main, Germany. He was the fourth son o f HolyRom
2 CONC an Emperor Louis I; his mother, Louis's second wife, wasJudit h of Bava
2 CONC ria. Judith's determination to secure a kingdomfor her only s on led to c
2 CONC ivil war with Louis's other twosurviving sons, Holy Roman E mperor Loth
2 CONC air I and King Louis IIof Germany. The war ended with the s igning of t
2 CONC he Treaty ofVerdun in 843. Charles received the western por tion of the
2 CONC empire, which from this time may be called the kingdom ofFr ance, or th
2 CONC e West Frankish Kingdom. Charles was a weak ruler;the grea t nobles wer
2 CONC e rapidly becoming independent, and theVikings pillaged th e country wi
2 CONC thout meeting much resistancefrom Charles, who preferred t o buy them o
2 CONC ff. Nevertheless, whenHoly Roman Emperor Louis II died i n 875, Charles r
2 CONC eceived theimperial crown through the favor of Pope Jo hn VIII. Charles w
2 CONC assucceeded as king of France by his son, Louis II , but theimperial th
2 CONC rone was vacant until 881."Son of the emperor Loui s I the Pious and hi
2 CONC s second wife,Judith, Charles was the unwitting ca use of violent disco
2 CONC rd when,in 829, he was granted lands by his father ; Louis's actionprec
2 CONC ipitated a series of civil wars, lasting until 838 , in whichthe three s
2 CONC ons of his first marriage, Lothair I, Louis (theG erman), and Pepin, st
2 CONC rove to maintain or to increase the rightsthat th ey had been guarantee
2 CONC d by the succession settlement of817, the Ordinat io imperii. Pepin die
2 CONC d in 838, but after thedeath of Louis I in 840 th e civil war resumed a
2 CONC nd continueduntil Louis the German joined with Ch arles to force Lothai
2 CONC r toaccept the Treaty of Verdun in 843, by whic h Charles receivedall t
2 CONC he lands west of a line roughly following the S cheldt, theMeuse, the S
2 CONC a"ne, the eastern mountains of the Massif Centr al,and the lower reache
2 CONC s of the Rh"ne, and Louis the German andLothai r received respectively t
2 CONC he lands of the East Franks(Germany) and th e middle kingdom, lying bet
2 CONC ween the other two.Until 864 Charles's poli tical situation was precari
2 CONC ous becausefew vassals were loyal to him. H is lands suffered from raid
2 CONC s byNorthmen, who left only after receivin g bribes; he was defeatedby t
2 CONC he Bretons and, in 858, faced an invasio n by Louis theGerman. Yet he s
2 CONC ucceeded in gaining control of Aquitain e afterthe capture of Pepin's s
2 CONC on in 864; and, by the Treaty of Meerse n(870) with Louis the German, h
2 CONC e received western Lorraine.When Lothai r's son, the emperor Louis II, d
2 CONC ied in 875, Charleswent to Italy and w as crowned emperor on December 2
2 CONC 5 by PopeJohn VIII. In 876, after th e death of Louis the German, Charl
2 CONC esinvaded Louis's possessions but wa s defeated at Andernach byLouis's s
2 CONC on, Louis the Younger. Charles's de ath in the next yearoccurred when a
2 CONC nother son of Louis the German, Car loman, wasmarching against him and w
2 CONC hen his own major vassals were inr evolt.During Charles's reign some of t
2 CONC he splendours of the Carolingian renaissance were revived, and his clos
2 CONC e collaboration with thechurch e nhanced his prestige and authority.Ref
2 CONC erences: 1. Frederick Weis, "Anc estral Roots", (Seventh Edition 1992,1
