2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Altschul, *A Baronial Family in Medieval  England: The Clares , 1
2 CONC 217-1314*, Baltimore MD (The Johns Hopkins Press) 1965, concernin g the p
2 CONC artition of the de CLARE estates after the death of the last Gi lbert, p 1
2 CONC 70-171:  "Hugh Despenser and Eleanor [Gilbert's sister] rece ived the l
2 CONC ordship of Glamorgan, the most important of all the Clare ho ldings, al
2 CONC ong with Rotherfield in Sussex and scattered manors in Devo n and Somer
2 CONC set.  In addition, each heir acquired two-ninths of the lib erty of Kil
2 CONC kenny in Ireland, although there is no evidence that any o f them every v
2 CONC isited it.  (P) The death of the countess in the summe r of 1320 comple
2 CONC ted the division of the estates among th heirs. Maud p robably died on J
2 CONC uly 2, and the properties she held in dower must hav e been partitioned s
2 CONC hortly thereafter.  Each received an equal portio n of her third of Kil
2 CONC kenny.  The partition of the Clare estates has be en described as "the m
2 CONC ost important territorial upheaval of the reign. " [Denham-Young *Vita E
2 CONC dwardi Secundi, pp xii-xiii*]"
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> GS5F-NV
2 CONT 1  NAME Eleanor /de Clare/
2 CONT 2  SOUR S304868
2 CONT 3  DATA
2 CONT 4  TEXT Date of Import: Nov 30, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1326 Eleanor's husband, Hugh Despenser, was captured and executed b y
2 CONC barons opposed to the king. The following year Eleanor was kidnappe d a
2 CONC ndraped by a knight called William la Zouche. The punishment for ra pe w
2 CONC asdeath but as Zouche was one of Edward's most loyal knights, th e king
2 CONC refused to take action against him. Eleanor was forced to accep t Willi
2 CONC amla Zouche as her husband.
