1 NOTE  Sir John de Grey, KG, of Rotherfield, b. 9 Oct 1300, d. 1 Sep 1359, 1s t L
2 CONC ord Grey of Rotherfield, KG 23 Apr 1349; m. (1) by 1 Mar 1311/2, Ca tha
2 CONC rine, daughter and heir of Sir Bryan fitz Alan; m. (2) Avice, daugh ter o
2 CONC f Sir John Marmion, Lord Marmion.  [Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BARONY OF GREY OF ROTHERFIELD (I)
2 CONT
2 CONT SIR JOHN DE GREY, son and heir, was born at Rotherfield, 9 October, an d b
2 CONC aptized 1 November 1300 in the church there. During minority his la nds w
2 CONC ere granted to various persons by the Crown, and an inspection o f Hard
2 CONC wick and Rotherfield was ordered, 28 September 1317, to safeguar d the i
2 CONC nterests of the heir. He was summoned for Military Service fro m 25 Mar
2 CONC ch 1322 to 4 December 1341; to Councils 14 September 1340, 1 2 June 134
2 CONC 1, 25 February 1341/2, and 20 November 1342; and to Parlimen t from 15 N
2 CONC ovember 1338 to 1 December 1357, by writs directed Johann i de Grey de R
2 CONC otherfeld, whereby he is held to have become LORD GREY o f Rotherfield. H
2 CONC e had letters of protection, 18 July 1322, on going wi th the King to S
2 CONC cotland, and on 5 September 1325 on going with the Kin g beyond seas. O
2 CONC n 1 September 1330 he had a grant of free warren in hi s demense lands i
2 CONC n Bucks, Berks, and cos. Oxford, Northampton and York . On 16 March 133
2 CONC 1/2 Sir Geoffrey le Scropc, who made the King's speec h, declared that M
2 CONC ons. John de Grey of Rotherfield and Mons. William d e la Zouche of Ash
2 CONC by, then at variance, were commanded to do no violen ce, but neverthele
2 CONC ss hot words passed between them in the presence o f the King and his C
2 CONC ouncil, and Grey put his hand to his knife and par tly drew it. Both ha
2 CONC d been sent to prison, and Zouche had been release d on bail till this p
2 CONC arliament. Grey had been given into the custody o f William de Clinton, a
2 CONC nd was present in that custody. The King aske d advice of the Lords in r
2 CONC espect of such an excess in his presence, an d the Lords returned that t
2 CONC hey thought Zouche not guilty, and that Gre y should be imprisoned duri
2 CONC ng the King's pleasure, but begged the King 's mercy for him. He appear
2 CONC s to have been pardoned not long after. O n 19 August 1337 he had licen
2 CONC ce to alienate in mortmain to the Friar s Minors of Oxford a plot of la
2 CONC nd. On 20 January 1341/2 he was urgentl y summoned to London to treat w
2 CONC ith the Council for what he and his me n should receive for their stay a
2 CONC t  Berwick-on-Tweed, the King desirin g that he should have custody of t
2 CONC hat town. He was in the Crécy expedi tion in 1346 in the 2nd Division. O
2 CONC n 10 December 1346 he had licence t o crenellate his dwelling-places of R
2 CONC otherfield and Sculcoates. He wa s made K.G. at the institution of that o
2 CONC rder,and was Steward of the Ho usehold, certainly from 1350 to 1356. On 2
2 CONC 0 May 1354. he had a grant o f free warren in his demesne lands of Corn
2 CONC well and Kingham (Oxford).
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1stly, before 1 March 1311/2, Catherine, younger daughte r a
2 CONC nd coheir of Sir Brian (FITZALAN), LORD FITZALAN, by his 2nd wife , Mau
2 CONC d. He married 2ndly, Avice, daughter of John (MARMION), LORD MARM ION, a
2 CONC nd sister and coheir of Robert, LORD MARMION. He died at Rotherf ield, 1 S
2 CONC eptember 1359. His widow, as one of his executors, had a prot ection, 2
2 CONC 5 September 1359, in consideration of his good services as St eward of t
2 CONC he Household. Mention is made, 28 February 1362/3, of the se rvice in W
2 CONC est Tanfield Church according to the ordinance of Avice, lat e the wife o
2 CONC f John de Grey of Rotherfi6ld. She was living 20 March 137 8/9, having a
2 CONC s dower the manors of Cogges and Hardwick and a moiety o f the manor an
2 CONC d advowson of Fringford (Oxford).  [Complete Peerage VI: 145-7, (tanscr
2 CONC ibed by Dave Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John got a license to crenellate his manor in Rotherfield in 1347.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Grey, 2nd Baron Grey of Rotherfield, one of the founders of th e O
2 CONC rder of the Garter, b. 1300, who in the 15th Edward II, making proo f h
2 CONC is age, had livery of his lands; and in the 1st Edward III, was i n the w
2 CONC ars of Scotland. In the 6th of the same reign, upon some differ ences b
2 CONC etween his lordship and William le Zouch, of Haryngworth, anoth er grea
2 CONC t baron, which was heard before the king, Lord Grey, under th e irritat
2 CONC ion of the moment, drew his knife upon Lord Zouch in the roya l presenc
2 CONC e, whereupon both lords were committed to prison; but the Lor d Zouch w
2 CONC as soon afterwards released, while Lord Grey was remanded an d his land
2 CONC s seized upon by the crown. He was, however, within a shor t time, upon m
2 CONC aking submission, restored to favour; and in three year s afterwards we f
2 CONC ind his lordship in Scotland upon the king's service , being of the ret
2 CONC inue with Henry, Earl of Lancaster. From this perio d for several years
2 CONC , he was engaged in the French wars, and in the 20t h of Edward's reign
2 CONC , he obtained license to fortify his houses at Roth erfield Grey, co. O
2 CONC xford, and Sculcotes, co. York, with embattled wall s of lime and stone
2 CONC . The next year there being a tournament held at El tham, in Kent, amon
2 CONC gst other accoutrements prepared for that militar y exercise, his lords
2 CONC hip had a hood of white cloth embroidered with da ncing men in blue hab
2 CONC its, buttoned before with large pearls presente d to him by the king. I
2 CONC n the 26th Edward III, he was one of the commis sioners in the cos. Oxf
2 CONC ord and Berks for arraying and arming all men o f ability within those s
2 CONC hires and leading them against the king's enem ies, invasion at that ti
2 CONC me threatened by the French. In the next yea r he was steward of the ki
2 CONC ng's household and had summons to parliamen t from the 1st to the 29th E
2 CONC dward III, inclusive. His lordship m. 1st , Katherine, dau. and co-heir
2 CONC ess of Bryan Fitz-Alan, of Bedall, co. Yo rk, and had issue, John, his s
2 CONC uccessor, and Maud, m. 1st to John de Bo tetourt, of Weoley, and 2ndly, t
2 CONC o Thomas de Harcourt. He m. 2ndly, Avi ce, dau. and co-heir of John, Lo
2 CONC rd Marmion, and had two sons, John an d Robert, who both assumed their m
2 CONC other's name of Marmion. [Sir Bernar d Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeit
2 CONC ed and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Pee rage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p
2 CONC . 247, Grey, Barons Grey, of Rothe rfield, co. Oxford]
