1 NOTE  William de Longespee,eldest son of William, Earl of Salisbury, "common l
2 CONC y called," says Sir William Dugdale, "by Matthew Paris, and most of o u
2 CONC r other historians, Earl of Salisbury, but erroneous, for all record s w
2 CONC herein mention is made of his do not give him that title, but calle d h
2 CONC im barely William Longespee. Nay, there is an old chronicle who sai th e
2 CONC xpressly, that, in anno 12233 (17th Henry III), he was girt with t he s
2 CONC word of knighthood, but not made Earl of Salisbury." This Willia m made a p
2 CONC ilgrimage to the Holy Land in 1240, and again in 1247, havin g assumed t
2 CONC he cross for a second pilgrimage, proceeded to Rome, and th us preferre
2 CONC d a suit to the sovereign pontiff. "Sir, you see that I a m signed with t
2 CONC he cross and am on my journey with the King of France t o fight in this p
2 CONC ilgrimage. My name is great and of note, viz., Willia m Longespee, but m
2 CONC y estate is slender, for the king of England, my kin sman and liege lor
2 CONC d, hath bereft me of the title of earl and of that e state, but this he d
2 CONC id judiciously, and not in displeasure, and by th e impulse of his will
2 CONC ; therefore I do not blame him for it. Howbeit , I am necessitated to h
2 CONC ave recourse to your holiness for favour, desi ring your assistance in t
2 CONC his distress. We see here (quoth he) that Ear l Richard (of Cornwall) w
2 CONC ho, though he is not signed with the cross, y et, through the especial g
2 CONC race of your holiness, he hath got very muc h money from those who are s
2 CONC igned, and therefore, I, who am signed an d in want, do intreat the lik
2 CONC e favour." The pope taking into considera tion the elegance of his mann
2 CONC er, the efficacy of his reasoning, and th e comeliness of his person, c
2 CONC onceded in part what he desire; whereupo n he received above 1,000 mark
2 CONC s from those who had been so signed. I n about two years after this, an
2 CONC no 1249, having received the blessin g of his noble mother, Ela, then a
2 CONC bbess of Lacock, he commenced his jo urney at the head of a company of 2
2 CONC 00 English horse and, being receive d with great respect by the king of F
2 CONC rance, joined that monarch's army . In Palestine he became subsequently p
2 CONC re-eminently distinguished an d fell, in 1250, in a great conflict with t
2 CONC he Saracens, near Damieta , having previously kill above 100 of the ene
2 CONC my with his own hand. I t was reported that, the bight before the battl
2 CONC e, his mother Ela, th e abbess, saw in a vision the heavens open and he
2 CONC r son armed at all pa rts (whose shield she well knew), received with j
2 CONC oy by the angels. Rem embering the occurrence when the news of his deat
2 CONC h reached her in si x months after, she held up her hands, and, with a c
2 CONC heerful countenanc e, said, "I, thy handmaid, give thanks to thee, O Lo
2 CONC rd, that out of m y sinful flesh thou hast caused such a champion again
2 CONC st thine enemie s to be born." It was also said that, in 1252, when mes
2 CONC sengers were se nt to the Soldan of Babylon for redemption of those who h
2 CONC ad been take n prisoner, he thus addressed them -- "I marvel at you, Ch
2 CONC ristians, wh o reverence the bones of the dead, why you inquire not for t
2 CONC hose of th e renowned and right noble William Longespee, because there b
2 CONC e many th ings reported of them (whether fabulous or not I cannot say), v
2 CONC iz., th at, in the dark of the night there have been appearances at his t
2 CONC omb , and that to some, who called upon his God, many things were besto
2 CONC we d from Heaven. For which cause, and in regard of his great worth and n o
2 CONC bility of birth, we have caused his body to be here intombed." Whereu p
2 CONC on the messenger desiring it, the remains were delivered to them by t h
2 CONC e Soldan, and thence conveyed to Acre where they were buried in the c h
2 CONC urch of St. Cross. This eminent and heroic personage m. Idonea, dau . a
2 CONC nd heir of Richard de Camville, and had issue, William de Longespee , h
2 CONC is son and heir. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited an d E
2 CONC xtinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 1 68, d
2 CONC 'Evereux, Earls of Salisbury]
