1 NOTE  William de Braose, born perhaps c1175, died Corfe or Windsor Castle 12 1
2 CONC 0, of starvation by order of King John, son of William de Braose, die d 1
2 CONC 211, Lord of Bramber, Sussex, by his wife Maud de St Valery.  [Magn a C
2 CONC harta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William did not accompany King Richard on Crusade but fought with Kin g J
2 CONC ohn against Philip in Normandy (1203/4).  King John demanded Willia m a
2 CONC s a hostage for his father's loyalty in 1208.  His mother Maud refu sed a
2 CONC nd the fled to Ireland.  In 1210 John prepared an expedition to I relan
2 CONC d. Maud and William escaped Ireland, but were apprehended in Scot land. W
2 CONC illiam the father was in Wales at the time.  It is believed tha t Maud a
2 CONC nd William were starved to death at Windsor Castle (Some say C orfe).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William, who perished by starvation with his mother at Windsor m. Maud , d
2 CONC au. of the Earl of Clare, with whom he had the town of Buckingham , in f
2 CONC rank marriage, and left a son, John. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant , Abey
2 CONC ant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., Lond on, En
2 CONC gland, 1883, p. 72, Braose, Baron Braose, of Gower]
