1 NOTE  Seneschal of Gascony appointed by his brother in law, King Henry III o f E
2 CONC ngland. Simon de Montfort is said to have first sought an asylum i n En
2 CONC gland from the hostility of Blanche, Queen of France, and to hav e obta
2 CONC ined a restitution of the Earldom of Leicester and stewardship o f Engl
2 CONC and from King Henry III through the petition of his brother, Alm aric [
2 CONC Amaury], then Earl of Montfort and constable of France. Certai n it is, h
2 CONC owever, that in 1232 (16th Henry III), he bore the title o f Earl of Le
2 CONC icester and had obtained a grant of all his mother's inher itance in
2 CONT England from his brother. In 1236, his lordship officiated as stewar d a
2 CONC t the nuptials of Henry III, and held the ewer in which the king wa she
2 CONC d. In two years afterwards, he obtained the hand of the king's sist er, E
2 CONC leanor, widow of William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, the marriag e cerem
2 CONC ony being performed by Walter, one of the royal chaplains at We stminst
2 CONC er, "within a little chappel at the corner of the king's chambe r." Thi
2 CONC s marriage was, however, opposed by the princess's other brothe r, Rich
2 CONC ard, Earl of Cornwall (afterwards King of the Romans), and th e kingdom a
2 CONC t large, because the lady had made in her widowhood a vow o f chastity i
2 CONC n the presence of Edmund, Archbishop of Canterbury and sev eral of the n
2 CONC obility. So strongly did public discontent manifest itsel f that the ea
2 CONC rl was obliged to repair in person to Rome for the purpos e of obtainin
2 CONC g a dispensation, which with considerable difficulty he a t length acco
2 CONC mplished; and returning to England was most graciously re ceived at cou
2 CONC rt by the king, who appointed him his chief counsellor. N otwithstandin
2 CONC g this, however, William de Abingdon, a Dominican friar , and many othe
2 CONC r of the clergy continued to exclaim against the marria ge. But before t
2 CONC he close of the same year, he experienced the capric e of royal favour. T
2 CONC he king observing him and his countess amongst th e nobility who attend
2 CONC ed the queen at her purification, called him an e xcommunicated person a
2 CONC nd prohibited his entering the church. "Which su dden unkindness," says D
2 CONC ugdale, "much dismaying him, he went away by w ater to Winchester House
2 CONC , which (the bishop being dead) the king had l ent him. But there he co
2 CONC uld not be permitted to stay, the king in grea t wrath causing him to b
2 CONC e put out of doors. Whereupon he returned sorr owing and weeping, yet c
2 CONC ould not appease his anger, the king plainly t elling him that he had a
2 CONC bused his sister before marriage, and that, th ough he afterward gave h
2 CONC er to him for a wife, it was unwillingly, an d to avoid scandal. Upbrai
2 CONC ding him that to ratify this, his unlawful m arriage, he went to Rome a
2 CONC nd there corrupted that court with large bri bes and promises, adding t
2 CONC hat, having failed in payment of the money , he ought justly
2 CONT be excommunicated." This storm ultimately drove his lordship from th e k
2 CONC ingdom, but only for a short period, as we find him returning in 12 40, a
2 CONC nd having then an honourable reception from the king and all hi s court
2 CONC . Soon after this he made a journey to Jerusalem, having previo usly di
2 CONC sposed of one of his woods to the knights hospitallers and cano ns of L
2 CONC eicester for somewhat less than £1000 to defray part of the nec essary e
2 CONC xpenses of the undertaking. Henceforward, he appears for a ser ies of y
2 CONC ears to have enjoyed the high favour of the king and to have f ully mer
2 CONC ited it by his eminent services.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 32nd Henry III [1248], his lordship was appointed commander-in- c
2 CONC hief of the forces in Gascony and in the end of that year he sat in t h
2 CONC e great convention of parliament held at London; about which time h e o
2 CONC btained from the king a grant of the custody of Kenilworth Castle , for E
2 CONC leanor, his wife, to hold during her life, and returning into G ascony, h
2 CONC e forced Guaston de Bearne, who had raised the standard of re bellion, t
2 CONC o an honourable truce. The earl came back to England the nex t year and w
2 CONC as received at court with great honour. Soon after which , in fulfillme
2 CONC nt of a vow he had made as penance for his marriage, h e began a journe
2 CONC y to the Holy Land, and in the 34th of the same reig n returned safely w
2 CONC ith his brother-in-law, Richard, Earl of Cornwall , and others. For the t
2 CONC wo following years he was actively and victorio usly employed in Gascon
2 CONC y until the king, hearkening to complaints agai nst him for his cruelty a
2 CONC nd oppression, which appear to have been unsu stainable, removed him fr
2 CONC om the seneschalship of that country. Upon th e subsequent insurrection o
2 CONC f the barons against the king, the Earl o f Leicester, siding with the f
2 CONC ormer, was appointed their general-in-ch ief, in which character he fou
2 CONC ght the great battle of Lewes where th e royal army sustained so signal a d
2 CONC efeat the king himself being mad e prisoner with Prince Edward, his son
2 CONC , his brother, Richard, King o f the Romans, and many other personages o
2 CONC f eminence attached to his ca use. This victory placing the government i
2 CONC n the hands of the earl an d his adherents, himself, the bishop of Chic
2 CONC hester, the Earl of Glouce ster, and a few others of less note were nom
2 CONC inated to discharge the ex ecutive functions. One of the earliest acts o
2 CONC f the usurpation was to s ummon a parliament in the king's name by writ
2 CONC s dated 24 December, 49t h Henry III [1265], directed to the bishops an
2 CONC d abbots and to such la y lords as could be relied upon by which, signi
2 CONC fying "the realm to b e then in peace and quiet, and the desire of the k
2 CONC ing to establish th e same to the honour of God, and benefit of his peo
2 CONC ple," they were sum moned to meet at London on the octaves of St. Hilar
2 CONC y, there to sit i n parliament, "to treat and give their advice." At th
2 CONC e same time, prec epts were issued to the sheriffs ordering them to ret
2 CONC urn two knights f or each county; to the cities and boroughs the like n
2 CONC umber of citizen s and burgesses; and to the barons of the Cinque Ports
2 CONC , a certain numb er of their discreetest men for the same purpose. This i
2 CONC s deemed the f irst precedent of a parliament, such as ever since has b
2 CONC een establishe d, and Sir William Dugdale thus speculates upon the caus
2 CONC es of the revo lution -- "If I may be so bold as to give my opinion, wh
2 CONC at reasons the se potent rebels then had, thus to alter the former anci
2 CONC ent usage, I s hall take leave to conjecture that it was because they, d
2 CONC iscerning wha t large retinues the nobility and other great men in thos
2 CONC e elder time s had , as also a great number of the king's tenants in ca
2 CONC pite, then c alled barones minores, it might have proved dangerous to t
2 CONC hemselves t o permit such a multitude to come together."  The new gover
2 CONC nment did n ot, however, endure long, for a breach taking place between t
2 CONC he two ch iefs, Leicester and Gloucester, the arms of those powerful pe
2 CONC rsons wer e directed against each other, and Prince Edward effecting hi
2 CONC s escap e about the same time, the Earl of Gloucester reared the royal s
2 CONC tandar d and formed a junction with the forces of the prince. With this a
2 CONC rmy , marching towards Kenilworth, they surprised young Simon Montfort, t
2 CONC h e earl's son, and made prisoners of no less than thirteen of his chie f a
2 CONC dherents, almost without resistance. Elated with this triumph, the y pr
2 CONC oceeded to Evesham, where the Earl of Leicester and his great forc e la
2 CONC y, expecting the arrival of his son whose banners the royal arms a s a s
2 CONC tratagem of war alone displayed and thereby completely deceived t his a
2 CONC ble commander. His lordship undismayed, however, drew out his arm y in o
2 CONC rder of battle and, fighting gallantly to the last, fell in th e midst o
2 CONC f his enemies, when victory declared for the royal cause. I t is said t
2 CONC hat, when the earl discerned the superiority and dispositio n of the ro
2 CONC yalist forces, he swore "by the arm of St. James (his usua l exclamatio
2 CONC n), they have done discreetly, but this they learned fro m me; let us t
2 CONC herefore commend our souls to God because our bodies ar e theirs." Neve
2 CONC rtheless, encouraging his men, he told them, "it was fo r the laws of t
2 CONC he land, yea, the cause of God and justice, that they w ere to fight." T
2 CONC he principal persons slain in the memorable engagemen t were the Earl h
2 CONC imself, Henry de Montfort, his eldest son, Hugh le De spenser, then jus
2 CONC tice of England, Ralph Basset, of Drayton, and abou t one hundred and s
2 CONC ixty knights and many other gentlemen of his party . Amongst the prison
2 CONC ers were Guy de Montfort, a younger son of the ear l; John Fitz-John, H
2 CONC umphrey de Bohun, the younger; John de Vesci, Pete r de Montfort, junr.
2 CONC , and Nicholas de Segrave. The body of the Earl wa s removed from the f
2 CONC ield of battle by some of his friends upon an ol d ladder covered with a p
2 CONC oor torn cloth, and thus conveyed to the abbe y of Evesham where, folde
2 CONC d in a sheet, it was committed to the grave . But within a short time, s
2 CONC ome of the monks alleging that the earl, b eing an excommunicated perso
2 CONC n and attainted of treason, his remains we re unworthy of Christian bur
2 CONC ial, the body was taken up and interred i n a remote place known but to f
2 CONC ew. Thus fell, in 1264, Simon de Montfo rt, Earl of Leicester; one of t
2 CONC he most eminent soldiers and statesme n of the period in which he lived a
2 CONC nd, under his attainder, the earldo m became extinct. his widow, Eleano
2 CONC r, the king's sister, it is state d that, after the fatal battle of Eve
2 CONC sham, she fled into France and
2 CONT took up her abode in the nunnery of the order of preachers, at Montarg e
2 CONC s, which had been founded by her husband's sister.  Henry fell at Eve s
2 CONC ham, leading the van of the baronial army. Simon, who for some time g a
2 CONC llantly defended the castle of Kenilworth, was eventually made prison e
2 CONC r in the Isle of Ely by Prince Edward; afterwards effecting his escap e
2 CONC , he fled into France and, in 1270, being at Viterbuirm, in Italy, h e j
2 CONC oined with his brother, Guy, in the murder of their cousin, Henry , eld
2 CONC est son of Richard, King of the Romans, in the church of St. Silv ester
2 CONC , as the prince assisted at mass. Guy fought in the van of the ba ronia
2 CONC l army at Evesham, and being made prisoner, was confined in Dove r Cast
2 CONC le from which escaping, he fled into Tuscany and there, acquirin g high r
2 CONC eputation as a soldier, he obtained the dau. and heiress of th e Earl R
2 CONC ufus for his wife. Meeting with Prince Henry, son of the Kin g of the R
2 CONC omans, Guy and his brother, Simon slew him in revenge in th e church of S
2 CONC t. Silvester, at Viterbuirm, for which barbarous at, bein g first excom
2 CONC municated by Pope Gregory
2 CONT X, he was thrown into prison but released in 1282 by Pope Martin II, a n
2 CONC d placed at the head of an army, in which situation he displayed hi s c
2 CONC haracteristic prowess. He subsequently, at the decease of his wife' s f
2 CONC ather, returned to Tuscany and inherited a very considerable fortun e. C
2 CONC harles I, King of Naples, made him Count de Nola. He d. in 1288, l eavi
2 CONC ng by Margaret, his wife, dau. of Rodolph, Count de Languillara, o n on
2 CONC ly dau., Anastasia de Montfort, Countess de Nola, m. to Raymond de s Ur
2 CONC sins. Almaric, who, when conveying his sister from France to be m . to L
2 CONC lewelyn, Prince of Wales, was taken prisoner with her at sea an d suffe
2 CONC red a long imprisonment. He was at last, however, restored to l iberty, a
2 CONC nd his posterity are said to have flourished in England unde r the name o
2 CONC f Wellesbourne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor, b. about Michaelmas, 1252, at Kenilworth, m., by proxy, earl y i
2 CONC n 1275, and in person at Worcester, 13 October, 1278, Llewelyn ap G rif
2 CONC fith, Prince of North Wales. By Llewelyn ap Griffith, who was slai n 10 D
2 CONC ecember, 1282, she had issue, two daus., Princess Catherine, hei ress o
2 CONC f the monarchs of North Wales, m. Philip ap Ivor, Lord of Cardig an, wh
2 CONC o was probably dead 20 September, 1334, the date of a quo warran to aga
2 CONC inst his brother, Owen ap Llewelyn; and a younger dau., Princes s Gwenl
2 CONC lian, b. 19 June, 1281, was a nun of Sempringham, and d. ther e 7 June, 1
2 CONC 337. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Ex tinct Peera
2 CONC ges, Burke's Peerage Ltd, London, England, 1883, p. 376-77 , Montfort, E
2 CONC arls of Leicester]
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Montfort, Earl of Leicester (b. c. 1208, Montfort, Ile-de-Fra n
2 CONC ce, Fr.--d. Aug. 4, 1265, Evesham, Worcestershire, Eng.), leader of t h
2 CONC e baronial revolt against King Henry III and ruler of England for les s t
2 CONC han a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Montfort, wholly French by birth and education, was the son o f S
2 CONC imon de Montfort l'Amaury, leader of the crusade against the hereti cal A
2 CONC lbigenses. On coming of age, he renounced to his eldest brother , Amaur
2 CONC y, his claims on the family lands in return for the sole righ t to revi
2 CONC ve the Montfort claim to the English earldom of Leicester. Th is claim d
2 CONC erived from his mother, Amicia, sister of Robert IV (died 12 04), the l
2 CONC ast Beaumont earl of Leicester, whose lands had been divide d between A
2 CONC micia and her younger sister Margaret, countess of Winchest er. King Jo
2 CONC hn had recognized Simon's father as earl (c. 1205) but ha d deprived hi
2 CONC m as a French subject (1207), and the Montfort claim ha d then lapsed. S
2 CONC imon came to England in 1229 and, helped by his cousi n Ranulf, earl of C
2 CONC hester, the tenant of the confiscated estates, obta ined the honour of L
2 CONC eicester and did homage to Henry III in 1231, thou gh he was not formal
2 CONC ly styled earl of Leicester
2 CONT until April 1239. He speedily became one of Henry's favourites, receiv i
2 CONC ng an annual fee of 500 marks to compensate for the divided inheritan c
2 CONC e and exercising the hereditary stewardship at the coronation of Quee n E
2 CONC leanor (Eleanor of Provence; 1236). Henry arranged for his sister E lea
2 CONC nor to marry Simon on Jan. 7, 1238, thus breaking Eleanor's earlie r vo
2 CONC w of chastity and offending the English noblemen, who were not con sult
2 CONC ed. Henry's brother, Richard, earl of Cornwall, led an angry baron ial p
2 CONC rotest, and Henry, alarmed, turned against Simon and Eleanor, dri ving t
2 CONC hem from England (August 1239). Simon went on crusade (1240-42 ) with R
2 CONC ichard, with whom he was now reconciled, and won great prestig e among t
2 CONC he lords of the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem, who asked their a bsentee k
2 CONC ing, the emperor Frederick II, to appoint Simon as his vicero y there. R
2 CONC eturning to England, Simon joined Henry's disastrous invasio n of Franc
2 CONC e (1242), winning distinction by covering Henry's escape aft er his def
2 CONC eat at Saintes. Reconciled with Henry, and accepting an unfa vourable s
2 CONC ettlement of Countess Eleanor's dower claims, Simon now mad e Kenilwort
2 CONC h Castle (a royal grant) his headquarters. He cultivated th e friendshi
2 CONC p of the
2 CONT radical reformer Robert Grosseteste, bishop of Lincoln, and took Rober t
2 CONC 's friend, the Oxford Franciscan Adam de Marisco, as spiritual direct o
2 CONC r. Although regarded as a king's man, Simon was one of the committe e o
2 CONC f 12 appointed to handle the acute crisis of 1244 between Henry an d hi
2 CONC s angry barons. He also took part in many important embassies to t he F
2 CONC rench, papal, and imperial courts, and as a result he won many inf luen
2 CONC tial friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1248 Henry asked Simon to pacify the English-held Duchy of Gascony , i
2 CONC n southwestern France. Simon, eager to join Louis IX's crusade, acc ept
2 CONC ed reluctantly, stipulating for full powers as regent for seven yea rs, w
2 CONC ithout fear of recall and with full refund of expenses incurred . Treat
2 CONC ing the Gascon nobles as faithless rebels outside the law, he r uthless
2 CONC ly crushed the revolt and restored order; the Gascons appeale d to Henr
2 CONC y, accusing Simon of illegal procedure and oppression and thr eatening t
2 CONC o renew their revolt. The matter was complicated by Simon' s personal c
2 CONC ontest with Gaston de Béarn, the leading rebel. Henry, fri ghtened, rec
2 CONC alled Simon for trial on the rebels' charges; the Englis h magnates acq
2 CONC uitted him (1252), and he returned to Gascony to suppres s the renewed r
2 CONC evolt, but Henry now terminated his lieutenancy. Simon , accepting a pa
2 CONC rtial financial settlement, withdrew to France, thoug h Henry had to im
2 CONC plore his help in his own campaign against the rebel s in 1253. Such wa
2 CONC s Simon's international reputation that when Louis I X's mother, Blanch
2 CONC e of Castile, died (November 1252) while Louis was s till on crusade, t
2 CONC he French magnates invited Simon to succeed her as r egent. King Henry'
2 CONC s behaviour over Gascony, though not wholly unjustif ied, convinced Sim
2 CONC on that Henry was unfit to rule, and the King's disa strous undertaking
2 CONC , at Pope Innocent IV's behest, to conquer Sicily fo r his son Edmund, s
2 CONC trengthened this conviction. Discussions with Gross eteste, Marisco, an
2 CONC d other Franciscan intellectuals had fired Simon' s mind with visions o
2 CONC f a new order in both church and state, and he jo ined the other leadin
2 CONC g English barons in forcing upon Henry the revolu tionary Provisions of O
2 CONC xford (June 1258). The reformers began well, bu t by October 1259 divis
2 CONC ions appeared between the conservative wing, le d by Richard de Clare, e
2 CONC arl of Gloucester, that sought only to limit a buses of royal power, an
2 CONC d the radical element, led by Simon, that soug ht to bind the entire ba
2 CONC ronage to observe the reforms forced upon th e King and his officers. S
2 CONC imon exacerbated the quarrel by his arrogan t vehemence and put himself i
2 CONC n the wrong by attempting to use Henry' s subordination to secure settl
2 CONC ement of his own and his wife's justifi able personal claims on Henry. H
2 CONC enry, allying with the Gloucester fact ion, shattered baronial unity ea
2 CONC rly in 1260, and Simon emerged leadin g the extremist defenders of the r
2 CONC eforms. By October 1261 Henry had is olated Simon, who went abroad; but t
2 CONC he King's annulment of the Provisi ons, after he hadreceived papal abso
2 CONC lution from his oath to observe th em, revived general disaffection (12
2 CONC 62), and Simon returned (April 126 3) to lead a rebellion that restored t
2 CONC he Provisions (July 1263). But b aronial unity had vanished, and, despi
2 CONC te passionate support from the l esser barons, the county knights, the m
2 CONC en of London and the Cinque Por ts, and many clergy, Simon was forced t
2 CONC o accept arbitration by Louis I X (December 1263). By the Mise of Amien
2 CONC s (January 1264) Louis totall y annulled the Provisions and all consequ
2 CONC ent reforms: Simon rejected t he award and after unsuccessfully attempt
2 CONC ing direct negotiations, defe ated Henry at Lewes (May 14, 1264), captu
2 CONC ring Henry and his son, the l ord Edward. Simon then governed England b
2 CONC y military dictatorship, stri ving unsuccessfully for a legal basis of c
2 CONC onsent, both by negotiation s with Henry's supporters and by calling re
2 CONC presentatives of both shire s and boroughs to Parliament (1265) to coun
2 CONC terbalance his lack of baro nial support. But his monopolization of pow
2 CONC er alienated his chief ally , the young Gilbert de Clare, earl of Glouc
2 CONC ester, who joined the royal ist Marcher lords and secured Lord Edward's e
2 CONC scape at Hereford (May 12 65). By rapid and skillful maneuvering, Edwar
2 CONC d isolated Simon behind t he Severn, destroyed at Kenilworth (August 1) t
2 CONC he large army coming t o his rescue, and trapped Simon's little force a
2 CONC t Evesham (Aug. 4, 126 5), slaying Simon and most of his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Encyclopedia Britannica, Treatise on Montfort, Simon
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists, 7th Edition, by Frederi c
2 CONC k Lewis Weis, additions by Walter Lee Shippard Jr., 1999
