2 CONT Sources: RC 233; Kings and Queens of Britain; AF; Some Prominent Virgi n
2 CONC ia Families; Ancestral Roots 1-13; Kirby; Pfafman; Young; Kings, Quee n
2 CONC s, Bones, and Bastards by David Hilliam.
2 CONT Roots: Egbert, born 775, died after 19 Nov. 838, son of Eahlmund, Kin g o
2 CONC f Kent. King of Wessex, 802-827, and was the first king of all Engl and
2 CONC , 827-836; married Raedburh. The male line of kings descends from h im t
2 CONC o Edward the Confessor and the female line to the present time.
2 CONT
2 CONT RC: King of Wessex 802-827. First King of England, 827-839. Male lin e o
2 CONC f kings descends from him to Edward the Confessor and the female li ne t
2 CONC o the present time (1992).
2 CONT
2 CONT VA Families: "He united the seven kingdoms of Britain, known as the He p
2 CONC tarchy, into the one Kingdom of England. Prior to that event he was K i
2 CONC ng ofthe Saxon Kingdom of Wessex."
2 CONT K&Q: Egbert (III), King of Wessex 802.
2 CONT Kirby: Ecgberht.
2 CONT Young: Egbert, died 839, King of Wessex, later of England.
2 CONT Hilliam: Egbert, King of Wessex. Reigned 802-839. "The seven Saxon kin g
2 CONC doms each lasted for about three hundred years, until finally King Eg b
2 CONC ert of Wessex [West Saxony] was hailed as Bretwalda' or sole ruler o f B
2 CONC ritain' round about the year AD 825. For this reason, Egbert is gen era
2 CONC lly recognised as the first English king..." See page 13.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: In 825 Egbert annexed Kent, Surrey, Essex and Sussex. In 829 Mer c
2 CONC ia fell to Egbert and Northumbria soon acknowledged his lordship. A s t
2 CONC he overlord of all the English Kings he is considered the first Kin g o
2 CONC f England. "The Oxford History of Britain" Kenneth O. Morgan. The H ous
2 CONC e of Wessex lineage traced from "The Oxford Illustrated History o f the B
2 CONC ritish Monarchy" John Cannon & Ralp Griffiths.
