2 CONT William de Warenne, Named in the Magna Charta 1215, 6th Earl of Surrey ,
2 CONC d. London 27 May 1240, son of Hamelin (bastard son of GeoffreyPlantag e
2 CONC net) and Isabella de Warenne; m. (2) before 13 Oct 1225 MaudMarshal .  [
2 CONC Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Warren (Plantagenet), Earl of Warren and Surrey, sided at t h
2 CONC ecommencement of the contest between King John and the barons and fo r a
2 CONC long time thereafter with his royal kinsman, but eventually joine d the
2 CONC banner of Lewis of France. On the death of King John, however, he retur
2 CONC ned to his allegiance and swore fealty to King Henry III, at thes olemn n
2 CONC uptials of which monarch he had the honour of serving the king, at the b
2 CONC anquet, with his royal cup in the Earl of Arundel's stead, who ,being i
2 CONC n minority, could not perform that office as he had not been g irtwith t
2 CONC he sword of knighthood. His lordship m. 1st, Lady Maud de Alb ini,dau. o
2 CONC f the Earl of Arundel, but by her ladyship had no issue. H e m.2ndly, M
2 CONC aud, dau., of William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, and wido w ofHugh Bigo
2 CONC t, Earl of Norfolk, by whom he had John, his successor, a ndIsabel. He d
2 CONC . in 1240, and was s. by his son, John de Warren(Plantag enet), Earl of W
2 CONC arren and Surrey. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant,Abeyant , Forfeited, and E
2 CONC xtinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London,1883 , p. 569, Warren, E
2 CONC arls of Surrey]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------- -
2 CONC --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT EARLDOM OF SURREY (VI) 1202
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM (DE WARENNE), EARL OF SURREY, son and heir. On 12 May 1202 the K
2 CONC ing advised his father's tenants that he had taken his homage. On 19A p
2 CONC ril 1205 he had a grant of Grantham and Stamford, Lincs, to compensat e
2 CONC him for the loss of his lands in Normandy. In 1206 he owed 100 mark s f
2 CONC oran advance made to him in Poitou, and 100 marks for robes provide d f
2 CONC orhim there. On 30 November 1206 he was directed to escort the Kin g of
2 CONC Scots to York. On 20 August 1212 the custody of the castles of Bam burg
2 CONC hand Newcastle-on-Tyne was committed to him and 2 others. In May 1 213 h
2 CONC ewas with the King at Dover, and was a party to John's submissio n to t
2 CONC hePope and resignation of his crown. In January 1214/5 he was am ong th
2 CONC osewho came to London with the Archbishop to discuss grievances , but o
2 CONC n 10May 1215 he was security for the King in his promise of con cession
2 CONC s tothe barons. On 24 May 1215 he took part with the barons i n the sei
2 CONC zure ofLondon; and on 15 June at Runnymede was one of those w ho advise
2 CONC d the Kingto grant Magna Carta; and he afterwards swore to ob serve the m
2 CONC andates ofthe 25 barons. On 16 May 1216 he was appointed Wa rden of the C
2 CONC inquePorts; but soon afterwards he joined Louis of France . On 22 June 1
2 CONC 217,however, he had renewed his fealty to the King and o n 24 August to
2 CONC ok partin the naval battle in which Eustace the Monk wa s defeated and s
2 CONC lain. In1217 he was sheriff of Surrey. In February 121 7/8 there was a d
2 CONC isputepending between the Earl and Engelard de Cigoig ny as to the coun
2 CONC ty ofSurrey. In 1220 he was appointed to meet the Kin g of Scots at Ber
2 CONC wickafter Whitsun, and in 1221 was granted the scutag e of Biham. In Fe
2 CONC bruary1222/3 he was on pilgrimage to St. James (Santi ago), in October 1
2 CONC 223 wasin North Wales, and in November on pilgrimag e to St. John. In A
2 CONC ugust 1224he was with the King at Bedford. On 5 Apr il 1225 he had ceas
2 CONC ed to holdthe castle of Hastings. On 30 June he wa s warned that he mus
2 CONC t observe theKing's liberties. In 1227 he joined t he Earl of Cornwall a
2 CONC t Stamford inhis revolt against the King; but a t Christmas he was with t
2 CONC he King atYork. In 1229 he was about to mak e a voyage on the King's se
2 CONC rvice. InJune 1230 he was taking an assiz e of arms in Sussex, and in J
2 CONC uly waswarden of the ports and seacoast o f Suffolk, Essex and Norfolk; a
2 CONC nd inthe same year he was appointed a j usticiar of England. In August 1
2 CONC 231 hewas in Wales. In February 1231/ 2 he was called to the Council to t
2 CONC reat onmatters concerning Llewelin , prince of Aberfraw; and in the sam
2 CONC e year wasone of the four Earls ap pointed to keep Hubert de Burgh in c
2 CONC ustody atDevizes Castle. In June 1 234 he, with another, was granted th
2 CONC e castles ofBramber and Knapp. A t the Coronation of Queen Eleanor at W
2 CONC estminster, 20January 1235/6, h e acted as Butler in place of his son-i
2 CONC n-law, the Earlof Arundel (i) ; and he claimed unsuccessfully to carry o
2 CONC ne of the Swords.In 1237 h e joined the King's council, and in 1238 was s
2 CONC ent to Oxford tocompos e the quarrel between the scholars and the Roman
2 CONC s who hadaccompanied t he Legate. He was reprimanded by Robert, Bishop o
2 CONC f Lincoln,for havin g had mass celebrated in his hall at Grantham thoug
2 CONC h it wasunconsecrat ed. He married, 1stly, Maud (m). He married, 2ndly, b
2 CONC efore 13October 1 225, Maud, widow of Hugh (BIGOD), EARL OF NORFOLK, da
2 CONC ughter and(afte r the death s.p. of her 5 brothers) coheir of William (
2 CONC MARSHAL), 4thEA RL OF PEMBROKE, by Isabel, daughter and (after her brot
2 CONC her's death inc hildhood) heir of Richard (FITZGILBERT), 2nd EARL OF PE
2 CONC MBROKE. He died 27 May 1240 in London and was buried before the high al
2 CONC tar in LewesPri ory. Maud died between 1 and 7 April 1248.  [Complete P
2 CONC eerageXII/1:500 -03, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT (i) Arundel had been excommunicated by the Archbishop of Canterbury,be c
2 CONC ause when the archbishop hunted in the Earl's forest in Sussex, theEa r
2 CONC l had taken his dogs.
2 CONT
2 CONT (m) She is alleged to have been daughter of an Earl of Arundel, to hav e
2 CONC d. 6 Feb 1215/6 and to have been buried in the chapter-house of Lewes P
2 CONC riory.  The date 6 Feb. (no year) as the date of death of a Maud,Coun t
2 CONC ess de Warenne, is confirmed by an Obit in the Beauchief AbbeyRegiste r
2 CONC .
