1 NOTE  Name Suffix:<NSFX> Sheriff of Hertford
2 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> Sheriff of Hertford
2 CONT REFN: HWS5593
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 9G8Z-FB
2 CONT OBJE: C:\LEGACY\PICTURES\C_Vicomte.gif
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose, Fourth Lord of Bramber (1140/1150 - August 9, 1211 ) a
2 CONC t his peak was also lord of Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth , Rad
2 CONC nor, Kington, Limerick, Skenfrith, Grosmont, and Whitecastle. Hi s rise a
2 CONC nd fall at the hands of king John is often taken as an exampl e of that k
2 CONC ing's arbitrary and capricious behavior towards his barons.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of William de Braose, Third Lord of Bramber and Be r
2 CONC tha of Hereford, daughter of Miles Fitz Walter, Earl of Hereford an d h
2 CONC is wife, formerly Sibyl de Neufmarche. From his father he inherite d th
2 CONC e Rape of Bramber, in Sussex, and through his mother he inherite d a la
2 CONC rge estate in the Welsh Marches.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1175, William carried out the Massacre of Abergavenny, killing seve r
2 CONC al Welsh princes to avenge the death of his uncle Henry, Earl of Here f
2 CONC ord, after having invited them to a feast at Abergavenny Castle. Thi s r
2 CONC esulted in great hostility against him among the Welsh, who named h im t
2 CONC he "Ogre of Abergavenny".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199, William fought beside King Richard the Lion-heart at Chalus , w
2 CONC here Richard was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was greatly favored by King John early in his reign. John granted h i
2 CONC m all that he might conquer from the Welsh in Radnor, gave him lordsh i
2 CONC p over Limerick in Ireland (save for the city itself), possession o f G
2 CONC lamorgan castle, and then lordship over Gower.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1203, William was put in charge of Arthur of Brittany, whom he ha d p
2 CONC ersonally captured the previous year. William was suspected of invo lve
2 CONC ment in Arthur's disappearance, although no concrete evidence eve r cam
2 CONC e to light. There is somewhat better evidence that he at least kn ew th
2 CONC e truth of the matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1206 John gave William the three great castles of Gwent (Skenfrith , G
2 CONC rosmont, and Whitecastle). At this point only an earldom separate d him f
2 CONC rom the greatest in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT But soon after William fell out of favor with the king. The precise re a
2 CONC sons remain obscure. John's stated reasons regard money de Braose owe d t
2 CONC he crown. But the king's actions went far beyond what would be nece ssa
2 CONC ry to recover the debt. Instead, he evidently wanted to break de Bu rgh
2 CONC , and to that end invaded Wales to seize the de Braose domains ther e. B
2 CONC eyond that, he sought de Braose's wife, who, the story goes, had m ade n
2 CONC o secret of her belief that John had murdered Arthur of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT De Braose fled to Ireland, then returned to Wales as John hunted him i n I
2 CONC reland. In Wales, William allied himself to the Welsh prince Llewel yn a
2 CONC nd helped him in rebellion against King John.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1210, William fled in disguise to France and died the following yea r a
2 CONC t Corbeil. William's wife, Maud de St. Valery, and eldest son, Will iam
2 CONC , were captured and murdered by King John, possibly starved to deat h.
2 CONT
2 CONT While William had aroused the jealousy of the other barons during hi s r
2 CONC ise, the arbitrary and violent manner of his fall very likely disco mfi
2 CONC ted them and played a role in the baronial uprisings of the next de cad
2 CONC e. The historian Sidney Painter, in his biography of King John, cal led i
2 CONC t "the greatest mistake John made during his reign, as the king r eveal
2 CONC ed to his barons once and for all his capacity for cruelty".
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, William's third son, Reginald de Braose reacquired some o f h
2 CONC is father's titles and lands. The middle son, Giles, was Bishop o f Her
2 CONC eford from 1200 until his death in 1215.
2 CONT
2 CONT William also had a daughter, Margaret, who married Walter de Lacy, Lor d o
2 CONC f Meath.
