1 NOTE  He was the 2nd Baron of Mortimer, was summoned to Parliament 1306-1326 . T
2 CONC his nobleman, notorious in our histories as the paramour of Isabel , Qu
2 CONC een Consort of Edward II, was in his sixteenth year at the death o f hi
2 CONC s father. He married Jaone, daughter of Peter de Genville, Lord o f Tri
2 CONC m, in Ireland. In 34th of Edward I, about 1306, he received the h onour o
2 CONC f Knighthood. He aided in the Scottish wars, and in 3rd of Edwa rd II, 1
2 CONC 310, he was made Governor of the Castle of Buelt, and later wa s Lord L
2 CONC ieutenant of Ireland. During the latter part of Edward II's re ign he a
2 CONC ttached himself to the Queen, and at length fled with her an d Prince E
2 CONC dward to France. He later returned and was made Earl of Marc h soon aft
2 CONC er the accession of Edward III. He here upon became proud be yond measu
2 CONC re (so that his son Geoffrey called him the King of Folly) a nd assumed r
2 CONC oyal authority. His career was not however of long continu ance, for Ki
2 CONC ng Edward III, becoming sensible of his folly and vices, h ad him seize
2 CONC d in the Castle of Queen Isabel in Nottingham and was conv icted under v
2 CONC arious charges, the first was complicity in the murder o f Edward II, a
2 CONC nd receiving sentence of death was hanged in 1330. He le ft by Joan de G
2 CONC eneville 4 sons and 7 daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT By marriage to Joanna de Geneville, a later Roger Mortimer (1287-1330 ) s
2 CONC ecured possession of Ludlow Castle. This became the family's princi pal p
2 CONC ower base for the next six generations.
2 CONT Roger Mortimer was a very powerful and ambitious Marcher Lord. He wa s t
2 CONC he first of several members of his family to attempt to seize the t hro
2 CONC ne of England. He fought the Scottish Wars and made attempts to rem ove t
2 CONC he King's favorites, at first with some success. In 1323 he was i mpris
2 CONC oned in the Tower of London, but escaped to France, an event he l ater c
2 CONC ommemorated by building St Peter's chapel in the outer bailey o f Ludlo
2 CONC w Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In France, Mortimer formed an alliance with Queen Isabella, who had de s
2 CONC erted her effeminate husband, King Edward II of England. They raise d a
2 CONC n army, invaded England and forced Edward to abdicate in favor of h is y
2 CONC oungest son, the future Edward III. Mortimer entertained Isabell a at h
2 CONC is castles on the Welsh borders and they became famous lovers. M eanwhi
2 CONC le, Edward II was cruelly murdered at Berkeley Castle in 1327.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Edward's death, Mortimer, acting as regent, was the virtua l r
2 CONC uler of England, but he over-reached himself and aroused the ange r of o
2 CONC ther barons. In October 1330 he was arrested at Nottingham and s entenc
2 CONC ed to death. He was executed at Tyburn in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, the ambitions of the Mortimers became part of the great dynasti c s
2 CONC truggles of the mid-15th century which became known as the "War o f the R
2 CONC oses."
2 CONT
2 CONT From Encyclopedia Britannica Online, article titled: "March, Roger Mor t
2 CONC imer, 1st Earl of, 8th Baron Wigmore"
2 CONT
2 CONT "lover of the English King Edward II's queen, Isabella of France, wit h w
2 CONC hom he contrived Edward's deposition and murder (1327). For three y ear
2 CONC s thereafter he was virtual king of England during the minority o f Edw
2 CONC ard III.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The descendant of Norman knights who had accompanied William the Conq u
2 CONC eror, he inherited wealthy family estates and fortunes, principally i n W
2 CONC ales and Ireland, and in 1304 became 8th Baron of Wigmore on the de ath o
2 CONC f his father, the 7th baron. He devoted the early years of his ma jorit
2 CONC y to obtaining effective control of his Irish lordships against h is wi
2 CONC fe's kinsmen, the Lacys, who summoned to their aid Edward Bruce , broth
2 CONC er of King Robert I of Scotland, when he was fighting to becom e king o
2 CONC f Ireland. In 1316 Mortimer was defeated at Kells and withdre w to Engl
2 CONC and, but afterward, as King Edward II's lieutenant in Irelan d (Novembe
2 CONC r 1316), he was largely instrumental in overcoming Bruce an d in drivin
2 CONC g the Lacys from Meath.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1317 he was associated with the Earl of Pembroke's "middle party " i
2 CONC n English politics; but distrust of the Despensers (see Despenser , Hug
2 CONC h Le and Hugh Le) drove him, in common with other marcher lords , into o
2 CONC pposition and violent conflict with the Despensers in South Wa les in 1
2 CONC 321. But, receiving no help from Edward II's other enemies, Ro ger and h
2 CONC is uncle Roger Mortimer of Chirk made their submission in Jan uary 1322
2 CONC . Imprisoned in the Tower of London, Roger escaped in 1323 an d fled to F
2 CONC rance, where in 1325 he was joined by Queen Isabella, who b ecame his m
2 CONC istress.  The exiles invaded England in September 1326; th e fall of th
2 CONC e Despensers was followed by the deposition of Edward II a nd his subse
2 CONC quent murder (1327), in which Mortimer was deeply implicat ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thereafter, as the queen's paramour, Mortimer virtually ruled England . H
2 CONC e used his position to further his own ends. Created Earl of Marc h in O
2 CONC ctober 1328, he secured for himself the lordships of Denbigh, Os westry
2 CONC , and Clun, formerly belonging to the Earl of Arundel; the march er lor
2 CONC dships of the Mortimers of Chirk; and Montgomery, granted to hi m by th
2 CONC e queen. His insatiable avarice, his arrogance, and his unpopul ar poli
2 CONC cy toward Scotland aroused against Mortimer a general revulsio n among h
2 CONC is fellow barons, and in October 1330 the young king Edward I II, at th
2 CONC e instigation of Henry of Lancaster, had him seized at Nottin gham and c
2 CONC onveyed to the Tower. Condemned for crimes declared to be no torious by h
2 CONC is peers in Parliament, he was hanged at Tyburn as a trait or, and his e
2 CONC states were forfeited to the crown."
2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 6983
2 CONT The Mortimers were descended from Roger de Mortemer of Mortemer-s ur-E a
2 CONC ulane in Normandy, a supporter of William t he Conqueror.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their main castle was at Wigmore, eight miles west of Ludlow. They ha d l
2 CONC ands mainly in Herefordshire and Shropshire, including Cleobury (Mo rti
2 CONC mer) on the edge of the hunting forest of Wyre. By marriage to Joan na d
2 CONC e Geneville, a later Roger Mortimer (1287-1330) secured
2 CONT possession of Ludlow Castle. This became the family's principal powe r b
2 CONC ase for the next six generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Mortimer was a very powerful and ambitious Marcher Lord. He wa s t
2 CONC he first of several members of his family to attempt to seize the t hro
2 CONC ne of England. He fought the Scottish Wars and made attempts to rem ove t
2 CONC he King's favorites, at first with some success.  In 1323 he wa s impri
2 CONC soned in the Tower of London, but escaped to France , an even t he late
2 CONC r
2 CONT commemorated by building St Peter's chapel in the outer bailey of Ludl o
2 CONC w Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In France, Mortimer formed an alliance with Queen Isabella , who had d e
2 CONC serted her effeminate husband, King Edward II of England . They raise d a
2 CONC n army, invaded England and forced Edward to abdicate in f avor o f his y
2 CONC oungest son, the future Edward III. Mortimer entertained Isabel la at h
2 CONC is castles on the Welsh borders and they became famous lover s . Meanwh
2 CONC ile, Edward II was cruelly murdered at Berkeley Castle in 13 27.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Edward's death, Mortimer, acting as regent, wa s the virtua l r
2 CONC uler of England, but he over-reached himself and arouse d the ange r of o
2 CONC ther barons. In October 1330 he was arrested at Nottingham and s entenc
2 CONC ed to death. He was executed at Tyburn in London.
2 CONT Later, the ambitions of the Mortimers became part of the great dynasti c s
2 CONC truggles of the mid-15th century which became known as the "War o f the R
2 CONC oses."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludlow Castle is first referred to by chroniclers in 1138 , but its da t
2 CONC e of origin is not certain. The architecture suggests that the curtai n w
2 CONC all of the inner bailey, its flanking towers and parts of the gateh ous
2 CONC e-keep date from the late 11th century. The site of Ludlow was i n a co
2 CONC rner of the important manor of Stanton, held since 1066 by the d e Lacy f
2 CONC amily.
2 CONT The level building surface and the steep slopes to the north and wes t m
2 CONC ade this a fine
2 CONT defensive position. The rivers Teme and Corve gave further protection . M
2 CONC ost of the castle was built of chunky Silurian limestone quarried f rom i
2 CONC ts own site. It was one of a line of Norman castles along the Mar ches, b
2 CONC uilt to pacify the countryside and hold back the unconquered We lsh.
2 CONT The de Lacys and their heirs retained the lordship until the late 13t h c
2 CONC entury, but in the civil wars of King Stephen's reign it was held b y t
2 CONC heir enemy, Joce de Dinan. In 1139 Stephen himself besieged the cas tle a
2 CONC nd showed great bravery by rescuing his ally, young Prince Henr y of Sc
2 CONC otland, from a grappling iron. The de Lacy's spent much of thei r time i
2 CONC n Ireland, where they won great estates; but Ludlow remaine d a major p
2 CONC ower base. At times it was taken into royal hands, as in 11 77 and afte
2 CONC rwards, when the Pipe Rolls record regular payments 'to th e keeper of L
2 CONC udlow Castle'. Many meetings were held here, as in 1224 , when Henry II
2 CONC I made a treaty with the Welsh prince Llywelyn ap Iorwe rth , with Arch
2 CONC bishop
2 CONT Langton as mediator.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the last male de Lacy died about 1240, the family estates were di v
2 CONC ided between his two daughters. The castle eventually came into the p o
2 CONC ssession of Geoffrey de Geneville, a French baron from Champagne wh o w
2 CONC as a  distant relative of Eleanor, queen to Edward I. Geoffrey spen t m
2 CONC ost of his time in Ireland and in 1283 he gave his lands at Ludlo w to h
2 CONC is son
2 CONT Peter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The refurbished castle made a useful base for Roger Mortimer of Wigmor e
2 CONC , who married a daughter of Peter de Geneville.  He was the leader o f a g
2 CONC roup of barons  who dethroned the unpopular Edward II in 1 326. M ortim
2 CONC er was created Earl of March but he over-reached himself and wa s depos
2 CONC ed
2 CONT !Fix This Location-919
2 CONT
2 CONT He Then Ruled England In The Name of Edward's Son, Edward II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources states that he was born on May 3, 1287.
