1 NOTE  Name Prefix:<NPFX> King
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8XJ4-1K
2 CONT
2 CONT SIGNED THE MAGNA CHARTA
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew Paris wrote, 'Foul as it is, hell itself is defiled by the pre s
2 CONC ence of King John', and this pretty well sums up John's reputation--u n
2 CONC til 1944, that is. For in that year Professor Galbraith demonstrate d i
2 CONC n a lecture to an astonished world that the chief chronicle sourc e for t
2 CONC he reign of John was utterly unreliable. Since then bad King Jo hn has b
2 CONC een getting better and better, until now he is nearly well aga in, and a l
2 CONC eading scholar in the field has seriously warned us that th e twentieth c
2 CONC entury could well create its own John myth.
2 CONT
2 CONT A man who can create so many myths, or rather have them created abou t h
2 CONC im, is clearly outstanding in some way, but the myths hide the trut h. P
2 CONC lainly the chroniclers who invented stories about him after his de ath c
2 CONC an tell us little, and we should not take too much notice of peop le wh
2 CONC o condemned John for carrying out his father's (and his brother' s offi
2 CONC cials'] policies and administrative routines, nor indeed those w ho con
2 CONC demned him because of the bitter troubles that happened in the s ucceed
2 CONC ing reign, troubles which were in no means entirely of John's ma king. R
2 CONC ecent historians have turned to the administrative records of h is reig
2 CONC n, and found there a very different picture; but still the ling ering d
2 CONC oubts remain--were these records the result of John's skill an d applic
2 CONC ation or of those of his able staff&#00063;
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a paunchy little man, five feet five inches tall, with erec t h
2 CONC ead, staring eyes, flaring nostrils and thick lips set in a cruel p out
2 CONC ,as his splendid monument at Worcester shows. He had the tempestou s na
2 CONC ture of all his family, and a driving demoniac energy: Professor B arlo
2 CONC w says that 'he prowled around his kingdom,' which is an evocativ e phr
2 CONC ase, but it would be truer to say that he raced around it. He wa s fast
2 CONC idious about his person--taking more baths than several other me dieval k
2 CONC ings put together, and owning the ultimate in luxury, for tha t time, a d
2 CONC ressing-gown. He loved good food and drink, and gambled a g reat deal, t
2 CONC hough he usually lost--the results of his typical impatien ce and carel
2 CONC essness are recorded on his expense rolls; above all thing s he loved w
2 CONC omen. Some say his 'elopment' was the cause of his loss o f Normandy. H
2 CONC e was generous to the poor (for instance, he remitted t o them the pena
2 CONC lties of the forest law), and to his servants; at the l east he went th
2 CONC rough the motions of being a Christian king. He was ext ortionate, thou
2 CONC gh if one considers the terrific increase in his outgoi ngs (a mercenar
2 CONC y soldier cost him 200 per cent more in wages than he w ould have in He
2 CONC nry II's day)one can understand some of his actions i n the field. He w
2 CONC as deeply concerned about justice, took care to atten d to court busine
2 CONC ss, and listened to supplicants with sympathy; he ha d also an urgent d
2 CONC esire for peace in the land, saying that his peace w as to be observed '
2 CONC even if we have granted it to a dog.' But for all t hat,he had two tota
2 CONC lly unredeeming vices; he was suspicious, and enjoy ed a cloak-and-dagg
2 CONC er atmosphere--simply he did not inspire trust in h is subjects. Dr. Wa
2 CONC rren says of him with some justice that if he had l ived in the twentie
2 CONC th centure he would have adored to run a secret pol ice.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Oxford on Christmas Eve 1167. He was oblated for a mon k a
2 CONC t the abbey of Fontevrault at the age of one year, but was back a t cou
2 CONC rt by the time he was six--plainly he had no vocation, but he pro bably p
2 CONC icked up at this early stage his fastidiousness and his passio n for bo
2 CONC oks: his library followed him wherever he went. He was his fat her's fa
2 CONC vourite, but he turned against the old man when his chance cam e, as he d
2 CONC id against Richard (who had been very generous to his brothe r) when th
2 CONC e latter was in captivity in 1193. The episode was a miserab le failure
2 CONC , but it possibly sowed the seeds of distrust for John in En gland, whe
2 CONC re they began to sprout luxuriantly in 1199 when Richard die d and John c
2 CONC ame to the throne.
